{
  "files" : {
    "LICENSE.txt" : "GateLab Essentials are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal.\nTo the extent possible under law, the authors have waived all copyright and related or\nneighboring rights to these cores. https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\n",
    "cores/analog_clock/README.md" : "# Analogue clock layer\n\nDraws a round clock over a video picture: an outer ring, hour ticks and (optionally) minute\nticks, hour, minute and second hands and a centre cap. Give it the time as `hour` (0–23),\n`minute` and `second` (0–59), e.g. from a microcontroller through `spi_registers`; the hour hand\nmoves on with the minutes and the minute hand with the seconds.\n\nIt chains like `sprite_layer`, `text_layer` and `bar_graph`: pixel stream in, the same stream 20\nclocks later with the clock on top.\n\n```verilog\nanalog_clock #(.X_BITS(12), .Y_BITS(11), .CENTER_X(960), .CENTER_Y(480), .RADIUS(150),\n               .HOUR_LENGTH(80), .HOUR_WIDTH(12), .MINUTE_LENGTH(120), .MINUTE_WIDTH(8),\n               .SECOND_LENGTH(132), .SECOND_WIDTH(3), .CAP(9)) u_clock (\n    .clk(pixel_clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .red(r0), .green(g0), .blue(b0),\n    .enable(1'b1), .hour(hour), .minute(minute), .second(second),\n    .x_out(x1), .y_out(y1), .de_out(de1), .hsync_out(hs1), .vsync_out(vs1),\n    .red_out(r1), .green_out(g1), .blue_out(b1), .covered()\n);\n```\n\n## How it draws\n\nA pipelined CORDIC (13 stages of adders) gives every pixel's angle, clockwise from 12 o'clock in\n1/16384 of a turn, and its distance from the centre. The ring, the ticks and the cap are tests\non those two. A hand covers a pixel within its length when the distance across the hand\n(radius × angle difference) is at most half its width, so the hands are equally wide along their\nwhole length; that takes one small multiply per hand. Near the centre a hand's base is a short\nwedge: a cap of at least 0.65 × the widest hand hides it.\n\nOrder from the top: cap, second hand, minute hand, hour hand, hour ticks, minute ticks, ring,\nface (with `FILL = 1`), the picture below.\n\n## Accuracy\n\nEdges are within half a pixel of the exact geometry. The testbench compares every pixel of a\nclock of radius 90 at four times with a reference computed in real numbers (`$sqrt`,\n`$atan2`), skipping only pixels within half a pixel of an edge. Planted errors that move a\nfeature by more than that are caught, e.g. a CORDIC angle 0.75° off.\n\n## Testbench\n\n`tb/analog_clock_tb.v`: 10:10:30, 3:45:05, 23:59:59 and 12:00:00 (all hands up, so their order\nshows), the picture below and the timing passed through unchanged; about 6 s in Icarus.\n",
    "cores/analog_clock/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Draws an analogue clock over a video picture: outer ring, hour and minute ticks, hour, minute and second hands of even width and a centre cap, from hour, minute and second inputs. Each pixel's angle and radius come from a pipelined CORDIC (adders only); three small multiplies test the hands. Layers chain like sprite_layer, text_layer and bar_graph; LATENCY = 20 clocks.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 126.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"MULT18X18D\" : 4,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 1948,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 1020,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 24\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 59.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 24,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 512,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 218,\n        \"CPE_COMP\" : 122,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 639,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 1068,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 1180,\n        \"CPE_MULT\" : 162,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 128,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 89.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 1220,\n        \"DFF\" : 1020,\n        \"DUMMY_CELL\" : 12,\n        \"LUT4\" : 911,\n        \"MULT18X18\" : 3,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 1,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 24\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 32,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 2740,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 3,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 4\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 61.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 2808,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 1020,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 24\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 175.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 248,\n        \"DSP48E1\" : 3,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 914,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 2335\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"analog_clock\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"clock\",\n    \"analogue\",\n    \"analog\",\n    \"watch\",\n    \"dial\",\n    \"dashboard\",\n    \"video\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"vga\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"overlay\",\n    \"hmi\",\n    \"cordic\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Zeichnet eine Analoguhr über ein Videobild: äußerer Ring, Stunden- und Minutenstriche, Stunden-, Minuten- und Sekundenzeiger gleichmäßiger Breite und eine Mittelkappe, aus den Eingängen für Stunde, Minute und Sekunde. Winkel und Radius jedes Pixels liefert ein CORDIC in Pipeline (nur Addierer); drei kleine Multiplikationen prüfen die Zeiger. Ebenen lassen sich verketten wie sprite_layer, text_layer und bar_graph; LATENCY = 20 Takte.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Analoguhr-Ebene\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Analogue clock layer\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 11,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of x (video_timing's x)\",\n      \"name\" : \"X_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of y\",\n      \"name\" : \"Y_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 160,\n      \"description\" : \"Screen column of the clock's centre\",\n      \"name\" : \"CENTER_X\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 120,\n      \"description\" : \"Screen row of the clock's centre\",\n      \"name\" : \"CENTER_Y\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 100,\n      \"description\" : \"Outer radius in pixels (up to 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"RADIUS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the outer ring in pixels (0 = none)\",\n      \"name\" : \"RING\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12,\n      \"description\" : \"Length of the hour ticks inside the ring (0 = no ticks)\",\n      \"name\" : \"TICK_LENGTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the hour ticks in pixels\",\n      \"name\" : \"TICK_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"1: minute ticks, half as long as the hour ticks\",\n      \"name\" : \"MINUTE_TICKS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the minute ticks in pixels\",\n      \"name\" : \"MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 55,\n      \"description\" : \"Length of the hour hand (0 = none)\",\n      \"name\" : \"HOUR_LENGTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the hour hand in pixels\",\n      \"name\" : \"HOUR_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 80,\n      \"description\" : \"Length of the minute hand (0 = none)\",\n      \"name\" : \"MINUTE_LENGTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 6,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the minute hand\",\n      \"name\" : \"MINUTE_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 88,\n      \"description\" : \"Length of the second hand (0 = none)\",\n      \"name\" : \"SECOND_LENGTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the second hand\",\n      \"name\" : \"SECOND_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 6,\n      \"description\" : \"Radius of the centre cap (0 = none); at least 0.65 × the widest hand hides the hands' bases\",\n      \"name\" : \"CAP\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: FACE_COLOUR inside the ring where nothing else is drawn\",\n      \"name\" : \"FILL\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The face as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"FACE_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The ring\",\n      \"name\" : \"RING_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The hour ticks\",\n      \"name\" : \"TICK_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The minute ticks\",\n      \"name\" : \"MINUTE_TICK_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The hour hand\",\n      \"name\" : \"HOUR_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The minute hand\",\n      \"name\" : \"MINUTE_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The second hand\",\n      \"name\" : \"SECOND_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The centre cap\",\n      \"name\" : \"CAP_COLOUR\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel column from video_timing (or the previous layer's x_out)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"x\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel row\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"y\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: the pixel is visible\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"0: the clock isn't drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"enable\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Hours, 0–23\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hour\",\n      \"width\" : 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Minutes, 0–59\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"minute\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Seconds, 0–59\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"second\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"x, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"x_out\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"y, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"y_out\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"de, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"de_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"hsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"vsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red with the clock drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"red_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green with the clock drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"green_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue with the clock drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The clock draws this output pixel\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"covered\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"analog_clock\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/analog_clock/rtl/analog_clock.v" : "// Analogue clock layer: draws a round clock face over the picture passing through — an outer\n// ring, hour ticks (and, if wanted, minute ticks), hour, minute and second hands and a centre\n// cap. The picture arrives as a pixel stream (position, timing and the colour below) and leaves\n// LATENCY = 20 clocks later with the clock on top; position and timing leave with it, so layers\n// chain like sprite_layer, text_layer and bar_graph.\n//\n// Every pixel's angle and distance from the centre come from a pipelined CORDIC (adders only,\n// 13 stages). The angle is measured clockwise from 12 o'clock in units of 1/16384 of a turn.\n// Ticks and the ring are tested by angle and radius; a hand covers a pixel when it is valid\n// the hand's length and its distance across the hand (radius × angle difference) is valid half\n// the hand's width, so hands have the same width along their length. Three small multiplies\n// (radius × angle difference) do that; everything else is adders and comparators.\n//\n// `hour` (0–23), `minute` and `second` (0–59) may change at any time; the hour hand moves on\n// with the minutes and the minute hand with the seconds. A hand of length 0 is not drawn.\n// RADIUS up to 1000 pixels.\n\nmodule analog_clock #(\n    parameter X_BITS = 11,\n    parameter Y_BITS = 10,\n    parameter CENTER_X = 160,\n    parameter CENTER_Y = 120,\n    parameter RADIUS = 100,\n    parameter RING = 4,\n    parameter TICK_LENGTH = 12,\n    parameter TICK_WIDTH = 4,\n    parameter MINUTE_TICKS = 1,\n    parameter MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH = 2,\n    parameter HOUR_LENGTH = 55,\n    parameter HOUR_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter MINUTE_LENGTH = 80,\n    parameter MINUTE_WIDTH = 6,\n    parameter SECOND_LENGTH = 88,\n    parameter SECOND_WIDTH = 2,\n    parameter CAP = 6,\n    parameter FILL = 0,\n    parameter [23:0] FACE_COLOUR = 24'h101820,\n    parameter [23:0] RING_COLOUR = 24'hC0C8D0,\n    parameter [23:0] TICK_COLOUR = 24'hFFFFFF,\n    parameter [23:0] MINUTE_TICK_COLOUR = 24'h808890,\n    parameter [23:0] HOUR_COLOUR = 24'hFFFFFF,\n    parameter [23:0] MINUTE_COLOUR = 24'hFFFFFF,\n    parameter [23:0] SECOND_COLOUR = 24'hFF4040,\n    parameter [23:0] CAP_COLOUR = 24'hFF4040\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire [X_BITS-1:0] x,\n    input  wire [Y_BITS-1:0] y,\n    input  wire de,\n    input  wire hsync,\n    input  wire vsync,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire enable,\n    input  wire [4:0] hour,\n    input  wire [5:0] minute,\n    input  wire [5:0] second,\n    output reg  [X_BITS-1:0] x_out = 0,\n    output reg  [Y_BITS-1:0] y_out = 0,\n    output reg  de_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  hsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  vsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [7:0] red_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] green_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] blue_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  covered = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam LATENCY = 20;\n    localparam ITERATIONS = 13;\n    localparam [13:0] HALF_TURN = 14'd8192;\n\n    // atan(2^-i) in 1/16384 turns.\n    function integer atan_step(input integer i);\n        case (i)\n            0: atan_step = 2048;  1: atan_step = 1209; 2: atan_step = 639; 3: atan_step = 324;\n            4: atan_step = 163;   5: atan_step = 81;   6: atan_step = 41;  7: atan_step = 20;\n            8: atan_step = 10;    9: atan_step = 5;    10: atan_step = 3;  11: atan_step = 1;\n            default: atan_step = 1;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    // A radius in pixels as the CORDIC reports it: × 32 (input scale) × 1.64676 (gain).\n    function integer scaled(input integer pixels);\n        scaled = (pixels * 53961 + 512) / 1024;\n    endfunction\n\n    // Half a tick's width in pixels, as an angle at `radius` (1/16384 turns, at least 1).\n    function integer tick_angle(input integer width, input integer radius);\n        tick_angle = (radius < 1) ? 1 : (((width * 1304 + radius / 2) / radius) < 1 ? 1 : (width * 1304 + radius / 2) / radius);\n    endfunction\n\n    function integer max3(input integer a, input integer b, input integer c);\n        max3 = (a > b) ? ((a > c) ? a : c) : ((b > c) ? b : c);\n    endfunction\n\n    // The CORDIC's width: a corner of the box (radius × √2 × the scale) with its sign.\n    localparam integer W = $clog2(scaled(RADIUS) * 3 / 2 + 1) + 1;\n    // The radius the hands' multiplies need: up to the longest hand, divided by 8.\n    localparam integer RB = $clog2(scaled(max3(HOUR_LENGTH, MINUTE_LENGTH, SECOND_LENGTH)) / 8 + 2);\n\n    localparam integer R_FACE = scaled(RADIUS);\n    localparam integer R_RING = scaled(RADIUS - RING);\n    localparam integer R_TICK = scaled(RADIUS - RING - TICK_LENGTH);\n    localparam integer R_MINUTE_TICK = scaled(RADIUS - RING - TICK_LENGTH / 2);\n    localparam integer R_HOUR = scaled(HOUR_LENGTH);\n    localparam integer R_MINUTE = scaled(MINUTE_LENGTH);\n    localparam integer R_SECOND = scaled(SECOND_LENGTH);\n    localparam integer R_HANDS = scaled(max3(HOUR_LENGTH, MINUTE_LENGTH, SECOND_LENGTH));\n    localparam integer R_CAP = scaled(CAP);\n    localparam integer TICK_SPAN = 12 * tick_angle(TICK_WIDTH, RADIUS - RING - TICK_LENGTH / 2);\n    localparam integer MINUTE_TICK_SPAN = 60 * tick_angle(MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH, RADIUS - RING - TICK_LENGTH / 4);\n    // Across-the-hand limits: (radius / 8) × angle ≤ width × 8588 (half the width in those units).\n    localparam integer HOUR_ACROSS = HOUR_WIDTH * 8588;\n    localparam integer MINUTE_ACROSS = MINUTE_WIDTH * 8588;\n    localparam integer SECOND_ACROSS = SECOND_WIDTH * 8588;\n\n    // ---- The hands' angles (1/16384 turns; × 4369 / 16 for 16384 / 60), by shifts and adds --\n    function [17:0] times4369(input [5:0] v);\n        times4369 = ({12'd0, v} << 12) + ({12'd0, v} << 8) + ({12'd0, v} << 4) + {12'd0, v};\n    endfunction\n    // Three registered steps (the time changes once a second; the clocks in between don't matter):\n    // the hour's step on the dial, the products, their sums.\n    reg [13:0] hour_angle = 0, minute_angle = 0, second_angle = 0;\n    reg [5:0] hour_step = 0, minute_q = 0, second_q = 0;\n    reg [17:0] second_4369 = 0, minute_4369 = 0, hour_4369 = 0;\n    reg [12:0] second_73 = 0, minute_91 = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        hour_step <= ((hour >= 5'd12) ? hour - 5'd12 : hour) * 3'd5;\n        minute_q <= minute;\n        second_q <= second;\n        second_4369 <= times4369(second_q);\n        minute_4369 <= times4369(minute_q);\n        hour_4369 <= times4369(hour_step);\n        second_73 <= ({7'd0, second_q} << 6) + ({7'd0, second_q} << 3) + {7'd0, second_q};   // up to 4307\n        minute_91 <= ({7'd0, minute_q} << 6) + ({7'd0, minute_q} << 4) + ({7'd0, minute_q} << 3) +\n                     ({7'd0, minute_q} << 1) + {7'd0, minute_q};\n        second_angle <= second_4369 >> 4;\n        minute_angle <= (minute_4369 >> 4) + (second_73 >> 4);\n        hour_angle <= (hour_4369 >> 4) + (minute_91 >> 2);\n    end\n\n    // ---- Stage 1: offset from the centre -------------------------------------------------------\n    wire signed [X_BITS+1:0] dx = $signed({2'b00, x}) - CENTER_X;\n    wire signed [Y_BITS+1:0] dy = $signed({2'b00, y}) - CENTER_Y;\n    reg signed [W-1:0] dx_1 = 0, dy_1 = 0;\n    reg in_box_1 = 1'b0;\n    // Each stage loads only when its pixel is in the clock's box (its valid bit): outside it the\n    // pipeline holds still (less switching, and a simulator has nothing to compute).\n    wire in_box = enable && de && dx >= -RADIUS && dx <= RADIUS && dy >= -RADIUS && dy <= RADIUS;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        in_box_1 <= in_box;\n        if (in_box) begin\n            dx_1 <= dx <<< 5;\n            dy_1 <= dy <<< 5;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Stage 2: into the right half-plane (clockwise from 12 o'clock: atan2(dx, -dy)) --------\n    // Packed per stage: stage k at [k*W +: W] (arrays written in a loop would be memories to Yosys).\n    reg [W*(ITERATIONS+1)-1:0] cx = 0;\n    reg [W*(ITERATIONS+1)-1:0] cy = 0;\n    reg [14*(ITERATIONS+1)-1:0] cz = 0;\n    reg [ITERATIONS:0] valid = 0;   // (`inside` and `within` are SystemVerilog keywords)\n\n    // ---- Stages 3–15: CORDIC vectoring (y driven to 0, the angle collected in z) ---------------\n    // Add or subtract as one adder: v ± t = v + (t ^ mask) + carry. One clocked block for all the\n    // stages (a simulator then wakes one process a clock, and only that test while the pipeline\n    // is empty); the loop unrolls into one adder per stage.\n    // Skipping the idle pipeline is for simulators only: in hardware the wide OR in front of every\n    // enable would cost speed, and each stage's own valid bit already holds it.\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    localparam SKIP_IDLE = 0;\n`else\n    localparam SKIP_IDLE = 1;\n`endif\n    integer k;\n    reg signed [W-1:0] xs, ys, tx, ty;\n    reg [13:0] step;\n    reg negative;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        valid <= {valid[ITERATIONS-1:0], in_box_1};\n        if (SKIP_IDLE == 0 || in_box_1 || valid[ITERATIONS-1:0] != 0) begin\n            // Stage 2, up = -dy: into the right half-plane (up < 0: turn by half a turn).\n            if (in_box_1 && dy_1 > 0) begin\n                cx[0 +: W] <= dy_1;\n                cy[0 +: W] <= -dx_1;\n                cz[0 +: 14] <= HALF_TURN;\n            end else if (in_box_1) begin\n                cx[0 +: W] <= -dy_1;\n                cy[0 +: W] <= dx_1;\n                cz[0 +: 14] <= 14'd0;\n            end\n            for (k = 0; k < ITERATIONS; k = k + 1)\n                if (valid[k]) begin\n                    xs = cx[k*W +: W];\n                    ys = cy[k*W +: W];\n                    negative = ys[W-1];\n                    ty = ys >>> k;\n                    tx = xs >>> k;\n                    step = atan_step(k);\n                    cx[(k+1)*W +: W] <= xs + (ty ^ {W{negative}}) + negative;\n                    cy[(k+1)*W +: W] <= ys + (tx ^ {W{!negative}}) + !negative;\n                    cz[(k+1)*14 +: 14] <= cz[k*14 +: 14] + (step ^ {14{negative}}) + negative;\n                end\n        end\n    end\n\n    wire [W-1:0] radius = cx[ITERATIONS*W +: W];\n    wire [13:0] angle = cz[ITERATIONS*14 +: 14];\n\n    // ---- Stage 16: radial tests, the angle differences and the tick angles ---------------------\n    reg in_3 = 1'b0, face_3 = 1'b0, ring_3 = 1'b0, tick_zone_3 = 1'b0, minute_zone_3 = 1'b0, cap_3 = 1'b0;\n    reg hour_len_3 = 1'b0, minute_len_3 = 1'b0, second_len_3 = 1'b0;\n    reg [RB-1:0] radius_3 = 0;\n    reg [13:0] hour_d_3 = 0, minute_d_3 = 0, second_d_3 = 0, around12_3 = 0, around60_3 = 0;\n\n    // ---- Stage 17: tick sectors, |angle difference| within 45° --------------------------------\n    function [11:0] near_angle(input [13:0] d);   // {below 45°, |d| as 11 bits}\n        reg [13:0] magnitude;\n        begin\n            magnitude = d[13] ? -d : d;\n            near_angle = {magnitude < 14'd2048, magnitude[10:0]};\n        end\n    endfunction\n    wire [11:0] hour_n = near_angle(hour_d_3);\n    wire [11:0] minute_n = near_angle(minute_d_3);\n    wire [11:0] second_n = near_angle(second_d_3);\n\n    reg in_4 = 1'b0, face_4 = 1'b0, ring_4 = 1'b0, tick_4 = 1'b0, minute_tick_4 = 1'b0, cap_4 = 1'b0;\n    reg hour_ok_4 = 1'b0, minute_ok_4 = 1'b0, second_ok_4 = 1'b0;\n    reg [RB-1:0] radius_4 = 0;\n    reg [10:0] hour_a_4 = 0, minute_a_4 = 0, second_a_4 = 0;\n\n    // ---- Stage 18: across the hands, (radius / 8) × angle difference ---------------------------\n    reg in_5 = 1'b0, face_5 = 1'b0, ring_5 = 1'b0, tick_5 = 1'b0, minute_tick_5 = 1'b0, cap_5 = 1'b0;\n    reg hour_ok_5 = 1'b0, minute_ok_5 = 1'b0, second_ok_5 = 1'b0;\n    reg [RB+10:0] hour_across_5 = 0, minute_across_5 = 0, second_across_5 = 0;\n\n    // ---- Stage 19: on the hands ----------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg in_6 = 1'b0, face_6 = 1'b0, ring_6 = 1'b0, tick_6 = 1'b0, minute_tick_6 = 1'b0, cap_6 = 1'b0;\n    reg hour_6 = 1'b0, minute_6 = 1'b0, second_6 = 1'b0;\n\n    // Stages 16–19 in one clocked block (one process for a simulator, skipped while idle).\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (SKIP_IDLE == 0 || valid[ITERATIONS] || in_3 || in_4 || in_5 || in_6) begin\n            in_3 <= valid[ITERATIONS];\n            if (valid[ITERATIONS]) begin\n                radius_3 <= (radius > R_HANDS) ? {RB{1'b1}} : radius >> 3;\n                face_3 <= radius <= R_FACE;\n                ring_3 <= RING > 0 && radius > R_RING && radius <= R_FACE;\n                tick_zone_3 <= TICK_LENGTH > 0 && radius > R_TICK && radius <= R_RING;\n                minute_zone_3 <= MINUTE_TICKS != 0 && TICK_LENGTH > 0 && radius > R_MINUTE_TICK && radius <= R_RING;\n                cap_3 <= CAP > 0 && radius <= R_CAP;\n                hour_len_3 <= HOUR_LENGTH > 0 && radius <= R_HOUR;\n                minute_len_3 <= MINUTE_LENGTH > 0 && radius <= R_MINUTE;\n                second_len_3 <= SECOND_LENGTH > 0 && radius <= R_SECOND;\n                hour_d_3 <= angle - hour_angle;\n                minute_d_3 <= angle - minute_angle;\n                second_d_3 <= angle - second_angle;\n                around12_3 <= (angle << 3) + (angle << 2);\n                around60_3 <= (angle << 6) - (angle << 2);\n            end\n            in_4 <= in_3;\n            if (in_3) begin\n                face_4 <= face_3;\n                ring_4 <= ring_3;\n                cap_4 <= cap_3;\n                tick_4 <= tick_zone_3 && (around12_3 < TICK_SPAN || around12_3 > 16384 - TICK_SPAN);\n                minute_tick_4 <= minute_zone_3 && (around60_3 < MINUTE_TICK_SPAN || around60_3 > 16384 - MINUTE_TICK_SPAN);\n                hour_ok_4 <= hour_len_3 && hour_n[11];\n                minute_ok_4 <= minute_len_3 && minute_n[11];\n                second_ok_4 <= second_len_3 && second_n[11];\n                radius_4 <= radius_3;\n                hour_a_4 <= hour_n[10:0];\n                minute_a_4 <= minute_n[10:0];\n                second_a_4 <= second_n[10:0];\n            end\n            in_5 <= in_4;\n            if (in_4) begin\n                {face_5, ring_5, tick_5, minute_tick_5, cap_5} <= {face_4, ring_4, tick_4, minute_tick_4, cap_4};\n                {hour_ok_5, minute_ok_5, second_ok_5} <= {hour_ok_4, minute_ok_4, second_ok_4};\n                hour_across_5 <= radius_4 * hour_a_4;\n                minute_across_5 <= radius_4 * minute_a_4;\n                second_across_5 <= radius_4 * second_a_4;\n            end\n            in_6 <= in_5;\n            if (in_5) begin\n                {face_6, ring_6, tick_6, minute_tick_6, cap_6} <= {face_5, ring_5, tick_5, minute_tick_5, cap_5};\n                hour_6 <= hour_ok_5 && hour_across_5 <= HOUR_ACROSS;\n                minute_6 <= minute_ok_5 && minute_across_5 <= MINUTE_ACROSS;\n                second_6 <= second_ok_5 && second_across_5 <= SECOND_ACROSS;\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- The picture waits 19 clocks; stage 20 draws on it ------------------------------------\n    localparam PW = X_BITS + Y_BITS + 3 + 24;\n    localparam STAGES = LATENCY - 1;\n    // A ring of STAGES - 1 words, one written a clock and read back with one more register: the\n    // picture STAGES clocks late (one write a clock instead of shifting every word — less for a\n    // simulator; on the FPGA LUT RAM or registers).\n    localparam DEPTH = STAGES - 1;\n    reg [PW-1:0] delay [0:DEPTH-1];\n    reg [$clog2(DEPTH)-1:0] slot = 0;\n    reg [PW-1:0] last = 0;\n\n    // The picture's ring and the topmost feature's colour (worked out only for pixels in the box).\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        delay[slot] <= {x, y, de, hsync, vsync, red, green, blue};\n        last <= delay[slot];\n        slot <= (slot == DEPTH - 1) ? 0 : slot + 1'b1;\n        {x_out, y_out, de_out, hsync_out, vsync_out} <= last[PW-1:24];\n        covered <= 1'b0;\n        {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= last[23:0];\n        if (in_6) begin\n            covered <= 1'b1;\n            if (cap_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= CAP_COLOUR;\n            else if (second_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= SECOND_COLOUR;\n            else if (minute_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= MINUTE_COLOUR;\n            else if (hour_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= HOUR_COLOUR;\n            else if (tick_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= TICK_COLOUR;\n            else if (minute_tick_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= MINUTE_TICK_COLOUR;\n            else if (ring_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= RING_COLOUR;\n            else if (FILL != 0 && face_6) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= FACE_COLOUR;\n            else covered <= 1'b0;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/analog_clock/tb/analog_clock_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for analog_clock. A screen of 192 × 184 visible pixels (206 × 192 in\n// all) shows a clock of radius 90 at (96, 92) with every feature on (ring, hour and minute ticks,\n// three hands, cap, filled face) at four times: 10:10:30, 3:45:05, 23:59:59 and 12:00:00 (all\n// hands up, so their order shows). Every output pixel is compared with a reference that uses\n// real geometry — sqrt and atan2, not the RTL's CORDIC — and the angles the documentation gives\n// for the hands. Pixels within half a pixel of an edge are skipped (the CORDIC rounds); each\n// feature must still be met at enough definite pixels in every frame, and the picture below\n// and the timing must pass through unchanged.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule analog_clock_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 206, H_ACTIVE = 192, V_TOTAL = 192, V_ACTIVE = 184;\n    localparam CX = 96, CY = 92, R = 90, RING = 3, TICK_LENGTH = 12, TICK_WIDTH = 3, MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH = 2;\n    localparam HOUR_LENGTH = 50, HOUR_WIDTH = 10, MINUTE_LENGTH = 72, MINUTE_WIDTH = 6, SECOND_LENGTH = 80, SECOND_WIDTH = 2, CAP = 7;\n    localparam [23:0] C_FACE = 24'h101820, C_RING = 24'hC0C8D0, C_TICK = 24'hFFFF00, C_MTICK = 24'h808890,\n                      C_HOUR = 24'h40FF40, C_MINUTE = 24'h4080FF, C_SECOND = 24'hFF4040, C_CAP = 24'hFF00FF;\n    localparam LATENCY = 20;\n    localparam real K4 = 32.0 * 1.6467602417619722;\n    localparam real TURN = 16384.0;\n    localparam real PI = 3.14159265358979;\n\n    reg [7:0] x = 0;\n    reg [7:0] y = 0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    wire [7:0] red = {x[6:0], 1'b1};\n    wire [7:0] green = {y[6:0], 1'b0};\n    wire [7:0] blue = 8'h5A;\n    reg [4:0] hour = 10;\n    reg [5:0] minute = 10, second = 30;\n\n    wire [7:0] x_out, y_out;\n    wire de_out, hsync_out, vsync_out, covered;\n    wire [7:0] red_out, green_out, blue_out;\n\n    analog_clock #(\n        .X_BITS(8), .Y_BITS(8), .CENTER_X(CX), .CENTER_Y(CY), .RADIUS(R), .RING(RING),\n        .TICK_LENGTH(TICK_LENGTH), .TICK_WIDTH(TICK_WIDTH), .MINUTE_TICKS(1), .MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH(MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH),\n        .HOUR_LENGTH(HOUR_LENGTH), .HOUR_WIDTH(HOUR_WIDTH), .MINUTE_LENGTH(MINUTE_LENGTH), .MINUTE_WIDTH(MINUTE_WIDTH),\n        .SECOND_LENGTH(SECOND_LENGTH), .SECOND_WIDTH(SECOND_WIDTH), .CAP(CAP), .FILL(1),\n        .FACE_COLOUR(C_FACE), .RING_COLOUR(C_RING), .TICK_COLOUR(C_TICK), .MINUTE_TICK_COLOUR(C_MTICK),\n        .HOUR_COLOUR(C_HOUR), .MINUTE_COLOUR(C_MINUTE), .SECOND_COLOUR(C_SECOND), .CAP_COLOUR(C_CAP)\n    ) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .enable(1'b1), .hour(hour), .minute(minute), .second(second),\n        .x_out(x_out), .y_out(y_out), .de_out(de_out), .hsync_out(hsync_out), .vsync_out(vsync_out),\n        .red_out(red_out), .green_out(green_out), .blue_out(blue_out), .covered(covered)\n    );\n\n    // ---- The reference: the RTL's documented quantities in real numbers --------------------\n    // A radius in pixels as the RTL compares it (its constants are rounded).\n    function real limit(input integer pixels);\n        limit = ((pixels * 53961 + 512) / 1024) / K4;\n    endfunction\n    function integer tick_angle(input integer width, input integer radius);\n        tick_angle = ((width * 1304 + radius / 2) / radius) < 1 ? 1 : (width * 1304 + radius / 2) / radius;\n    endfunction\n    function real wrap(input real a);   // into [-TURN/2, TURN/2)\n        begin\n            wrap = a;\n            while (wrap >= TURN / 2) wrap = wrap - TURN;\n            while (wrap < -TURN / 2) wrap = wrap + TURN;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function real absr(input real v);\n        absr = v < 0 ? -v : v;\n    endfunction\n\n    localparam real EDGE = 0.5;   // pixels: closer to an edge than this is not checked\n    real r, a, hour_a, minute_a, second_a;\n    reg ambiguous;\n    integer cls;   // 0 below, 1 face, 2 ring, 3 minute tick, 4 tick, 5 hour, 6 minute, 7 second, 8 cap\n\n    // Radial test r <= limit(px): sets `ambiguous` when r is within EDGE of the limit.\n    function radial(input real radius, input integer pixels);\n        begin\n            radial = radius <= limit(pixels);\n            if (absr(radius - limit(pixels)) < EDGE) ambiguous = 1'b1;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    // Inside a tick sector: the angle × n, modulo a turn, within ±span.\n    function sector(input real angle, input integer n, input integer span, input real radius);\n        real residual;\n        begin\n            residual = wrap(angle * n);\n            sector = absr(residual) < span;\n            // Distance to the sector's edge along the circle, in pixels.\n            if (absr(absr(residual) - span) / n * 2.0 * PI / TURN * radius < EDGE) ambiguous = 1'b1;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    // On a hand: within its length, within 45° of it, and radius × angle within half its width.\n    function hand(input real radius, input real angle, input real hand_angle, input integer length, input integer width);\n        real d, across;\n        begin\n            d = absr(wrap(angle - hand_angle));\n            across = radius * d * 2.0 * PI / TURN;\n            hand = length > 0 && radius <= limit(length) && d < 2048 && across <= width / 2.0;\n            if (absr(across - width / 2.0) < EDGE && radius <= limit(length) + EDGE) ambiguous = 1'b1;\n            if (absr(radius - limit(length)) < EDGE && across <= width / 2.0 + EDGE) ambiguous = 1'b1;\n            // The 45° limit near the centre: the CORDIC's angle is a unit or two off.\n            if (absr(d - 2048) < 24 && radius <= limit(length) + EDGE && across <= width / 2.0 + EDGE) ambiguous = 1'b1;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task reference(input integer px, input integer py, output integer klass);\n        integer dx, dy;\n        reg face, ring, tick, mtick, on_hour, on_minute, on_second, cap;\n        begin\n            dx = px - CX;\n            dy = py - CY;\n            ambiguous = 1'b0;\n            klass = 0;\n            if (dx >= -R && dx <= R && dy >= -R && dy <= R) begin\n                r = $sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);\n                // Reals: Icarus passes a negated integer to $atan2 as unsigned.\n                a = $atan2(1.0 * dx, -1.0 * dy) / (2.0 * PI) * TURN;\n                if (a < 0) a = a + TURN;\n                face = radial(r, R);\n                ring = !radial(r, R - RING) && face;\n                tick = !radial(r, R - RING - TICK_LENGTH) && radial(r, R - RING) &&\n                       sector(a, 12, 12 * tick_angle(TICK_WIDTH, R - RING - TICK_LENGTH / 2), r) ? 1'b1 : 1'b0;\n                mtick = !radial(r, R - RING - TICK_LENGTH / 2) && radial(r, R - RING) &&\n                        sector(a, 60, 60 * tick_angle(MINUTE_TICK_WIDTH, R - RING - TICK_LENGTH / 4), r) ? 1'b1 : 1'b0;\n                cap = radial(r, CAP);\n                on_hour = hand(r, a, hour_a, HOUR_LENGTH, HOUR_WIDTH);\n                on_minute = hand(r, a, minute_a, MINUTE_LENGTH, MINUTE_WIDTH);\n                on_second = hand(r, a, second_a, SECOND_LENGTH, SECOND_WIDTH);\n                if (cap) klass = 8;\n                else if (on_second) klass = 7;\n                else if (on_minute) klass = 6;\n                else if (on_hour) klass = 5;\n                else if (tick) klass = 4;\n                else if (mtick) klass = 3;\n                else if (ring) klass = 2;\n                else if (face) klass = 1;\n                // The CORDIC's angle is rough very near the centre: the cap covers it.\n                if (r <= CAP - 0.5) ambiguous = 1'b0;\n                else if (r < CAP + 1.5) ambiguous = 1'b1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    function [23:0] class_colour(input integer klass, input [23:0] below);\n        case (klass)\n            1: class_colour = C_FACE;   2: class_colour = C_RING;  3: class_colour = C_MTICK;\n            4: class_colour = C_TICK;   5: class_colour = C_HOUR;  6: class_colour = C_MINUTE;\n            7: class_colour = C_SECOND; 8: class_colour = C_CAP;   default: class_colour = below;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- Scan, delay the inputs, compare --------------------------------------------------\n    integer hx = 0, vy = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        hx <= (hx == H_TOTAL - 1) ? 0 : hx + 1;\n        if (hx == H_TOTAL - 1) vy <= (vy == V_TOTAL - 1) ? 0 : vy + 1;\n    end\n    always @(*) begin\n        x = hx < H_ACTIVE ? hx : 0;\n        y = vy < V_ACTIVE ? vy : 0;\n        de = hx < H_ACTIVE && vy < V_ACTIVE;\n        hsync = hx >= H_ACTIVE + 4 && hx < H_ACTIVE + 8;\n        vsync = vy == V_ACTIVE + 3;\n    end\n\n    reg [7:0] x_d [0:LATENCY-1];\n    reg [7:0] y_d [0:LATENCY-1];\n    reg [LATENCY-1:0] de_d = 0, hs_d = 0, vs_d = 0;\n    integer k;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        x_d[0] <= x; y_d[0] <= y;\n        de_d <= {de_d[LATENCY-2:0], de};\n        hs_d <= {hs_d[LATENCY-2:0], hsync};\n        vs_d <= {vs_d[LATENCY-2:0], vsync};\n        for (k = 1; k < LATENCY; k = k + 1) begin\n            x_d[k] <= x_d[k - 1];\n            y_d[k] <= y_d[k - 1];\n        end\n    end\n\n    integer errors = 0, checked = 0, skipped = 0, warm = 0, klass;\n    integer seen [0:8];\n    reg checking = 1'b0;\n    reg [23:0] want, below;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (warm < LATENCY + 2) warm <= warm + 1;\n        else begin\n            if (x_out !== x_d[LATENCY-1] || y_out !== y_d[LATENCY-1] || de_out !== de_d[LATENCY-1] ||\n                hsync_out !== hs_d[LATENCY-1] || vsync_out !== vs_d[LATENCY-1]) begin\n                errors = errors + 1;\n                if (errors < 10) $display(\"FAIL: timing not passed through (%0d, %0d)\", x_d[LATENCY-1], y_d[LATENCY-1]);\n            end\n            if (checking && de_out) begin\n                reference(x_out, y_out, klass);\n                below = {x_out[6:0], 1'b1, y_out[6:0], 1'b0, 8'h5A};\n                if (ambiguous) skipped = skipped + 1;\n                else begin\n                    checked = checked + 1;\n                    seen[klass] = seen[klass] + 1;\n                    want = class_colour(klass, below);\n                    if ({red_out, green_out, blue_out} !== want || covered !== (klass != 0)) begin\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                        if (errors < 12)\n                            $display(\"FAIL: %0d:%0d:%0d (%0d, %0d) r %0.2f angle %0.1f shows %h covered %b, expected %h (class %0d)\",\n                                     hour, minute, second, x_out, y_out, r, a, {red_out, green_out, blue_out}, covered, want, klass);\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Four frames, the time set in the blanking before each -----------------------------\n    task set_time(input integer h, input integer m, input integer s);\n        begin\n            hour = h; minute = m; second = s;\n            // The hands' angles as documented: s × 4369 / 16 (16384 / 60 per step).\n            second_a = (s * 4369) / 16;\n            minute_a = (m * 4369) / 16 + (s * 73) / 16;\n            hour_a = (((h % 12) * 5) * 4369) / 16 + (m * 91) / 4;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task frame;\n        integer c;\n        begin\n            for (c = 0; c < 9; c = c + 1) seen[c] = 0;\n            // Wait for the frame to start through the pipeline, then check one whole frame.\n            @(posedge clk); while (!(hx == 0 && vy == 0)) @(posedge clk);\n            repeat (LATENCY) @(posedge clk);\n            checking = 1'b1;\n            repeat (H_TOTAL * V_ACTIVE) @(posedge clk);\n            checking = 1'b0;\n            $display(\"%0d:%0d:%0d definite pixels per feature: %0d %0d %0d %0d %0d %0d %0d %0d\", hour, minute, second,\n                     seen[1], seen[2], seen[3], seen[4], seen[5], seen[6], seen[7], seen[8]);\n            for (c = 1; c < 9; c = c + 1)\n                if (seen[c] < 3 && !(c == 5 || c == 6) || seen[c] < 2) begin\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                    $display(\"FAIL: %0d:%0d:%0d feature %0d met at only %0d definite pixels\", hour, minute, second, c, seen[c]);\n                end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        for (k = 0; k < 9; k = k + 1) seen[k] = 0;\n        set_time(10, 10, 30); frame;\n        set_time(3, 45, 5);   frame;\n        set_time(23, 59, 59); frame;\n        set_time(12, 0, 0);   frame;\n        if (skipped * 4 > checked) begin\n            errors = errors + 1;\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d pixels skipped near edges, %0d checked\", skipped, checked);\n        end\n        $display(\"%0d pixels checked, %0d near an edge skipped\", checked, skipped);\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #(10 * H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * 6);\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/async_fifo/README.md" : "# FIFO (two clocks)\n\nMoves words from one clock domain to another. Each side only uses its own clock and signals:\nthe writer `wr_en`/`full`, the reader `rd_en`/`empty`/`rd_data` (one `rd_clk` cycle after\n`rd_en`). The pointers cross as Gray code through two flip-flops, so no multi-bit value is ever\nsampled mid-change.\n\n`full` and `empty` are pessimistic by a few cycles of the other clock — the FIFO never\noverflows or underflows, but it may report full or empty briefly after room or data appeared.\nHold both resets together for a few cycles of the slower clock.\n\nTiming: tell the place-and-route tool the two clocks are unrelated (separate clock constraints)\nso it doesn't try to time the synchroniser paths.\n",
    "cores/async_fifo/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"First-in first-out buffer between two unrelated clocks: Gray-code pointers through two-flop synchronisers, the safe way to cross clock domains with data.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 175.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 61,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 48,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 109.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 4,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 15,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 37,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 44,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 79\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 321.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 12,\n        \"DFF\" : 48,\n        \"LUT4\" : 53,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 2,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 56.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 102,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 102.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 54,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 48,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"async_fifo\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"fifo\",\n    \"cdc\",\n    \"clock domain crossing\",\n    \"async\",\n    \"dual clock\",\n    \"buffer\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"First-in-first-out-Puffer zwischen zwei unabhängigen Takten: Gray-Code-Zeiger über Zwei-Flipflop-Synchronisierer, der sichere Weg, Daten über Taktdomänen zu bringen.\",\n      \"name\" : \"FIFO (zwei Takte)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"FIFO (two clocks)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"Bits per word\",\n      \"name\" : \"WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"2^DEPTH_LOG2 words (at least 2)\",\n      \"name\" : \"DEPTH_LOG2\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Write clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Reset, synchronous to wr_clk, active high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_rst\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Word to write\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Write wr_data (ignored when full)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"No room (wr_clk domain)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"full\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Read clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rd_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Reset, synchronous to rd_clk, active high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rd_rst\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Read a word (ignored when empty)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rd_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The word read, one rd_clk cycle after rd_en\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rd_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"No word to read (rd_clk domain)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"empty\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"async_fifo\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/async_fifo/rtl/async_fifo.v" : "// Asynchronous FIFO: first in, first out between two unrelated clocks. 2^DEPTH_LOG2 entries.\n//\n// The pointers cross clock domains in Gray code (one bit changes per step) through two\n// flip-flops each, so a pointer is never seen half-updated. `full` (write side) and `empty`\n// (read side) are conservative: they may stay set a few cycles longer than needed, never too\n// short. The read is registered: `rd_data` shows the word one rd_clk cycle after `rd_en` and\n// keeps it until the next read.\n// Reset both sides together (each reset synchronous to its own clock, active high).\n\nmodule async_fifo #(\n    parameter WIDTH      = 8,\n    parameter DEPTH_LOG2 = 4\n) (\n    input  wire             wr_clk,\n    input  wire             wr_rst,\n    input  wire [WIDTH-1:0] wr_data,\n    input  wire             wr_en,\n    output reg              full = 1'b0,\n\n    input  wire             rd_clk,\n    input  wire             rd_rst,\n    input  wire             rd_en,\n    output reg  [WIDTH-1:0] rd_data = 0,\n    output reg              empty = 1'b1\n);\n    localparam A = DEPTH_LOG2;\n\n    reg [WIDTH-1:0] memory [0:(1 << A)-1];\n\n    // Binary pointers address the memory; Gray pointers cross to the other side.\n    reg [A:0] wr_bin = 0, wr_gray = 0;\n    reg [A:0] rd_bin = 0, rd_gray = 0;\n\n    // Write side.\n    reg [A:0] rd_gray_w1 = 0, rd_gray_w2 = 0;           // the read pointer, synchronised\n    wire [A:0] wr_bin_next  = wr_bin + (wr_en && !full);\n    wire [A:0] wr_gray_next = (wr_bin_next >> 1) ^ wr_bin_next;\n\n    always @(posedge wr_clk) begin\n        if (wr_en && !full) memory[wr_bin[A-1:0]] <= wr_data;\n    end\n\n    always @(posedge wr_clk) begin\n        if (wr_rst) begin\n            wr_bin <= 0; wr_gray <= 0; full <= 1'b0;\n            rd_gray_w1 <= 0; rd_gray_w2 <= 0;\n        end else begin\n            wr_bin <= wr_bin_next;\n            wr_gray <= wr_gray_next;\n            {rd_gray_w2, rd_gray_w1} <= {rd_gray_w1, rd_gray};\n            // Full: the next write pointer is a full turn ahead of the read pointer — in Gray\n            // code the two top bits differ and the rest are equal.\n            full <= (wr_gray_next == {~rd_gray_w2[A:A-1], rd_gray_w2[A-2:0]});\n        end\n    end\n\n    // Read side.\n    reg [A:0] wr_gray_r1 = 0, wr_gray_r2 = 0;           // the write pointer, synchronised\n    wire [A:0] rd_bin_next  = rd_bin + (rd_en && !empty);\n    wire [A:0] rd_gray_next = (rd_bin_next >> 1) ^ rd_bin_next;\n\n    always @(posedge rd_clk) begin\n        if (rd_en && !empty) rd_data <= memory[rd_bin[A-1:0]];\n    end\n\n    always @(posedge rd_clk) begin\n        if (rd_rst) begin\n            rd_bin <= 0; rd_gray <= 0; empty <= 1'b1;\n            wr_gray_r1 <= 0; wr_gray_r2 <= 0;\n        end else begin\n            rd_bin <= rd_bin_next;\n            rd_gray <= rd_gray_next;\n            {wr_gray_r2, wr_gray_r1} <= {wr_gray_r1, wr_gray};\n            empty <= (rd_gray_next == wr_gray_r2);\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/async_fifo/tb/async_fifo_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for async_fifo (8 bits × 8 entries) between a 100 MHz writer and a\n// 37 MHz reader, then the other way round: random bursts on both sides, every word checked in\n// order against what was written, nothing read while empty, nothing lost or duplicated, and\n// `full` reached when the reader stalls.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule async_fifo_tb;\n    reg wr_clk = 1'b0, rd_clk = 1'b0;\n    reg rst = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] wr_data = 8'd0;\n    reg wr_en = 1'b0, rd_en = 1'b0;\n    wire full, empty;\n    wire [7:0] rd_data;\n\n    async_fifo #(.WIDTH(8), .DEPTH_LOG2(3)) dut (\n        .wr_clk(wr_clk), .wr_rst(rst), .wr_data(wr_data), .wr_en(wr_en), .full(full),\n        .rd_clk(rd_clk), .rd_rst(rst), .rd_en(rd_en), .rd_data(rd_data), .empty(empty)\n    );\n\n    real wr_half = 5.0, rd_half = 13.5;\n    always #(wr_half) wr_clk = ~wr_clk;\n    always #(rd_half) rd_clk = ~rd_clk;\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer written = 0, read = 0;\n    reg [7:0] next_write = 8'd0, next_read = 8'd0;\n    reg pending = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] held = 8'd0;\n    reg saw_full = 1'b0;\n    integer phase = 0;     // 0: random, 1: reader stalled\n\n    // Writer: counts up, so order and loss are easy to check.\n    always @(posedge wr_clk) begin\n        if (!rst) begin\n            if (wr_en && !full) begin\n                written = written + 1;\n                next_write = next_write + 1;\n            end\n            if (full) saw_full = 1'b1;\n        end\n    end\n    always @(negedge wr_clk) begin\n        wr_en <= !rst && written < 300 && ($random % 3 != 0);\n        wr_data <= next_write;\n    end\n\n    // Reader: checks each word the cycle after it was taken.\n    always @(posedge rd_clk) begin\n        if (pending) begin\n            if (rd_data !== next_read) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: read %h, expected %h (word %0d)\", rd_data, next_read, read);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            next_read = next_read + 1;\n            read = read + 1;\n            pending = 1'b0;\n        end else if (!rst && rd_data !== held) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: rd_data changed to %h without a read\", rd_data);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        held = rd_data;\n        if (!rst && rd_en && !empty) pending = 1'b1;\n    end\n    always @(negedge rd_clk) rd_en <= !rst && phase == 0 && ($random % 2 == 0);\n\n    task run(input real writer_half, input real reader_half);\n        begin\n            wr_half = writer_half; rd_half = reader_half;\n            rst = 1'b1; written = 0; read = 0; next_write = 0; next_read = 0; pending = 0; saw_full = 0; phase = 0;\n            #200 rst = 1'b0;\n            // The reader stalls for a while: the FIFO must fill and report full.\n            #300 phase = 1;\n            #600 phase = 0;\n            wait (written == 300);\n            #5000;\n            if (read != written) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: wrote %0d words, read %0d\", written, read);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            if (!saw_full) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: never full while the reader stalled\");\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            if (!empty) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: not empty at the end\");\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"async_fifo_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, async_fifo_tb);\n        run(5.0, 13.5);     // fast writer, slow reader\n        run(13.5, 5.0);     // slow writer, fast reader\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #2000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/bar_graph/README.md" : "# Bar graph layer\n\nDraws a bar graph over a video picture — temperatures per hour, power per day, anything with a\nrow of values. Each bar has two values: series A in front (`INSET` pixels narrower on each side)\nand series B behind it, e.g. today and yesterday. Values are heights in pixels, written at any\ntime through the write port (top bit of `write_address` = the series).\n\nIt chains like `text_layer` and `sprite_layer`: pixel stream in, the same stream 3 clocks later\nwith the graph on top. `x` must count up by one per clock (as `video_timing`'s does): the bar is\nfound by counting along the line, without a divider.\n\n```verilog\nbar_graph #(.BARS(24), .BAR_X(160), .BAR_Y(600), .BAR_WIDTH(48), .GAP(16), .HEIGHT(320),\n            .VALUE_BITS(9), .INSET(8), .GRID_STEP(64)) u_hours (\n    .clk(pixel_clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .red(r0), .green(g0), .blue(b0),\n    .write(wr && wr_addr[11]), .write_address(wr_addr[5:0]), .write_value({1'b0, wr_data}),\n    .x_out(x1), .y_out(y1), .de_out(de1), .hsync_out(hs1), .vsync_out(vs1),\n    .red_out(r1), .green_out(g1), .blue_out(b1), .covered()\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/bar_graph/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Draws a bar graph over a video picture: up to BARS bars with two values each (series A in front, inset; series B behind, e.g. today and yesterday), heights in pixels written at any time, optional baseline, grid lines and background. Layers chain like text_layer and sprite_layer; LATENCY = 3 clocks.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 166.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 217,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 200,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 12\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 93.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 7,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 25,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 36,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 149,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 174,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 428,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 120,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 127.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 64,\n        \"DFF\" : 200,\n        \"LUT4\" : 257,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 3,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 12\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 54.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 311,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 96.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 236,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 200,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 12\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 243.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 7,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 188,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 140\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"bar_graph\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"bar\",\n    \"graph\",\n    \"chart\",\n    \"histogram\",\n    \"dashboard\",\n    \"video\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"vga\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"overlay\",\n    \"hmi\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Zeichnet ein Balkendiagramm über ein Videobild: bis zu BARS Balken mit je zwei Werten (Reihe A vorne, schmaler; Reihe B dahinter, z. B. heute und gestern), Höhen in Pixeln, jederzeit beschreibbar, optional Grundlinie, Rasterlinien und Hintergrund. Ebenen lassen sich verketten wie text_layer und sprite_layer; LATENCY = 3 Takte.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Balkendiagramm-Ebene\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Bar graph layer\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 11,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of x (video_timing's x)\",\n      \"name\" : \"X_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of y\",\n      \"name\" : \"Y_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 24,\n      \"description\" : \"Number of bars\",\n      \"name\" : \"BARS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Screen column of the first bar's left edge\",\n      \"name\" : \"BAR_X\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Screen row of the bars' top\",\n      \"name\" : \"BAR_Y\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 16,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of a bar in pixels\",\n      \"name\" : \"BAR_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"Pixels between bars\",\n      \"name\" : \"GAP\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 256,\n      \"description\" : \"Height of the bars' area in pixels\",\n      \"name\" : \"HEIGHT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 9,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of a value (a height in pixels; more than HEIGHT fills the column)\",\n      \"name\" : \"VALUE_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 3,\n      \"description\" : \"Series A is this many pixels narrower on each side than series B\",\n      \"name\" : \"INSET\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"A grid line every GRID_STEP pixels of height (a power of two; 0 = none)\",\n      \"name\" : \"GRID_STEP\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"1: a line one pixel below the bars\",\n      \"name\" : \"BASELINE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: BACKGROUND_COLOUR inside the graph where nothing is drawn\",\n      \"name\" : \"BACKGROUND\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Series A as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"COLOUR_A\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Series B as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"COLOUR_B\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Grid lines as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"GRID_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The baseline as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"BASELINE_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The background as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"BACKGROUND_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Hex file with series A's starting values, one per bar; empty: 0\",\n      \"name\" : \"INIT_A\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Hex file with series B's starting values; empty: 0\",\n      \"name\" : \"INIT_B\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel column from video_timing (or the previous layer's x_out); counts up by one per clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"x\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel row\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"y\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: the pixel is visible\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Write write_value to the bar at write_address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Top bit: series (0 = A, 1 = B); below it: the bar\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write_address\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The bar's height in pixels\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write_value\",\n      \"width\" : 9\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"x, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"x_out\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"y, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"y_out\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"de, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"de_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"hsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"vsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red with the graph drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"red_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green with the graph drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"green_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue with the graph drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A bar draws this output pixel\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"covered\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"bar_graph\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/bar_graph/rtl/bar_graph.v" : "// Bar graph layer: BARS vertical bars in a rectangle on the screen, two values per bar — series A\n// (e.g. today) drawn in front, INSET pixels narrower on each side, and series B (e.g. yesterday)\n// behind it at full width. Like text_layer and sprite_layer it takes a pixel stream (position,\n// timing and the colour below) and passes it on LATENCY = 3 clocks later with the graph on top,\n// so layers chain.\n//\n// The bars start at (BAR_X, BAR_Y): BARS slots of BAR_WIDTH pixels with GAP pixels between them,\n// HEIGHT pixels high, so the graph is BARS × (BAR_WIDTH + GAP) − GAP pixels wide. A value is the\n// bar's height in pixels from the bottom (0 = no bar; more than HEIGHT fills the column). The\n// slot is counted along the line instead of divided, so `x` must count up by one per clock\n// through the graph, as video_timing's does.\n//\n// Optional: a baseline one pixel below the bars, horizontal grid lines every GRID_STEP pixels of\n// height (a power of two; 0 = none) where no bar is, and a background inside the rectangle.\n// Values live in a small RAM of their own (starting from INIT_A / INIT_B, or 0), written at any time on `clk`: write_address holds the\n// series in its top bit (0 = A, 1 = B) and the bar below it. `covered` marks pixels a bar draws.\n\nmodule bar_graph #(\n    parameter X_BITS = 11,\n    parameter Y_BITS = 10,\n    parameter BARS = 24,\n    parameter BAR_X = 0,\n    parameter BAR_Y = 0,\n    parameter BAR_WIDTH = 16,\n    parameter GAP = 4,\n    parameter HEIGHT = 256,\n    parameter VALUE_BITS = 9,\n    parameter INSET = 3,\n    parameter GRID_STEP = 0,\n    parameter BASELINE = 1,\n    parameter BACKGROUND = 0,\n    parameter [23:0] COLOUR_A = 24'hFFAA00,\n    parameter [23:0] COLOUR_B = 24'h555555,\n    parameter [23:0] GRID_COLOUR = 24'h333333,\n    parameter [23:0] BASELINE_COLOUR = 24'hAAAAAA,\n    parameter [23:0] BACKGROUND_COLOUR = 24'h000000,\n    // Hex files with the starting values, one per bar (empty: 0).\n    parameter INIT_A = \"\",\n    parameter INIT_B = \"\"\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire [X_BITS-1:0] x,\n    input  wire [Y_BITS-1:0] y,\n    input  wire de,\n    input  wire hsync,\n    input  wire vsync,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire write,\n    input  wire [$clog2(BARS):0] write_address,\n    input  wire [VALUE_BITS-1:0] write_value,\n    output reg  [X_BITS-1:0] x_out = 0,\n    output reg  [Y_BITS-1:0] y_out = 0,\n    output reg  de_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  hsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  vsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [7:0] red_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] green_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] blue_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  covered = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam IB = $clog2(BARS);\n    localparam PITCH = BAR_WIDTH + GAP;\n    localparam WIDTH = BARS * PITCH - GAP;\n    localparam OB = $clog2(PITCH);\n    localparam SB = $clog2(BARS + 1);\n    localparam LB = $clog2(HEIGHT);\n    localparam BOTTOM = BAR_Y + HEIGHT - 1;\n    localparam GB = GRID_STEP > 1 ? $clog2(GRID_STEP) : 1;\n\n    // ---- The values -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [VALUE_BITS-1:0] series_a [0:BARS-1];\n    reg [VALUE_BITS-1:0] series_b [0:BARS-1];\n    integer i;\n    initial begin\n        for (i = 0; i < BARS; i = i + 1) begin\n            series_a[i] = 0;\n            series_b[i] = 0;\n        end\n        if (INIT_A != \"\") $readmemh(INIT_A, series_a);\n        if (INIT_B != \"\") $readmemh(INIT_B, series_b);\n    end\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        if (write) begin\n            if (write_address[IB]) series_b[write_address[IB-1:0]] <= write_value;\n            else series_a[write_address[IB-1:0]] <= write_value;\n        end\n\n    // ---- Stage 1: where the pixel is ---------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [SB-1:0] slot = 0;\n    reg [OB-1:0] offset = 0;\n    reg in_x = 1'b0, in_y = 1'b0, base_row = 1'b0;\n    reg [LB-1:0] level = 0;\n    reg [X_BITS-1:0] x1 = 0;\n    reg [Y_BITS-1:0] y1 = 0;\n    reg de1 = 1'b0, hs1 = 1'b0, vs1 = 1'b0;\n    reg [23:0] below1 = 24'd0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (x == BAR_X) begin\n            slot <= 0;\n            offset <= 0;\n        end else if (offset == PITCH - 1) begin\n            slot <= slot + 1'b1;\n            offset <= 0;\n        end else offset <= offset + 1'b1;\n        in_x <= x >= BAR_X && x < BAR_X + WIDTH;\n        in_y <= y >= BAR_Y && y <= BOTTOM;\n        level <= BOTTOM - y;\n        base_row <= BASELINE != 0 && y == BOTTOM + 1;\n        x1 <= x; y1 <= y; de1 <= de; hs1 <= hsync; vs1 <= vsync;\n        below1 <= {red, green, blue};\n    end\n\n    // ---- Stage 2: the bar's values ------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [VALUE_BITS-1:0] value_a = 0, value_b = 0;\n    reg [OB-1:0] offset2 = 0;\n    reg in_x2 = 1'b0, in_y2 = 1'b0, base2 = 1'b0;\n    reg [LB-1:0] level2 = 0;\n    reg [X_BITS-1:0] x2 = 0;\n    reg [Y_BITS-1:0] y2 = 0;\n    reg de2 = 1'b0, hs2 = 1'b0, vs2 = 1'b0;\n    reg [23:0] below2 = 24'd0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        value_a <= series_a[slot[IB-1:0]];\n        value_b <= series_b[slot[IB-1:0]];\n        offset2 <= offset;\n        in_x2 <= in_x; in_y2 <= in_y; base2 <= base_row; level2 <= level;\n        x2 <= x1; y2 <= y1; de2 <= de1; hs2 <= hs1; vs2 <= vs1;\n        below2 <= below1;\n    end\n\n    // ---- Stage 3: the colour ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire in_bar = offset2 < BAR_WIDTH;\n    wire in_front = offset2 >= INSET && offset2 < BAR_WIDTH - INSET;\n    wire draw_a = in_x2 && in_y2 && in_front && level2 < value_a;\n    wire draw_b = in_x2 && in_y2 && in_bar && level2 < value_b;\n    wire [GB-1:0] grid_bits = level2[GB-1:0];\n    wire grid = GRID_STEP > 1 && in_x2 && in_y2 && grid_bits == {GB{1'b1}};\n    wire in_rect = in_x2 && (in_y2 || base2);\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        x_out <= x2; y_out <= y2; de_out <= de2; hsync_out <= hs2; vsync_out <= vs2;\n        covered <= draw_a || draw_b;\n        if (draw_a) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= COLOUR_A;\n        else if (draw_b) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= COLOUR_B;\n        else if (in_x2 && base2) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= BASELINE_COLOUR;\n        else if (grid) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= GRID_COLOUR;\n        else if (BACKGROUND != 0 && in_rect) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= BACKGROUND_COLOUR;\n        else {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= below2;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/bar_graph/tb/bar_graph_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for bar_graph. A small screen (32 × 26 visible, 40 × 30 in all) is\n// scanned for two frames; the values are written through the write port before each frame.\n// Graph: 3 bars at (3, 2), 5 pixels wide with gaps of 2, 16 pixels high, series A inset by 1,\n// grid every 4 pixels, baseline and background on. The values cover an empty bar, a full one,\n// one taller than the graph, B taller than A and A taller than B. Every output pixel (colour,\n// covered, and the position and timing passed through) is compared with a reference model that\n// computes the slot by division, apart from the RTL's counter.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule bar_graph_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 40, H_ACTIVE = 32, V_TOTAL = 30, V_ACTIVE = 26;\n    localparam BARS = 3, BAR_X = 3, BAR_Y = 2, BAR_WIDTH = 5, GAP = 2, HEIGHT = 16, INSET = 1, GRID_STEP = 4;\n    localparam [23:0] CA = 24'hF0A010, CB = 24'h4060C0, CG = 24'h303030, CL = 24'hB0B0B0, CBG = 24'h0A0A14;\n\n    reg [5:0] x = 0;\n    reg [4:0] y = 0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] red = 0, green = 0, blue = 0;\n    reg write = 1'b0;\n    reg [2:0] write_address = 0;\n    reg [4:0] write_value = 0;\n\n    wire [5:0] x_out;\n    wire [4:0] y_out;\n    wire de_out, hsync_out, vsync_out, covered;\n    wire [7:0] red_out, green_out, blue_out;\n\n    bar_graph #(\n        .X_BITS(6), .Y_BITS(5), .BARS(BARS), .BAR_X(BAR_X), .BAR_Y(BAR_Y), .BAR_WIDTH(BAR_WIDTH), .GAP(GAP),\n        .HEIGHT(HEIGHT), .VALUE_BITS(5), .INSET(INSET), .GRID_STEP(GRID_STEP), .BASELINE(1), .BACKGROUND(1),\n        .COLOUR_A(CA), .COLOUR_B(CB), .GRID_COLOUR(CG), .BASELINE_COLOUR(CL), .BACKGROUND_COLOUR(CBG)\n    ) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .write(write), .write_address(write_address), .write_value(write_value),\n        .x_out(x_out), .y_out(y_out), .de_out(de_out), .hsync_out(hsync_out), .vsync_out(vsync_out),\n        .red_out(red_out), .green_out(green_out), .blue_out(blue_out), .covered(covered)\n    );\n\n    // The picture below: every pixel differs.\n    function [23:0] below(input integer px, input integer py);\n        reg [7:0] mix;\n        begin\n            mix = 8'h5A ^ (px * 7 + py);\n            below = {px[5:0], 2'b10, py[4:0], 3'b011, mix};\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    integer value_a [0:BARS-1];\n    integer value_b [0:BARS-1];\n\n    // The reference: what pixel (px, py) must show, and whether a bar covers it.\n    reg [23:0] want_colour;\n    reg want_covered;\n    task reference(input integer px, input integer py);\n        integer slot, offset, level, width;\n        reg in_x, in_y, base, a, b, grid;\n        begin\n            width = BARS * (BAR_WIDTH + GAP) - GAP;\n            in_x = px >= BAR_X && px < BAR_X + width;\n            in_y = py >= BAR_Y && py < BAR_Y + HEIGHT;\n            base = py == BAR_Y + HEIGHT;\n            slot = (px - BAR_X) / (BAR_WIDTH + GAP);\n            offset = (px - BAR_X) % (BAR_WIDTH + GAP);\n            level = BAR_Y + HEIGHT - 1 - py;\n            a = in_x && in_y && offset >= INSET && offset < BAR_WIDTH - INSET && level < value_a[slot];\n            b = in_x && in_y && offset < BAR_WIDTH && level < value_b[slot];\n            grid = in_x && in_y && (level + 1) % GRID_STEP == 0;\n            want_covered = a || b;\n            if (a) want_colour = CA;\n            else if (b) want_colour = CB;\n            else if (in_x && base) want_colour = CL;\n            else if (grid) want_colour = CG;\n            else if (in_x && (in_y || base)) want_colour = CBG;\n            else want_colour = below(px, py);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer errors = 0, checked = 0;\n    // The outputs belong to the pixel three clocks earlier: compare against x_out / y_out.\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        #1;\n        if (de_out) begin\n            reference(x_out, y_out);\n            checked = checked + 1;\n            if ({red_out, green_out, blue_out} !== want_colour || covered !== want_covered) begin\n                if (errors < 10)\n                    $display(\"FAIL: (%0d, %0d) shows %h covered %b, expected %h covered %b\",\n                             x_out, y_out, {red_out, green_out, blue_out}, covered, want_colour, want_covered);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // The timing passes through unchanged, three clocks late.\n    reg [5:0] x_d [0:2];\n    reg [4:0] y_d [0:2];\n    reg [2:0] de_d = 3'b000, hs_d = 3'b000, vs_d = 3'b000;\n    initial begin x_d[0] = 0; x_d[1] = 0; x_d[2] = 0; y_d[0] = 0; y_d[1] = 0; y_d[2] = 0; end\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        x_d[0] <= x; x_d[1] <= x_d[0]; x_d[2] <= x_d[1];\n        y_d[0] <= y; y_d[1] <= y_d[0]; y_d[2] <= y_d[1];\n        de_d <= {de_d[1:0], de}; hs_d <= {hs_d[1:0], hsync}; vs_d <= {vs_d[1:0], vsync};\n        #1;\n        if (x_out !== x_d[2] || y_out !== y_d[2] || de_out !== de_d[2] || hsync_out !== hs_d[2] || vsync_out !== vs_d[2]) begin\n            if (errors < 10) $display(\"FAIL: timing not passed through at (%0d, %0d)\", x_d[2], y_d[2]);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    end\n\n    task put(input integer series, input integer bar, input integer v);\n        begin\n            @(negedge clk);\n            write = 1'b1; write_address = {series[0], bar[1:0]}; write_value = v;\n            @(negedge clk);\n            write = 1'b0;\n            if (series == 0) value_a[bar] = v; else value_b[bar] = v;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer frame, px, py;\n    initial begin\n        for (px = 0; px < BARS; px = px + 1) begin value_a[px] = 0; value_b[px] = 0; end\n        for (frame = 0; frame < 2; frame = frame + 1) begin\n            if (frame == 0) begin\n                put(0, 0, 0);  put(1, 0, 16);   // empty front, full back\n                put(0, 1, 7);  put(1, 1, 3);    // A taller than B\n                put(0, 2, 20); put(1, 2, 9);    // A taller than the graph\n            end else begin\n                put(0, 0, 5);  put(1, 0, 1);\n                put(0, 1, 0);  put(1, 1, 12);   // B only\n                put(0, 2, 16); put(1, 2, 0);\n            end\n            for (py = 0; py < V_TOTAL; py = py + 1)\n                for (px = 0; px < H_TOTAL; px = px + 1) begin\n                    @(negedge clk);\n                    x = px; y = py;\n                    de = px < H_ACTIVE && py < V_ACTIVE;\n                    hsync = px >= 34 && px < 37;\n                    vsync = py == 27;\n                    {red, green, blue} = below(px, py);\n                end\n        end\n        repeat (5) @(negedge clk);\n        if (checked != 2 * H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d pixels checked, expected %0d\", checked, 2 * H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/clock_divider/README.md" : "# Clock divider\n\n`tick` pulses for one cycle `HZ` times per second; `square` is high for the first half of each\nperiod. Both stay in the `clk` domain: drive slower logic with `if (tick) …` instead of using a\ndivided signal as a clock, which FPGA tools route badly and time wrongly.\n\n```verilog\nclock_divider #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .HZ(2)) u_blink (.clk(clk), .tick(), .square(led_on));\n```\n",
    "cores/clock_divider/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"A clock-enable tick and a 50 % square wave at a lower rate, from the system clock.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 218.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 53,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 26\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 99.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 20,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 16,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 24,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 70\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 162.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 46,\n        \"DFF\" : 26,\n        \"LUT4\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 72.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 53\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 131.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 58,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 26\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"clock_divider\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"clock\",\n    \"divide\",\n    \"tick\",\n    \"enable\",\n    \"timer\",\n    \"blink\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein Clock-Enable-Tick und ein 50-%-Rechtecksignal mit niedrigerer Rate, aus dem Systemtakt.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Taktteiler\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Clock divider\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"Ticks per second, and the square wave's frequency\",\n      \"name\" : \"HZ\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse HZ times per second\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tick\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"50 % square wave at HZ\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"square\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"clock_divider\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/clock_divider/rtl/clock_divider.v" : "// Clock divider: a one-cycle `tick` HZ times per second, and `square`, a 50 % square wave at HZ.\n// Both are ordinary signals in the clk domain — use `tick` as a clock enable rather than as a\n// clock (FPGA clock nets want real clocks from a PLL or a pin). CLK_HZ / HZ is rounded down.\n\nmodule clock_divider #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter HZ     = 1\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    output reg  tick = 1'b0,\n    output reg  square = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam PERIOD       = CLK_HZ / HZ;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = PERIOD > 1 ? $clog2(PERIOD) : 1;\n\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        tick <= (clocks == PERIOD - 1);\n        square <= (clocks < PERIOD / 2);\n        clocks <= (clocks == PERIOD - 1) ? 0 : clocks + 1'b1;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/clock_divider/tb/clock_divider_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for clock_divider: 1 MHz down to 1 kHz. Ticks must come exactly every\n// 1000 cycles, and the square wave must be high for 500 of every 1000.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule clock_divider_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    wire tick, square;\n\n    clock_divider #(.CLK_HZ(1000000), .HZ(1000)) dut (.clk(clk), .tick(tick), .square(square));\n\n    always #500 clk = ~clk;\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer cycle = 0, last_tick = -1, ticks = 0, high = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        cycle = cycle + 1;\n        if (cycle > 2000 && cycle <= 12000 && square) high = high + 1;\n        if (tick) begin\n            if (last_tick >= 0 && cycle - last_tick != 1000) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: ticks %0d cycles apart, expected 1000\", cycle - last_tick);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            last_tick = cycle;\n            ticks = ticks + 1;\n        end\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"clock_divider_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, clock_divider_tb);\n        repeat (12010) @(posedge clk);   // the first tick comes after 1000 cycles + 1 (registered)\n        #1;\n        if (ticks != 12) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d ticks in 12010 cycles, expected 12\", ticks);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (high != 5000) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: square high for %0d of 10000 cycles, expected 5000\", high);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    // A broken design must not hang the simulation.\n    initial begin\n        #20000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/debouncer/README.md" : "# Debouncer\n\nMechanical buttons bounce for a few milliseconds when pressed or released. `out` follows `in`\nonly once it has kept its new level for `MS` milliseconds (10 ms is enough for most buttons).\nThe input is synchronised, so it can come straight from a pin. For an active-low button, invert\n`out` (or the pin).\n\nTo act once per press, follow it with `edge_detect`:\n\n```verilog\nwire pressed;\ndebouncer #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ)) u_debounce (.clk(clk), .in(~btn), .out(pressed));\nedge_detect u_edge (.clk(clk), .in(pressed), .rise(press), .fall());\n```\n",
    "cores/debouncer/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Cleans up a bouncing button or switch: synchronised input, output changes only after the input is stable for a set time.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 236.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 30,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 20\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 100.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 4,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 21,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 21,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 37\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 195.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 18,\n        \"DFF\" : 20\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 76.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 31\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 146.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 31,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 20\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"debouncer\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"button\",\n    \"switch\",\n    \"bounce\",\n    \"debounce\",\n    \"key\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Entprellt einen Taster oder Schalter: synchronisierter Eingang, der Ausgang ändert sich erst, wenn der Eingang eine Zeit lang stabil ist.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Entpreller\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Debouncer\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"How long the input must be stable, in milliseconds\",\n      \"name\" : \"MS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Raw input from the pin (asynchronous)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Debounced level, starts low\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"out\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"debouncer\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/debouncer/rtl/debouncer.v" : "// Debouncer for a button or switch. Synchronises `in` with two flip-flops, then changes `out`\n// only after the input has kept its new level for MS milliseconds: bounces shorter than that\n// are ignored. `out` starts low.\n\nmodule debouncer #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter MS     = 10\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire in,\n    output reg  out = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam STABLE_CLKS  = (CLK_HZ / 1000) * MS;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = STABLE_CLKS > 1 ? $clog2(STABLE_CLKS) : 1;\n\n    reg [1:0] sync = 2'b00;\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        sync <= {sync[0], in};\n\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (sync[1] == out) begin\n            clocks <= 0;\n        end else if (clocks == STABLE_CLKS - 1) begin\n            clocks <= 0;\n            out <= sync[1];\n        end else begin\n            clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/debouncer/tb/debouncer_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for debouncer: a bouncing press and release, and a short spike that\n// must not get through. 1 MHz clock, 1 ms: the input must be stable for 1000 cycles.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule debouncer_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    reg in = 1'b0;\n    wire out;\n\n    debouncer #(.CLK_HZ(1000000), .MS(1)) dut (.clk(clk), .in(in), .out(out));\n\n    always #500 clk = ~clk;            // 1 MHz\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer changes = 0;\n    reg last = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (out !== last) changes = changes + 1;\n        last = out;\n    end\n\n    task bounce(input level);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) begin\n                in = level; #(37000 + i * 11000);      // 37–92 us\n                in = !level; #(23000);\n            end\n            in = level;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task check(input expected, input integer expected_changes, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            if (out !== expected || changes != expected_changes) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: out = %b after %0d changes, expected %b after %0d\", what, out, changes, expected, expected_changes);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"debouncer_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, debouncer_tb);\n        #20000;\n\n        bounce(1'b1);\n        #500000;                         // stable for 0.5 ms: not yet\n        check(1'b0, 0, \"half a debounce time after the press\");\n        #700000;\n        check(1'b1, 1, \"after the press settled\");\n\n        bounce(1'b0);\n        #1200000;\n        check(1'b0, 2, \"after the release settled\");\n\n        // A 0.8 ms spike is shorter than the debounce time.\n        in = 1'b1; #800000; in = 1'b0;\n        #2000000;\n        check(1'b0, 2, \"after a short spike\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    // A broken design must not hang the simulation.\n    initial begin\n        #20000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/edge_detect/README.md" : "# Edge detector\n\nTurns level changes into one-cycle pulses: `rise` after `in` goes high, `fall` after it goes\nlow. Use it to do something once per button press (after a `debouncer`) or once per change of a\nstatus line. `in` must be synchronous to `clk`; put a synchroniser or a `debouncer` in front of\na raw pin.\n",
    "cores/edge_detect/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulses on the rising and falling edges of a synchronous signal.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 331.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 2,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 226.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DFF\" : 3,\n        \"LUT4\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 136,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 280.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 2,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"edge_detect\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"edge\",\n    \"pulse\",\n    \"rising\",\n    \"falling\",\n    \"press\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein-Takt-Pulse bei steigenden und fallenden Flanken eines synchronen Signals.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Flankenerkennung\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Edge detector\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Signal in the clk domain\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse after in went high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rise\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse after in went low\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"fall\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"edge_detect\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/edge_detect/rtl/edge_detect.v" : "// Edge detector: one-cycle pulses on `rise` and `fall` when `in` changes. `in` must already be\n// synchronous to `clk` (from a debouncer, a synchroniser or other logic in the same clock\n// domain). The pulses come one cycle after the change.\n\nmodule edge_detect (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire in,\n    output reg  rise = 1'b0,\n    output reg  fall = 1'b0\n);\n    reg last = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        last <= in;\n        rise <= in && !last;\n        fall <= !in && last;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/edge_detect/tb/edge_detect_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for edge_detect: every change of `in` gives exactly one pulse of the\n// right kind, one cycle long, and a steady input none.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule edge_detect_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    reg in = 1'b0;\n    wire rise, fall;\n\n    edge_detect dut (.clk(clk), .in(in), .rise(rise), .fall(fall));\n\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer rises = 0, falls = 0;\n    reg rise_last = 1'b0, fall_last = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (rise && !rise_last) rises = rises + 1;\n        if (fall && !fall_last) falls = falls + 1;\n        if (rise && rise_last) begin $display(\"FAIL: rise longer than one cycle\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (fall && fall_last) begin $display(\"FAIL: fall longer than one cycle\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (rise && fall) begin $display(\"FAIL: rise and fall together\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        rise_last = rise;\n        fall_last = fall;\n    end\n\n    // `in` changes after the clock edge, like a register output.\n    task set(input level, input integer cycles);\n        begin\n            @(negedge clk) in = level;\n            repeat (cycles) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"edge_detect_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, edge_detect_tb);\n        repeat (4) @(posedge clk);\n        set(1, 5); set(0, 5); set(1, 1); set(0, 1); set(1, 3); set(0, 3);\n        repeat (10) @(posedge clk);       // steady: nothing more\n        #1;\n        if (rises != 3 || falls != 3) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d rises and %0d falls, expected 3 and 3\", rises, falls);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    // A broken design must not hang the simulation.\n    initial begin\n        #100000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_arp/README.md" : "# Ethernet ARP\n\nAnswers ARP requests (RFC 826) for the design's IPv4 address, so other hosts can find its MAC address: \"who has\n`ip`?\" — \"`ip` is at `mac`\".\n\nIt watches every frame `eth_mac` receives and checks a request while it arrives: to the broadcast address or to\n`mac`, EtherType 0806, hardware type 1, protocol 0800, lengths 6 and 4, operation 1, target `ip`. When the frame's\nlast byte comes with `rx_good`, the reply is queued: 42 bytes on a valid/ready/last stream for `eth_mac` (which pads\nit to 60), or for a multiplexer when other cores send too (`eth_udp_stack` has one). `replied` pulses when a reply\nis queued. One reply waits at a time; a request that arrives meanwhile isn't answered (the requester asks again).\n\n```verilog\neth_arp u_arp (\n    .clk(eth_clk), .mac(48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01), .ip({8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}),\n    .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last), .rx_good(rx_good),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready), .replied()\n);\n```\n\n`mac` should be a locally administered address (the second-lowest bit of the first byte set: 02:…) unless you own\na block of addresses.\n",
    "cores/eth_arp/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_mac\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"ARP responder (RFC 826, Ethernet/IPv4): watches eth_mac's received frames and answers a request for `ip` — broadcast or to `mac` — with a 42-byte reply on a valid/ready/last stream (eth_mac pads it). Checked as it arrives, queued when the frame is good; one reply waits at a time. `mac` and `ip` are inputs: constants, switches or a register.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 158.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 436,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 607\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 57.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 3,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 28,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 136,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 337,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 603,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 816\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 92.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 66,\n        \"DFF\" : 607,\n        \"LUT4\" : 618\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 50.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 717,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 4\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 277.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 4,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 607,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 392\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_arp\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"arp\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"address\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"ARP-Antwort (RFC 826, Ethernet/IPv4): beobachtet die empfangenen Rahmen von eth_mac und beantwortet eine Anfrage nach `ip` — Broadcast oder an `mac` — mit einer 42-Byte-Antwort als valid/ready/last-Strom (eth_mac füllt auf). Beim Eintreffen geprüft, in die Warteschlange gestellt, wenn der Rahmen gut ist; eine Antwort wartet zugleich. `mac` und `ip` sind Eingänge: Konstanten, Schalter oder ein Register.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-ARP\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet ARP\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock (eth_mac's)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_last\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_good\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The reply's byte (to eth_mac's tx_data)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A reply is waiting\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the reply's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's tx_ready\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"replied\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_arp\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_arp/rtl/eth_arp.v" : "// ARP responder: answers \"who has `ip`?\" with \"`ip` is at `mac`\" (RFC 826, Ethernet and IPv4 only).\n//\n// It watches eth_mac's received frames (all of them: it picks its own) and checks a request while it arrives: to\n// the broadcast address or to `mac`, EtherType 0806, hardware type 1, protocol 0800, lengths 6 and 4, operation 1,\n// target `ip`. When the frame's last byte comes with `rx_good`, the reply is queued and offered on the tx side — 42\n// bytes from the requester's MAC on (eth_mac pads to 60) — with valid/ready/last. `replied` pulses when a reply is\n// queued. One reply waits at a time: a request that arrives while one is waiting or going out isn't answered (the\n// requester asks again).\n//\n// For speed every comparison is against a byte shifted out of a register, and the reply is a register shifting out a\n// byte for each one taken: `tx_valid` stays high from the clock after the reply is queued to its last byte, so a byte\n// can go on every clock (eth_mac at 1000 Mbit/s) or every fourth (100 Mbit/s).\n\nmodule eth_arp (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    // From eth_mac\n    input  wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    input  wire        rx_valid,\n    input  wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_good,\n    // The reply, to eth_mac (or a multiplexer in front of it)\n    output wire [7:0]  tx_data,\n    output wire        tx_valid,\n    output wire        tx_last,\n    input  wire        tx_ready,\n    output reg         replied = 1'b0\n);\n    // ---- The request ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [5:0]  index = 6'd0;        // byte of the frame (stops at 63)\n    reg        to_us = 1'b1, to_all = 1'b1, fields_ok = 1'b1, for_us = 1'b1;\n    reg [47:0] mac_left = 48'd0;    // `mac`, a byte shifted out for each address byte\n    reg [31:0] ip_left = 32'd0;     // `ip`, a byte shifted out for each target byte\n    reg [79:0] fixed_left = 80'h0806_0001_0800_0604_0001;   // bytes 12 … 21 of a request\n    reg [47:0] sender_mac = 48'd0;\n    reg [31:0] sender_ip = 32'd0;\n    reg        pending = 1'b0;\n    reg [47:0] reply_mac = 48'd0;\n    reg [31:0] reply_ip = 32'd0;\n\n    wire done_sending = tx_valid && tx_ready && tx_last;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        replied <= 1'b0;\n        if (done_sending) pending <= 1'b0;\n        if (rx_valid) begin\n            if (index != 6'd63) index <= index + 6'd1;\n            if (index < 6'd6) begin\n                if (rx_data != (index == 6'd0 ? mac[47:40] : mac_left[47:40])) to_us <= 1'b0;\n                if (rx_data != 8'hFF) to_all <= 1'b0;\n                mac_left <= {(index == 6'd0 ? mac[39:0] : mac_left[39:0]), 8'd0};\n            end\n            if (index >= 6'd12 && index <= 6'd21) begin\n                if (rx_data != fixed_left[79:72]) fields_ok <= 1'b0;\n                fixed_left <= {fixed_left[71:0], 8'd0};\n            end\n            if (index >= 6'd22 && index <= 6'd27) sender_mac <= {sender_mac[39:0], rx_data};\n            if (index >= 6'd28 && index <= 6'd31) sender_ip <= {sender_ip[23:0], rx_data};\n            if (index == 6'd37) ip_left <= ip;\n            if (index >= 6'd38 && index <= 6'd41) begin\n                if (rx_data != ip_left[31:24]) for_us <= 1'b0;\n                ip_left <= {ip_left[23:0], 8'd0};\n            end\n            if (rx_last) begin\n                // A request with a good FCS is at least 60 bytes: its target is in by now.\n                if (rx_good && (to_us || to_all) && fields_ok && for_us && index >= 6'd41 && !pending && !done_sending) begin\n                    pending <= 1'b1;\n                    reply_mac <= sender_mac;\n                    reply_ip <= sender_ip;\n                    replied <= 1'b1;\n                end\n                index <= 6'd0;\n                to_us <= 1'b1;\n                to_all <= 1'b1;\n                fields_ok <= 1'b1;\n                for_us <= 1'b1;\n                fixed_left <= 80'h0806_0001_0800_0604_0001;\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- The reply ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // The 42 bytes as a register that shifts a byte out for each one taken, loaded when the reply is queued.\n    reg [335:0] reply = 336'd0;\n    reg [5:0]   out = 6'd0;\n    reg         loaded = 1'b0;    // `reply` holds this request's bytes\n    assign tx_data = reply[335:328];\n    assign tx_valid = pending && loaded;\n    assign tx_last = out == 6'd41;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (replied) begin\n            reply <= {reply_mac, mac, 80'h0806_0001_0800_0604_0002, mac, ip, reply_mac, reply_ip};\n            out <= 6'd0;\n            loaded <= 1'b1;\n        end else if (tx_valid && tx_ready) begin\n            out <= out + 6'd1;\n            reply <= {reply[327:0], 8'd0};\n            if (tx_last) loaded <= 1'b0;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_arp/tb/eth_arp_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_arp: frames as eth_mac delivers them (a byte every fourth clock, rx_last and\n// rx_good with the last) and a MAC on the send side that takes a byte every fourth clock. Checks:\n//   - a broadcast request for our IP gets exactly the reply (42 bytes: the requester's MAC, ours, 0806 0001 0800\n//     06 04 0002, our MAC and IP, the requester's MAC and IP), `replied` pulses once;\n//   - a request to our MAC (unicast) is answered too;\n//   - no reply to: another IP, a frame with rx_good low, an ARP reply (operation 2), a request to another host's\n//     MAC, an IPv4 frame, a request whose hardware type isn't 1;\n//   - a reply that waits keeps its requester while another request arrives (which isn't answered);\n//   - a consumer that's ready on every clock gets the same reply, a byte on every clock with no pause (Gigabit:\n//     eth_mac takes a byte a clock and doesn't wait), also for requests that arrive a byte every clock;\n//   - a changed `ip` input is answered at once.\n// Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_arp_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n    reg [31:0] ip = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222};\n\n    reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        rx_valid = 1'b0, rx_last = 1'b0, rx_good = 1'b0;\n    wire [7:0] tx_data;\n    wire       tx_valid, tx_last, replied;\n    reg        tx_ready = 1'b0;\n    reg hold = 1'b0;     // the MAC busy with another frame: the reply waits\n    reg greedy = 1'b0;   // a consumer ready on every clock: every byte offered must be right, with no pause\n    reg dense = 1'b0;    // requests a byte on every clock, as eth_mac delivers them at 1000 Mbit/s\n    eth_arp dut (.clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(ip), .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last),\n        .rx_good(rx_good), .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready),\n        .replied(replied));\n\n    // ---- The MAC's send side: a byte every fourth clock while tx_valid ----------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] reply [0:63];\n    integer reply_n = 0, replies = 0, replied_pulses = 0, stalls = 0;\n    reg in_reply = 1'b0;\n    reg [1:0] slot = 2'd0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        slot <= slot + 2'd1;\n        if (replied) replied_pulses = replied_pulses + 1;\n        if (greedy && in_reply && !tx_valid) stalls = stalls + 1;   // a pause inside the reply\n        if (tx_valid && tx_ready) begin\n            reply[reply_n] = tx_data;\n            reply_n = reply_n + 1;\n            in_reply = !tx_last;\n            if (tx_last) replies = replies + 1;\n        end\n    end\n    always @(negedge clk) tx_ready = (greedy || slot == 2'd0) && !hold;\n\n    // ---- Frames in -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:63];\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n\n    task make_arp(input [47:0] to, input [15:0] htype, input [15:0] oper, input [31:0] target);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            put48(0, to); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0806);\n            put16(14, htype); put16(16, 16'h0800); frame[18] = 8'd6; frame[19] = 8'd4; put16(20, oper);\n            put48(22, HOST); put32(28, HOST_IP); put48(32, 48'h0); put32(38, target);\n            for (i = 42; i < 60; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'hA0 + i;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task deliver(input good);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            for (i = 0; i < 60; i = i + 1) begin\n                rx_data = frame[i];\n                rx_valid = 1'b1;\n                rx_last = (i == 59);\n                rx_good = (i == 59) && good;\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                if (!dense) begin\n                    rx_valid = 1'b0; rx_last = 1'b0; rx_good = 1'b0;\n                    repeat (3) @(posedge clk);\n                    #1;\n                end\n            end\n            rx_valid = 1'b0; rx_last = 1'b0; rx_good = 1'b0;\n            repeat (400) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    function [47:0] got48(input integer at); got48 = {reply[at], reply[at + 1], reply[at + 2], reply[at + 3], reply[at + 4], reply[at + 5]}; endfunction\n    function [31:0] got32(input integer at); got32 = {reply[at], reply[at + 1], reply[at + 2], reply[at + 3]}; endfunction\n\n    task expect_reply(input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            if (replies != 1 || reply_n != 42) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d replies, %0d bytes\", what, replies, reply_n);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else begin\n                if (got48(0) !== HOST || got48(6) !== MAC) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: Ethernet addresses\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if ({reply[12], reply[13], reply[14], reply[15], reply[16], reply[17], reply[18], reply[19]} !== 64'h0806_0001_0800_0604)\n                    begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: types and lengths\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if ({reply[20], reply[21]} !== 16'h0002) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: operation\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (got48(22) !== MAC || got32(28) !== ip) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: sender\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (got48(32) !== HOST || got32(38) !== HOST_IP) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: target\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n            end\n            if (replied_pulses != 1) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d replied pulses\", what, replied_pulses); errors = errors + 1; end\n            replies = 0; reply_n = 0; replied_pulses = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task expect_none(input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            if (replies != 0 || reply_n != 0 || replied_pulses != 0) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: answered\", what);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            replies = 0; reply_n = 0; replied_pulses = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_reply(\"a broadcast request\");\n        make_arp(MAC, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip);                deliver(1'b1); expect_reply(\"a unicast request\");\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd99}); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another IP\");\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b0); expect_none(\"rx_good low\");\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0002, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"an ARP reply\");\n        make_arp(48'h02_47_4C_00_00_02, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another host's MAC\");\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0006, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"hardware type 6\");\n        make_arp(MAC, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); put16(12, 16'h0800); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"an IPv4 frame\");\n        // A reply waiting while another request (from 10.0.100.11, MAC …C4) arrives: the waiting one stays as it was,\n        // the new one isn't answered (one at a time).\n        hold = 1'b1;\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1);\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); put48(6, HOST + 1); put48(22, HOST + 1);\n        put32(28, HOST_IP + 1); deliver(1'b1);\n        if (replied_pulses != 1) fail(\"a request answered while another waited\");\n        replied_pulses = 1;\n        hold = 1'b0;\n        repeat (400) @(posedge clk);\n        expect_reply(\"a reply that waited\");\n        greedy = 1'b1;\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_reply(\"to a consumer always ready\");\n        dense = 1'b1;\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_reply(\"dense, always ready\");\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd99}); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"dense, another IP\");\n        if (stalls != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0d pauses inside replies to a consumer always ready\", stalls); errors = errors + 1; end\n        dense = 1'b0;\n        greedy = 1'b0;\n        ip = {8'd192, 8'd168, 8'd1, 8'd50};\n        make_arp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 16'h0001, 16'h0001, ip); deliver(1'b1); expect_reply(\"a changed IP\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #5_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_icmp_echo/README.md" : "# Ethernet ping (ICMP echo)\n\nAnswers `ping`: an ICMP echo request (RFC 792) to the design's MAC and IPv4 address gets an echo reply with the\nsame identifier, sequence number and data.\n\nEvery frame `eth_mac` receives is kept in a buffer (2^`ADDR_BITS` bytes, one block RAM at the default 11) while it is\nchecked: to `mac` (a broadcast ping isn't answered), EtherType 0800, IPv4 without options, **not a fragment** (a\nfragmented ping's first piece looks like a whole request), protocol 1, destination `ip`, a correct header checksum,\ntype 8, code 0, and an IP total length that fits the frame and the buffer. When the frame's last byte comes with\n`rx_good`, the reply goes out from the buffer: the Ethernet and IP addresses swapped, type 0, the ICMP checksum\nadjusted for the new type (RFC 1624) — the IP header's checksum doesn't change.\n\nThe reply is a valid/ready/last stream for `eth_mac`; `tx_valid` drops for two clocks after each byte while the\nnext one is read from the buffer, well within the MAC's four clocks a byte. One reply waits at a time; frames that\narrive meanwhile aren't kept.\n\n```verilog\neth_icmp_echo u_ping (\n    .clk(eth_clk), .mac(48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01), .ip({8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}),\n    .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last), .rx_good(rx_good),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready), .replied()\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_icmp_echo/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_mac\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"ICMP echo responder (ping, RFC 792) for IPv4 over Ethernet: keeps eth_mac's received frames in a block RAM, checks an echo request to `mac` and `ip` (IPv4 without options, not a fragment, header checksum, type 8 code 0) and sends the reply from the buffer with the addresses swapped, type 0 and the ICMP checksum adjusted (RFC 1624). Up to 2^ADDR_BITS bytes a frame (1514 at 11); one reply waits at a time.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 100,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DP16KD\" : 1,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 749,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 299\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 48.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 13,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 175,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 127,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 380,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 368,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 741,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 50,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 67.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 330,\n        \"BSRAM\" : 1,\n        \"DFF\" : 299,\n        \"LUT4\" : 500,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 74,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 29,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 12,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 44.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 746,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 4\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 202.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 36,\n        \"RAMB18E1\" : 1,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 270,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 513\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_icmp_echo\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"icmp\",\n    \"ping\",\n    \"echo\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"network\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"ICMP-Echo-Antwort (Ping, RFC 792) für IPv4 über Ethernet: hält die empfangenen Rahmen von eth_mac in einem Block-RAM, prüft eine Echo-Anfrage an `mac` und `ip` (IPv4 ohne Optionen, kein Fragment, Header-Prüfsumme, Typ 8 Code 0) und sendet die Antwort aus dem Puffer mit vertauschten Adressen, Typ 0 und angepasster ICMP-Prüfsumme (RFC 1624). Bis zu 2^ADDR_BITS Bytes je Rahmen (1514 bei 11); eine Antwort wartet zugleich.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-Ping\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet ping (ICMP echo)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 11,\n      \"description\" : \"The buffer holds 2^ADDR_BITS bytes (11: 2048, one block RAM, pings up to 1472 bytes of data)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ADDR_BITS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock (eth_mac's)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_last\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_good\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The reply's byte (to eth_mac's tx_data)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte of the reply is ready (low for two clocks after each byte)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the reply's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's tx_ready\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"replied\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_icmp_echo\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_icmp_echo/rtl/eth_icmp_echo.v" : "// ICMP echo responder (\"ping\", RFC 792) for IPv4 over Ethernet: answers an echo request to `mac` and `ip` with an\n// echo reply carrying the same identifier, sequence number and data.\n//\n// It watches eth_mac's received frames and keeps each one in a buffer of 2^ADDR_BITS bytes (one block RAM at 11)\n// while checking it: to `mac` (not broadcast), EtherType 0800, IPv4 with a 20-byte header (45), not a fragment (MF\n// clear, offset 0 — a fragmented ping's first piece looks like a whole request), protocol 1, destination `ip`, a\n// correct header checksum, ICMP type 8, code 0, and the IP total length within the frame and the buffer. When the\n// frame's last byte comes with `rx_good`, the reply goes out from the buffer with the addresses swapped, type 0\n// and the ICMP checksum adjusted (RFC 1624: +0800); the IP header's checksum stays as it is. The reply is offered\n// on a valid/ready/last stream — `tx_valid` drops for two clocks after each byte while the next one is read\n// (eth_mac asks every fourth clock). Frames that arrive while a reply waits or goes out aren't kept.\n\nmodule eth_icmp_echo #(\n    parameter integer ADDR_BITS = 11\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    input  wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    input  wire        rx_valid,\n    input  wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_good,\n    output wire [7:0]  tx_data,\n    output wire        tx_valid,\n    output wire        tx_last,\n    input  wire        tx_ready,\n    output reg         replied = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam integer SIZE = 1 << ADDR_BITS;\n\n    reg [7:0]  buffer [0:SIZE-1];\n    reg [7:0]  rd_data = 8'd0;\n\n    // ---- The request ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // Addresses are compared against bytes shifted out of registers; the IP header's words are summed without\n    // folding (20 bits hold ten of them) and the frame is judged over three clocks after its last byte (the next\n    // frame can't start for more than 48).\n    reg [11:0] index = 12'd0;   // bytes so far (stops at 4095)\n    reg        ok = 1'b1;\n    reg [47:0] mac_left = 48'd0;\n    reg [31:0] ip_left = 32'd0;\n    reg [15:0] total = 16'd0;   // the IP total length\n    reg [19:0] header_sum = 20'd0;\n    reg [7:0]  high = 8'd0;     // a 16-bit word's first byte\n    reg [15:0] icmp_sum = 16'd0;\n    reg        busy = 1'b0;     // a reply waits or goes out\n    reg [11:0] reply_last = 12'd0;\n    reg [15:0] reply_sum = 16'd0;\n\n    // The end of a frame: 1 the facts latched, 2 the sums folded and the lengths compared, 3 the decision.\n    reg        judge1 = 1'b0, judge2 = 1'b0;\n    reg        cand = 1'b0;\n    reg [11:0] count = 12'd0;\n    reg [16:0] fold1 = 17'd0;\n    reg [16:0] adjusted = 17'd0;\n    reg        lengths_ok = 1'b0;\n\n    function [7:0] fixed(input [11:0] i);\n        case (i)\n            12'd12: fixed = 8'h08;\n            12'd14: fixed = 8'h45;\n            12'd23: fixed = 8'h01;\n            12'd34: fixed = 8'h08;\n            default: fixed = 8'h00;   // 13, 35 (code 0)\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    wire sending_last = tx_valid && tx_ready && tx_last;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        replied <= 1'b0;\n        judge1 <= 1'b0;\n        judge2 <= 1'b0;\n        if (sending_last) busy <= 1'b0;\n        if (rx_valid) begin\n            if (!busy && index < SIZE) buffer[index[ADDR_BITS-1:0]] <= rx_data;\n            if (index != 12'd4095) index <= index + 12'd1;\n            if (index < 12'd6) begin\n                if (rx_data != (index == 12'd0 ? mac[47:40] : mac_left[47:40])) ok <= 1'b0;\n                mac_left <= {(index == 12'd0 ? mac[39:0] : mac_left[39:0]), 8'd0};\n            end\n            if (index == 12'd29) ip_left <= ip;\n            if (index >= 12'd30 && index <= 12'd33) begin\n                if (rx_data != ip_left[31:24]) ok <= 1'b0;\n                ip_left <= {ip_left[23:0], 8'd0};\n            end\n            if ((index == 12'd12 || index == 12'd13 || index == 12'd14 || index == 12'd23 || index == 12'd34 || index == 12'd35)\n                && rx_data != fixed(index)) ok <= 1'b0;\n            if (index == 12'd17) total <= {high, rx_data};\n            if (index == 12'd20 && rx_data[5:0] != 6'd0) ok <= 1'b0;   // MF and the offset's high bits\n            if (index == 12'd21 && rx_data != 8'd0) ok <= 1'b0;        // the offset's low bits\n            if (!index[0]) high <= rx_data;\n            if (index >= 12'd14 && index <= 12'd33 && index[0]) header_sum <= header_sum + {4'd0, high, rx_data};\n            if (index == 12'd37) icmp_sum <= {high, rx_data};\n            if (rx_last) begin\n                judge1 <= 1'b1;\n                cand <= rx_good && ok && !busy && !sending_last && index >= 12'd41;\n                count <= index + 12'd1;\n                index <= 12'd0;\n                ok <= 1'b1;\n            end\n        end\n        if (judge1) begin\n            judge2 <= 1'b1;\n            fold1 <= {1'b0, header_sum[15:0]} + {13'd0, header_sum[19:16]};\n            adjusted <= {1'b0, icmp_sum} + 17'h0800;\n            lengths_ok <= total >= 16'd28 && {4'd0, count} >= total + 16'd14 && total + 16'd14 <= SIZE;\n            header_sum <= 20'd0;\n        end\n        if (judge2) begin\n            if (cand && lengths_ok && fold1[15:0] + {15'd0, fold1[16]} == 16'hFFFF) begin\n                busy <= 1'b1;\n                reply_last <= total[11:0] + 12'd13;\n                reply_sum <= adjusted[15:0] + {15'd0, adjusted[16]};\n                replied <= 1'b1;\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- The reply ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // Byte d of the reply comes from the buffer at source(d), then is rewritten. A read is issued whenever the\n    // two-byte queue in front of tx_* will have room for it when it arrives (the read takes a clock), so with\n    // `tx_ready` on every clock a byte goes on every clock.\n    reg [11:0] next = 12'd0;          // the reply byte to read next\n    reg [11:0] reading = 12'd0;       // the one whose data arrives on this clock\n    reg        arriving = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0]  q0 = 8'd0, q1 = 8'd0;\n    reg        l0 = 1'b0, l1 = 1'b0;\n    reg [1:0]  queued = 2'd0;\n\n    function [11:0] source(input [11:0] d);\n        if (d < 12'd6) source = d + 12'd6;\n        else if (d >= 12'd26 && d < 12'd30) source = d + 12'd4;\n        else if (d >= 12'd30 && d < 12'd34) source = d - 12'd4;\n        else source = d;\n    endfunction\n\n    assign tx_data = q0;\n    assign tx_valid = busy && queued != 2'd0;\n    assign tx_last = l0;\n\n    wire       take = tx_valid && tx_ready;\n    wire [7:0] arrived = reading >= 12'd6 && reading < 12'd12 ? mac[47 - 8 * (reading - 12'd6) -: 8]\n                       : reading == 12'd34 ? 8'h00\n                       : reading == 12'd36 ? reply_sum[15:8]\n                       : reading == 12'd37 ? reply_sum[7:0]\n                       : rd_data;\n    wire       arrived_last = reading == reply_last;\n    wire [1:0] after = queued + {1'b0, arriving} - {1'b0, take};\n    wire       issue = busy && next <= reply_last && after <= 2'd1;\n    wire [11:0] read_at = source(next);\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rd_data <= buffer[read_at[ADDR_BITS-1:0]];\n        if (!busy) begin\n            next <= 12'd0;\n            arriving <= 1'b0;\n            queued <= 2'd0;\n        end else begin\n            arriving <= issue;\n            if (issue) begin\n                reading <= next;\n                next <= next + 12'd1;\n            end\n            queued <= after;\n            // A read is issued only when the queue will hold at most one byte as its data arrives: taking and\n            // arriving together find exactly one queued.\n            if (take && arriving) begin\n                q0 <= arrived; l0 <= arrived_last;\n            end else if (take) begin\n                q0 <= q1; l0 <= l1;\n            end else if (arriving) begin\n                if (queued == 2'd0) begin q0 <= arrived; l0 <= arrived_last; end\n                else begin q1 <= arrived; l1 <= arrived_last; end\n            end\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_icmp_echo/tb/eth_icmp_echo_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_icmp_echo: echo requests as eth_mac delivers them (a byte every fourth clock,\n// rx_last and rx_good with the last; short ones padded to 60 with non-zero bytes) and a MAC on the send side that\n// takes a byte every fourth clock. A reply must have the request's length without padding (14 + the IP total\n// length), the Ethernet and IP addresses swapped, type 0, a valid IP header checksum and ICMP checksum, the same\n// identifier, sequence and data. Payloads of 56, 1472, 18 and 1 bytes are answered, and one whose checksum carries\n// when it's adjusted (F800 or more); none of these are: a fragment\n// (MF set), a later fragment (an offset), a broadcast, another IP, a wrong header checksum, rx_good low, an echo\n// reply (type 0), code 3, a UDP packet, a frame shorter than its IP length. A reply that waits keeps its contents while another request arrives (not answered).\n// Then at 1000 Mbit/s: requests a byte on every clock and a consumer ready on every clock, which must get a byte on\n// every clock of the reply (eth_mac doesn't wait).\n// Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_icmp_echo_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}, HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n\n    reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        rx_valid = 1'b0, rx_last = 1'b0, rx_good = 1'b0;\n    wire [7:0] tx_data;\n    wire       tx_valid, tx_last, replied;\n    reg        tx_ready = 1'b0;\n    eth_icmp_echo dut (.clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP), .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last),\n        .rx_good(rx_good), .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready),\n        .replied(replied));\n\n    // ---- The MAC's send side --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] reply [0:2047];\n    integer reply_expected = 0;\n    integer reply_n = 0, replies = 0, replied_pulses = 0, asked_unready = 0;\n    reg [1:0] slot = 2'd0;\n    reg hold = 1'b0;\n    reg greedy = 1'b0;   // ready on every clock\n    reg dense = 1'b0;    // requests a byte on every clock\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        slot <= slot + 2'd1;\n        if (replied) replied_pulses = replied_pulses + 1;\n        if (tx_valid && tx_ready) begin\n            reply[reply_n] = tx_data;\n            reply_n = reply_n + 1;\n            if (tx_last) replies = replies + 1;\n        end\n        // eth_mac doesn't wait: once a reply has started, every clock it asks must find a byte.\n        if (tx_ready && reply_n > 0 && reply_n < reply_expected && !tx_valid) asked_unready = asked_unready + 1;\n    end\n    always @(negedge clk) tx_ready = (greedy || slot == 2'd0) && !hold;\n\n    // ---- Requests ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:2047];\n    integer frame_n;\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n\n    function [15:0] csum(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {frame[i], (i + 1 < to) ? frame[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            while (s[31:16] != 0) s = s[15:0] + s[31:16];\n            csum = ~s[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task make_ping(input [47:0] to, input [31:0] target, input integer payload, input [15:0] seq, input [15:0] flags, input [7:0] kind);\n        integer i, total;\n        begin\n            total = 28 + payload;\n            put48(0, to); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0800);\n            frame[14] = 8'h45; frame[15] = 8'h00; put16(16, total[15:0]); put16(18, 16'h1C46 + seq);\n            put16(20, flags); frame[22] = 8'd64; frame[23] = 8'd1; put16(24, 16'h0000);\n            put32(26, HOST_IP); put32(30, target);\n            put16(24, csum(14, 34));\n            frame[34] = kind; frame[35] = 8'd0; put16(36, 16'h0000); put16(38, 16'hBEEF); put16(40, seq);\n            for (i = 0; i < payload; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = (i * 37 + seq) & 8'hFF;\n            put16(36, csum(34, 14 + total));\n            frame_n = 14 + total;\n            for (i = frame_n; i < 60; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'hC0 + i;\n            if (frame_n < 60) frame_n = 60;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task deliver(input good);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            for (i = 0; i < frame_n; i = i + 1) begin\n                rx_data = frame[i];\n                rx_valid = 1'b1;\n                rx_last = (i == frame_n - 1);\n                rx_good = (i == frame_n - 1) && good;\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                if (!dense) begin\n                    rx_valid = 1'b0; rx_last = 1'b0; rx_good = 1'b0;\n                    repeat (3) @(posedge clk);\n                    #1;\n                end\n            end\n            rx_valid = 1'b0; rx_last = 1'b0; rx_good = 1'b0;\n            repeat (60) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [7:0] sent [0:2047];\n    task keep_request;\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < 2048; i = i + 1) sent[i] = frame[i];\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    function [15:0] reply_sum(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {reply[i], (i + 1 < to) ? reply[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            while (s[31:16] != 0) s = s[15:0] + s[31:16];\n            reply_sum = s[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task expect_reply(input integer payload, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        integer i, total, wrong;\n        begin\n            total = 28 + payload;\n            while (replies == 0) @(posedge clk);\n            if (replies != 1 || reply_n != 14 + total) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d replies, %0d bytes (expected %0d)\", what, replies, reply_n, 14 + total);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else begin\n                if ({reply[0], reply[1], reply[2], reply[3], reply[4], reply[5]} !== HOST ||\n                    {reply[6], reply[7], reply[8], reply[9], reply[10], reply[11]} !== MAC) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: MACs\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if ({reply[26], reply[27], reply[28], reply[29]} !== IP || {reply[30], reply[31], reply[32], reply[33]} !== HOST_IP)\n                    begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: IPs\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (reply_sum(14, 34) !== 16'hFFFF) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: IP checksum\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (reply[34] !== 8'd0 || reply[35] !== 8'd0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: type\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (reply_sum(34, 14 + total) !== 16'hFFFF) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: ICMP checksum\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                wrong = 0;\n                for (i = 38; i < 14 + total; i = i + 1) if (reply[i] !== sent[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n                for (i = 12; i < 26; i = i + 1) if (reply[i] !== sent[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n                if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes not as requested\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            end\n            if (replied_pulses != 1) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d replied pulses\", what, replied_pulses); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (asked_unready != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: no byte ready %0d times\", what, asked_unready); errors = errors + 1; end\n            replies = 0; reply_n = 0; replied_pulses = 0; asked_unready = 0;\n            repeat (20) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task expect_none(input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            repeat (400) @(posedge clk);\n            if (replies != 0 || reply_n != 0 || replied_pulses != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: answered\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n            replies = 0; reply_n = 0; replied_pulses = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task ping(input integer payload, input [15:0] seq);\n        begin\n            make_ping(MAC, IP, payload, seq, 16'h4000, 8'd8);\n            keep_request;\n            reply_expected = 42 + payload;\n            deliver(1'b1);\n            expect_reply(payload, \"a ping\");\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [15:0] carry_seq;\n    initial begin\n        ping(56, 16'd1);\n        ping(1472, 16'd2);\n        ping(18, 16'd3);\n        ping(1, 16'd4);\n        // A request whose ICMP checksum is F800 or more: +0800 carries out of 16 bits (the end-around carry).\n        carry_seq = 16'd100;\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, carry_seq, 16'h4000, 8'd8);\n        while ({frame[36], frame[37]} < 16'hF800) begin\n            carry_seq = carry_seq + 16'd1;\n            make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, carry_seq, 16'h4000, 8'd8);\n        end\n        ping(56, carry_seq);\n        // A frame shorter than its IP length says (the header checksum right for the longer length).\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd16, 16'h4000, 8'd8); put16(16, 16'd200); put16(24, 16'h0000); put16(24, csum(14, 34));\n        deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a truncated frame\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd15, 16'h4000, 8'd8); frame[35] = 8'd3; put16(36, 16'h0000); put16(36, csum(34, 98));\n        deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"code 3\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd5, 16'h2000, 8'd8); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a first fragment (MF)\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd6, 16'h00B9, 8'd8); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a later fragment\");\n        make_ping(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, IP, 56, 16'd7, 16'h4000, 8'd8); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a broadcast\");\n        make_ping(MAC, {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd99}, 56, 16'd8, 16'h4000, 8'd8); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another IP\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd9, 16'h4000, 8'd8); frame[24] = frame[24] ^ 8'h01; deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a wrong header checksum\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd10, 16'h4000, 8'd8); deliver(1'b0); expect_none(\"rx_good low\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd11, 16'h4000, 8'd0); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"an echo reply\");\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 56, 16'd12, 16'h4000, 8'd8); frame[23] = 8'd17; put16(24, 16'h0000); put16(24, csum(14, 34));\n        deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"UDP\");\n\n        // A reply waits while another request arrives.\n        hold = 1'b1;\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 40, 16'd13, 16'h4000, 8'd8);\n        keep_request;\n        reply_expected = 82;\n        deliver(1'b1);\n        make_ping(MAC, IP, 40, 16'd14, 16'h4000, 8'd8);\n        deliver(1'b1);\n        if (replied_pulses != 1) fail(\"a request answered while another waited\");\n        replied_pulses = 1;\n        hold = 1'b0;\n        expect_reply(40, \"a reply that waited\");\n\n        // 1000 Mbit/s: a byte a clock in and out.\n        greedy = 1'b1; dense = 1'b1;\n        ping(56, 16'd21);\n        ping(1472, 16'd22);\n        ping(1, 16'd23);\n        make_ping(MAC, {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd99}, 56, 16'd24, 16'h4000, 8'd8); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another IP, dense\");\n        greedy = 1'b0; dense = 1'b0;\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #10_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_mac/README.md" : "# Ethernet MAC\n\nThe MAC layer for `eth_rmii_phy` at 100 Mbit/s, on the PHY's 50 MHz clock. Frames go in and out from the\ndestination MAC address to the end of the payload.\n\n- **Receive:** the FCS is checked and removed. Bytes come out as they arrive (one every fourth clock at most), and\n  the frame's last byte carries `rx_last` and `rx_good`: the CRC-32 is right, RX_ER wasn't seen, and the frame was\n  64 to 1522 bytes long with its FCS. A bad frame is only known at its last byte — whatever stores frames must\n  drop the stored bytes then.\n- **Send:** offer a frame with `tx_valid` and `tx_last` on its last byte; a byte is taken when `tx_valid` and\n  `tx_ready` are both high. The MAC adds 7 × 55 and D5, pads to 60 bytes with zeros, adds the FCS and keeps the\n  inter-frame gap. It asks for a byte every fourth clock and doesn't wait: have the next one ready (from registers\n  or a block RAM read one clock ahead). A byte that isn't ready goes out as zero, and the FCS is inverted so every\n  receiver drops the frame; `tx_underrun` says so.\n\n```verilog\nwire [7:0] rx_data;\nwire rx_valid, rx_last, rx_good, tx_ready;\neth_mac u_mac (\n    .clk(eth_clk),\n    .phy_rx_data(phy_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(phy_rx_end), .phy_rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n    .phy_tx_data(phy_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(phy_tx_ready),\n    .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last), .rx_good(rx_good),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready), .tx_underrun()\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_mac/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_rmii_phy\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Ethernet MAC for eth_rmii_phy (100 Mbit/s): receives frames with the FCS checked and removed (`rx_good` with the last byte: CRC-32 right, no RX_ER, 64 to 1522 bytes) and sends them with preamble, padding to 60 bytes, FCS and the 96-bit inter-frame gap added. Bytes move one every fourth clock; a sender that falls behind gets its frame sent with an inverted FCS (every receiver drops it) and `tx_underrun`.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 155.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 323,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 151\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 55.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 5,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 23,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 92,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 206,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 170,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 492,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 8\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 70.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 64,\n        \"DFF\" : 151,\n        \"LUT4\" : 449,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 105,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 30,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 10,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 55,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 349,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 269.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 10,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 119,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 283\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_mac\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"mac\",\n    \"crc32\",\n    \"fcs\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"frame\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ethernet-MAC für eth_rmii_phy (100 Mbit/s): empfängt Rahmen mit geprüfter und entfernter FCS (`rx_good` mit dem letzten Byte: CRC-32 richtig, kein RX_ER, 64 bis 1522 Bytes) und sendet sie mit Präambel, Auffüllung auf 60 Bytes, FCS und der 96-Bit-Pause zwischen Rahmen. Bytes laufen eines je vierten Takt; kommt der Sender nicht nach, geht sein Rahmen mit invertierter FCS hinaus (jeder Empfänger verwirft ihn) und `tx_underrun` wird gesetzt.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-MAC\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet MAC\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 52,\n      \"description\" : \"Clocks of quiet after a frame's last byte is taken (48 = 96 bit times at 50 MHz, plus the PHY's last byte)\",\n      \"name\" : \"GAP_CLOCKS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's 50 MHz reference clock (as eth_rmii_phy)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_rmii_phy's rx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_rmii_phy's rx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_rmii_phy's rx_end\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_end\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_rmii_phy's rx_error\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_error\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To eth_rmii_phy's tx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To eth_rmii_phy's tx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_rmii_phy's tx_ready\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_tx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Received byte: destination MAC to the payload's end, the FCS removed\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses with each received byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the frame's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With rx_last: the frame is good (FCS, no RX_ER, length) — drop it otherwise\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to send: destination MAC to the payload's end\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte is offered (keep it ready: the MAC doesn't wait)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the frame's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High when tx_data is taken (with tx_valid)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The frame being sent had an underrun (it goes out with a bad FCS)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_underrun\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_mac\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_mac/rtl/eth_mac.v" : "// Ethernet MAC for eth_rmii_phy (100 Mbit/s): frames to and from the PHY's byte stream, from the destination MAC\n// to the end of the payload.\n//\n// Receive: the PHY's bytes are held back five deep, so the FCS (the last four) never comes out and the frame's last\n// byte can carry `rx_last` and `rx_good`. `rx_good` (with `rx_last`) means the CRC-32 over the frame and its FCS\n// left the residue DEBB20E3, no RX_ER, and 64 to 1522 bytes with the FCS. Bytes come out as they arrive, one every\n// fourth clock at most — a frame with a bad FCS is only known to be bad at its last byte, so whatever stores it must\n// drop it then. Frames shorter than five bytes give nothing.\n//\n// Send: offer a frame with `tx_valid`, `tx_last` on its last byte; a byte is taken when `tx_valid` and `tx_ready`\n// are both high. The MAC sends 7 × 55 and D5 first, pads to 60 bytes with zeros, adds the FCS and keeps the line\n// quiet for GAP_CLOCKS (the 96-bit inter-frame gap: 48 clocks at 50 MHz, plus the PHY's last byte). It asks for a\n// byte every fourth clock and doesn't wait: the next byte must be valid when it's asked for. If it isn't (an\n// underrun), a zero goes out instead and the FCS is sent inverted, so every receiver drops the frame; the rest of\n// the frame is still taken.\n\nmodule eth_mac #(\n    parameter integer GAP_CLOCKS = 52\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    // From and to eth_rmii_phy\n    input  wire [7:0] phy_rx_data,\n    input  wire       phy_rx_valid,\n    input  wire       phy_rx_end,\n    input  wire       phy_rx_error,\n    output reg  [7:0] phy_tx_data,\n    output wire       phy_tx_valid,\n    input  wire       phy_tx_ready,\n    // Received frames\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        rx_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_last = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_good = 1'b0,\n    // Frames to send\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data,\n    input  wire       tx_valid,\n    input  wire       tx_last,\n    output wire       tx_ready,\n    // An underrun happened in the frame being sent (cleared by the next frame)\n    output reg        tx_underrun = 1'b0\n);\n    function [31:0] crc_byte(input [31:0] c, input [7:0] d);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] x;\n        begin\n            x = c;\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1)\n                x = (x[0] ^ d[i]) ? ((x >> 1) ^ 32'hEDB8_8320) : (x >> 1);\n            crc_byte = x;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- Receive --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [39:0] held = 40'd0;      // the last five bytes, the oldest in [39:32]\n    reg [2:0]  held_n = 3'd0;\n    reg [10:0] rx_count = 11'd0;  // bytes with the FCS (stops at 2047)\n    reg [31:0] rx_crc = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rx_valid <= 1'b0;\n        rx_last <= 1'b0;\n        rx_good <= 1'b0;\n        if (phy_rx_valid) begin\n            held <= {held[31:0], phy_rx_data};\n            rx_crc <= crc_byte(rx_crc, phy_rx_data);\n            if (rx_count != 11'd2047) rx_count <= rx_count + 11'd1;\n            if (held_n == 3'd5) begin\n                rx_data <= held[39:32];\n                rx_valid <= 1'b1;\n            end else held_n <= held_n + 3'd1;\n        end\n        if (phy_rx_end) begin\n            if (held_n == 3'd5) begin\n                rx_data <= held[39:32];\n                rx_valid <= 1'b1;\n                rx_last <= 1'b1;\n                rx_good <= rx_crc == 32'hDEBB_20E3 && !phy_rx_error && rx_count >= 11'd64 && rx_count <= 11'd1522;\n            end\n            held_n <= 3'd0;\n            rx_count <= 11'd0;\n            rx_crc <= 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Send -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    localparam IDLE = 3'd0, PREAMBLE = 3'd1, DATA = 3'd2, PAD = 3'd3, FCS = 3'd4, GAP = 3'd5;\n    reg [2:0]  state = IDLE;\n    reg [2:0]  pre_n = 3'd0;      // preamble bytes sent\n    reg [5:0]  count = 6'd0;      // frame bytes sent, up to 60\n    reg [1:0]  fcs_n = 2'd0;\n    reg [31:0] tx_crc = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n    reg [6:0]  gap = 7'd0;\n\n    assign phy_tx_valid = state == PREAMBLE || state == DATA || state == PAD || state == FCS;\n    assign tx_ready = state == DATA && phy_tx_ready && tx_valid;\n    wire [31:0] fcs = ~tx_crc ^ (tx_underrun ? 32'h0000_00FF : 32'd0);\n\n    always @(*) begin\n        case (state)\n            PREAMBLE: phy_tx_data = pre_n == 3'd7 ? 8'hD5 : 8'h55;\n            DATA:     phy_tx_data = tx_valid ? tx_data : 8'h00;\n            FCS:      phy_tx_data = fcs[8 * fcs_n +: 8];\n            default:  phy_tx_data = 8'h00;\n        endcase\n    end\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        case (state)\n            IDLE:\n                if (tx_valid) begin\n                    state <= PREAMBLE;\n                    pre_n <= 3'd0;\n                    count <= 6'd0;\n                    tx_crc <= 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n                    tx_underrun <= 1'b0;\n                end\n            PREAMBLE:\n                if (phy_tx_ready) begin\n                    pre_n <= pre_n + 3'd1;\n                    if (pre_n == 3'd7) state <= DATA;\n                end\n            DATA:\n                if (phy_tx_ready) begin\n                    tx_crc <= crc_byte(tx_crc, phy_tx_data);\n                    if (count != 6'd60) count <= count + 6'd1;\n                    if (!tx_valid) tx_underrun <= 1'b1;\n                    if (tx_valid && tx_last) begin\n                        fcs_n <= 2'd0;\n                        state <= count >= 6'd59 ? FCS : PAD;\n                    end\n                end\n            PAD:\n                if (phy_tx_ready) begin\n                    tx_crc <= crc_byte(tx_crc, 8'h00);\n                    count <= count + 6'd1;\n                    if (count == 6'd59) state <= FCS;\n                end\n            FCS:\n                if (phy_tx_ready) begin\n                    fcs_n <= fcs_n + 2'd1;\n                    if (fcs_n == 2'd3) begin\n                        state <= GAP;\n                        gap <= 7'd0;\n                    end\n                end\n            default: begin\n                gap <= gap + 7'd1;\n                if (gap == GAP_CLOCKS - 1) state <= IDLE;\n            end\n        endcase\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_mac/tb/eth_mac_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_mac on eth_rmii_phy, against a model of a PHY's RMII side (outputs changed 4 ns\n// after the reference clock's rising edge).\n// Receive: a 64-byte frame, a 1518-byte one, a bad FCS, a runt (44 bytes), one too long (1523), one with RX_ER —\n// each frame's bytes without the FCS must come out in order, `rx_last` on the last, `rx_good` only for the good\n// ones.\n// Send: 14 bytes (padded to 60), exactly 60, 59 (one zero), 100 and straight after it 30 more (the gap between them\n// at least 48 clocks with TX_EN low), and one with an underrun. Each must go out as 7 × 55, D5, the bytes, the zeros,\n// the FCS — two of them checked against FCS values computed with Python's zlib (60 bytes (i*7+1): ed 70 90 df; 14\n// padded: 6b de f2 d2), the rest with the residue. The underrun's frame must fail its FCS and say so.\n// Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_mac_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;   // 50 MHz\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    reg  [1:0] rxd = 2'b00;\n    reg        crs_dv = 1'b0, rx_er = 1'b0;\n    wire [1:0] txd;\n    wire       tx_en;\n    wire [7:0] p_rx_data, p_tx_data;\n    wire       p_rx_valid, p_rx_end, p_rx_error, p_tx_valid, p_tx_ready;\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire       rx_valid, rx_last, rx_good, tx_ready, tx_underrun;\n    reg  [7:0] tx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        tx_valid = 1'b0, tx_last = 1'b0;\n\n    eth_rmii_phy u_phy (.clk(clk), .rmii_rxd(rxd), .rmii_crs_dv(crs_dv), .rmii_rx_er(rx_er), .rmii_txd(txd),\n        .rmii_tx_en(tx_en), .rx_data(p_rx_data), .rx_valid(p_rx_valid), .rx_end(p_rx_end), .rx_error(p_rx_error),\n        .tx_data(p_tx_data), .tx_valid(p_tx_valid), .tx_ready(p_tx_ready));\n    eth_mac dut (.clk(clk), .phy_rx_data(p_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(p_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(p_rx_end),\n        .phy_rx_error(p_rx_error), .phy_tx_data(p_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(p_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(p_tx_ready),\n        .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last), .rx_good(rx_good),\n        .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready), .tx_underrun(tx_underrun));\n\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // A CRC-32 written differently from the core's.\n    function [31:0] crc_update(input [31:0] c, input [7:0] d);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] x;\n        begin\n            x = c ^ {24'd0, d};\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) x = {1'b0, x[31:1]} ^ (x[0] ? 32'hEDB8_8320 : 32'd0);\n            crc_update = x;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- Received frames ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] got [0:2047];\n    integer got_n = 0, lasts = 0, last_at = -1;\n    reg good = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        if (rx_valid) begin\n            got[got_n] = rx_data;\n            if (rx_last) begin lasts = lasts + 1; last_at = got_n; good = rx_good; end\n            got_n = got_n + 1;\n        end\n\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:2047];\n    task put_dibit(input [1:0] d, input dv);\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #4;\n            rxd = d;\n            crs_dv = dv;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // `n` bytes of frame[] then their FCS (`bad`: one bit flipped), RX_ER during byte `er_at`.\n    task send(input integer n, input bad, input integer er_at);\n        integer i, j;\n        reg [31:0] c;\n        reg [7:0] b;\n        begin\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, frame[i]);\n            c = ~c ^ (bad ? 32'h0040_0000 : 32'd0);\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < 31; i = i + 1) put_dibit(2'b01, 1'b1);\n            put_dibit(2'b11, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < n + 4; i = i + 1) begin\n                b = i < n ? frame[i] : c[8 * (i - n) +: 8];\n                for (j = 0; j < 4; j = j + 1) begin\n                    rx_er = (i == er_at);\n                    put_dibit(b[2 * j +: 2], 1'b1);\n                end\n            end\n            rx_er = 1'b0;\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b0);\n            repeat (8) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task check_rx(input integer n, input expect_good, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong;\n        begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (got_n != n) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes out, expected %0d\", what, got_n, n); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (got[i] !== frame[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (lasts != 1 || last_at != n - 1) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d rx_last, at byte %0d\", what, lasts, last_at);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else if (good !== expect_good) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: rx_good %b\", what, good);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            got_n = 0;\n            lasts = 0;\n            last_at = -1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- Sent frames: decoded from TXD while TX_EN is up -----------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] wire_bytes [0:2047];\n    integer wire_n = 0, dibits = 0, frames_out = 0, quiet = 0, min_gap = 1000000;\n    reg [7:0] shift = 8'd0;\n    reg tx_en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (tx_en) begin\n            if (!tx_en_was && frames_out > 0 && quiet < min_gap) min_gap = quiet;\n            shift = {txd, shift[7:2]};\n            dibits = dibits + 1;\n            if (dibits % 4 == 0) begin wire_bytes[wire_n] = shift; wire_n = wire_n + 1; end\n        end else begin\n            if (tx_en_was) frames_out = frames_out + 1;\n            quiet = tx_en_was ? 1 : quiet + 1;\n        end\n        tx_en_was = tx_en;\n    end\n\n    reg [7:0] out [0:2047];\n    // Offers out[0 .. n-1]; `stall_at`: the byte withheld for 8 clocks once it's asked for (-1 none).\n    task offer(input integer n, input integer stall_at);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) begin\n                tx_data = out[i];\n                tx_last = (i == n - 1);\n                tx_valid = 1'b1;\n                if (i == stall_at) begin\n                    tx_valid = 1'b0;\n                    @(negedge clk);\n                    while (!p_tx_ready || dut.state != 3'd2) @(negedge clk);   // asked for and not there\n                    repeat (8) @(posedge clk);\n                    #1;\n                    tx_valid = 1'b1;\n                end\n                @(negedge clk);\n                while (!tx_ready) @(negedge clk);\n                @(posedge clk);\n                #1;\n            end\n            tx_valid = 1'b0;\n            tx_last = 1'b0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Waits for the frame to have gone out, then checks it: preamble, bytes, padding, FCS (residue; `fcs` if not 0).\n    task check_tx(input integer n, input [31:0] fcs, input expect_bad, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        integer i, padded, wrong, start;\n        reg [31:0] c;\n        begin\n            while (frames_out == 0) @(posedge clk);\n            padded = n < 60 ? 60 : n;\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (!expect_bad && wire_n != 8 + padded + 4) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes on the wire, expected %0d\", what, wire_n, 12 + padded); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else begin\n                for (i = 0; i < 7; i = i + 1) if (wire_bytes[i] !== 8'h55) wrong = wrong + 1;\n                if (wire_bytes[7] !== 8'hD5) wrong = wrong + 1;\n                if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: the preamble\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                wrong = 0;\n                for (i = 0; i < padded; i = i + 1)\n                    if (wire_bytes[8 + i] !== (i < n ? out[i] : 8'h00)) wrong = wrong + 1;\n                if (wrong != 0 && !expect_bad) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n                c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n                for (i = 8; i < wire_n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, wire_bytes[i]);\n                if ((c === 32'hDEBB_20E3) === expect_bad) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: the FCS\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                start = 8 + padded;\n                if (fcs != 0 && {wire_bytes[start], wire_bytes[start + 1], wire_bytes[start + 2], wire_bytes[start + 3]} !== fcs) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: %0s: FCS %h %h %h %h, zlib says %h\", what, wire_bytes[start], wire_bytes[start + 1],\n                             wire_bytes[start + 2], wire_bytes[start + 3], fcs);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            wire_n = 0;\n            dibits = 0;\n            frames_out = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer k;\n    initial begin\n        for (k = 0; k < 2048; k = k + 1) frame[k] = (k * 8'h5B) ^ 8'h3C ^ (k >> 4);\n\n        // ---- Receive\n        send(60, 0, -1);    check_rx(60, 1'b1, \"64 bytes\");\n        send(1514, 0, -1);  check_rx(1514, 1'b1, \"1518 bytes\");\n        send(60, 1, -1);    check_rx(60, 1'b0, \"a bad FCS\");\n        send(40, 0, -1);    check_rx(40, 1'b0, \"a runt\");\n        send(1519, 0, -1);  check_rx(1519, 1'b0, \"1523 bytes\");\n        send(60, 0, 30);    check_rx(60, 1'b0, \"RX_ER\");\n        send(61, 0, -1);    check_rx(61, 1'b1, \"65 bytes\");\n\n        // ---- Send\n        for (k = 0; k < 60; k = k + 1) out[k] = (k * 7 + 1) & 8'hFF;\n        offer(14, -1);  check_tx(14, 32'h6BDE_F2D2, 1'b0, \"14 bytes, padded\");\n        offer(60, -1);  check_tx(60, 32'hED70_90DF, 1'b0, \"60 bytes\");\n        offer(59, -1);  check_tx(59, 0, 1'b0, \"59 bytes\");\n        for (k = 0; k < 2048; k = k + 1) out[k] = (k * 8'h29) ^ 8'h96 ^ (k >> 5);\n        offer(100, -1);\n        min_gap = 1000000;\n        offer(30, -1);   // straight after: the MAC waits for the gap\n        while (frames_out < 2) @(posedge clk);\n        if (min_gap < 48) begin $display(\"FAIL: an inter-frame gap of %0d clocks\", min_gap); errors = errors + 1; end\n        wire_n = 0; dibits = 0; frames_out = 0;\n        offer(1514, -1); check_tx(1514, 0, 1'b0, \"1514 bytes\");\n        offer(80, 20);   check_tx(80, 0, 1'b1, \"an underrun\");\n        if (tx_underrun !== 1'b1) fail(\"tx_underrun not set\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_mdio/README.md" : "# Ethernet MDIO\n\nAn MDIO master (IEEE 802.3 clause 22) that also sets the PHY up and watches the link.\n\n- After `SETTLE_MS` (a PHY answers about 150 ms after its reset) it writes `ADVERTISE` into register 4 — by default\n  0x0101, 100BASE-TX full duplex only, which is what `eth_rmii_phy` speaks — and restarts auto-negotiation\n  (register 0 = 0x1200). `ADVERTISE = 0` leaves the PHY's own setting.\n- Then it reads register 1 `POLL_HZ` times a second: `link_up` is its bit 2. `setup_done` is high after the first read.\n- Between polls the design runs its own transactions: with `ready` high, `go` starts a read (into `rdata`) or a\n  write (`write`, `wdata`) of register `reg_addr`; `done` pulses when it's over.\n\nMDC runs at `MDC_HZ` or slower (2 MHz by default; the standard allows 2.5). MDIO is push-pull and the core lets go of\nit for the PHY's answer; it needs a pull-up — the Tang Primer 20K Dock has none on the board, so its `eth_mdio` pin\nasks for the FPGA's.\n\n```verilog\nwire mdio_out, mdio_oe, link_up;\nassign eth_mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\neth_mdio #(.CLK_HZ(50_000_000), .PHY_ADDR(5'd1)) u_mdio (\n    .clk(eth_clk), .mdc(eth_mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(eth_mdio),\n    .link_up(link_up), .setup_done(),\n    .go(1'b0), .write(1'b0), .reg_addr(5'd0), .wdata(16'd0), .ready(), .done(), .rdata()\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_mdio/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"MDIO master for an Ethernet PHY (clause 22) with the setup built in: after SETTLE_MS it advertises ADVERTISE (default 100 full duplex only) in register 4 and restarts auto-negotiation, then reads the link status POLL_HZ times a second (`link_up`). Between polls the design reads and writes registers with go/ready/done. MDC at most MDC_HZ; read bits taken just before MDC rises. MDIO is push-pull through mdio_out/mdio_oe/mdio_in.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 148.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 350,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 188\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 64.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 54,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 59,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 153,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 88,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 251\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 102.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 124,\n        \"DFF\" : 188,\n        \"LUT4\" : 231,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 9\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 44.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 334,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 236.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 18,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 185,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 303\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_mdio\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"mdio\",\n    \"mdc\",\n    \"smi\",\n    \"phy\",\n    \"link\",\n    \"autonegotiation\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO-Master für einen Ethernet-PHY (Clause 22) mit eingebauter Einrichtung: nach SETTLE_MS meldet er ADVERTISE (Vorgabe: nur 100 Vollduplex) in Register 4 an und startet die Aushandlung neu, dann liest er den Link-Status POLL_HZ-mal je Sekunde (`link_up`). Zwischen den Abfragen liest und schreibt das Design Register mit go/ready/done. MDC höchstens MDC_HZ; gelesene Bits kurz vor der steigenden MDC-Flanke übernommen. MDIO ist Gegentakt über mdio_out/mdio_oe/mdio_in.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-MDIO\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet MDIO\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 50000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Highest MDC frequency in Hz (the standard allows 2.5 MHz)\",\n      \"name\" : \"MDC_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's address (from its straps)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PHY_ADDR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 257,\n      \"description\" : \"Register 4 after the settling time: 0x0101 = 100BASE-TX full duplex only; 0 leaves the PHY's own\",\n      \"name\" : \"ADVERTISE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 200,\n      \"description\" : \"Milliseconds before the first transaction (the PHY needs about 150 after its reset)\",\n      \"name\" : \"SETTLE_MS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"Link polls a second\",\n      \"name\" : \"POLL_HZ\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDC to the PHY (the pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO's value while mdio_oe is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Drive MDIO (assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO as it is (the pin, with a pull-up)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY reports a link (register 1, bit 2)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"link_up\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High after the setup and the first link poll\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"setup_done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Starts a transaction (with ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"go\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With go: a write of wdata (else a read)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With go: the register\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"reg_addr\",\n      \"width\" : 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With go and write: the value\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wdata\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A transaction can start\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when the transaction is over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The last read's value (with done)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rdata\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_mdio\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.1\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_mdio/rtl/eth_mdio.v" : "// MDIO master for an Ethernet PHY (IEEE 802.3 clause 22), with the PHY's setup and a link watch built in.\n//\n// After SETTLE_MS (the PHY needs about 150 ms after its reset before it answers), the core writes ADVERTISE into\n// register 4 and restarts auto-negotiation (register 0 = 1200) — unless ADVERTISE is 0 — and from then on reads\n// register 1 (BMSR) POLL_HZ times a second: `link_up` is its bit 2. `setup_done` goes high after the first read.\n// Between polls it runs the design's own transactions (a poll that's due goes first, so they can't starve it): with `ready` high, `go` starts one — `write` with `wdata`, or\n// a read into `rdata` — on register `reg_addr` of PHY_ADDR; `done` pulses when it's over.\n//\n// A frame is 32 ones, 01, the operation (10 read, 01 write), the PHY's address, the register, the turnaround and 16\n// data bits, MSB first. MDC runs at no more than MDC_HZ; MDIO changes while MDC is low, the PHY samples on its rising\n// edge. In a read the core lets go of MDIO at the turnaround's first bit and takes each data bit just before MDC\n// rises (the PHY changes it up to 300 ns after the previous rising edge). MDIO is push-pull: at the top\n// `assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz; assign mdio_in = mdio;` — with a pull-up on the pin (many boards have\n// none: ask for the FPGA's).\n\nmodule eth_mdio #(\n    parameter integer CLK_HZ = 50_000_000,\n    parameter integer MDC_HZ = 2_000_000,\n    parameter [4:0]   PHY_ADDR = 5'd1,\n    parameter [15:0]  ADVERTISE = 16'h0101,   // register 4: 100BASE-TX full duplex only (0: leave the PHY's own)\n    parameter integer SETTLE_MS = 200,\n    parameter integer POLL_HZ = 10\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    output reg         mdc = 1'b0,\n    output reg         mdio_out = 1'b1,\n    output reg         mdio_oe = 1'b0,\n    input  wire        mdio_in,\n    output reg         link_up = 1'b0,\n    output reg         setup_done = 1'b0,\n    input  wire        go,\n    input  wire        write,\n    input  wire [4:0]  reg_addr,\n    input  wire [15:0] wdata,\n    output wire        ready,\n    output reg         done = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [15:0] rdata = 16'd0\n);\n    localparam integer HALF = (CLK_HZ + 2 * MDC_HZ - 1) / (2 * MDC_HZ);   // clocks per MDC half period, rounded up\n    localparam integer SETTLE = CLK_HZ / 1000 * SETTLE_MS;\n    localparam integer POLL = CLK_HZ / POLL_HZ;\n\n    // ---- One frame ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg        busy = 1'b0;\n    reg        frame_done = 1'b0;\n    reg        reading = 1'b0;\n    reg [63:0] frame = 64'd0;\n    reg [5:0]  bit_n = 6'd0;\n    reg [15:0] phase = 16'd0;   // 0 … 2 HALF − 1 within a bit\n    reg [1:0]  in_sync = 2'b11;\n    reg [15:0] shift_in = 16'd0;\n    reg        start = 1'b0;\n    reg        start_write = 1'b0;\n    reg [4:0]  start_reg = 5'd0;\n    reg [15:0] start_data = 16'd0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        in_sync <= {in_sync[0], mdio_in};\n        frame_done <= 1'b0;\n        if (!busy) begin\n            mdc <= 1'b0;\n            mdio_oe <= 1'b0;\n            if (start) begin\n                busy <= 1'b1;\n                bit_n <= 6'd0;\n                phase <= 16'd0;\n                reading <= !start_write;\n                frame <= {32'hFFFF_FFFF, 2'b01, start_write ? 2'b01 : 2'b10, PHY_ADDR, start_reg, 2'b10, start_data};\n            end\n        end else begin\n            phase <= phase + 16'd1;\n            if (phase == 16'd0) begin\n                mdc <= 1'b0;\n                mdio_out <= frame[63];\n                frame <= {frame[62:0], 1'b0};\n                mdio_oe <= !(reading && bit_n >= 6'd46);\n            end\n            if (phase == HALF - 1 && reading && bit_n >= 6'd48) shift_in <= {shift_in[14:0], in_sync[1]};\n            if (phase == HALF) mdc <= 1'b1;\n            if (phase == 2 * HALF - 1) begin\n                phase <= 16'd0;\n                bit_n <= bit_n + 6'd1;\n                if (bit_n == 6'd63) begin\n                    busy <= 1'b0;\n                    frame_done <= 1'b1;\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Setup, polls and the design's transactions ------------------------------------------------------------\n    localparam S_SETTLE = 3'd0, S_ADVERTISE = 3'd1, S_RESTART = 3'd2, S_POLL = 3'd3, S_IDLE = 3'd4, S_USER = 3'd5;\n    reg [2:0]  step = S_SETTLE;\n    reg        waiting = 1'b0;   // a frame of ours is under way\n    reg [31:0] timer = 32'd0;\n\n    assign ready = step == S_IDLE && !waiting && timer < POLL;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        start <= 1'b0;\n        done <= 1'b0;\n        // The timer runs from the start — through transactions too, so polls come due however busy the design keeps\n        // the core; whoever uses it clears it.\n        if (timer != 32'hFFFF_FFFF) timer <= timer + 32'd1;\n        if (waiting) begin\n            if (frame_done) begin\n                waiting <= 1'b0;\n                case (step)\n                    S_ADVERTISE: step <= S_RESTART;\n                    S_RESTART:   step <= S_POLL;\n                    S_POLL: begin\n                        link_up <= shift_in[2];\n                        setup_done <= 1'b1;\n                        step <= S_IDLE;\n                    end\n                    default: begin   // S_USER\n                        if (reading) rdata <= shift_in;\n                        done <= 1'b1;\n                        step <= S_IDLE;\n                    end\n                endcase\n            end\n        end else begin\n            case (step)\n                S_SETTLE:\n                    if (timer >= SETTLE) begin\n                        timer <= 32'd0;\n                        step <= ADVERTISE != 16'd0 ? S_ADVERTISE : S_POLL;\n                    end\n                S_ADVERTISE: begin\n                    start <= 1'b1; start_write <= 1'b1; start_reg <= 5'd4; start_data <= ADVERTISE; waiting <= 1'b1;\n                end\n                S_RESTART: begin\n                    start <= 1'b1; start_write <= 1'b1; start_reg <= 5'd0; start_data <= 16'h1200; waiting <= 1'b1;\n                end\n                S_POLL: begin\n                    start <= 1'b1; start_write <= 1'b0; start_reg <= 5'd1; start_data <= 16'd0; waiting <= 1'b1;\n                end\n                S_IDLE:\n                    // The link poll first when it's due: a design that asks for transactions back to back must not\n                    // starve it (link_up would keep the first poll's answer, read while auto-negotiation restarted).\n                    if (timer >= POLL) begin\n                        timer <= 32'd0;\n                        step <= S_POLL;\n                    end else if (go) begin\n                        start <= 1'b1; start_write <= write; start_reg <= reg_addr; start_data <= wdata;\n                        waiting <= 1'b1;\n                        step <= S_USER;\n                    end\n                default: ;\n            endcase\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_mdio/tb/eth_mdio_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_mdio against a model of a PHY's MDIO side (clause 22, its own address only, data\n// changed 10 or 290 ns after MDC rises (the spec allows 0 to 300), a register file; register 1's bit 2 the link). Two cores on two PHYs: the default\n// (ADVERTISE 0101) and one with ADVERTISE 0. Checks:\n//   - MDC never faster than 2.5 MHz; MDIO never driven by both sides at once;\n//   - nothing before SETTLE_MS; then register 4 = 0101 and register 0 = 1200, in that order (none with ADVERTISE 0);\n//   - `link_up` follows the PHY's link (up, down, up again) through the polls;\n//   - the design's read of register 3 and write of register 0x1F reach the PHY and come back; `done` pulses once each;\n//   - with the design asking for transactions back to back, the polls still run and `link_up` follows the link.\n// Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule phy_mdio_model #(parameter [4:0] ADDR = 5'd1, parameter real DELAY = 100.0) (\n    input  wire mdc,\n    inout  wire mdio,\n    input  wire master_oe\n);\n    reg [15:0] regs [0:31];\n    integer i;\n    initial begin\n        for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) regs[i] = 16'h0000;\n        regs[0] = 16'h3100; regs[1] = 16'h782D; regs[2] = 16'h001C; regs[3] = 16'hC816; regs[4] = 16'h01E1;\n    end\n    integer ones = 0, n = -1, writes = 0, contention = 0;\n    reg [1:0] op;\n    reg [4:0] pa, ra;\n    reg [15:0] data;\n    reg drive = 1'b0, value = 1'b1;\n    reg [4:0] write_regs [0:15];\n    assign mdio = drive ? value : 1'bz;\n\n    always @(posedge mdc) begin : slave\n        reg b;\n        b = mdio;\n        if (n < 0) begin\n            if (b === 1'b1) ones = ones + 1;\n            else begin\n                if (ones >= 32) n = 32;\n                ones = 0;\n            end\n        end else begin\n            n = n + 1;\n            if (n == 33 && b !== 1'b1) n = -1;\n            else if (n == 34 || n == 35) op = {op[0], b};\n            else if (n >= 36 && n <= 40) pa = {pa[3:0], b};\n            else if (n >= 41 && n <= 45) begin\n                ra = {ra[3:0], b};\n                if (n == 45) data = regs[ra];\n            end else if (op == 2'b10 && pa == ADDR) begin\n                if (n == 46) #(DELAY) begin drive = 1'b1; value = 1'b0; end\n                else if (n >= 47 && n <= 62) #(DELAY) value = data[62 - n];\n                else if (n == 63) begin #(DELAY) drive = 1'b0; n = -1; end\n            end else if (op == 2'b01 && pa == ADDR) begin\n                if (n >= 48) data = {data[14:0], b};\n                if (n == 63) begin\n                    regs[ra] = data;\n                    if (writes < 16) write_regs[writes] = ra;\n                    writes = writes + 1;\n                    n = -1;\n                end\n            end else if (n == 63) n = -1;\n        end\n    end\n    always @(master_oe or drive) if (master_oe === 1'b1 && drive) contention = contention + 1;\nendmodule\n\nmodule eth_mdio_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;   // 50 MHz\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    wire mdc_a, mdio_a, out_a, oe_a, link_a, setup_a, ready_a, done_a;\n    wire [15:0] rdata_a;\n    reg go = 1'b0, write = 1'b0;\n    reg [4:0] reg_addr = 5'd0;\n    reg [15:0] wdata = 16'd0;\n    pullup (mdio_a);\n    assign mdio_a = oe_a ? out_a : 1'bz;\n    eth_mdio #(.CLK_HZ(50_000_000), .MDC_HZ(2_000_000), .SETTLE_MS(1), .POLL_HZ(2000)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .mdc(mdc_a), .mdio_out(out_a), .mdio_oe(oe_a), .mdio_in(mdio_a), .link_up(link_a),\n        .setup_done(setup_a), .go(go), .write(write), .reg_addr(reg_addr), .wdata(wdata), .ready(ready_a),\n        .done(done_a), .rdata(rdata_a));\n    phy_mdio_model #(.ADDR(5'd1), .DELAY(10.0)) phy_a (.mdc(mdc_a), .mdio(mdio_a), .master_oe(oe_a));\n\n    wire mdc_b, mdio_b, out_b, oe_b, link_b, setup_b;\n    pullup (mdio_b);\n    assign mdio_b = oe_b ? out_b : 1'bz;\n    eth_mdio #(.CLK_HZ(50_000_000), .MDC_HZ(2_000_000), .ADVERTISE(16'h0000), .SETTLE_MS(1), .POLL_HZ(2000)) dut_b (\n        .clk(clk), .mdc(mdc_b), .mdio_out(out_b), .mdio_oe(oe_b), .mdio_in(mdio_b), .link_up(link_b),\n        .setup_done(setup_b), .go(1'b0), .write(1'b0), .reg_addr(5'd0), .wdata(16'd0), .ready(), .done(), .rdata());\n    phy_mdio_model #(.ADDR(5'd1), .DELAY(290.0)) phy_b (.mdc(mdc_b), .mdio(mdio_b), .master_oe(oe_b));\n\n    // MDC's shortest period, and the first MDC edge's time.\n    realtime last_rise = 0, shortest = 1e9, first_rise = 0;\n    always @(posedge mdc_a) begin\n        if (last_rise > 0 && $realtime - last_rise < shortest) shortest = $realtime - last_rise;\n        if (first_rise == 0) first_rise = $realtime;\n        last_rise = $realtime;\n    end\n\n    integer dones = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (done_a) dones = dones + 1;\n\n    // A design that asks for a read of register 2 whenever the core is ready.\n    reg busy_reads = 1'b0;\n    always @(negedge clk) if (busy_reads) begin go = ready_a; write = 1'b0; reg_addr = 5'd2; end\n\n    task wait_ready;\n        begin\n            @(negedge clk);\n            while (!ready_a) @(negedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        // Setup.\n        wait (setup_a === 1'b1);\n        if (first_rise < 1_000_000) fail(\"MDC ran before SETTLE_MS\");\n        if (phy_a.writes != 2 || phy_a.write_regs[0] != 5'd4 || phy_a.write_regs[1] != 5'd0) fail(\"the setup writes (4, then 0)\");\n        if (phy_a.regs[4] !== 16'h0101) fail(\"register 4 not 0101\");\n        if (phy_a.regs[0] !== 16'h1200) fail(\"register 0 not 1200\");\n        if (link_a !== 1'b1) fail(\"link_up after the first poll\");\n\n        // The link goes down and comes back.\n        phy_a.regs[1] = 16'h782D & ~16'h0004;\n        #1_200_000;\n        if (link_a !== 1'b0) fail(\"link_up didn't follow the link down\");\n        phy_a.regs[1] = 16'h782D;\n        #1_200_000;\n        if (link_a !== 1'b1) fail(\"link_up didn't follow the link up\");\n\n        // The design's own read and write.\n        wait_ready;\n        go = 1'b1; write = 1'b0; reg_addr = 5'd3;\n        @(posedge clk); #1; go = 1'b0;\n        wait (done_a === 1'b1);\n        @(posedge clk); #1;\n        if (rdata_a !== 16'hC816) fail(\"the read of register 3\");\n        wait_ready;\n        go = 1'b1; write = 1'b1; reg_addr = 5'd31; wdata = 16'h5A3C;\n        @(posedge clk); #1; go = 1'b0;\n        wait (done_a === 1'b1);\n        @(posedge clk); #1;\n        if (phy_a.regs[31] !== 16'h5A3C) fail(\"the write of register 31\");\n        if (dones != 2) fail(\"done pulses\");\n\n        // A design asking for transactions back to back (the Arty spike did): the polls still run, link_up follows.\n        busy_reads = 1'b1;\n        phy_a.regs[1] = 16'h782D & ~16'h0004;\n        #1_500_000;\n        if (link_a !== 1'b0) fail(\"link_up starved by back-to-back transactions (link down)\");\n        phy_a.regs[1] = 16'h782D;\n        #1_500_000;\n        if (link_a !== 1'b1) fail(\"link_up starved by back-to-back transactions (link up)\");\n        busy_reads = 1'b0;\n\n        if (shortest < 400) fail(\"MDC faster than 2.5 MHz\");\n        if (phy_a.contention != 0 || phy_b.contention != 0) fail(\"MDIO driven by both sides\");\n        if (phy_b.writes != 0) fail(\"ADVERTISE 0 still wrote\");\n        if (setup_b !== 1'b1 || link_b !== 1'b1) fail(\"ADVERTISE 0: no polls\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_mii_phy/README.md" : "# Ethernet MII PHY\n\nThe MII side of a 10/100 Ethernet PHY at 100 Mbit/s (TI DP83848J on the Arty A7, Microchip KSZ8081 in MII mode …),\nwith the same byte interface as `eth_rmii_phy`, so `eth_mac` and the protocol cores work on it unchanged.\n\nMII has two clocks of its own from the PHY — `mii_rx_clk` and `mii_tx_clk`, 25 MHz at 100 Mbit/s — and a byte every\nsecond clock, faster than the cores' pace. So the core runs the byte side on a system clock `clk` of at least four\ntimes that (100 MHz) and crosses to the nibble clocks through `async_fifo`:\n\n- **Receive (rx_clk):** after RX_DV rises, nibbles 5 … 5 then D start the frame; two nibbles make a byte, the low one\n  first. Bytes and the frame's end come out on `clk` as `rx_valid` and `rx_end` (with `rx_error` when RX_ER was seen\n  or the frame ended on a stray nibble).\n- **Send (clk):** `tx_ready` comes at most every fourth clock — `eth_mac` and the cores in front of it rely on that\n  pace — while the FIFO has room; `tx_valid` falling after a frame queues its end. On `tx_clk` a frame starts once\n  bytes are queued (it waits 8 nibble clocks so the FIFO fills first), its nibbles go out low first, and TX_EN stays\n  low for at least 24 nibble clocks between frames (the 96-bit gap).\n\nThe PHY usually needs a 25 MHz reference from the FPGA (the Arty's `eth_ref_clk`: the 100 MHz clock divided by 4 is\nenough). `eth_udp_stack_mii` puts it together with the MAC, MDIO and the protocols.\n\n```verilog\neth_mii_phy u_phy (\n    .clk(clk), .mii_rx_clk(eth_rx_clk), .mii_rxd(eth_rxd), .mii_rx_dv(eth_rx_dv), .mii_rx_er(eth_rxerr),\n    .mii_tx_clk(eth_tx_clk), .mii_txd(eth_txd), .mii_tx_en(eth_tx_en),\n    .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_end(rx_end), .rx_error(rx_error),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_ready(tx_ready)\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_mii_phy/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"async_fifo\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"MII interface to a 10/100 Ethernet PHY at 100 Mbit/s (DP83848 on the Arty A7, KSZ8081 …) with the same byte interface as eth_rmii_phy, on a system clock of its own (at least four times the 25 MHz nibble clock, e.g. 100 MHz): the PHY's rx_clk and tx_clk are crossed through async_fifo. Received bytes after the SFD with rx_end/rx_error; bytes to send paced one every fourth clock, the frame's end queued when tx_valid falls, the 96-bit gap kept on tx_clk.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 139.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 211,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 163,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 6\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 59.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 5,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 12,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 50,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 239,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 315,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 495,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 47,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 55,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 101.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 36,\n        \"DFF\" : 163,\n        \"LUT4\" : 233,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 14,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 4,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 2,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 6\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 49.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 306,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 301.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 11,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 115,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 222\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_mii_phy\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"mii\",\n    \"phy\",\n    \"dp83848\",\n    \"arty\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"100base-tx\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"MII-Schnittstelle zu einem 10/100-Ethernet-PHY bei 100 Mbit/s (DP83848 auf dem Arty A7, KSZ8081 …) mit derselben Byte-Schnittstelle wie eth_rmii_phy, an einem eigenen Systemtakt (mindestens das Vierfache des 25-MHz-Nibbletakts, z. B. 100 MHz): rx_clk und tx_clk des PHY werden über async_fifo überquert. Empfangene Bytes nach dem SFD mit rx_end/rx_error; zu sendende Bytes eines je vierten Takt, das Rahmenende eingereiht, wenn tx_valid fällt, die 96-Bit-Pause auf tx_clk eingehalten.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet MII-PHY\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet MII PHY\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock (at least 4 × the nibble clock: 100 MHz for 100 Mbit/s)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RX_CLK from the PHY (25 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RXD from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rxd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RX_DV from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rx_dv\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RX_ER from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rx_er\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII TX_CLK from the PHY (25 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_tx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII TXD to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_txd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII TX_EN to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_tx_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Received byte (after the SFD, the FCS included), on clk\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses with each received byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a frame is over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_end\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With rx_end: RX_ER was seen, or the frame ended on a stray nibble\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_error\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to send (preamble, SFD and FCS included)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High for every byte of a frame; falling ends it\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High when tx_data is taken (with tx_valid), at most every fourth clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_mii_phy\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_mii_phy/rtl/eth_mii_phy.v" : "// MII interface to an Ethernet PHY at 100 Mbit/s (TI DP83848, Microchip KSZ8081 in MII mode …), with the same byte\n// interface as eth_rmii_phy, on a system clock of its own: the PHY's rx_clk and tx_clk (25 MHz) are crossed through\n// async_fifo. The system clock must be at least four times the nibble clock (100 MHz for 25).\n//\n// Receive (rx_clk): after RX_DV rises, nibbles 5 … 5 then D (the SFD's high nibble) start the frame; two nibbles make a\n// byte, the low one first. Bytes and the frame's end (RX_DV low; with an error when RX_ER was seen or a nibble was left\n// over) cross to `clk`, where they come out as `rx_valid` and `rx_end` / `rx_error` pulses.\n//\n// Send (clk): `tx_ready` comes at most every fourth clock (eth_mac and the cores in front of it need that pace) while\n// the FIFO has room; bytes go into the FIFO, and `tx_valid` falling after a frame marks its end. On tx_clk a frame\n// starts 8 nibble clocks after its first byte is queued (the FIFO fills meanwhile), its nibbles go out low first with TX_EN, and TX_EN stays low for at least 24 nibble\n// clocks after it (the 96-bit gap).\n\nmodule eth_mii_phy (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    // The PHY's pins\n    input  wire       mii_rx_clk,\n    input  wire [3:0] mii_rxd,\n    input  wire       mii_rx_dv,\n    input  wire       mii_rx_er,\n    input  wire       mii_tx_clk,\n    output reg  [3:0] mii_txd = 4'd0,\n    output reg        mii_tx_en = 1'b0,\n    // Bytes on clk, as eth_rmii_phy's\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        rx_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_end = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_error = 1'b0,\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data,\n    input  wire       tx_valid,\n    output wire       tx_ready\n);\n    // ---- Receive, on rx_clk ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [3:0] rxd = 4'd0;\n    reg       dv = 1'b0, er = 1'b0;\n    localparam R_IDLE = 2'd0, R_PREAMBLE = 2'd1, R_DATA = 2'd2;\n    reg [1:0] r_state = R_IDLE;\n    reg       r_high = 1'b0;       // the next nibble is a byte's high one\n    reg [3:0] r_low = 4'd0;\n    reg       r_er_seen = 1'b0;\n    reg       r_write = 1'b0;\n    reg [9:0] r_word = 10'd0;      // {end, error, byte} or a byte\n\n    always @(posedge mii_rx_clk) begin\n        rxd <= mii_rxd;\n        dv <= mii_rx_dv;\n        er <= mii_rx_er;\n        r_write <= 1'b0;\n        case (r_state)\n            R_IDLE:\n                if (dv) r_state <= rxd == 4'hD ? R_DATA : R_PREAMBLE;\n            R_PREAMBLE:\n                if (!dv) r_state <= R_IDLE;\n                else if (rxd == 4'hD) begin\n                    r_state <= R_DATA;\n                    r_high <= 1'b0;\n                    r_er_seen <= 1'b0;\n                end\n            default:\n                if (!dv) begin\n                    r_state <= R_IDLE;\n                    r_write <= 1'b1;\n                    r_word <= {1'b1, r_er_seen || r_high, 8'd0};\n                end else begin\n                    if (er) r_er_seen <= 1'b1;\n                    r_high <= !r_high;\n                    if (!r_high) r_low <= rxd;\n                    else begin\n                        r_write <= 1'b1;\n                        r_word <= {2'b00, rxd, r_low};\n                    end\n                end\n        endcase\n        if (r_state == R_IDLE && dv) begin\n            r_high <= 1'b0;\n            r_er_seen <= 1'b0;\n        end\n    end\n\n    wire [9:0] rx_word;\n    wire       rx_empty;\n    reg        rx_read = 1'b0, rx_pending = 1'b0;\n    async_fifo #(.WIDTH(10), .DEPTH_LOG2(4)) u_rx_fifo (\n        .wr_clk(mii_rx_clk), .wr_rst(1'b0), .wr_data(r_word), .wr_en(r_write), .full(),\n        .rd_clk(clk), .rd_rst(1'b0), .rd_en(rx_read), .rd_data(rx_word), .empty(rx_empty));\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rx_valid <= 1'b0;\n        rx_end <= 1'b0;\n        rx_read <= !rx_empty && !rx_read && !rx_pending;\n        rx_pending <= rx_read;\n        if (rx_pending) begin\n            if (rx_word[9]) begin\n                rx_end <= 1'b1;\n                rx_error <= rx_word[8];\n            end else begin\n                rx_valid <= 1'b1;\n                rx_data <= rx_word[7:0];\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Send: into the FIFO, on clk --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // A frame's end: tx_valid falling after a byte was taken. It is latched (the FIFO may be full just then) and queued\n    // as an end marker before the next frame's first byte is taken.\n    wire       tx_full;\n    reg [1:0]  pace = 2'd0;\n    reg        in_frame = 1'b0;      // bytes of a frame have been taken\n    reg        end_pending = 1'b0;   // its end waits for room in the FIFO\n    wire       ending = end_pending && !tx_full;\n    assign tx_ready = tx_valid && !tx_full && pace == 2'd0 && !end_pending;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (pace != 2'd0) pace <= pace + 2'd1;\n        else if (tx_ready) pace <= 2'd1;\n        if (tx_ready) in_frame <= 1'b1;\n        if (in_frame && !tx_valid && !end_pending) begin\n            end_pending <= 1'b1;\n            in_frame <= 1'b0;\n        end\n        if (ending) end_pending <= 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    wire [8:0] tx_word;\n    wire       tx_empty;\n    reg        t_read = 1'b0;\n    async_fifo #(.WIDTH(9), .DEPTH_LOG2(4)) u_tx_fifo (\n        .wr_clk(clk), .wr_rst(1'b0), .wr_data(ending ? 9'h100 : {1'b0, tx_data}), .wr_en(tx_ready || ending),\n        .full(tx_full), .rd_clk(mii_tx_clk), .rd_rst(1'b0), .rd_en(t_read), .rd_data(tx_word), .empty(tx_empty));\n\n    // ---- Send: out of the FIFO, on tx_clk ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // A word is read one clock ahead (rd_data follows rd_en a clock later): `t_have` says tx_word holds the next one.\n    reg [3:0]  t_high = 4'd0;\n    reg        t_second = 1'b0;    // the byte's high nibble goes out next\n    reg        t_have = 1'b0;\n    reg        t_sending = 1'b0;\n    reg [4:0]  t_gap = 5'd24;      // nibble clocks since the last frame (stops at 24)\n    reg [3:0]  t_fill = 4'd0;      // nibble clocks a frame's first byte has waited (the FIFO fills meanwhile)\n\n    always @(posedge mii_tx_clk) begin\n        t_read <= 1'b0;\n        if (t_read) t_have <= 1'b1;\n        if (!t_sending) begin\n            mii_tx_en <= 1'b0;\n            mii_txd <= 4'd0;\n            if (t_gap != 5'd24) t_gap <= t_gap + 5'd1;\n            if (!t_have) t_fill <= 4'd0;\n            if (!t_have && !t_read && !tx_empty) t_read <= 1'b1;\n            else if (t_have && tx_word[8]) t_have <= 1'b0;   // an end with no frame: drop it\n            else if (t_have) begin\n                // The FIFO's `empty` reaches tx_clk a few clocks late: let it fill before the first nibble, so a\n                // byte written meanwhile is never taken for the frame's end.\n                if (t_fill != 4'd8) t_fill <= t_fill + 4'd1;\n                else if (t_gap == 5'd24) t_sending <= 1'b1;\n            end\n        end else if (!t_second) begin\n            // A byte's low nibble, or the frame's end.\n            if (!t_have || tx_word[8]) begin\n                t_sending <= 1'b0;\n                t_have <= 1'b0;\n                t_gap <= 5'd0;\n                mii_tx_en <= 1'b0;\n                mii_txd <= 4'd0;\n            end else begin\n                mii_tx_en <= 1'b1;\n                mii_txd <= tx_word[3:0];\n                t_high <= tx_word[7:4];\n                t_second <= 1'b1;\n                t_have <= 1'b0;\n                if (!tx_empty) t_read <= 1'b1;\n            end\n        end else begin\n            mii_txd <= t_high;\n            t_second <= 1'b0;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_mii_phy/tb/eth_mii_phy_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_mii_phy on a 100 MHz system clock, against a model of a PHY's MII side: rx_clk at\n// 25 MHz and tx_clk at 24.97 MHz with another phase (the FIFOs really cross); received nibbles changed 10 ns after\n// rx_clk rises. Receive: frames of 64 and 300 bytes (15 × 5, D, then the bytes low nibble first), one with RX_ER, one\n// ending on a stray nibble, a false carrier (RX_DV without an SFD) — every byte must come out once, in order, then one\n// rx_end with the right rx_error. Send: frames of 1, 12 and 200 bytes offered with valid/ready; tx_ready never on two\n// clocks less than four apart, TX_EN up exactly for the frame's nibbles (low first), and two 80-byte frames offered back to\n// back (the FIFO full at the first one's end) sent as two, at least 24 nibble clocks apart. A false carrier leaves RXD at\n// D with RX_DV low: nothing may start. Prints PASS or every failed\n// check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_mii_phy_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0, rx_clk = 1'b0, tx_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    always #20 rx_clk = ~rx_clk;\n    initial begin #13; forever #20.024 tx_clk = ~tx_clk; end\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    reg  [3:0] rxd = 4'd0;\n    reg        rx_dv = 1'b0, rx_er = 1'b0;\n    wire [3:0] txd;\n    wire       tx_en;\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire       rx_valid, rx_end, rx_error, tx_ready;\n    reg  [7:0] tx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        tx_valid = 1'b0;\n    eth_mii_phy dut (.clk(clk), .mii_rx_clk(rx_clk), .mii_rxd(rxd), .mii_rx_dv(rx_dv), .mii_rx_er(rx_er),\n        .mii_tx_clk(tx_clk), .mii_txd(txd), .mii_tx_en(tx_en), .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid),\n        .rx_end(rx_end), .rx_error(rx_error), .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_ready(tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- Receive ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] got [0:511];\n    integer got_n = 0, ends = 0;\n    reg last_error = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (rx_valid) begin got[got_n] = rx_data; got_n = got_n + 1; end\n        if (rx_end) begin ends = ends + 1; last_error = rx_error; end\n    end\n\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:511];\n    task nibble(input [3:0] v, input dv);\n        begin @(posedge rx_clk); #10; rxd = v; rx_dv = dv; end\n    endtask\n    // `odd`: a stray nibble after the bytes; `er_at`: RX_ER during that byte (-1 none).\n    task send(input integer n, input odd, input integer er_at);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < 15; i = i + 1) nibble(4'h5, 1'b1);\n            nibble(4'hD, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) begin\n                rx_er = (i == er_at);\n                nibble(frame[i][3:0], 1'b1);\n                nibble(frame[i][7:4], 1'b1);\n            end\n            rx_er = 1'b0;\n            if (odd) nibble(4'hA, 1'b1);\n            nibble(4'h0, 1'b0);\n            repeat (20) @(posedge rx_clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n    task check_received(input integer n, input expect_error, input [8*32-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong;\n        begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (got_n != n) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes, expected %0d\", what, got_n, n); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (got[i] !== frame[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (ends != 1 || last_error !== expect_error) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d ends, rx_error %b\", what, ends, last_error);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            got_n = 0; ends = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- Send -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] wire_bytes [0:511];\n    integer wire_n = 0, nibbles = 0, frames = 0, idle = 1000, min_gap = 1000;\n    reg [7:0] cur = 8'd0;\n    reg en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge tx_clk) begin\n        if (tx_en) begin\n            if (!en_was && frames > 0 && idle < min_gap) min_gap = idle;\n            cur = {txd, cur[7:4]};\n            nibbles = nibbles + 1;\n            if (nibbles % 2 == 0) begin wire_bytes[wire_n] = cur; wire_n = wire_n + 1; end\n        end else begin\n            if (en_was) frames = frames + 1;\n            idle = en_was ? 1 : idle + 1;\n        end\n        en_was = tx_en;\n    end\n\n    // tx_ready's pace: never on two clocks less than four apart.\n    integer since_ready = 100, fast_ready = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (tx_ready) begin\n            if (since_ready < 3) fast_ready = fast_ready + 1;   // three clocks between: four apart\n            since_ready = 0;\n        end else since_ready = since_ready + 1;\n    end\n\n    reg [7:0] sent [0:511];\n    task offer(input integer n, input [7:0] seed);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #1;   // offer between edges, so no handshake happens before it's watched for\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) begin\n                sent[i] = (seed + i * 8'h3D) ^ (i >> 2);\n                tx_data = sent[i];\n                tx_valid = 1'b1;\n                @(negedge clk);\n                while (!tx_ready) @(negedge clk);\n                @(posedge clk);\n                #1;\n            end\n            tx_valid = 1'b0;\n            tx_data = 8'hXX;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task check_sent(input integer n, input [8*32-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong, t;\n        begin\n            t = 0;\n            while (frames == 0 && t < 20000) begin @(posedge tx_clk); t = t + 1; end\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (frames != 1 || wire_n != n) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d frames, %0d bytes (expected %0d)\", what, frames, wire_n, n);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (wire_bytes[i] !== sent[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            frames = 0; wire_n = 0; nibbles = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer k, i;\n    initial begin\n        for (k = 0; k < 512; k = k + 1) frame[k] = (k * 8'h47) ^ 8'hC3 ^ (k >> 3);\n\n        send(64, 1'b0, -1);  check_received(64, 1'b0, \"64 bytes\");\n        send(300, 1'b0, -1); check_received(300, 1'b0, \"300 bytes\");\n        send(64, 1'b0, 20);  check_received(64, 1'b1, \"RX_ER\");\n        send(64, 1'b1, -1);  check_received(64, 1'b1, \"a stray nibble\");\n        for (k = 0; k < 12; k = k + 1) nibble(4'h5, 1'b1);\n        // RX_DV low again, and RXD left at D (a PHY's RXD means nothing without RX_DV): no frame may start.\n        for (k = 0; k < 6; k = k + 1) nibble(4'hD, 1'b0);\n        nibble(4'h0, 1'b0);\n        repeat (20) @(posedge rx_clk);\n        if (got_n != 0 || ends != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: a false carrier gave %0d bytes, %0d ends\", got_n, ends); errors = errors + 1; end\n        got_n = 0; ends = 0;\n        send(64, 1'b0, -1);  check_received(64, 1'b0, \"after a false carrier\");\n\n        offer(1, 8'h11);   check_sent(1, \"one byte\");\n        offer(12, 8'h5A);  check_sent(12, \"12 bytes\");\n        offer(200, 8'hA7); check_sent(200, \"200 bytes\");\n        // Two frames back to back: the second waits for the gap.\n        min_gap = 1000;\n        // 80 bytes each: the FIFO is full when the first one's end comes.\n        offer(80, 8'h21);\n        offer(80, 8'h42);\n        k = 0;\n        while (frames < 2 && k < 20000) begin @(posedge tx_clk); k = k + 1; end\n        if (frames != 2) begin $display(\"FAIL: two frames back to back: %0d frames\", frames); errors = errors + 1; end\n        // Every byte of both (the second's are still in sent[]).\n        k = 0;\n        for (i = 0; i < 80; i = i + 1) if (wire_bytes[i] !== (((8'h21 + i * 8'h3D) ^ (i >> 2)) & 8'hFF)) k = k + 1;\n        for (i = 0; i < 80; i = i + 1) if (wire_bytes[80 + i] !== sent[i]) k = k + 1;\n        if (wire_n != 160 || k != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: two frames back to back: %0d bytes, %0d wrong\", wire_n, k); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (min_gap < 24) begin $display(\"FAIL: a gap of %0d nibble clocks\", min_gap); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (fast_ready != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: tx_ready %0d times less than four clocks apart\", fast_ready); errors = errors + 1; end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #5_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_rgmii_phy/README.md" : "# Ethernet RGMII PHY\n\nThe RGMII side of a Gigabit Ethernet PHY at 1000 Mbit/s (Realtek RTL8211E on the Kintex-7 Eco R5 …), with the same\nbyte interface as `eth_rmii_phy` and `eth_mii_phy`, so `eth_mac` and the protocol cores work on it unchanged — at a\nbyte a clock. AMD Xilinx 7-series only: it uses IDDR, ODDR and a BUFG.\n\nRGMII carries a nibble on each edge of a 125 MHz clock. The PHY must add both RGMII delays itself (the RTL8211E's\nRXDLY and TXDLY straps, or its registers over MDIO): the core samples and sends edge-aligned.\n\n- **Receive (`rgmii_rx_clk`, a clock-capable pin):** IDDR on a BUFG from that pin takes RXD and RX_CTL on both edges\n  and the bytes are put together on the same clock. (BUFIO and BUFR would be the usual choice; openXC7's Kintex\n  database has no route from a clock pin to a BUFR.) After the preamble D5 starts the frame; bytes and the frame's end cross to\n  `clk` through `async_fifo` and come out as `rx_valid` and `rx_end` (with `rx_error` when the PHY flagged an error).\n- **Send (`clk`):** `tx_ready` is always high — a byte a clock. Bits 3:0 go out on the rising half, 7:4 on the\n  falling, TX_CTL = `tx_valid` on both, and TX_CLK is forwarded from `clk` through an ODDR. `eth_mac` keeps the gap\n  between frames (`GAP_CLOCKS` 12 at this speed).\n\n`RX_LATE` says which edge starts a received byte: 1 (the default) a falling edge and the next rising one, 0 a rising\nedge and the falling one after it. The BUFG's insertion delay is about half a 125 MHz period, so the nibbles reach the\nIDDR half a clock late and 1 is right (both PHYs of the Kintex-7 Eco R5); with the wrong one no frame passes its FCS.\n\n`clk` is 125 MHz from an MMCM or PLL; it doesn't need to be locked to the PHY's clock. `eth_udp_stack_rgmii` puts it\ntogether with the MAC, MDIO and the protocols.\n\n```verilog\neth_rgmii_phy u_phy (\n    .clk(clk125), .rgmii_rx_clk(eth_rx_clk), .rgmii_rxd(eth_rxd), .rgmii_rx_ctl(eth_rx_ctl),\n    .rgmii_tx_clk(eth_tx_clk), .rgmii_txd(eth_txd), .rgmii_tx_ctl(eth_tx_ctl),\n    .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_end(rx_end), .rx_error(rx_error),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_ready(tx_ready)\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_rgmii_phy/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"async_fifo\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"RGMII interface to a Gigabit Ethernet PHY at 1000 Mbit/s (Realtek RTL8211E on the Kintex-7 Eco R5 …) with the same byte interface as eth_rmii_phy and eth_mii_phy, a byte a clock on a 125 MHz system clock: IDDR on a BUFG from the RX clock pin for receiving, crossed to clk through async_fifo; ODDR for sending, TX_CLK forwarded from clk. AMD Xilinx 7-series only. The PHY adds both RGMII delays (RXDLY/TXDLY). RX_LATE picks which clock edge starts a received byte.\",\n  \"id\" : \"eth_rgmii_phy\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"rgmii\",\n    \"phy\",\n    \"gigabit\",\n    \"1000base-t\",\n    \"rtl8211e\",\n    \"kintex\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"iddr\",\n    \"oddr\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII-Schnittstelle zu einem Gigabit-Ethernet-PHY bei 1000 Mbit/s (Realtek RTL8211E auf dem Kintex-7 Eco R5 …) mit derselben Byte-Schnittstelle wie eth_rmii_phy und eth_mii_phy, ein Byte je Takt an einem 125-MHz-Systemtakt: IDDR an einem BUFG vom RX-Taktpin für den Empfang, über async_fifo zu clk überquert; ODDR zum Senden, TX_CLK von clk weitergegeben. Nur AMD Xilinx 7-Serie. Beide RGMII-Verzögerungen fügt der PHY ein (RXDLY/TXDLY). RX_LATE wählt, mit welcher Taktflanke ein empfangenes Byte beginnt.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet RGMII-PHY\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet RGMII PHY\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"1: a received byte starts on a falling edge (the nibbles arrive half a clock late); 0: on a rising edge\",\n      \"name\" : \"RX_LATE\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock, 125 MHz (from an MMCM or PLL)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII RX_CLK from the PHY (125 MHz; a clock-capable pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_rx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII RXD from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_rxd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII RX_CTL from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_rx_ctl\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII TX_CLK to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_tx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII TXD to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_txd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII TX_CTL to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_tx_ctl\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Received byte (after the SFD, the FCS included), on clk\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses with each received byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a frame is over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_end\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With rx_end: the PHY flagged an error in the frame\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_error\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to send (preamble, SFD and FCS included)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High for every byte of a frame; falling ends it\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Always high: a byte a clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"requiresFamilies\" : [\n    \"xilinx7\"\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_rgmii_phy\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_rgmii_phy/rtl/eth_rgmii_phy.v" : "// RGMII interface to a Gigabit Ethernet PHY at 1000 Mbit/s (Realtek RTL8211E, as on the Kintex-7 Eco R5), with the\n// same byte interface as eth_rmii_phy and eth_mii_phy, on a 125 MHz system clock of its own. AMD Xilinx 7-series only\n// (IDDR, ODDR, BUFG). The PHY must add both RGMII delays itself (RXDLY and TXDLY strapped or set over MDIO).\n//\n// Receive (the PHY's rx_clk, 125 MHz): IDDR on a BUFG from the pin takes a nibble on each edge (RX_CTL: DV on the\n// first, DV ^ ER on the second), and the bytes are put together on the same BUFG. (BUFIO and BUFR would be the usual\n// choice; openXC7's Kintex database has no route from a clock pin to a BUFR, 2026-11-05.) RX_LATE says which edge\n// starts a byte: 1 a falling edge and the next rising one — the BUFG's insertion delay is about half a 125 MHz period,\n// so the nibbles reach the IDDR half a clock late (checked on the Kintex's two PHYs, 2026-11-05); 0 the rising edge\n// (bits 3:0) and the falling one after it. After the preamble, D5 starts the frame; its bytes and its end (DV low; an\n// error when ER was seen) cross to `clk` through async_fifo and come out as `rx_valid` and `rx_end` / `rx_error`\n// pulses, a byte a clock at most (with gaps when `clk` runs faster).\n//\n// Send (clk): `tx_ready` is always high — a byte a clock, as eth_mac sends it (preamble, FCS and the gap are eth_mac's:\n// GAP_CLOCKS 12). Each byte goes out through ODDR, bits 3:0 on the rising half and 7:4 on the falling, TX_CTL = tx_valid\n// on both halves, and TX_CLK is an ODDR of 1/0 on `clk` (the PHY's TXDLY centres it).\n\nmodule eth_rgmii_phy #(\n    parameter RX_LATE = 1\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,             // 125 MHz\n    // The PHY's pins\n    input  wire       rgmii_rx_clk,\n    input  wire [3:0] rgmii_rxd,\n    input  wire       rgmii_rx_ctl,\n    output wire       rgmii_tx_clk,\n    output wire [3:0] rgmii_txd,\n    output wire       rgmii_tx_ctl,\n    // Bytes on clk, as eth_rmii_phy's\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        rx_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_end = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_error = 1'b0,\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data,\n    input  wire       tx_valid,\n    output wire       tx_ready\n);\n    // ---- Receive: the pins ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire       rclk;                   // rx_clk on a BUFG\n    wire [4:0] rise, fall;             // {ctl, rxd} at the rising and the falling edge, both on the rising edge after\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    BUFG u_bufg (.I(rgmii_rx_clk), .O(rclk));\n    genvar i;\n    generate for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) begin : lane\n        IDDR #(.DDR_CLK_EDGE(\"SAME_EDGE_PIPELINED\")) u_iddr (.C(rclk), .CE(1'b1), .R(1'b0), .S(1'b0),\n            .D(i < 4 ? rgmii_rxd[i] : rgmii_rx_ctl), .Q1(rise[i]), .Q2(fall[i]));\n    end endgenerate\n`else\n    // Behaviour of IDDR SAME_EDGE_PIPELINED: the rising edge's sample and the falling one after it, on the next edge.\n    assign rclk = rgmii_rx_clk;\n    reg [4:0] r_rise = 5'd0, r_fall = 5'd0, s_rise = 5'd0, s_fall = 5'd0;\n    always @(posedge rgmii_rx_clk) begin r_rise <= {rgmii_rx_ctl, rgmii_rxd}; s_rise <= r_rise; s_fall <= r_fall; end\n    always @(negedge rgmii_rx_clk) r_fall <= {rgmii_rx_ctl, rgmii_rxd};\n    assign rise = s_rise;\n    assign fall = s_fall;\n`endif\n\n    // ---- Receive: bytes, on rclk ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg  [4:0] fall_was = 5'd0;\n    always @(posedge rclk) fall_was <= fall;\n    // A byte and its two control halves: DV, then DV ^ ER.\n    wire [7:0] r_byte = RX_LATE ? {rise[3:0], fall_was[3:0]} : {fall[3:0], rise[3:0]};\n    wire       r_dv   = RX_LATE ? fall_was[4] : rise[4];\n    wire       r_er   = RX_LATE ? fall_was[4] ^ rise[4] : rise[4] ^ fall[4];\n\n    localparam R_IDLE = 2'd0, R_PREAMBLE = 2'd1, R_DATA = 2'd2;\n    reg [1:0] r_state = R_IDLE;\n    reg       r_er_seen = 1'b0;\n    reg       r_write = 1'b0;\n    reg [9:0] r_word = 10'd0;          // {end, error, byte}\n\n    always @(posedge rclk) begin\n        r_write <= 1'b0;\n        case (r_state)\n            R_IDLE:\n                if (r_dv) r_state <= r_byte == 8'hD5 ? R_DATA : R_PREAMBLE;\n            R_PREAMBLE:\n                if (!r_dv) r_state <= R_IDLE;\n                else if (r_byte == 8'hD5) r_state <= R_DATA;\n            default:\n                if (!r_dv) begin\n                    r_state <= R_IDLE;\n                    r_write <= 1'b1;\n                    r_word <= {1'b1, r_er_seen, 8'd0};\n                end else begin\n                    if (r_er) r_er_seen <= 1'b1;\n                    r_write <= 1'b1;\n                    r_word <= {2'b00, r_byte};\n                end\n        endcase\n        if (r_state != R_DATA) r_er_seen <= 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    wire [9:0] rx_word;\n    wire       rx_empty;\n    reg        rx_pending = 1'b0;\n    wire       rx_read = !rx_empty;\n    async_fifo #(.WIDTH(10), .DEPTH_LOG2(5)) u_rx_fifo (\n        .wr_clk(rclk), .wr_rst(1'b0), .wr_data(r_word), .wr_en(r_write), .full(),\n        .rd_clk(clk), .rd_rst(1'b0), .rd_en(rx_read), .rd_data(rx_word), .empty(rx_empty));\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rx_valid <= 1'b0;\n        rx_end <= 1'b0;\n        rx_pending <= rx_read;\n        if (rx_pending) begin\n            if (rx_word[9]) begin\n                rx_end <= 1'b1;\n                rx_error <= rx_word[8];\n            end else begin\n                rx_valid <= 1'b1;\n                rx_data <= rx_word[7:0];\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Send, on clk ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    assign tx_ready = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] t_byte = 8'd0;\n    reg       t_en = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        t_byte <= tx_valid ? tx_data : 8'd0;\n        t_en <= tx_valid;\n    end\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    genvar k;\n    generate for (k = 0; k < 4; k = k + 1) begin : out\n        ODDR #(.DDR_CLK_EDGE(\"SAME_EDGE\")) u_oddr (.C(clk), .CE(1'b1), .R(1'b0), .S(1'b0),\n            .D1(t_byte[k]), .D2(t_byte[k + 4]), .Q(rgmii_txd[k]));\n    end endgenerate\n    ODDR #(.DDR_CLK_EDGE(\"SAME_EDGE\")) u_ctl (.C(clk), .CE(1'b1), .R(1'b0), .S(1'b0), .D1(t_en), .D2(t_en), .Q(rgmii_tx_ctl));\n    ODDR #(.DDR_CLK_EDGE(\"SAME_EDGE\")) u_clk (.C(clk), .CE(1'b1), .R(1'b0), .S(1'b0), .D1(1'b1), .D2(1'b0), .Q(rgmii_tx_clk));\n`else\n    // Behaviour of ODDR SAME_EDGE: what was taken at a rising edge, D1 while clk is high and D2 while it is low.\n    reg [7:0] o_byte = 8'd0;\n    reg       o_en = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin o_byte <= t_byte; o_en <= t_en; end\n    assign rgmii_txd = clk ? o_byte[3:0] : o_byte[7:4];\n    assign rgmii_tx_ctl = o_en;\n    assign rgmii_tx_clk = clk;\n`endif\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_rgmii_phy/tb/eth_rgmii_phy_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_rgmii_phy (its behavioural model; the board run checks IDDR/ODDR/BUFIO/BUFR): a PHY's\n// RGMII side with rx_clk at 125 MHz and the system clock at 124.99 MHz with another phase (the FIFO really crosses).\n// RX_LATE 0: each nibble centred on the edge that takes it, the low one on the rising edge; RX_LATE 1: half a clock\n// later (the low nibble on a falling edge). Receive: frames of 64 and 1500 bytes, two of them with only the gap and\n// the preamble between, one with RX_ER, garbage that never sends D5 — every byte out once, in order, then one rx_end with\n// the right rx_error. Send: frames of 60 and 1514 bytes offered a byte a clock (tx_ready never low), decoded on\n// TX_CLK's halves: TX_CTL high exactly for the frame, every byte right. Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_rgmii_phy_tb;\n    parameter RX_LATE = 1;\n    reg clk = 1'b0, rx_clk = 1'b0;\n    initial begin #1.3; forever #4.0002 clk = ~clk; end    // 124.99 MHz\n    always #4 rx_clk = ~rx_clk;                           // 125 MHz\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    reg  [3:0] rxd = 4'd0;\n    reg        rx_ctl = 1'b0;\n    wire       tx_clk, tx_ctl;\n    wire [3:0] txd;\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire       rx_valid, rx_end, rx_error, tx_ready;\n    reg  [7:0] tx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        tx_valid = 1'b0;\n    eth_rgmii_phy #(.RX_LATE(RX_LATE)) dut (.clk(clk), .rgmii_rx_clk(rx_clk), .rgmii_rxd(rxd), .rgmii_rx_ctl(rx_ctl),\n        .rgmii_tx_clk(tx_clk), .rgmii_txd(txd), .rgmii_tx_ctl(tx_ctl), .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid),\n        .rx_end(rx_end), .rx_error(rx_error), .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_ready(tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- Receive ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] got [0:4095];\n    integer got_n = 0, ends = 0;\n    reg last_error = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (rx_valid) begin got[got_n] = rx_data; got_n = got_n + 1; end\n        if (rx_end) begin ends = ends + 1; last_error = rx_error; end\n    end\n\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:4095];\n    // A nibble and its RX_CTL value, set 2 ns after the opposite edge so it's centred on the edge that takes it.\n    task half(input low_half, input [3:0] v, input c);\n        begin\n            if (low_half ^ RX_LATE) @(negedge rx_clk); else @(posedge rx_clk);\n            #2 rxd = v; rx_ctl = c;\n        end\n    endtask\n    // One byte: the low nibble with DV, the high one with DV ^ ER.\n    task byte_out(input [7:0] b, input dv, input er);\n        begin half(1'b1, b[3:0], dv); half(1'b0, b[7:4], dv ^ er); end\n    endtask\n    // A frame: 7 × 55, D5, n bytes from frame[] (ER during byte er_at, -1 none), then `gap` idle bytes.\n    task send(input integer n, input integer er_at, input integer gap);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < 7; i = i + 1) byte_out(8'h55, 1'b1, 1'b0);\n            byte_out(8'hD5, 1'b1, 1'b0);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) byte_out(frame[i], 1'b1, i == er_at);\n            for (i = 0; i < gap; i = i + 1) byte_out(8'h00, 1'b0, 1'b0);\n        end\n    endtask\n    task check_received(input integer n, input integer frames, input expect_error, input [8*32-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong, f;\n        begin\n            repeat (60) @(posedge clk);\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (got_n != n * frames) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes, expected %0d\", what, got_n, n * frames); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else for (f = 0; f < frames; f = f + 1) for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (got[f * n + i] !== frame[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (ends != frames || last_error !== expect_error) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d ends, rx_error %b\", what, ends, last_error);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            got_n = 0; ends = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- Send: decoded on TX_CLK's halves ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] wire_bytes [0:4095];\n    integer wire_n = 0, frames_out = 0;\n    reg [3:0] low;\n    reg low_ctl, en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge tx_clk) begin\n        #1 low = txd; low_ctl = tx_ctl;\n        @(negedge tx_clk); #1;\n        if (low_ctl !== tx_ctl) begin $display(\"FAIL: TX_CTL differs between the halves\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (low_ctl) begin wire_bytes[wire_n] = {txd, low}; wire_n = wire_n + 1; end\n        else if (en_was) frames_out = frames_out + 1;\n        en_was = low_ctl;\n    end\n    reg [7:0] sent [0:4095];\n    integer not_ready = 0;\n    task offer(input integer n, input [7:0] seed);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) begin\n                sent[i] = (seed + i * 8'h3D) ^ (i >> 3);\n                tx_data = sent[i]; tx_valid = 1'b1;\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                if (!tx_ready) not_ready = not_ready + 1;\n            end\n            tx_valid = 1'b0;\n            repeat (20) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n    task check_sent(input integer n, input [8*32-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong;\n        begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (frames_out != 1 || wire_n != n) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d frames, %0d bytes (expected %0d)\", what, frames_out, wire_n, n); errors = errors + 1;\n            end else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (wire_bytes[i] !== sent[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            frames_out = 0; wire_n = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer k;\n    initial begin\n        for (k = 0; k < 4096; k = k + 1) frame[k] = (k * 8'h47) ^ 8'hC3 ^ (k >> 5);\n        repeat (10) @(posedge rx_clk);\n        send(64, -1, 12);    check_received(64, 1, 1'b0, \"64 bytes\");\n        send(1500, -1, 12);  check_received(1500, 1, 1'b0, \"1500 bytes\");\n        send(64, 20, 12);    check_received(64, 1, 1'b1, \"RX_ER\");\n        send(1500, -1, 12);  send(1500, -1, 12); check_received(1500, 2, 1'b0, \"two back to back\");\n        // A carrier that never sends D5: nothing comes out.\n        for (k = 0; k < 20; k = k + 1) byte_out(8'h55, 1'b1, 1'b0);\n        for (k = 0; k < 8; k = k + 1) byte_out(8'h3C, 1'b1, 1'b0);\n        for (k = 0; k < 12; k = k + 1) byte_out(8'h00, 1'b0, 1'b0);\n        repeat (60) @(posedge clk);\n        if (got_n != 0 || ends != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: no SFD gave %0d bytes, %0d ends\", got_n, ends); errors = errors + 1; end\n        got_n = 0; ends = 0;\n        send(64, -1, 12);    check_received(64, 1, 1'b0, \"after no SFD\");\n\n        offer(60, 8'h11);    check_sent(60, \"60 bytes\");\n        offer(1514, 8'hA7);  check_sent(1514, \"1514 bytes\");\n        if (not_ready != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: tx_ready low %0d times\", not_ready); errors = errors + 1; end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n    initial begin #3_000_000; $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\"); $finish; end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_rmii_phy/README.md" : "# Ethernet RMII PHY\n\nThe RMII side of a 10/100 Ethernet PHY at 100 Mbit/s (Realtek RTL8201F on the Tang Primer 20K Dock, Microchip\nLAN8720, TI DP83848 in RMII mode): bytes to and from di-bits on the PHY's 50 MHz reference clock, the low di-bit\nfirst. Everything runs on that clock, so connect the PHY's REF_CLK (on the Dock: `eth_clk`) to `clk`.\n\n- **Receive:** the bytes after the SFD, the frame's FCS included, each with a one-clock `rx_valid`; `rx_end`\n  pulses when the frame is over, with `rx_error` if RX_ER was seen or the frame ended inside a byte. CRS_DV\n  toggling at a frame's end (the PHY's FIFO still emptying) is handled.\n- **Send:** hold `tx_valid` high for every byte of a frame — preamble, SFD and FCS included; a byte is taken when\n  `tx_valid` and `tx_ready` are both high (every fourth clock). `tx_valid` low at a byte's end ends the frame.\n\n`eth_mac` adds and checks preamble, FCS, padding and the inter-frame gap; `eth_udp_stack` puts it all together.\n\n```verilog\nwire [7:0] rx_data, tx_data;\nwire rx_valid, rx_end, rx_error, tx_valid, tx_ready;\neth_rmii_phy u_phy (\n    .clk(eth_clk), .rmii_rxd(eth_rxd), .rmii_crs_dv(eth_crs_dv), .rmii_rx_er(eth_rx_er),\n    .rmii_txd(eth_txd), .rmii_tx_en(eth_tx_en),\n    .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_end(rx_end), .rx_error(rx_error),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_ready(tx_ready)\n);\n```\n\n100 Mbit/s only: at 10 Mbit/s RMII repeats each di-bit ten times. Advertise 100 full duplex with `eth_mdio`.\n",
    "cores/eth_rmii_phy/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"RMII interface to a 10/100 Ethernet PHY at 100 Mbit/s (RTL8201F, LAN8720, DP83848): bytes to and from di-bits on the PHY's 50 MHz reference clock. Received bytes come after the SFD with their FCS (eth_mac checks it); CRS_DV toggling at a frame's end is handled; `rx_end` pulses when a frame is over, `rx_error` says RX_ER was seen or it ended inside a byte. Bytes to send go out with valid/ready, one every fourth clock; TX_EN falls when valid is low at a byte's end.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 257.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 33,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 38\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 142.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 7,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 31,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 54\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 137.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DFF\" : 38,\n        \"LUT4\" : 52,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 93,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 55\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 435.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 38,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 27\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_rmii_phy\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"rmii\",\n    \"phy\",\n    \"rtl8201\",\n    \"lan8720\",\n    \"dp83848\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"100base-tx\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII-Schnittstelle zu einem 10/100-Ethernet-PHY bei 100 Mbit/s (RTL8201F, LAN8720, DP83848): Bytes zu und von Di-Bits am 50-MHz-Referenztakt des PHY. Empfangene Bytes kommen nach dem SFD mit ihrer FCS (eth_mac prüft sie); CRS_DV, das am Rahmenende wechselt, wird berücksichtigt; `rx_end` pulst am Rahmenende, `rx_error` meldet RX_ER oder ein Ende mitten im Byte. Zu sendende Bytes gehen mit valid/ready hinaus, eines je vierten Takt; TX_EN fällt, wenn valid am Ende eines Bytes low ist.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet RMII-PHY\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet RMII PHY\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's 50 MHz reference clock (RMII REF_CLK)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII RXD from the PHY (the pins)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_rxd\",\n      \"width\" : 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII CRS_DV from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_crs_dv\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII RX_ER from the PHY (tie low if the board doesn't have it)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_rx_er\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII TXD to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_txd\",\n      \"width\" : 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII TX_EN to the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_tx_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Received byte (after the SFD, the FCS included)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses with each received byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a frame is over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_end\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With rx_end: RX_ER was seen, or the frame ended inside a byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_error\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to send (preamble, SFD and FCS included)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High for every byte of a frame; low at a byte's end ends it\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High when tx_data is taken (with tx_valid)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_rmii_phy\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_rmii_phy/rtl/eth_rmii_phy.v" : "// RMII interface to an Ethernet PHY at 100 Mbit/s (Realtek RTL8201F, Microchip LAN8720, TI DP83848 in RMII mode):\n// bytes to and from di-bits on the 50 MHz reference clock, the low di-bit first.\n//\n// Receive: after CRS_DV rises, the bytes after the SFD (the preamble's last di-bit 11) come out one at a time with a\n// one-clock `rx_valid` — the frame as sent, its FCS included (eth_mac checks and removes it). CRS_DV may toggle at a\n// frame's end while the PHY still has data (low on a nibble's first di-bit, high on its second): the frame ends when\n// CRS_DV is low on two clocks in a row, and `rx_end` pulses then. `rx_error` comes with `rx_end`: RX_ER was seen\n// during the frame, or it ended inside a byte.\n//\n// Send: `tx_valid` up for every byte of a frame — preamble, SFD and FCS included (eth_mac adds them). A byte is\n// taken when `tx_valid` and `tx_ready` are both high, every fourth clock while a frame goes out; when `tx_valid` is\n// low at a byte's end, TX_EN falls and the frame is over. The PHY needs its inter-frame gap from the sender.\n\nmodule eth_rmii_phy (\n    input  wire       clk,          // the 50 MHz reference (RMII REF_CLK)\n    input  wire [1:0] rmii_rxd,\n    input  wire       rmii_crs_dv,\n    input  wire       rmii_rx_er,\n    output reg  [1:0] rmii_txd = 2'b00,\n    output reg        rmii_tx_en = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        rx_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_end = 1'b0,\n    output reg        rx_error = 1'b0,\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data,\n    input  wire       tx_valid,\n    output wire       tx_ready\n);\n    // ---- Receive --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [1:0] rxd = 2'b00;\n    reg       crs = 1'b0, crs_last = 1'b0, er = 1'b0;\n    localparam IDLE = 2'd0, PREAMBLE = 2'd1, DATA = 2'd2;\n    reg [1:0] state = IDLE;\n    reg [1:0] dibit = 2'd0;\n    reg [5:0] shift = 6'd0;\n    reg       er_seen = 1'b0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rxd <= rmii_rxd;\n        crs <= rmii_crs_dv;\n        er <= rmii_rx_er;\n        crs_last <= crs;\n        rx_valid <= 1'b0;\n        rx_end <= 1'b0;\n        case (state)\n            IDLE:\n                if (crs) state <= PREAMBLE;\n            PREAMBLE:\n                if (!crs && !crs_last) state <= IDLE;\n                else if (crs && rxd == 2'b11) begin   // the SFD's last di-bit\n                    state <= DATA;\n                    dibit <= 2'd0;\n                    er_seen <= 1'b0;\n                end\n            default:\n                if (!crs && !crs_last) begin\n                    // The first low sample was shifted in as a di-bit: a frame that ended on a byte has one.\n                    state <= IDLE;\n                    rx_end <= 1'b1;\n                    rx_error <= er_seen || dibit != 2'd1;\n                end else begin\n                    if (er) er_seen <= 1'b1;\n                    dibit <= dibit + 2'd1;\n                    shift <= {rxd, shift[5:2]};\n                    if (dibit == 2'd3) begin\n                        rx_data <= {rxd, shift};\n                        rx_valid <= 1'b1;\n                    end\n                end\n        endcase\n    end\n\n    // ---- Send -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // A byte goes out over four clocks: its first di-bit on the clock that takes it, then the other three.\n    reg [1:0] tx_dibit = 2'd0;\n    reg [5:0] tx_shift = 6'd0;\n    assign tx_ready = tx_valid && tx_dibit == 2'd0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (tx_dibit != 2'd0) begin\n            rmii_txd <= tx_shift[1:0];\n            tx_shift <= {2'b00, tx_shift[5:2]};\n            tx_dibit <= tx_dibit + 2'd1;\n        end else if (tx_ready) begin\n            rmii_txd <= tx_data[1:0];\n            tx_shift <= tx_data[7:2];\n            tx_dibit <= 2'd1;\n            rmii_tx_en <= 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            rmii_txd <= 2'b00;\n            rmii_tx_en <= 1'b0;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_rmii_phy/tb/eth_rmii_phy_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_rmii_phy: a model of a PHY's RMII side (outputs changed 4 ns after the reference\n// clock's rising edge) sends frames — CRS_DV up two clocks before the preamble, 7 × 55, D5, asymmetric bytes —\n// plainly, with CRS_DV toggling over the last bytes, with RX_ER, ending inside a byte, and a false carrier (CRS_DV\n// with 10 di-bits and no SFD). Every byte must arrive once, in order, then one rx_end with the right rx_error. The\n// sender gets frames of 1, 2 and 9 bytes with gaps of different lengths in its tx_valid; TX_EN must be up exactly\n// while their di-bits go out, low di-bit first. Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_rmii_phy_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;   // 50 MHz\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    reg  [1:0] rxd = 2'b00;\n    reg        crs_dv = 1'b0, rx_er = 1'b0;\n    wire [1:0] txd;\n    wire       tx_en;\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire       rx_valid, rx_end, rx_error;\n    reg  [7:0] tx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        tx_valid = 1'b0;\n    wire       tx_ready;\n\n    eth_rmii_phy dut (.clk(clk), .rmii_rxd(rxd), .rmii_crs_dv(crs_dv), .rmii_rx_er(rx_er), .rmii_txd(txd),\n        .rmii_tx_en(tx_en), .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_end(rx_end), .rx_error(rx_error),\n        .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_ready(tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- What arrives ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] got [0:255];\n    integer got_n = 0, ends = 0;\n    reg last_error = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (rx_valid) begin got[got_n] = rx_data; got_n = got_n + 1; end\n        if (rx_end) begin ends = ends + 1; last_error = rx_error; end\n    end\n\n    // ---- The PHY sending a frame ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:255];\n    task put_dibit(input [1:0] d, input dv);\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #4;\n            rxd = d;\n            crs_dv = dv;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // `toggle`: CRS_DV low on each nibble's first di-bit over the last `toggle` bytes. `extra`: di-bits after the\n    // last byte (a frame ending inside a byte). `er_at`: the byte during which RX_ER is high (-1 none).\n    task send(input integer n, input integer toggle, input integer extra, input integer er_at);\n        integer i, j;\n        begin\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b1);\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < 31; i = i + 1) put_dibit(2'b01, 1'b1);   // 7 × 55 and D5's first three\n            put_dibit(2'b11, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1)\n                for (j = 0; j < 4; j = j + 1) begin\n                    rx_er = (i == er_at);\n                    put_dibit(frame[i][2 * j +: 2], (i >= n - toggle) ? (j % 2 == 1) : 1'b1);\n                end\n            rx_er = 1'b0;\n            for (i = 0; i < extra; i = i + 1) put_dibit(2'b10, 1'b1);\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b0);\n            repeat (6) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task check_received(input integer n, input expect_error, input [8*24-1:0] what);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            if (got_n != n) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes, expected %0d\", what, got_n, n); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1)\n                if (got[i] !== frame[i]) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: %0s: byte %0d is %h, expected %h\", what, i, got[i], frame[i]);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n            if (ends != 1) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d rx_end pulses\", what, ends); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else if (last_error !== expect_error) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: rx_error %b\", what, last_error);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            got_n = 0;\n            ends = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- The sender: what goes out on TXD while TX_EN is up --------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] sent [0:63];\n    integer sent_n = 0, out_dibits = 0, frames_out = 0;\n    reg [7:0] out_byte = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] on_wire [0:63];\n    reg tx_en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (tx_en) begin\n            out_byte = {txd, out_byte[7:2]};\n            out_dibits = out_dibits + 1;\n            if (out_dibits % 4 == 0) on_wire[out_dibits / 4 - 1] = out_byte;\n        end\n        if (tx_en_was && !tx_en) frames_out = frames_out + 1;\n        tx_en_was = tx_en;\n    end\n\n    // The handshake: on the clock after valid and ready were both high, that byte's first di-bit is on TXD.\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        if (tx_valid && tx_ready) begin : handshake\n            reg [7:0] taken;\n            taken = tx_data;\n            #2;\n            if (tx_en !== 1'b1 || txd !== taken[1:0]) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: a byte taken (%h) didn't start on the next clock\", taken);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n\n    // Offers `n` bytes as a frame: each byte held until taken; tx_valid low for `gap` clocks before the next frame.\n    task offer(input integer n, input [7:0] seed);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) begin\n                sent[i] = seed ^ (i * 8'h3B) ^ 8'h1D;\n                tx_data = sent[i];\n                tx_valid = 1'b1;\n                @(negedge clk);\n                while (!tx_ready) @(negedge clk);   // ready depends on valid: read it between edges\n                @(posedge clk);\n                #1;\n            end\n            tx_valid = 1'b0;\n            tx_data = 8'hXX;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task check_sent(input integer n, input [8*24-1:0] what);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            repeat (8) @(posedge clk);\n            if (frames_out != 1) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: TX_EN fell %0d times\", what, frames_out); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (out_dibits != 4 * n) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d di-bits with TX_EN, expected %0d\", what, out_dibits, 4 * n);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1)\n                if (on_wire[i] !== sent[i]) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: %0s: byte %0d went out as %h, expected %h\", what, i, on_wire[i], sent[i]);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n            frames_out = 0;\n            out_dibits = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer k;\n    initial begin\n        for (k = 0; k < 256; k = k + 1) frame[k] = (k * 8'h47) ^ 8'hC3 ^ (k >> 3);   // no palindromes: 0xC3 → C3, 84, …\n        frame[0] = 8'h1E;\n\n        send(64, 0, 0, -1);\n        check_received(64, 1'b0, \"a plain frame\");\n        send(70, 2, 0, -1);\n        check_received(70, 1'b0, \"CRS_DV toggling\");\n        send(64, 0, 0, 20);\n        check_received(64, 1'b1, \"RX_ER\");\n        send(64, 0, 1, -1);\n        check_received(64, 1'b1, \"ending inside a byte\");\n\n        // A false carrier: CRS_DV with 10 di-bits, no SFD — nothing at all.\n        for (k = 0; k < 20; k = k + 1) put_dibit(2'b10, 1'b1);\n        put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b0);\n        repeat (6) @(posedge clk);\n        if (got_n != 0 || ends != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: a false carrier gave %0d bytes and %0d ends\", got_n, ends); errors = errors + 1; end\n        got_n = 0;\n        ends = 0;\n\n        // A frame that comes straight after the last: the preamble starts two clocks after CRS_DV fell.\n        send(64, 0, 0, -1);\n        check_received(64, 1'b0, \"the next frame\");\n\n        offer(1, 8'h00);\n        check_sent(1, \"one byte\");\n        repeat (3) @(posedge clk);\n        offer(2, 8'h55);\n        check_sent(2, \"two bytes\");\n        offer(9, 8'hA7);\n        check_sent(9, \"nine bytes\");\n        if (tx_en !== 1'b0) begin $display(\"FAIL: TX_EN stays up\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #2_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp/README.md" : "# Ethernet UDP\n\nUDP (RFC 768) over IPv4 and Ethernet on one port, `PORT`: receives datagrams sent to it and sends datagrams from it.\n\n**Receive.** Every frame `eth_mac` receives is checked while it arrives: to `mac` or broadcast, IPv4 without options,\nnot a fragment, protocol 17, to `ip` or 255.255.255.255, a correct header checksum, destination port `PORT`, a UDP\nlength that matches, and the UDP checksum unless the sender left it 0. The payload goes into a buffer (2^`ADDR_BITS`\nbytes). Once the frame's last byte comes with `rx_good`, the datagram is handed over: `rx_new` pulses, `rx_length`\nsays how long it is, `peer_mac`/`peer_ip`/`peer_port` say who sent it, and the bytes come on\n`rx_data`/`rx_valid`/`rx_last` as fast as `rx_ready` takes them (`rx_valid` drops for two clocks after each while\nthe next is read). A datagram that arrives before the design has taken the last byte of the one before is dropped\n(`rx_dropped`).\n\n**Send.** While `send_ready` is high, `send` starts a datagram of `send_length` bytes (up to 1472) to\n`dst_mac`/`dst_ip`/`dst_port`. The core builds the Ethernet, IP and UDP headers (UDP checksum 0) and then asks for\nthe payload with `payload_ready`. `eth_mac` doesn't wait, so each payload byte must be valid within three clocks of\nthe one before — a register, a block RAM read ahead, or this core's own receive side:\n\n```verilog\n// An echo: what arrives on port 1234 goes back to its sender.\neth_udp #(.PORT(16'd1234)) u_udp (\n    .clk(eth_clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP),\n    .frame_data(rx_data), .frame_valid(rx_valid), .frame_last(rx_last), .frame_good(rx_good),\n    .rx_data(udp_data), .rx_valid(udp_valid), .rx_last(), .rx_ready(payload_ready), .rx_length(udp_length),\n    .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(udp_new), .rx_dropped(),\n    .send(udp_new), .send_length(udp_length), .dst_mac(peer_mac), .dst_ip(peer_ip), .dst_port(peer_port),\n    .send_ready(), .payload_data(udp_data), .payload_valid(udp_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready),\n    .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready)\n);\n```\n\nTo send to a host that hasn't sent anything, use its MAC, or broadcast: `dst_mac` FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF and the\nsubnet's broadcast IP (10.0.255.255 in 10.0.0.0/16). There is no ARP lookup.\n",
    "cores/eth_udp/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_mac\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"UDP over IPv4 and Ethernet on one port: datagrams to PORT are checked (unicast or broadcast, not a fragment, header and UDP checksums, lengths), kept in a block RAM and handed to the design with the sender's MAC, IP and port once the frame is good; datagrams of up to 1472 bytes are sent to any MAC, IP and port with the headers and IP checksum built in. The receive side can feed the send side directly (an echo).\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 125.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DP16KD\" : 1,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 1606,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 830\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 52.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 16,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 266,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 272,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 782,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 805,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 1555,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 49,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 67.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 576,\n        \"BSRAM\" : 1,\n        \"DFF\" : 830,\n        \"LUT4\" : 1666,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 324,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 127,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 52,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 17\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 32.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 1761,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 4,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 5\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 161.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 111,\n        \"RAMB18E1\" : 1,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 801,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 1436\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_udp\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"udp\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"datagram\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"socket\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"UDP über IPv4 und Ethernet auf einem Port: Datagramme an PORT werden geprüft (Unicast oder Broadcast, kein Fragment, Header- und UDP-Prüfsumme, Längen), in einem Block-RAM gehalten und dem Design mit MAC, IP und Port des Absenders übergeben, sobald der Rahmen gut ist; Datagramme bis 1472 Bytes gehen an beliebige MAC, IP und Ports, Header und IP-Prüfsumme baut der Kern. Die Empfangsseite kann die Sendeseite direkt speisen (ein Echo).\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-UDP\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet UDP\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1234,\n      \"description\" : \"The UDP port received on and sent from\",\n      \"name\" : \"PORT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 11,\n      \"description\" : \"The receive buffer holds 2^ADDR_BITS bytes of payload (11: 2048, one block RAM)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ADDR_BITS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock (eth_mac's)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_last\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's rx_good\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A received datagram's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte is there (low for two clocks after each)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the datagram's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The design takes the byte (with rx_valid)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Bytes in the datagram handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's MAC (of the datagram handed over last)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram is handed over (also an empty one)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_new\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a good datagram was dropped (the one before wasn't taken yet)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_dropped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Starts a datagram (with send_ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: payload bytes (0 … 1472)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's MAC (FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF for broadcast)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A datagram can be started\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is there (within three clocks of the last one taken)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is taken\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To eth_mac's tx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To eth_mac's tx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To eth_mac's tx_last\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From eth_mac's tx_ready\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_udp\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_udp/rtl/eth_udp.v" : "// UDP over IPv4 and Ethernet (RFC 768): receives datagrams to one port and sends datagrams from it.\n//\n// Receive: eth_mac's frames are checked while they arrive — to `mac` or broadcast, EtherType 0800, IPv4 without\n// options, not a fragment, protocol 17, to `ip` or 255.255.255.255, a correct header checksum, destination port\n// PORT, a UDP length that matches the IP length and fits the frame, and the UDP checksum unless it is 0 — and the\n// payload is kept in a buffer of 2^ADDR_BITS bytes. When the frame's last byte comes with `rx_good`, the datagram is\n// handed to the design (`rx_new` pulses, also for an empty one): `rx_length` bytes on rx_data/rx_valid/rx_last with `rx_ready`, and the sender in peer_mac,\n// peer_ip and peer_port (they stay until the next datagram is handed over). Reads run ahead into a two-byte queue, so\n// the design can take a byte on every clock. A datagram whose frame starts arriving before the design has taken the\n// last byte of the one before is dropped (`rx_dropped` pulses).\n//\n// Send: with `send_ready` high, `send` starts a datagram of `send_length` bytes (0 … 1472) to dst_mac, dst_ip and\n// dst_port from port PORT (latched then). The core puts out the Ethernet, IP (identification counting, don't\n// fragment, TTL 64, the header checksum) and UDP headers (checksum 0: none), then asks for the payload with\n// `payload_ready`, one byte a request (a byte is taken when `payload_valid` and `payload_ready` are both high). The\n// header goes out a byte a clock if asked; the MAC doesn't wait for the payload: at 100 Mbit/s it asks every fourth\n// clock, at 1000 Mbit/s on every clock, and the byte must be valid when it's asked for (eth_udp's own rx side\n// qualifies at both speeds, so an echo can send straight from it). tx_data/tx_valid/tx_last/tx_ready go to eth_mac or\n// a multiplexer.\n\nmodule eth_udp #(\n    parameter [15:0] PORT = 16'd1234,\n    parameter integer ADDR_BITS = 11\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    // From eth_mac\n    input  wire [7:0]  frame_data,\n    input  wire        frame_valid,\n    input  wire        frame_last,\n    input  wire        frame_good,\n    // Received datagrams\n    output wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    output wire        rx_valid,\n    output wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_ready,\n    output reg  [10:0] rx_length = 11'd0,\n    output reg  [47:0] peer_mac = 48'd0,\n    output reg  [31:0] peer_ip = 32'd0,\n    output reg  [15:0] peer_port = 16'd0,\n    output reg         rx_new = 1'b0,\n    output reg         rx_dropped = 1'b0,\n    // Datagrams to send\n    input  wire        send,\n    input  wire [10:0] send_length,\n    input  wire [47:0] dst_mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] dst_ip,\n    input  wire [15:0] dst_port,\n    output wire        send_ready,\n    input  wire [7:0]  payload_data,\n    input  wire        payload_valid,\n    output wire        payload_ready,\n    // To eth_mac\n    output reg  [7:0]  tx_data,\n    output wire        tx_valid,\n    output wire        tx_last,\n    input  wire        tx_ready\n);\n    localparam integer SIZE = 1 << ADDR_BITS;\n\n    // ---- Receive: checks and the buffer ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // For speed, addresses are compared against bytes shifted out of registers, the checksums are summed without\n    // folding, and a frame is judged over three clocks after its last byte (the next can't start for at least 20 at\n    // 1000 Mbit/s: the gap and the preamble).\n    reg [7:0]  buffer [0:SIZE-1];\n    reg [7:0]  rd_data = 8'd0;\n\n    reg [11:0] index = 12'd0;\n    reg        to_us = 1'b1, to_all = 1'b1, ok = 1'b1, ip_ok = 1'b1, ip_all = 1'b1;\n    reg [47:0] mac_left = 48'd0;\n    reg [31:0] ip_left = 32'd0;\n    reg [7:0]  high = 8'd0;\n    reg [19:0] header_sum = 20'd0;\n    reg [27:0] udp_sum = 28'd0;     // the pseudo header's addresses and the UDP segment\n    reg [15:0] total = 16'd0, udp_len = 16'd0, udp_check = 16'd0, dport = 16'd0, sport = 16'd0;\n    reg [11:0] segment_end = 12'd0; // 14 + the IP total length: the first byte after the segment\n    reg [47:0] src_mac = 48'd0;\n    reg [31:0] src_ip = 32'd0;\n    reg        holding = 1'b0;      // a datagram is being handed to the design\n    reg        odd_left = 1'b0;     // the segment's last byte was a word's first …\n    reg [7:0]  odd_byte = 8'd0;     // … and this (`high` is overwritten by the padding after it)\n    reg        blocked = 1'b0;      // this frame began while a datagram was being handed over\n\n    // The end of a frame: 1 the facts latched, 2 sums and lengths, 3 the folds, 4 the decision.\n    reg        judge1 = 1'b0, judge2 = 1'b0, judge3 = 1'b0;\n    reg        cand = 1'b0, lengths_ok = 1'b0, port_ok = 1'b0;\n    reg [11:0] count = 12'd0;\n    reg [27:0] udp_end = 28'd0, udp_all = 28'd0;\n    reg [16:0] header_fold = 17'd0, udp_fold = 17'd0;\n\n    wire        in_segment = index >= 12'd34 && index < segment_end;\n    wire        rx_taken_last = rx_valid && rx_ready && rx_last;\n    // The frame's last byte is still in the segment when there is no padding: its word is completed at the end.\n    wire [15:0] last_word = (in_segment && index[0]) ? {high, frame_data}\n                          : (in_segment && !index[0]) ? {frame_data, 8'h00}\n                          : odd_left ? {odd_byte, 8'h00} : 16'd0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rx_dropped <= 1'b0;\n        rx_new <= 1'b0;\n        judge1 <= 1'b0;\n        judge2 <= 1'b0;\n        judge3 <= 1'b0;\n        if (rx_taken_last || (holding && rx_length == 11'd0)) holding <= 1'b0;\n        if (frame_valid) begin\n            if (index != 12'd4095) index <= index + 12'd1;\n            if (index == 12'd0) blocked <= holding;\n            if (!(index == 12'd0 ? holding : blocked) && index >= 12'd42 && index - 12'd42 < SIZE)\n                buffer[index - 12'd42] <= frame_data;\n            if (index < 12'd6) begin\n                if (frame_data != (index == 12'd0 ? mac[47:40] : mac_left[47:40])) to_us <= 1'b0;\n                if (frame_data != 8'hFF) to_all <= 1'b0;\n                mac_left <= {(index == 12'd0 ? mac[39:0] : mac_left[39:0]), 8'd0};\n            end\n            if (index >= 12'd6 && index < 12'd12) src_mac <= {src_mac[39:0], frame_data};\n            case (index)\n                12'd12: if (frame_data != 8'h08) ok <= 1'b0;\n                12'd13: if (frame_data != 8'h00) ok <= 1'b0;\n                12'd14: if (frame_data != 8'h45) ok <= 1'b0;\n                12'd17: total <= {high, frame_data};\n                12'd18: segment_end <= total[11:0] + 12'd14;\n                12'd20: if (frame_data[5:0] != 6'd0) ok <= 1'b0;   // MF and the offset\n                12'd21: if (frame_data != 8'd0) ok <= 1'b0;\n                12'd23: if (frame_data != 8'd17) ok <= 1'b0;\n                12'd29: ip_left <= ip;\n                12'd35: sport <= {high, frame_data};\n                12'd37: dport <= {high, frame_data};\n                12'd39: udp_len <= {high, frame_data};\n                12'd41: udp_check <= {high, frame_data};\n                default: ;\n            endcase\n            if (index >= 12'd26 && index < 12'd30) src_ip <= {src_ip[23:0], frame_data};\n            if (index >= 12'd30 && index < 12'd34) begin\n                if (frame_data != ip_left[31:24]) ip_ok <= 1'b0;\n                if (frame_data != 8'hFF) ip_all <= 1'b0;\n                ip_left <= {ip_left[23:0], 8'd0};\n            end\n            // Words: a byte at an even index is a word's high byte.\n            if (!index[0]) high <= frame_data;\n            if (index >= 12'd14 && index < 12'd34 && index[0]) header_sum <= header_sum + {4'd0, high, frame_data};\n            // The pseudo header's addresses (26 … 33) and the segment (34 …); the rest of the pseudo header at the end.\n            if (index >= 12'd26 && index < 12'd34 && index[0]) udp_sum <= udp_sum + {12'd0, high, frame_data};\n            if (in_segment) begin\n                if (index[0]) udp_sum <= udp_sum + {12'd0, high, frame_data};\n                odd_left <= !index[0];\n                odd_byte <= frame_data;\n            end\n            if (frame_last) begin\n                judge1 <= 1'b1;\n                cand <= frame_good && (to_us || to_all) && ok && (ip_ok || ip_all);\n                count <= index + 12'd1;\n                // The last word: the frame's own last byte when there is no padding (then it isn't summed above).\n                udp_end <= udp_sum + {12'd0, last_word};\n                index <= 12'd0;\n                to_us <= 1'b1; to_all <= 1'b1; ok <= 1'b1; ip_ok <= 1'b1; ip_all <= 1'b1;\n                odd_left <= 1'b0;\n            end\n        end\n        if (judge1) begin\n            judge2 <= 1'b1;\n            udp_all <= udp_end + {12'd0, udp_len} + 28'd17;\n            header_fold <= {1'b0, header_sum[15:0]} + {13'd0, header_sum[19:16]};\n            lengths_ok <= udp_len >= 16'd8 && udp_len + 16'd20 == total && count >= segment_end\n                          && udp_len - 16'd8 <= SIZE;\n            port_ok <= dport == PORT;\n            header_sum <= 20'd0;\n            udp_sum <= 28'd0;\n        end\n        if (judge2) begin\n            judge3 <= 1'b1;\n            udp_fold <= {1'b0, udp_all[15:0]} + {5'd0, udp_all[27:16]};\n            header_fold <= {1'b0, header_fold[15:0] + {15'd0, header_fold[16]}};\n        end\n        if (judge3) begin\n            if (cand && lengths_ok && port_ok && header_fold[15:0] == 16'hFFFF\n                && (udp_check == 16'd0 || udp_fold[15:0] + {15'd0, udp_fold[16]} == 16'hFFFF)) begin\n                if (blocked) rx_dropped <= 1'b1;\n                else begin\n                    holding <= 1'b1;\n                    rx_new <= 1'b1;\n                    rx_length <= udp_len[10:0] - 11'd8;\n                    peer_mac <= src_mac;\n                    peer_ip <= src_ip;\n                    peer_port <= sport;\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Receive: handing the datagram over -----------------------------------------------------------------\n    // Reads run ahead into a two-byte queue in front of rx_*: one is issued whenever the queue will hold at most one\n    // byte as its data arrives (a read takes a clock), so a byte can be taken on every clock.\n    reg [10:0] next = 11'd0;           // the payload byte to read next\n    reg [10:0] reading = 11'd0;        // the one whose data arrives on this clock\n    reg        arriving = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0]  q0 = 8'd0, q1 = 8'd0;\n    reg        l0 = 1'b0, l1 = 1'b0;\n    reg [1:0]  queued = 2'd0;\n    assign rx_data = q0;\n    assign rx_valid = holding && queued != 2'd0;\n    assign rx_last = l0;\n\n    wire       taken = rx_valid && rx_ready;\n    wire [1:0] after = queued + {1'b0, arriving} - {1'b0, taken};\n    wire       issue = holding && next < rx_length && after <= 2'd1;\n    wire       arrived_last = reading == rx_length - 11'd1;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rd_data <= buffer[next[ADDR_BITS-1:0]];\n        if (!holding) begin\n            next <= 11'd0;\n            arriving <= 1'b0;\n            queued <= 2'd0;\n        end else begin\n            arriving <= issue;\n            if (issue) begin\n                reading <= next;\n                next <= next + 11'd1;\n            end\n            queued <= after;\n            // Taking and arriving together find exactly one queued (see `issue`).\n            if (taken && arriving) begin\n                q0 <= rd_data; l0 <= arrived_last;\n            end else if (taken) begin\n                q0 <= q1; l0 <= l1;\n            end else if (arriving) begin\n                if (queued == 2'd0) begin q0 <= rd_data; l0 <= arrived_last; end\n                else begin q1 <= rd_data; l1 <= arrived_last; end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Send -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // The IP header checksum is summed a term a clock after `send` (done after 11 clocks; it's needed at byte 24).\n    // Each header byte is looked up a clock ahead for the position that comes next, so the header can go out a byte a\n    // clock.\n    reg        sending = 1'b0;\n    reg [10:0] length = 11'd0;\n    reg [47:0] to_mac = 48'd0;\n    reg [31:0] to_ip = 32'd0;\n    reg [15:0] to_port = 16'd0;\n    reg [15:0] ident = 16'd0;\n    reg [15:0] ip_total = 16'd0, udp_length = 16'd0;\n    reg [11:0] o = 12'd0, last_o = 12'd0;\n    reg [3:0]  term = 4'd0;\n    reg [19:0] check_sum = 20'd0;\n    reg [15:0] ip_check = 16'd0;\n    reg [7:0]  header_byte = 8'd0;   // byte `o` of the header\n    wire       header = o < 12'd42;\n\n    assign send_ready = !sending;\n    assign tx_valid = sending && (header ? 1'b1 : payload_valid);\n    assign tx_last = o == last_o;\n    assign payload_ready = sending && !header && tx_ready;\n    always @(*) tx_data = header ? header_byte : payload_data;\n\n    reg [15:0] term_value;\n    always @(*) begin\n        case (term)\n            4'd0: term_value = 16'h4500;\n            4'd1: term_value = ip_total;\n            4'd2: term_value = ident;\n            4'd3: term_value = 16'h4000;\n            4'd4: term_value = 16'h4011;\n            4'd5: term_value = ip[31:16];\n            4'd6: term_value = ip[15:0];\n            4'd7: term_value = to_ip[31:16];\n            default: term_value = to_ip[15:0];\n        endcase\n    end\n\n    wire        sent = tx_valid && tx_ready;\n    wire [11:0] o_next = sent ? o + 12'd1 : o;\n\n    // Byte `ahead` (= o + 1, up to 63) of the header, from registers alone: no arithmetic in front of the\n    // multiplexer (timing, 2026-11-05).\n    reg  [5:0]  ahead = 6'd1;\n    reg  [7:0]  ahead_byte;\n    always @(*) begin\n        case (ahead)\n            6'd0: ahead_byte = to_mac[47:40];       6'd1: ahead_byte = to_mac[39:32];\n            6'd2: ahead_byte = to_mac[31:24];       6'd3: ahead_byte = to_mac[23:16];\n            6'd4: ahead_byte = to_mac[15:8];        6'd5: ahead_byte = to_mac[7:0];\n            6'd6: ahead_byte = mac[47:40];          6'd7: ahead_byte = mac[39:32];\n            6'd8: ahead_byte = mac[31:24];          6'd9: ahead_byte = mac[23:16];\n            6'd10: ahead_byte = mac[15:8];          6'd11: ahead_byte = mac[7:0];\n            6'd12: ahead_byte = 8'h08;              6'd13: ahead_byte = 8'h00;\n            6'd14: ahead_byte = 8'h45;              6'd15: ahead_byte = 8'h00;\n            6'd16: ahead_byte = ip_total[15:8];     6'd17: ahead_byte = ip_total[7:0];\n            6'd18: ahead_byte = ident[15:8];        6'd19: ahead_byte = ident[7:0];\n            6'd20: ahead_byte = 8'h40;              6'd21: ahead_byte = 8'h00;\n            6'd22: ahead_byte = 8'd64;              6'd23: ahead_byte = 8'd17;\n            6'd24: ahead_byte = ip_check[15:8];     6'd25: ahead_byte = ip_check[7:0];\n            6'd26: ahead_byte = ip[31:24];          6'd27: ahead_byte = ip[23:16];\n            6'd28: ahead_byte = ip[15:8];           6'd29: ahead_byte = ip[7:0];\n            6'd30: ahead_byte = to_ip[31:24];       6'd31: ahead_byte = to_ip[23:16];\n            6'd32: ahead_byte = to_ip[15:8];        6'd33: ahead_byte = to_ip[7:0];\n            6'd34: ahead_byte = PORT[15:8];         6'd35: ahead_byte = PORT[7:0];\n            6'd36: ahead_byte = to_port[15:8];      6'd37: ahead_byte = to_port[7:0];\n            6'd38: ahead_byte = udp_length[15:8];   6'd39: ahead_byte = udp_length[7:0];\n            default: ahead_byte = 8'h00;                // 40, 41: no UDP checksum\n        endcase\n    end\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (!sending) begin\n            if (send) begin\n                sending <= 1'b1;\n                length <= send_length;\n                to_mac <= dst_mac;\n                to_ip <= dst_ip;\n                to_port <= dst_port;\n                ip_total <= {5'd0, send_length} + 16'd28;\n                udp_length <= {5'd0, send_length} + 16'd8;\n                last_o <= {1'b0, send_length} + 12'd41;\n                o <= 12'd0;\n                ahead <= 6'd1;\n                term <= 4'd0;\n                check_sum <= 20'd0;\n                header_byte <= dst_mac[47:40];\n            end\n        end else begin\n            // The checksum: nine terms, then two folds.\n            if (term < 4'd9) check_sum <= check_sum + {4'd0, term_value};\n            else if (term == 4'd9) check_sum <= {3'd0, {1'b0, check_sum[15:0]} + {13'd0, check_sum[19:16]}};\n            else if (term == 4'd10) ip_check <= ~(check_sum[15:0] + {15'd0, check_sum[16]});\n            if (term != 4'd11) term <= term + 4'd1;\n            o <= o_next;\n            if (sent && tx_last) begin\n                sending <= 1'b0;\n                ident <= ident + 16'd1;\n            end\n            // The next byte is looked up from `o` alone, `sent` only loads it (timing: the multiplexer's ready\n            // mustn't reach the lookup). Its sources are settled by then: ip_check from term 10, read at o = 24.\n            if (sent) header_byte <= ahead_byte;\n            if (sent && ahead != 6'd63) ahead <= ahead + 6'd1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp/tb/eth_udp_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_udp (PORT 1234): frames as eth_mac delivers them (a byte every fourth clock,\n// frame_last and frame_good with the last; short ones padded with non-zero bytes) and a MAC on the send side that\n// takes a byte every fourth clock and counts the times it found no byte (an underrun).\n// Receive: payloads of 5 (with a UDP checksum), 300 (checksum 0), 33 (odd, checksummed) and 18 bytes (a 60-byte\n// frame without padding: the last byte is in the checksum) are handed over exactly, with rx_new, rx_length and the\n// sender; so is a broadcast to 255.255.255.255 and an empty datagram (rx_new, no bytes). Dropped: a wrong UDP\n// checksum, another port, a fragment, a frame shorter than its IP length, another IP, rx_good low, a wrong header checksum, a UDP length that doesn't\n// match. A datagram arriving while the one before is still being taken is dropped (rx_dropped), the first intact.\n// Send: 0, 5, 1472 and 9 bytes (the last to an address whose header sum carries), and 12 to a consumer that's ready on\n// every clock — the headers field by field (a new identification each time), a valid IP checksum, the payload as offered by a source\n// that answers three clocks after each byte. And an echo: the received stream wired straight into the send side.\n// Then at 1000 Mbit/s: frames a byte on every clock, a consumer ready on every clock that must find a byte on every\n// clock (no underrun), a payload source that answers on every clock, the handover to a design taking a byte every\n// clock without a pause, and the echo at that pace.\n// Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule eth_udp_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}, HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n    localparam [15:0] HOST_PORT = 16'd50123;\n\n    reg  [7:0]  frame_data = 8'd0;\n    reg         frame_valid = 1'b0, frame_last = 1'b0, frame_good = 1'b0;\n    wire [7:0]  rx_data;\n    wire        rx_valid, rx_last, rx_new, rx_dropped;\n    reg         rx_ready = 1'b1;\n    wire [10:0] rx_length;\n    wire [47:0] peer_mac;\n    wire [31:0] peer_ip;\n    wire [15:0] peer_port;\n    reg         send = 1'b0;\n    reg  [10:0] send_length = 11'd0;\n    reg  [47:0] dst_mac = 48'd0;\n    reg  [31:0] dst_ip = 32'd0;\n    reg  [15:0] dst_port = 16'd0;\n    wire        send_ready, payload_ready;\n    reg  [7:0]  src_data = 8'd0;\n    reg         src_valid = 1'b0;\n    reg         echo = 1'b0;    // the send side's payload is the received stream\n    wire [7:0]  payload_data = echo ? rx_data : src_data;\n    wire        payload_valid = echo ? rx_valid : src_valid;\n    wire [7:0]  tx_data;\n    wire        tx_valid, tx_last;\n    reg         tx_ready = 1'b0;\n    wire        rx_ready_w = echo ? payload_ready : rx_ready;\n\n    eth_udp #(.PORT(16'd1234)) dut (.clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP),\n        .frame_data(frame_data), .frame_valid(frame_valid), .frame_last(frame_last), .frame_good(frame_good),\n        .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last), .rx_ready(rx_ready_w), .rx_length(rx_length),\n        .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(rx_new), .rx_dropped(rx_dropped),\n        .send(send), .send_length(send_length), .dst_mac(dst_mac), .dst_ip(dst_ip), .dst_port(dst_port),\n        .send_ready(send_ready), .payload_data(payload_data), .payload_valid(payload_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready),\n        .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- Received datagrams -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] got [0:2047];\n    integer got_n = 0, news = 0, drops = 0, lasts = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (rx_valid && rx_ready_w) begin\n            got[got_n] = rx_data;\n            got_n = got_n + 1;\n            if (rx_last) lasts = lasts + 1;\n        end\n        if (rx_new) news = news + 1;\n        if (rx_dropped) drops = drops + 1;\n    end\n\n    reg greedy = 1'b0;   // a consumer that takes a byte on every clock it's offered one\n    reg dense = 1'b0;    // frames a byte on every clock\n    reg fast = 1'b0;     // a payload source that answers on every clock\n    // Pauses in the handover: the design ready, the datagram not all taken, no byte offered.\n    integer rx_gaps = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (fast && dut.holding && got_n > 0 && rx_ready_w && !rx_valid && lasts == 0) rx_gaps = rx_gaps + 1;\n\n    // ---- The MAC's send side ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] wire_bytes [0:2047];\n    integer wire_n = 0, frames = 0, underruns = 0;\n    reg [1:0] slot = 2'd0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        slot <= slot + 2'd1;\n        // The MAC doesn't wait: whenever it asks during a datagram, a byte must be there.\n        // (A consumer always ready with the slow source finds no byte between them: that pairing checks only the bytes.)\n        if (tx_ready && dut.sending && !tx_valid && (!greedy || fast)) underruns = underruns + 1;\n        if (tx_valid && tx_ready) begin\n            wire_bytes[wire_n] = tx_data;\n            wire_n = wire_n + 1;\n            if (tx_last) frames = frames + 1;\n        end\n    end\n    always @(negedge clk) tx_ready = greedy || (slot == 2'd0);\n\n    // A payload source that answers three clocks after each byte taken.\n    reg [7:0] payload [0:2047];\n    integer next_byte = 0, wait_clocks = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (!echo) begin\n        if (src_valid && payload_ready && fast) begin\n            next_byte = next_byte + 1;\n            src_data <= payload[next_byte];\n        end else if (src_valid && payload_ready) begin\n            src_valid <= 1'b0;\n            next_byte = next_byte + 1;\n            wait_clocks = 2;\n        end else if (!src_valid && dut.sending && dut.o >= 12'd38) begin\n            if (wait_clocks > 0) wait_clocks = wait_clocks - 1;\n            else begin src_data <= payload[next_byte]; src_valid <= 1'b1; end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // ---- Frames in -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:2047];\n    integer frame_n;\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n\n    function [31:0] sum16(input integer from, input integer to);   // the words from..to-1, not folded\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {frame[i], (i + 1 < to) ? frame[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            sum16 = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function [15:0] fold(input [31:0] s);\n        reg [31:0] t;\n        begin\n            t = s;\n            while (t[31:16] != 0) t = t[15:0] + t[31:16];\n            fold = t[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // A datagram of `n` bytes to `to_ip`:`port`; `checksum`: with a UDP checksum (else 0).\n    task make_udp(input [47:0] to, input [31:0] to_ip, input [15:0] port, input integer n, input checksum, input [15:0] flags);\n        integer i, total;\n        reg [15:0] c;\n        begin\n            total = 28 + n;\n            put48(0, to); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0800);\n            frame[14] = 8'h45; frame[15] = 8'h00; put16(16, total[15:0]); put16(18, 16'h7A01 + n);\n            put16(20, flags); frame[22] = 8'd64; frame[23] = 8'd17; put16(24, 16'h0000);\n            put32(26, HOST_IP); put32(30, to_ip);\n            put16(24, ~fold(sum16(14, 34)));\n            put16(34, HOST_PORT); put16(36, port); put16(38, (8 + n)); put16(40, 16'h0000);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = (i * 53 + n) ^ 8'h5A;\n            if (checksum) begin\n                c = ~fold(sum16(26, 34) + sum16(34, 42 + n) + 32'd17 + (8 + n));\n                put16(40, c == 16'h0000 ? 16'hFFFF : c);\n            end\n            frame_n = 14 + total;\n            for (i = frame_n; i < 60; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'hE0 + i;\n            if (frame_n < 60) frame_n = 60;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task deliver(input good);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            for (i = 0; i < frame_n; i = i + 1) begin\n                frame_data = frame[i];\n                frame_valid = 1'b1;\n                frame_last = (i == frame_n - 1);\n                frame_good = (i == frame_n - 1) && good;\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                if (!dense) begin\n                    frame_valid = 1'b0; frame_last = 1'b0; frame_good = 1'b0;\n                    repeat (3) @(posedge clk);\n                    #1;\n                end\n            end\n            frame_valid = 1'b0; frame_last = 1'b0; frame_good = 1'b0;\n            repeat (40) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [7:0] kept [0:2047];\n    task expect_datagram(input integer n, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) kept[i] = frame[42 + i];\n            repeat (4 * n + 40) @(posedge clk);\n            wrong = 0;\n            if (news != 1 || got_n != n || rx_length != n || (n > 0 && lasts != 1)) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d new, %0d bytes (length %0d), %0d last\", what, news, got_n, rx_length, lasts);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (got[i] !== kept[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (peer_mac !== HOST || peer_ip !== HOST_IP || peer_port !== HOST_PORT) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: the sender\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n            news = 0; got_n = 0; lasts = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task expect_none(input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            repeat (200) @(posedge clk);\n            if (news != 0 || got_n != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: handed over\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n            news = 0; got_n = 0; lasts = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- Sending ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    function [31:0] wire_sum(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {wire_bytes[i], (i + 1 < to) ? wire_bytes[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            wire_sum = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function [47:0] w48(input integer at); w48 = {wire_bytes[at], wire_bytes[at + 1], wire_bytes[at + 2], wire_bytes[at + 3], wire_bytes[at + 4], wire_bytes[at + 5]}; endfunction\n    function [31:0] w32(input integer at); w32 = {wire_bytes[at], wire_bytes[at + 1], wire_bytes[at + 2], wire_bytes[at + 3]}; endfunction\n    function [15:0] w16(input integer at); w16 = {wire_bytes[at], wire_bytes[at + 1]}; endfunction\n\n    reg [15:0] last_ident = 16'd0;\n    integer sent_before = 0;\n    task check_sent(input integer n, input [47:0] to, input [31:0] to_ip, input [15:0] port, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        integer i, wrong;\n        begin\n            while (frames == 0) @(posedge clk);\n            if (wire_n != 42 + n) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d bytes sent\", what, wire_n); errors = errors + 1; end\n            else begin\n                if (w48(0) !== to || w48(6) !== MAC || w16(12) !== 16'h0800) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: Ethernet header\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (wire_bytes[14] !== 8'h45 || w16(16) !== 28 + n || w16(20) !== 16'h4000 || wire_bytes[22] !== 8'd64 || wire_bytes[23] !== 8'd17)\n                    begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: IP header\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (sent_before && w16(18) === last_ident) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: the same identification as the datagram before\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                last_ident = w16(18);\n                sent_before = 1;\n                if (fold(wire_sum(14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: IP checksum\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (w32(26) !== IP || w32(30) !== to_ip) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: IP addresses\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                if (w16(34) !== 16'd1234 || w16(36) !== port || w16(38) !== 8 + n || w16(40) !== 16'h0000)\n                    begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: UDP header\", what); errors = errors + 1; end\n                wrong = 0;\n                for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) if (wire_bytes[42 + i] !== kept[i]) wrong = wrong + 1;\n                if (wrong != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d payload bytes wrong\", what, wrong); errors = errors + 1; end\n            end\n            if (underruns != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0s: %0d underruns\", what, underruns); errors = errors + 1; end\n            wire_n = 0; frames = 0; underruns = 0;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task send_datagram(input integer n, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            send_to(n, {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd255, 8'd255}, what);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task send_to(input integer n, input [31:0] to_ip, input [8*40-1:0] what);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) begin payload[i] = (i * 29) ^ 8'hC5 ^ (i >> 6); kept[i] = payload[i]; end\n            next_byte = 0;\n            src_valid <= 1'b0;   // a byte fetched for the datagram before is stale\n            @(negedge clk);\n            while (!send_ready) @(negedge clk);\n            send = 1'b1; send_length = n; dst_mac = 48'h00_11_22_33_44_55; dst_ip = to_ip; dst_port = 16'd4321;\n            @(posedge clk); #1; send = 1'b0;\n            check_sent(n, 48'h00_11_22_33_44_55, to_ip, 16'd4321, what);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer k;\n    reg [31:0] carry_ip = 32'd0;\n    initial begin\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 5, 1, 16'h4000);    deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(5, \"5 bytes, checksummed\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 300, 0, 16'h4000);  deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(300, \"300 bytes, no checksum\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 33, 1, 16'h4000);   deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(33, \"33 bytes\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 18, 1, 16'h4000);   deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(18, \"18 bytes, no padding\");\n        make_udp(48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF, 32'hFFFF_FFFF, 16'd1234, 7, 1, 16'h0000); deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(7, \"a broadcast\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 0, 1, 16'h4000);    deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(0, \"an empty datagram\");\n\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 5, 1, 16'h4000); frame[45] = frame[45] ^ 8'h10; deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a wrong UDP checksum\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1235, 5, 1, 16'h4000);    deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another port\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 5, 1, 16'h2000);    deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a fragment\");\n        make_udp(MAC, {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd99}, 16'd1234, 5, 1, 16'h4000); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another IP\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 5, 1, 16'h4000);    deliver(1'b0); expect_none(\"rx_good low\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 5, 1, 16'h4000); frame[25] = frame[25] ^ 8'h01; deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a wrong header checksum\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 5, 0, 16'h4000); put16(38, 16'd12); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a wrong UDP length\");\n        // A frame shorter than its IP length says (the lengths agree with each other, the header checksum is right).\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 40, 0, 16'h4000); put16(16, 16'd88); put16(38, 16'd68); put16(24, 16'h0000);\n        put16(24, ~fold(sum16(14, 34))); deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"a truncated frame\");\n\n        // Taken slowly: the next datagram is dropped, the first stays.\n        rx_ready = 1'b0;\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 20, 1, 16'h4000); deliver(1'b1);\n        for (k = 0; k < 20; k = k + 1) kept[k] = frame[42 + k];\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 25, 1, 16'h4000); deliver(1'b1);\n        if (drops != 1) fail(\"rx_dropped for a datagram while another was held\");\n        rx_ready = 1'b1;\n        repeat (200) @(posedge clk);\n        if (news != 1 || got_n != 20) fail(\"the held datagram\");\n        else for (k = 0; k < 20; k = k + 1) if (got[k] !== kept[k]) begin fail(\"the held datagram's bytes\"); k = 20; end\n        news = 0; got_n = 0; lasts = 0;\n\n        send_datagram(0, \"sending 0 bytes\");\n        send_datagram(5, \"sending 5 bytes\");\n        send_datagram(1472, \"sending 1472 bytes\");\n        // A destination whose header sum carries out of 16 bits when it's folded (the end-around carry).\n        begin : carry\n            reg [31:0] base, total_sum;\n            integer low;\n            @(posedge clk); #1;   // the identification counts up on the clock edge the last datagram ended on\n            base = 32'h4500 + 32'h4000 + 32'h4011 + IP[31:16] + IP[15:0] + (28 + 9) + dut.ident + 32'h0A00;\n            for (low = 0; low < 65536; low = low + 1) begin\n                total_sum = base + low;\n                if (total_sum[15:0] + total_sum[31:16] > 32'hFFFF && carry_ip == 0) carry_ip = {16'h0A00, low[15:0]};\n            end\n        end\n        send_to(9, carry_ip, \"a header sum that carries\");\n        // A consumer that is ready on every clock: every byte offered must be the right one.\n        greedy = 1'b1;\n        send_datagram(12, \"sending to a consumer always ready\");\n        greedy = 1'b0;\n\n        // An echo: what arrives goes back to its sender, straight from the receive side.\n        echo = 1'b1;\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 64, 1, 16'h4000);\n        for (k = 0; k < 64; k = k + 1) kept[k] = frame[42 + k];\n        fork\n            deliver(1'b1);\n            begin\n                @(posedge rx_new);\n                @(negedge clk);\n                send = 1'b1; send_length = rx_length; dst_mac = peer_mac; dst_ip = peer_ip; dst_port = peer_port;\n                @(posedge clk); #1; send = 1'b0;\n            end\n        join\n        check_sent(64, HOST, HOST_IP, HOST_PORT, \"the echo\");\n        echo = 1'b0;\n\n        // 1000 Mbit/s: a byte a clock everywhere.\n        greedy = 1'b1; dense = 1'b1; fast = 1'b1;\n        news = 0; got_n = 0; lasts = 0; drops = 0;\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 300, 1, 16'h4000);  deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(300, \"300 bytes, dense\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 1472, 0, 16'h4000); deliver(1'b1); expect_datagram(1472, \"1472 bytes, dense\");\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1235, 5, 1, 16'h4000);    deliver(1'b1); expect_none(\"another port, dense\");\n        if (rx_gaps != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0d pauses handing datagrams over\", rx_gaps); errors = errors + 1; end\n        send_datagram(1472, \"sending 1472 bytes a byte a clock\");\n        send_datagram(1, \"sending 1 byte a byte a clock\");\n        echo = 1'b1;\n        make_udp(MAC, IP, 16'd1234, 1000, 1, 16'h4000);\n        for (k = 0; k < 1000; k = k + 1) kept[k] = frame[42 + k];\n        fork\n            deliver(1'b1);\n            begin\n                @(posedge rx_new);\n                @(negedge clk);\n                send = 1'b1; send_length = rx_length; dst_mac = peer_mac; dst_ip = peer_ip; dst_port = peer_port;\n                @(posedge clk); #1; send = 1'b0;\n            end\n        join\n        check_sent(1000, HOST, HOST_IP, HOST_PORT, \"the echo a byte a clock\");\n        echo = 1'b0; greedy = 1'b0; dense = 1'b0; fast = 1'b0;\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_core/README.md" : "# Ethernet UDP core\n\nThe PHY-independent part of GateLab's IPv4 stack, for designs that bring their own PHY adapter — usually you want\n`eth_udp_stack` (RMII) or `eth_udp_stack_mii` (MII), which wrap it:\n\n- `eth_mac` (FCS, padding, the gap) and `eth_mdio` (100 full duplex advertised, `link_up`);\n- `eth_arp` and `eth_icmp_echo`, answering ARP and ping on their own;\n- `eth_udp` on port `PORT` for the design;\n- the multiplexer sharing the MAC among the three senders (one frame to its last byte; ARP, then ping, then UDP).\n\nIts `phy_*` ports take `eth_rmii_phy`'s or `eth_mii_phy`'s byte interface: received bytes after the SFD with\n`rx_end`, and bytes to send with valid/ready at most every fourth clock. `clk` is the RMII reference (50 MHz) or the\nMII adapter's system clock (100 MHz).\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_core/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_mac\",\n    \"eth_mdio\",\n    \"eth_arp\",\n    \"eth_icmp_echo\",\n    \"eth_udp\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"The PHY-independent part of GateLab's IPv4 stack: MAC, MDIO setup (100 full duplex) and link watch, ARP and ping answered on their own, UDP on one port for the design, and the multiplexer sharing the MAC among them — on the byte interface of eth_rmii_phy or eth_mii_phy (eth_udp_stack and eth_udp_stack_mii put them together). About three block RAMs.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 100.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DP16KD\" : 2,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 3221,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 2010\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 43.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 37,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 539,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 791,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 1785,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 1935,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 3770,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 35,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 106,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 54.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 1180,\n        \"BSRAM\" : 2,\n        \"DFF\" : 2010,\n        \"LUT4\" : 3347,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 448,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 152,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 60,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 21\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 27.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 3784,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 8,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 7\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 153.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 181,\n        \"RAMB18E1\" : 2,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 1931,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 2897\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_udp_core\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"udp\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"arp\",\n    \"ping\",\n    \"icmp\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"stack\",\n    \"mac\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Der PHY-unabhängige Teil des IPv4-Stacks von GateLab: MAC, MDIO-Einrichtung (100 Vollduplex) und Link-Überwachung, ARP und Ping beantwortet er selbst, UDP auf einem Port für das Design und der Multiplexer, der den MAC unter ihnen teilt — an der Byte-Schnittstelle von eth_rmii_phy oder eth_mii_phy (eth_udp_stack und eth_udp_stack_mii setzen sie zusammen). Etwa drei Block-RAMs.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-UDP-Kern\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet UDP core\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1234,\n      \"description\" : \"The UDP port received on and sent from\",\n      \"name\" : \"PORT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 50000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz (the RMII reference or the MII system clock)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's MDIO address (from its straps)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PHY_ADDR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 200,\n      \"description\" : \"Milliseconds before the first MDIO transaction\",\n      \"name\" : \"SETTLE_MS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 257,\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO register 4 (16'h0101: 100 full duplex only; 16'h0001: no 10/100 mode, for a Gigabit link)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ADVERTISE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 52,\n      \"description\" : \"eth_mac's gap between frames, in clocks (52 at 100 Mbit/s, 12 at 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"GAP_CLOCKS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The RMII reference (50 MHz) or the MII system clock (100 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the PHY adapter's rx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the PHY adapter's rx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the PHY adapter's rx_end\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_end\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the PHY adapter's rx_error\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_rx_error\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the PHY adapter's tx_data\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the PHY adapter's tx_valid\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_tx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the PHY adapter's tx_ready\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"phy_tx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDC (the pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO's value while mdio_oe is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Drive MDIO (assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO as it is (the pin, with a pull-up)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY reports a link\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"link_up\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a good FCS (to anyone)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a bad FCS or RX_ER\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_bad\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an ARP reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"arp_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an echo reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ping_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A received datagram's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte is there\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the datagram's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The design takes the byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Bytes in the datagram handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram is handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_new\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram was dropped (the one before wasn't taken yet)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_dropped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Starts a datagram (with send_ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: payload bytes (0 … 1472)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A datagram can be started\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is there (within three clocks of the last)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is taken\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_udp_core\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_core/rtl/eth_udp_core.v" : "// The PHY-independent part of GateLab's small IPv4 stack: eth_mac and eth_mdio, eth_arp and eth_icmp_echo answering ARP\n// and ping on their own, eth_udp on port PORT for the design, and the multiplexer that shares the MAC among the three\n// senders (it keeps one frame to its last byte: ARP replies first, then echo replies, then the design's datagrams).\n// The PHY's byte interface (phy_*) is eth_rmii_phy's or eth_mii_phy's; eth_udp_stack and eth_udp_stack_mii put the\n// two together. Everything on `clk`: the RMII reference (50 MHz), or a system clock for MII (100 MHz).\n//\n// The design's side is eth_udp's (see there): received datagrams on rx_*/peer_*, datagrams to send on send_*/\n// payload_*. Pulses for LEDs and counters: `frame_good` (a frame with a good FCS, to anyone), `frame_bad` (a bad FCS\n// or RX_ER), `arp_replied`, `ping_replied`. MDIO advertises ADVERTISE (register 4: 100 full duplex by default) and\n// reports `link_up`. For 1000 Mbit/s (eth_udp_stack_rgmii): ADVERTISE 0001 (no 10/100 mode, the PHY's own 1000 in\n// register 9), GAP_CLOCKS 12, and every sender goes a byte a clock.\n\nmodule eth_udp_core #(\n    parameter [15:0]  PORT = 16'd1234,\n    parameter integer CLK_HZ = 50_000_000,\n    parameter [4:0]   PHY_ADDR = 5'd1,\n    parameter integer SETTLE_MS = 200,\n    parameter [15:0]  ADVERTISE = 16'h0101,    // eth_mdio's register 4\n    parameter integer GAP_CLOCKS = 52          // eth_mac's inter-frame gap: 96 bit times in clocks of `clk`\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,          // the RMII reference or the MII system clock\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    // The PHY's byte interface (eth_rmii_phy or eth_mii_phy)\n    input  wire [7:0]  phy_rx_data,\n    input  wire        phy_rx_valid,\n    input  wire        phy_rx_end,\n    input  wire        phy_rx_error,\n    output wire [7:0]  phy_tx_data,\n    output wire        phy_tx_valid,\n    input  wire        phy_tx_ready,\n    // MDIO (the pins)\n    output wire        mdc,\n    output wire        mdio_out,\n    output wire        mdio_oe,\n    input  wire        mdio_in,\n    // Status\n    output wire        link_up,\n    output wire        frame_good,\n    output wire        frame_bad,\n    output wire        arp_replied,\n    output wire        ping_replied,\n    // UDP: received datagrams\n    output wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    output wire        rx_valid,\n    output wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_ready,\n    output wire [10:0] rx_length,\n    output wire [47:0] peer_mac,\n    output wire [31:0] peer_ip,\n    output wire [15:0] peer_port,\n    output wire        rx_new,\n    output wire        rx_dropped,\n    // UDP: datagrams to send\n    input  wire        send,\n    input  wire [10:0] send_length,\n    input  wire [47:0] dst_mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] dst_ip,\n    input  wire [15:0] dst_port,\n    output wire        send_ready,\n    input  wire [7:0]  payload_data,\n    input  wire        payload_valid,\n    output wire        payload_ready\n);\n    // ---- MAC and MDIO ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] frame_data, tx_data;\n    wire       frame_valid, frame_last, frame_ok, tx_valid, tx_last, tx_ready;\n    eth_mac #(.GAP_CLOCKS(GAP_CLOCKS)) u_mac (\n        .clk(clk), .phy_rx_data(phy_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(phy_rx_end),\n        .phy_rx_error(phy_rx_error), .phy_tx_data(phy_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(phy_tx_ready),\n        .rx_data(frame_data), .rx_valid(frame_valid), .rx_last(frame_last), .rx_good(frame_ok),\n        .tx_data(tx_data), .tx_valid(tx_valid), .tx_last(tx_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready), .tx_underrun());\n    assign frame_good = frame_valid && frame_last && frame_ok;\n    assign frame_bad = frame_valid && frame_last && !frame_ok;\n\n    eth_mdio #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .PHY_ADDR(PHY_ADDR), .ADVERTISE(ADVERTISE), .SETTLE_MS(SETTLE_MS)) u_mdio (\n        .clk(clk), .mdc(mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(mdio_in), .link_up(link_up),\n        .setup_done(), .go(1'b0), .write(1'b0), .reg_addr(5'd0), .wdata(16'd0), .ready(), .done(), .rdata());\n\n    // ---- The three senders --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] arp_data, ping_data, udp_data;\n    wire       arp_valid, arp_last, ping_valid, ping_last, udp_valid, udp_last;\n    reg  [1:0] sel = 2'd0;        // 0 none, 1 ARP, 2 ping, 3 UDP\n\n    eth_arp u_arp (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(mac), .ip(ip),\n        .rx_data(frame_data), .rx_valid(frame_valid), .rx_last(frame_last), .rx_good(frame_ok),\n        .tx_data(arp_data), .tx_valid(arp_valid), .tx_last(arp_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready && sel == 2'd1),\n        .replied(arp_replied));\n\n    eth_icmp_echo u_ping (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(mac), .ip(ip),\n        .rx_data(frame_data), .rx_valid(frame_valid), .rx_last(frame_last), .rx_good(frame_ok),\n        .tx_data(ping_data), .tx_valid(ping_valid), .tx_last(ping_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready && sel == 2'd2),\n        .replied(ping_replied));\n\n    eth_udp #(.PORT(PORT)) u_udp (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(mac), .ip(ip),\n        .frame_data(frame_data), .frame_valid(frame_valid), .frame_last(frame_last), .frame_good(frame_ok),\n        .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .rx_last(rx_last), .rx_ready(rx_ready), .rx_length(rx_length),\n        .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(rx_new), .rx_dropped(rx_dropped),\n        .send(send), .send_length(send_length), .dst_mac(dst_mac), .dst_ip(dst_ip), .dst_port(dst_port),\n        .send_ready(send_ready), .payload_data(payload_data), .payload_valid(payload_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready),\n        .tx_data(udp_data), .tx_valid(udp_valid), .tx_last(udp_last), .tx_ready(tx_ready && sel == 2'd3));\n\n    // ---- The multiplexer: a sender keeps the MAC until its frame's last byte is taken ----------------------------\n    assign tx_data  = sel == 2'd1 ? arp_data  : sel == 2'd2 ? ping_data  : udp_data;\n    assign tx_valid = sel == 2'd1 ? arp_valid : sel == 2'd2 ? ping_valid : sel == 2'd3 ? udp_valid : 1'b0;\n    assign tx_last  = sel == 2'd1 ? arp_last  : sel == 2'd2 ? ping_last  : udp_last;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (sel == 2'd0) begin\n            if (arp_valid) sel <= 2'd1;\n            else if (ping_valid) sel <= 2'd2;\n            else if (udp_valid) sel <= 2'd3;\n        end else if (tx_valid && tx_ready && tx_last) sel <= 2'd0;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_core/tb/eth_udp_core_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_udp_core on its PHY byte interface: a model of a PHY adapter (eth_rmii_phy's and\n// eth_mii_phy's interface) hands it a host's frames — the bytes after the SFD with their FCS, one every fourth clock,\n// then rx_end — and takes what it sends one byte every fourth clock (7 × 55, D5, the frame, the FCS checked), plus\n// the PHY's MDIO side (link up). The design is a UDP echo, wired as in eth_udp's README, plus a datagram of its own on\n// demand. Checks as eth_udp_stack's: MDIO setup and link_up; ARP; ping; a UDP echo; the design's own broadcast datagram\n// while a ping is answered, and a ping arriving while the design's 300-byte datagram goes out (both intact each time);\n// ARP and ping back to back; frame_bad for a bad FCS. Prints PASS or every\n// failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule core_phy_mdio (input wire mdc, inout wire mdio);\n    reg [15:0] regs [0:31];\n    integer i, ones = 0, n = -1;\n    reg [1:0] op;\n    reg [4:0] pa, ra;\n    reg [15:0] data;\n    reg drive = 1'b0, value = 1'b1;\n    assign mdio = drive ? value : 1'bz;\n    initial begin\n        for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) regs[i] = 16'h0000;\n        regs[1] = 16'h782D; regs[2] = 16'h001C; regs[3] = 16'hC816;\n    end\n    always @(posedge mdc) begin : slave\n        reg b;\n        b = mdio;\n        if (n < 0) begin\n            if (b === 1'b1) ones = ones + 1;\n            else begin if (ones >= 32) n = 32; ones = 0; end\n        end else begin\n            n = n + 1;\n            if (n == 33 && b !== 1'b1) n = -1;\n            else if (n == 34 || n == 35) op = {op[0], b};\n            else if (n >= 36 && n <= 40) pa = {pa[3:0], b};\n            else if (n >= 41 && n <= 45) begin ra = {ra[3:0], b}; if (n == 45) data = regs[ra]; end\n            else if (op == 2'b10 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n == 46) #50 begin drive = 1'b1; value = 1'b0; end\n                else if (n >= 47 && n <= 62) #50 value = data[62 - n];\n                else if (n == 63) begin #50 drive = 1'b0; n = -1; end\n            end else if (op == 2'b01 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n >= 48) data = {data[14:0], b};\n                if (n == 63) begin regs[ra] = data; n = -1; end\n            end else if (n == 63) n = -1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule eth_udp_core_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}, HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n\n    reg  [7:0]  p_rx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg         p_rx_valid = 1'b0, p_rx_end = 1'b0;\n    wire [7:0]  p_tx_data;\n    wire        p_tx_valid;\n    reg         p_tx_ready = 1'b0;\n    wire         mdc, mdio_out, mdio_oe, mdio, link_up, frame_good, frame_bad, arp_replied, ping_replied;\n    pullup (mdio);\n    assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\n    core_phy_mdio u_phy_mdio (.mdc(mdc), .mdio(mdio));\n\n    wire [7:0]  udp_data;\n    wire        udp_valid, udp_last, rx_new, payload_ready, send_ready;\n    wire [10:0] udp_length;\n    wire [47:0] peer_mac;\n    wire [31:0] peer_ip;\n    wire [15:0] peer_port;\n    reg         own = 1'b0;          // the design's own datagram instead of the echo\n    reg         own_send = 1'b0;\n    reg  [10:0] own_length = 11'd10;\n    reg  [3:0]  own_index = 4'd0;\n    wire        send = own ? own_send : rx_new;\n    wire [7:0]  payload = own ? 8'hB0 + own_index : udp_data;\n    wire        payload_valid = own ? 1'b1 : udp_valid;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (own && payload_ready) own_index <= own_index + 4'd1;\n\n    eth_udp_core #(.PORT(16'd1234), .SETTLE_MS(1)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP),\n        .phy_rx_data(p_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(p_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(p_rx_end), .phy_rx_error(1'b0),\n        .phy_tx_data(p_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(p_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(p_tx_ready), .mdc(mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(mdio), .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good), .frame_bad(frame_bad), .arp_replied(arp_replied), .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(udp_data), .rx_valid(udp_valid), .rx_last(udp_last), .rx_ready(own ? 1'b0 : payload_ready),\n        .rx_length(udp_length), .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(), .send(send), .send_length(own ? own_length : udp_length),\n        .dst_mac(own ? 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF : peer_mac), .dst_ip(own ? 32'h0A00_FFFF : peer_ip),\n        .dst_port(own ? 16'd4321 : peer_port), .send_ready(send_ready), .payload_data(payload),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready));\n\n    integer goods = 0, bads = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (frame_good) goods = goods + 1;\n        if (frame_bad) bads = bads + 1;\n    end\n\n    // ---- CRC and checksums ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    function [31:0] crc_update(input [31:0] c, input [7:0] d);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] x;\n        begin\n            x = c ^ {24'd0, d};\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) x = {1'b0, x[31:1]} ^ (x[0] ? 32'hEDB8_8320 : 32'd0);\n            crc_update = x;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function [15:0] fold(input [31:0] s);\n        reg [31:0] t;\n        begin\n            t = s;\n            while (t[31:16] != 0) t = t[15:0] + t[31:16];\n            fold = t[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- The host sending -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:1599];\n    integer frame_n;\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n    function [31:0] sum16(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {frame[i], (i + 1 < to) ? frame[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            sum16 = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task ipv4(input [7:0] proto, input integer payload_bytes);\n        begin\n            put48(0, MAC); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0800);\n            frame[14] = 8'h45; frame[15] = 8'h00; put16(16, 20 + payload_bytes); put16(18, 16'h3E01);\n            put16(20, 16'h4000); frame[22] = 8'd64; frame[23] = proto; put16(24, 16'h0000);\n            put32(26, HOST_IP); put32(30, IP);\n            put16(24, ~fold(sum16(14, 34)));\n            frame_n = 34 + payload_bytes;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_arp;\n        begin\n            put48(0, 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0806); put16(14, 16'h0001); put16(16, 16'h0800);\n            frame[18] = 8'd6; frame[19] = 8'd4; put16(20, 16'h0001); put48(22, HOST); put32(28, HOST_IP); put48(32, 48'h0);\n            put32(38, IP);\n            frame_n = 42;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_ping(input integer data_bytes);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd1, 8 + data_bytes);\n            frame[34] = 8'd8; frame[35] = 8'd0; put16(36, 16'h0000); put16(38, 16'h1234); put16(40, 16'h0007);\n            for (i = 0; i < data_bytes; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = i * 3 + 1;\n            put16(36, ~fold(sum16(34, 42 + data_bytes)));\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_udp(input integer n);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd17, 8 + n);\n            put16(34, 16'd50123); put16(36, 16'd1234); put16(38, 8 + n); put16(40, 16'h0000);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = (i * 41) ^ 8'h6C;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // The adapter's receive side: a byte every fourth clock, then rx_end.\n    task transmit(input bad);\n        integer i, n;\n        reg [31:0] c;\n        begin\n            n = frame_n < 60 ? 60 : frame_n;\n            for (i = frame_n; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'h00;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, frame[i]);\n            c = ~c ^ (bad ? 32'h0001_0000 : 32'd0);\n            for (i = 0; i < n + 4; i = i + 1) begin\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                p_rx_data = i < n ? frame[i] : c[8 * (i - n) +: 8];\n                p_rx_valid = 1'b1;\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                p_rx_valid = 1'b0;\n                repeat (2) @(posedge clk);\n            end\n            @(posedge clk); #1; p_rx_end = 1'b1;\n            @(posedge clk); #1; p_rx_end = 1'b0;\n            repeat (60) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- What comes back: frames in a list, each with its FCS checked ------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] back [0:7][0:1599];\n    integer back_n [0:7];\n    integer backs = 0, raw_n = 0;\n    reg [7:0] raw [0:1607];\n    // The adapter's send side: a byte every fourth clock while tx_valid; the frame ends when tx_valid falls.\n    reg [1:0] slot = 2'd0;\n    always @(posedge clk) slot <= slot + 2'd1;\n    always @(negedge clk) p_tx_ready = (slot == 2'd0) && p_tx_valid;\n    reg was_valid = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (p_tx_valid && p_tx_ready) begin raw[raw_n] = p_tx_data; raw_n = raw_n + 1; end\n        if (was_valid && !p_tx_valid) begin : done\n            integer i;\n            reg [31:0] c;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 8; i < raw_n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, raw[i]);\n            if (c !== 32'hDEBB_20E3 || raw[7] !== 8'hD5) fail(\"a frame sent with a bad FCS or preamble\");\n            if (backs < 8) begin\n                for (i = 8; i < raw_n - 4; i = i + 1) back[backs][i - 8] = raw[i];\n                back_n[backs] = raw_n - 12;\n            end\n            backs = backs + 1;\n            raw_n = 0;\n        end\n        was_valid = p_tx_valid;\n    end\n    function [15:0] b16(input integer f, input integer at); b16 = {back[f][at], back[f][at + 1]}; endfunction\n    function [31:0] bsum(input integer f, input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {back[f][i], (i + 1 < to) ? back[f][i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            bsum = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task wait_backs(input integer n);\n        integer t;\n        begin\n            t = 0;\n            while (backs < n && t < 40000) begin @(posedge clk); t = t + 1; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Finds the frame sent back with this EtherType and IP protocol (0 for ARP), or -1.\n    function integer find(input [15:0] ethertype, input [7:0] proto);\n        integer f;\n        begin\n            find = -1;\n            for (f = 0; f < backs && f < 8; f = f + 1)\n                if (b16(f, 12) == ethertype && (ethertype == 16'h0806 || back[f][23] == proto) && find < 0) find = f;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    integer f, i, wrong;\n    initial begin\n        wait (link_up === 1'b1);\n        if (u_phy_mdio.regs[4] !== 16'h0101) fail(\"MDIO didn't advertise 100 full duplex\");\n\n        // ARP.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0806, 8'd0);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no ARP reply\");\n        else if (b16(f, 20) !== 16'h0002 || {back[f][28], back[f][29], back[f][30], back[f][31]} !== IP) fail(\"the ARP reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // Ping.\n        make_ping(56); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no echo reply\");\n        else if (back[f][34] !== 8'd0 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF || fold(bsum(f, 34, 98)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // UDP echo.\n        make_udp(37); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no UDP echo\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 37; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== (((i * 41) ^ 8'h6C) & 8'hFF)) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd50123 || b16(f, 38) !== 16'd45 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF)\n                fail(\"the UDP echo\");\n        end\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // The design's own datagram while a ping is answered: both intact.\n        own = 1'b1;\n        make_ping(200);\n        fork\n            transmit(1'b0);\n            begin\n                // Once the echo reply is going out (its tx_valid drops between bytes), the design sends: the datagram\n                // must wait for the reply's last byte.\n                wait (dut.sel == 2'd2 && dut.u_mac.state == 3'd2);\n                repeat (40) @(posedge clk);\n                @(negedge clk); own_send = 1'b1; @(posedge clk); #1; own_send = 1'b0;\n            end\n        join\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (backs != 2) fail(\"the design's datagram and the echo reply: not both sent\");\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"the design's datagram\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== 8'hB0 + i) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 0) !== 16'hFFFF || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd4321) fail(\"the design's datagram's contents\");\n        end\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0 || fold(bsum(f, 34, 242)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply next to the datagram\");\n        own = 1'b0;\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // ARP and ping back to back.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0);\n        make_ping(20); transmit(1'b0);\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (find(16'h0806, 8'd0) < 0 || find(16'h0800, 8'd1) < 0) fail(\"ARP and ping back to back\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // A ping arriving while the design's long datagram goes out: its reply waits for the datagram's last byte.\n        own = 1'b1;\n        own_length = 11'd300;\n        @(negedge clk); own_send = 1'b1; @(posedge clk); #1; own_send = 1'b0;\n        wait (dut.sel == 2'd3);\n        make_ping(24); transmit(1'b0);\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (backs != 2) fail(\"a ping during the design's datagram: not both sent\");\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0 || back_n[f] != 66 || fold(bsum(f, 34, 66)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply after the datagram\");\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0 || back_n[f] != 342) fail(\"the datagram with a ping waiting\");\n        own = 1'b0;\n        own_length = 11'd10;\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // A bad FCS.\n        goods = 0; bads = 0;\n        make_ping(10); transmit(1'b1);\n        repeat (2000) @(posedge clk);\n        if (bads != 1 || goods != 0 || backs != 0) fail(\"a bad FCS\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack/README.md" : "# Ethernet UDP stack\n\nEverything a design needs to be on the network through a 10/100 PHY on RMII (the Tang Primer 20K Dock's\nRTL8201F), in one core — `eth_rmii_phy` and `eth_udp_core` (for an MII PHY: `eth_udp_stack_mii`):\n\n- `eth_rmii_phy` and `eth_mac` (FCS, padding, the inter-frame gap) on the PHY's 50 MHz reference clock;\n- `eth_mdio`: after `SETTLE_MS` it advertises 100BASE-TX full duplex and restarts auto-negotiation, then watches\n  the link (`link_up`);\n- `eth_arp` and `eth_icmp_echo`: other hosts find the design and can `ping` it, without any logic of yours;\n- `eth_udp` on port `PORT`: datagrams to it are handed to the design with their sender, and the design sends\n  datagrams to any MAC, IP and port (see `eth_udp`'s README for the handshakes).\n\nThe three senders share the MAC through a multiplexer that keeps one frame to its last byte (ARP replies first,\nthen echo replies, then the design's datagrams). `frame_good`, `frame_bad`, `arp_replied` and `ping_replied` pulse\nfor LEDs or counters. About three block RAMs (the echo's frame buffer and UDP's receive buffer, 2 KB each).\n\n```verilog\nlocalparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01;          // locally administered\nlocalparam [31:0] IP  = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222};\nwire mdio_out, mdio_oe;\nassign eth_mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\neth_udp_stack #(.PORT(16'd1234)) u_net (\n    .clk(eth_clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP),\n    .rmii_rxd(eth_rxd), .rmii_crs_dv(eth_crs_dv), .rmii_rx_er(eth_rx_er), .rmii_txd(eth_txd), .rmii_tx_en(eth_tx_en),\n    .mdc(eth_mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(eth_mdio),\n    .link_up(link_up), .frame_good(), .frame_bad(), .arp_replied(), .ping_replied(),\n    // an echo: what arrives goes back to its sender\n    .rx_data(udp_data), .rx_valid(udp_valid), .rx_last(), .rx_ready(payload_ready), .rx_length(udp_length),\n    .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(udp_new), .rx_dropped(),\n    .send(udp_new), .send_length(udp_length), .dst_mac(peer_mac), .dst_ip(peer_ip), .dst_port(peer_port),\n    .send_ready(), .payload_data(udp_data), .payload_valid(udp_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready)\n);\n```\n\nThe PHY's reset is the design's: hold it low for 10 ms from a clock that runs without the PHY (on the Dock the\n50 MHz comes from the PHY, so its reset runs on the board's 27 MHz `clk`).\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_rmii_phy\",\n    \"eth_udp_core\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"A small IPv4 stack for a 10/100 Ethernet PHY on RMII, in one core (eth_rmii_phy and eth_udp_core): PHY interface, MAC, MDIO setup (100 full duplex) and link watch, ARP and ping answered on their own, and UDP on one port for the design (received datagrams with their sender, datagrams sent anywhere). Everything on the PHY's 50 MHz; `mac` and `ip` are inputs. About three block RAMs.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 94.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DP16KD\" : 2,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 3295,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 2048\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 44.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 39,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 546,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 712,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 1814,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 1962,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 3781,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 39,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 106,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 53.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 1170,\n        \"BSRAM\" : 2,\n        \"DFF\" : 2048,\n        \"LUT4\" : 2836,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 138,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 21,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 4,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 28.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 3862,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 8,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 7\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 159.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 179,\n        \"RAMB18E1\" : 2,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 1961,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 2915\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_udp_stack\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"udp\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"arp\",\n    \"ping\",\n    \"icmp\",\n    \"rmii\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"stack\",\n    \"rtl8201\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein kleiner IPv4-Stack für einen 10/100-Ethernet-PHY an RMII, in einem Kern (eth_rmii_phy und eth_udp_core): PHY-Schnittstelle, MAC, MDIO-Einrichtung (100 Vollduplex) und Link-Überwachung, ARP und Ping beantwortet er selbst, und UDP auf einem Port für das Design (empfangene Datagramme mit Absender, gesendete an beliebige Ziele). Alles am 50-MHz-Takt des PHY; `mac` und `ip` sind Eingänge. Etwa drei Block-RAMs.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-UDP-Stack\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet UDP stack\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1234,\n      \"description\" : \"The UDP port received on and sent from\",\n      \"name\" : \"PORT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 50000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz (the PHY's reference)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's MDIO address (from its straps)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PHY_ADDR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 200,\n      \"description\" : \"Milliseconds before the first MDIO transaction\",\n      \"name\" : \"SETTLE_MS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's 50 MHz reference clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII RXD (the pins)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_rxd\",\n      \"width\" : 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII CRS_DV\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_crs_dv\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII RX_ER (tie low if absent)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_rx_er\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII TXD\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_txd\",\n      \"width\" : 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RMII TX_EN\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rmii_tx_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDC (the pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO's value while mdio_oe is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Drive MDIO (assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO as it is (the pin, with a pull-up)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY reports a link\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"link_up\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a good FCS (to anyone)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a bad FCS or RX_ER\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_bad\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an ARP reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"arp_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an echo reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ping_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A received datagram's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte is there\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the datagram's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The design takes the byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Bytes in the datagram handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram is handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_new\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram was dropped (the one before wasn't taken yet)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_dropped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Starts a datagram (with send_ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: payload bytes (0 … 1472)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A datagram can be started\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is there (within three clocks of the last)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is taken\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_udp_stack\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.2.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack/rtl/eth_udp_stack.v" : "// A small IPv4 stack for a 10/100 PHY on RMII: eth_rmii_phy and eth_udp_core (eth_mac and eth_mdio, with eth_arp and\n// eth_icmp_echo answering ARP and ping on their own, and eth_udp on port PORT for the design). Everything on the PHY's\n// 50 MHz reference clock. eth_udp_stack_mii is the same for MII PHYs.\n//\n// The three senders share the MAC through a multiplexer that takes one frame at a time and keeps it until its last\n// byte: ARP replies first, then echo replies, then the design's datagrams. MDIO advertises 100 full duplex\n// (eth_mdio's ADVERTISE) and reports `link_up`. `mac` and `ip` are inputs (constants, switches or a register).\n//\n// The design's side is eth_udp's (see there): received datagrams on rx_*/peer_*, datagrams to send on send_*/\n// payload_*. Pulses for LEDs and counters: `frame_good` (a frame with a good FCS, to anyone), `frame_bad` (a bad FCS\n// or RX_ER), `arp_replied`, `ping_replied`.\n\nmodule eth_udp_stack #(\n    parameter [15:0]  PORT = 16'd1234,\n    parameter integer CLK_HZ = 50_000_000,\n    parameter [4:0]   PHY_ADDR = 5'd1,\n    parameter integer SETTLE_MS = 200\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,          // the PHY's 50 MHz reference\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    // The PHY's pins\n    input  wire [1:0]  rmii_rxd,\n    input  wire        rmii_crs_dv,\n    input  wire        rmii_rx_er,\n    output wire [1:0]  rmii_txd,\n    output wire        rmii_tx_en,\n    output wire        mdc,\n    output wire        mdio_out,\n    output wire        mdio_oe,\n    input  wire        mdio_in,\n    // Status\n    output wire        link_up,\n    output wire        frame_good,\n    output wire        frame_bad,\n    output wire        arp_replied,\n    output wire        ping_replied,\n    // UDP: received datagrams\n    output wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    output wire        rx_valid,\n    output wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_ready,\n    output wire [10:0] rx_length,\n    output wire [47:0] peer_mac,\n    output wire [31:0] peer_ip,\n    output wire [15:0] peer_port,\n    output wire        rx_new,\n    output wire        rx_dropped,\n    // UDP: datagrams to send\n    input  wire        send,\n    input  wire [10:0] send_length,\n    input  wire [47:0] dst_mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] dst_ip,\n    input  wire [15:0] dst_port,\n    output wire        send_ready,\n    input  wire [7:0]  payload_data,\n    input  wire        payload_valid,\n    output wire        payload_ready\n);\n\n    // ---- The PHY ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] phy_rx_data, phy_tx_data;\n    wire       phy_rx_valid, phy_rx_end, phy_rx_error, phy_tx_valid, phy_tx_ready;\n    eth_rmii_phy u_phy (\n        .clk(clk), .rmii_rxd(rmii_rxd), .rmii_crs_dv(rmii_crs_dv), .rmii_rx_er(rmii_rx_er),\n        .rmii_txd(rmii_txd), .rmii_tx_en(rmii_tx_en),\n        .rx_data(phy_rx_data), .rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .rx_end(phy_rx_end), .rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n        .tx_data(phy_tx_data), .tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .tx_ready(phy_tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- MAC, MDIO, ARP, ping, UDP ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    eth_udp_core #(.PORT(PORT), .CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .PHY_ADDR(PHY_ADDR), .SETTLE_MS(SETTLE_MS)) u_core (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(mac), .ip(ip),\n        .phy_rx_data(phy_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(phy_rx_end), .phy_rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n        .phy_tx_data(phy_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(phy_tx_ready),\n        .mdc(mdc),\n        .mdio_out(mdio_out),\n        .mdio_oe(mdio_oe),\n        .mdio_in(mdio_in),\n        .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good),\n        .frame_bad(frame_bad),\n        .arp_replied(arp_replied),\n        .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(rx_data),\n        .rx_valid(rx_valid),\n        .rx_last(rx_last),\n        .rx_ready(rx_ready),\n        .rx_length(rx_length),\n        .peer_mac(peer_mac),\n        .peer_ip(peer_ip),\n        .peer_port(peer_port),\n        .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(rx_dropped),\n        .send(send),\n        .send_length(send_length),\n        .dst_mac(dst_mac),\n        .dst_ip(dst_ip),\n        .dst_port(dst_port),\n        .send_ready(send_ready),\n        .payload_data(payload_data),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid),\n        .payload_ready(payload_ready));\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack/tb/eth_udp_stack_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_udp_stack end to end over RMII: a host on the wire (frames with preamble and FCS,\n// outputs changed 4 ns after the reference clock's edge; everything sent back decoded, its FCS checked) and a PHY's\n// MDIO side (link up). The design is a UDP echo, wired as in eth_udp's README, plus a datagram of its own on demand.\n// Checks: MDIO setup and link_up; an ARP request answered; a ping answered with valid checksums; a UDP datagram echoed\n// to its sender; the design's own broadcast datagram sent while a ping is answered (both frames intact: the\n// multiplexer keeps a sender to its last byte); an ARP request and a ping arriving back to back both answered;\n// frame_bad for a bad FCS, frame_good for good frames. Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule stack_phy_mdio (input wire mdc, inout wire mdio);\n    reg [15:0] regs [0:31];\n    integer i, ones = 0, n = -1;\n    reg [1:0] op;\n    reg [4:0] pa, ra;\n    reg [15:0] data;\n    reg drive = 1'b0, value = 1'b1;\n    assign mdio = drive ? value : 1'bz;\n    initial begin\n        for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) regs[i] = 16'h0000;\n        regs[1] = 16'h782D; regs[2] = 16'h001C; regs[3] = 16'hC816;\n    end\n    always @(posedge mdc) begin : slave\n        reg b;\n        b = mdio;\n        if (n < 0) begin\n            if (b === 1'b1) ones = ones + 1;\n            else begin if (ones >= 32) n = 32; ones = 0; end\n        end else begin\n            n = n + 1;\n            if (n == 33 && b !== 1'b1) n = -1;\n            else if (n == 34 || n == 35) op = {op[0], b};\n            else if (n >= 36 && n <= 40) pa = {pa[3:0], b};\n            else if (n >= 41 && n <= 45) begin ra = {ra[3:0], b}; if (n == 45) data = regs[ra]; end\n            else if (op == 2'b10 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n == 46) #50 begin drive = 1'b1; value = 1'b0; end\n                else if (n >= 47 && n <= 62) #50 value = data[62 - n];\n                else if (n == 63) begin #50 drive = 1'b0; n = -1; end\n            end else if (op == 2'b01 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n >= 48) data = {data[14:0], b};\n                if (n == 63) begin regs[ra] = data; n = -1; end\n            end else if (n == 63) n = -1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule eth_udp_stack_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}, HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n\n    reg  [1:0]  rxd = 2'b00;\n    reg         crs_dv = 1'b0;\n    wire [1:0]  txd;\n    wire        tx_en, mdc, mdio_out, mdio_oe, mdio, link_up, frame_good, frame_bad, arp_replied, ping_replied;\n    pullup (mdio);\n    assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\n    stack_phy_mdio u_phy_mdio (.mdc(mdc), .mdio(mdio));\n\n    wire [7:0]  udp_data;\n    wire        udp_valid, udp_last, rx_new, payload_ready, send_ready;\n    wire [10:0] udp_length;\n    wire [47:0] peer_mac;\n    wire [31:0] peer_ip;\n    wire [15:0] peer_port;\n    reg         own = 1'b0;          // the design's own datagram instead of the echo\n    reg         own_send = 1'b0;\n    reg  [3:0]  own_index = 4'd0;\n    wire        send = own ? own_send : rx_new;\n    wire [7:0]  payload = own ? 8'hB0 + own_index : udp_data;\n    wire        payload_valid = own ? 1'b1 : udp_valid;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (own && payload_ready) own_index <= own_index + 4'd1;\n\n    eth_udp_stack #(.PORT(16'd1234), .SETTLE_MS(1)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP), .rmii_rxd(rxd), .rmii_crs_dv(crs_dv), .rmii_rx_er(1'b0), .rmii_txd(txd),\n        .rmii_tx_en(tx_en), .mdc(mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(mdio), .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good), .frame_bad(frame_bad), .arp_replied(arp_replied), .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(udp_data), .rx_valid(udp_valid), .rx_last(udp_last), .rx_ready(own ? 1'b0 : payload_ready),\n        .rx_length(udp_length), .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(), .send(send), .send_length(own ? 11'd10 : udp_length),\n        .dst_mac(own ? 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF : peer_mac), .dst_ip(own ? 32'h0A00_FFFF : peer_ip),\n        .dst_port(own ? 16'd4321 : peer_port), .send_ready(send_ready), .payload_data(payload),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready));\n\n    integer goods = 0, bads = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (frame_good) goods = goods + 1;\n        if (frame_bad) bads = bads + 1;\n    end\n\n    // ---- CRC and checksums ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    function [31:0] crc_update(input [31:0] c, input [7:0] d);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] x;\n        begin\n            x = c ^ {24'd0, d};\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) x = {1'b0, x[31:1]} ^ (x[0] ? 32'hEDB8_8320 : 32'd0);\n            crc_update = x;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function [15:0] fold(input [31:0] s);\n        reg [31:0] t;\n        begin\n            t = s;\n            while (t[31:16] != 0) t = t[15:0] + t[31:16];\n            fold = t[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- The host sending -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:1599];\n    integer frame_n;\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n    function [31:0] sum16(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {frame[i], (i + 1 < to) ? frame[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            sum16 = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task ipv4(input [7:0] proto, input integer payload_bytes);\n        begin\n            put48(0, MAC); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0800);\n            frame[14] = 8'h45; frame[15] = 8'h00; put16(16, 20 + payload_bytes); put16(18, 16'h3E01);\n            put16(20, 16'h4000); frame[22] = 8'd64; frame[23] = proto; put16(24, 16'h0000);\n            put32(26, HOST_IP); put32(30, IP);\n            put16(24, ~fold(sum16(14, 34)));\n            frame_n = 34 + payload_bytes;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_arp;\n        begin\n            put48(0, 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0806); put16(14, 16'h0001); put16(16, 16'h0800);\n            frame[18] = 8'd6; frame[19] = 8'd4; put16(20, 16'h0001); put48(22, HOST); put32(28, HOST_IP); put48(32, 48'h0);\n            put32(38, IP);\n            frame_n = 42;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_ping(input integer data_bytes);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd1, 8 + data_bytes);\n            frame[34] = 8'd8; frame[35] = 8'd0; put16(36, 16'h0000); put16(38, 16'h1234); put16(40, 16'h0007);\n            for (i = 0; i < data_bytes; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = i * 3 + 1;\n            put16(36, ~fold(sum16(34, 42 + data_bytes)));\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_udp(input integer n);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd17, 8 + n);\n            put16(34, 16'd50123); put16(36, 16'd1234); put16(38, 8 + n); put16(40, 16'h0000);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = (i * 41) ^ 8'h6C;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task put_dibit(input [1:0] d, input dv);\n        begin @(posedge clk); #4; rxd = d; crs_dv = dv; end\n    endtask\n    task transmit(input bad);\n        integer i, j, n;\n        reg [31:0] c;\n        reg [7:0] b;\n        begin\n            n = frame_n < 60 ? 60 : frame_n;\n            for (i = frame_n; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'h00;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, frame[i]);\n            c = ~c ^ (bad ? 32'h0001_0000 : 32'd0);\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < 31; i = i + 1) put_dibit(2'b01, 1'b1);\n            put_dibit(2'b11, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < n + 4; i = i + 1) begin\n                b = i < n ? frame[i] : c[8 * (i - n) +: 8];\n                for (j = 0; j < 4; j = j + 1) put_dibit(b[2 * j +: 2], 1'b1);\n            end\n            put_dibit(2'b00, 1'b0);\n            repeat (30) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- What comes back: frames in a list, each with its FCS checked ------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] back [0:7][0:1599];\n    integer back_len [0:7];\n    integer backs = 0, dibits = 0, raw_n = 0;\n    reg [7:0] shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] raw [0:1607];\n    reg tx_en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (tx_en) begin\n            shift = {txd, shift[7:2]};\n            dibits = dibits + 1;\n            if (dibits % 4 == 0) begin raw[raw_n] = shift; raw_n = raw_n + 1; end\n        end else if (tx_en_was) begin : done\n            integer i;\n            reg [31:0] c;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 8; i < raw_n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, raw[i]);\n            if (c !== 32'hDEBB_20E3 || raw[7] !== 8'hD5) fail(\"a frame sent with a bad FCS or preamble\");\n            if (backs < 8) begin\n                for (i = 8; i < raw_n - 4; i = i + 1) back[backs][i - 8] = raw[i];\n                back_len[backs] = raw_n - 12;\n            end\n            backs = backs + 1;\n            raw_n = 0;\n            dibits = 0;\n        end\n        tx_en_was = tx_en;\n    end\n    function [15:0] b16(input integer f, input integer at); b16 = {back[f][at], back[f][at + 1]}; endfunction\n    function [31:0] bsum(input integer f, input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {back[f][i], (i + 1 < to) ? back[f][i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            bsum = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task wait_backs(input integer n);\n        integer t;\n        begin\n            t = 0;\n            while (backs < n && t < 40000) begin @(posedge clk); t = t + 1; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Finds the frame sent back with this EtherType and IP protocol (0 for ARP), or -1.\n    function integer find(input [15:0] ethertype, input [7:0] proto);\n        integer f;\n        begin\n            find = -1;\n            for (f = 0; f < backs && f < 8; f = f + 1)\n                if (b16(f, 12) == ethertype && (ethertype == 16'h0806 || back[f][23] == proto) && find < 0) find = f;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    integer f, i, wrong;\n    initial begin\n        wait (link_up === 1'b1);\n        if (u_phy_mdio.regs[4] !== 16'h0101) fail(\"MDIO didn't advertise 100 full duplex\");\n\n        // ARP.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0806, 8'd0);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no ARP reply\");\n        else if (b16(f, 20) !== 16'h0002 || {back[f][28], back[f][29], back[f][30], back[f][31]} !== IP) fail(\"the ARP reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // Ping.\n        make_ping(56); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no echo reply\");\n        else if (back[f][34] !== 8'd0 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF || fold(bsum(f, 34, 98)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // UDP echo.\n        make_udp(37); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no UDP echo\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 37; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== (((i * 41) ^ 8'h6C) & 8'hFF)) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd50123 || b16(f, 38) !== 16'd45 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF)\n                fail(\"the UDP echo\");\n        end\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // The design's own datagram while a ping is answered: both intact.\n        own = 1'b1;\n        make_ping(200);\n        fork\n            transmit(1'b0);\n            begin\n                // Once the echo reply is going out (its tx_valid drops between bytes), the design sends: the datagram\n                // must wait for the reply's last byte.\n                wait (dut.u_core.sel == 2'd2 && dut.u_core.u_mac.state == 3'd2);\n                repeat (40) @(posedge clk);\n                @(negedge clk); own_send = 1'b1; @(posedge clk); #1; own_send = 1'b0;\n            end\n        join\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (backs != 2) fail(\"the design's datagram and the echo reply: not both sent\");\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"the design's datagram\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== 8'hB0 + i) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 0) !== 16'hFFFF || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd4321) fail(\"the design's datagram's contents\");\n        end\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0 || fold(bsum(f, 34, 242)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply next to the datagram\");\n        own = 1'b0;\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // ARP and ping back to back.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0);\n        make_ping(20); transmit(1'b0);\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (find(16'h0806, 8'd0) < 0 || find(16'h0800, 8'd1) < 0) fail(\"ARP and ping back to back\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // A bad FCS.\n        goods = 0; bads = 0;\n        make_ping(10); transmit(1'b1);\n        repeat (2000) @(posedge clk);\n        if (bads != 1 || goods != 0 || backs != 0) fail(\"a bad FCS\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_mii/README.md" : "# Ethernet UDP stack (MII)\n\n`eth_udp_stack` for a 10/100 PHY on MII (the Arty A7-35T's TI DP83848J): `eth_mii_phy` and `eth_udp_core` — MAC, MDIO\nsetup and link watch, ARP and ping on their own, and UDP on port `PORT` for the design, with the same design-side ports\nas `eth_udp_stack` (see `eth_udp`'s README for the handshakes).\n\nThe byte side runs on `clk`, at least four times the PHY's 25 MHz nibble clocks — the Arty's 100 MHz clock; the MII\nadapter crosses to `mii_rx_clk` and `mii_tx_clk` through FIFOs. The PHY's own 25 MHz reference is the design's to\nmake (on the Arty: `eth_ref_clk`, the 100 MHz clock divided by 4), and so is its reset.\n\n```verilog\nreg [1:0] ref_div = 0;\nalways @(posedge clk) ref_div <= ref_div + 1'b1;\nassign eth_ref_clk = ref_div[1];                         // 100 MHz / 4\nwire mdio_out, mdio_oe;\nassign eth_mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\neth_udp_stack_mii #(.PORT(16'd1234), .CLK_HZ(100_000_000)) u_net (\n    .clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP),\n    .mii_rx_clk(eth_rx_clk), .mii_rxd(eth_rxd), .mii_rx_dv(eth_rx_dv), .mii_rx_er(eth_rxerr),\n    .mii_tx_clk(eth_tx_clk), .mii_txd(eth_txd), .mii_tx_en(eth_tx_en),\n    .mdc(eth_mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(eth_mdio),\n    /* status and UDP as eth_udp_stack's */ ...\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_mii/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_mii_phy\",\n    \"eth_udp_core\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"A small IPv4 stack for a 10/100 Ethernet PHY on MII (the Arty A7's DP83848J), in one core (eth_mii_phy and eth_udp_core): MAC, MDIO setup (100 full duplex) and link watch, ARP and ping answered on their own, and UDP on one port for the design. The byte side on a system clock of at least four times the PHY's 25 MHz (100 MHz); the PHY's own reference is the design's to make. The same design-side ports as eth_udp_stack.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 83.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DP16KD\" : 2,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 3475,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 2173,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 6\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 40.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 44,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 557,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 796,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 2033,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 2249,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 4216,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 86,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 170,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 56.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 1216,\n        \"BSRAM\" : 2,\n        \"DFF\" : 2173,\n        \"LUT4\" : 3098,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 189,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 37,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 13,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 3,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 6\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 28.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 4118,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 10,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 7\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 139,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 212,\n        \"RAMB18E1\" : 2,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 2038,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 3258\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"eth_udp_stack_mii\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"udp\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"arp\",\n    \"ping\",\n    \"icmp\",\n    \"mii\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"stack\",\n    \"dp83848\",\n    \"arty\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein kleiner IPv4-Stack für einen 10/100-Ethernet-PHY an MII (der DP83848J des Arty A7), in einem Kern (eth_mii_phy und eth_udp_core): MAC, MDIO-Einrichtung (100 Vollduplex) und Link-Überwachung, ARP und Ping beantwortet er selbst, und UDP auf einem Port für das Design. Die Byte-Seite an einem Systemtakt von mindestens dem Vierfachen der 25 MHz des PHY (100 MHz); den Referenztakt des PHY erzeugt das Design. Dieselben Ports auf der Design-Seite wie eth_udp_stack.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-UDP-Stack (MII)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet UDP stack (MII)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1234,\n      \"description\" : \"The UDP port received on and sent from\",\n      \"name\" : \"PORT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 100000000,\n      \"description\" : \"The system clock in Hz (at least 4 × the nibble clock)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's MDIO address (from its straps)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PHY_ADDR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 200,\n      \"description\" : \"Milliseconds before the first MDIO transaction\",\n      \"name\" : \"SETTLE_MS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The system clock (100 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RX_CLK from the PHY (25 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RXD (the pins)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rxd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RX_DV\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rx_dv\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII RX_ER\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_rx_er\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII TX_CLK from the PHY (25 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_tx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII TXD\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_txd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MII TX_EN\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mii_tx_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDC (the pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO's value while mdio_oe is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Drive MDIO (assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO as it is (the pin, with a pull-up)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY reports a link\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"link_up\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a good FCS (to anyone)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a bad FCS or RX_ER\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_bad\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an ARP reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"arp_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an echo reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ping_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A received datagram's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte is there\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the datagram's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The design takes the byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Bytes in the datagram handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram is handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_new\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram was dropped (the one before wasn't taken yet)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_dropped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Starts a datagram (with send_ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: payload bytes (0 … 1472)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A datagram can be started\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is there (within three clocks of the last)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is taken\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_udp_stack_mii\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_mii/rtl/eth_udp_stack_mii.v" : "// A small IPv4 stack for a 10/100 PHY on MII (the Arty A7's DP83848J): eth_mii_phy and eth_udp_core (eth_mac and\n// eth_mdio, with eth_arp and eth_icmp_echo answering ARP and ping on their own, and eth_udp on port PORT for the design).\n// The byte side runs on `clk`, at least four times the PHY's 25 MHz nibble clocks (100 MHz); eth_mii_phy crosses to\n// mii_rx_clk and mii_tx_clk. The PHY's own 25 MHz reference (eth_ref_clk on the Arty) is the design's to make.\n//\n// The design's side is eth_udp's (see there), the same as eth_udp_stack's: received datagrams on rx_*/peer_*, datagrams to\n// send on send_*/payload_*. Pulses for LEDs and counters: `frame_good`, `frame_bad`, `arp_replied`, `ping_replied`.\n\nmodule eth_udp_stack_mii #(\n    parameter [15:0]  PORT = 16'd1234,\n    parameter integer CLK_HZ = 100_000_000,\n    parameter [4:0]   PHY_ADDR = 5'd1,\n    parameter integer SETTLE_MS = 200\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,          // the system clock: at least 4 × the nibble clock (100 MHz)\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    // The PHY's pins\n    input  wire        mii_rx_clk,\n    input  wire [3:0]  mii_rxd,\n    input  wire        mii_rx_dv,\n    input  wire        mii_rx_er,\n    input  wire        mii_tx_clk,\n    output wire [3:0]  mii_txd,\n    output wire        mii_tx_en,\n    output wire        mdc,\n    output wire        mdio_out,\n    output wire        mdio_oe,\n    input  wire        mdio_in,\n    // Status\n    output wire        link_up,\n    output wire        frame_good,\n    output wire        frame_bad,\n    output wire        arp_replied,\n    output wire        ping_replied,\n    // UDP: received datagrams\n    output wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    output wire        rx_valid,\n    output wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_ready,\n    output wire [10:0] rx_length,\n    output wire [47:0] peer_mac,\n    output wire [31:0] peer_ip,\n    output wire [15:0] peer_port,\n    output wire        rx_new,\n    output wire        rx_dropped,\n    // UDP: datagrams to send\n    input  wire        send,\n    input  wire [10:0] send_length,\n    input  wire [47:0] dst_mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] dst_ip,\n    input  wire [15:0] dst_port,\n    output wire        send_ready,\n    input  wire [7:0]  payload_data,\n    input  wire        payload_valid,\n    output wire        payload_ready\n);\n\n    // ---- The PHY ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] phy_rx_data, phy_tx_data;\n    wire       phy_rx_valid, phy_rx_end, phy_rx_error, phy_tx_valid, phy_tx_ready;\n    eth_mii_phy u_phy (\n        .clk(clk), .mii_rx_clk(mii_rx_clk), .mii_rxd(mii_rxd), .mii_rx_dv(mii_rx_dv), .mii_rx_er(mii_rx_er),\n        .mii_tx_clk(mii_tx_clk), .mii_txd(mii_txd), .mii_tx_en(mii_tx_en),\n        .rx_data(phy_rx_data), .rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .rx_end(phy_rx_end), .rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n        .tx_data(phy_tx_data), .tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .tx_ready(phy_tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- MAC, MDIO, ARP, ping, UDP ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    eth_udp_core #(.PORT(PORT), .CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .PHY_ADDR(PHY_ADDR), .SETTLE_MS(SETTLE_MS)) u_core (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(mac), .ip(ip),\n        .phy_rx_data(phy_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(phy_rx_end), .phy_rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n        .phy_tx_data(phy_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(phy_tx_ready),\n        .mdc(mdc),\n        .mdio_out(mdio_out),\n        .mdio_oe(mdio_oe),\n        .mdio_in(mdio_in),\n        .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good),\n        .frame_bad(frame_bad),\n        .arp_replied(arp_replied),\n        .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(rx_data),\n        .rx_valid(rx_valid),\n        .rx_last(rx_last),\n        .rx_ready(rx_ready),\n        .rx_length(rx_length),\n        .peer_mac(peer_mac),\n        .peer_ip(peer_ip),\n        .peer_port(peer_port),\n        .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(rx_dropped),\n        .send(send),\n        .send_length(send_length),\n        .dst_mac(dst_mac),\n        .dst_ip(dst_ip),\n        .dst_port(dst_port),\n        .send_ready(send_ready),\n        .payload_data(payload_data),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid),\n        .payload_ready(payload_ready));\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_mii/tb/eth_udp_stack_mii_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_udp_stack_mii end to end over MII: a host on the wire through a model of the PHY's\n// MII side (rx_clk at 25 MHz and tx_clk at 24.98 MHz with another phase, received nibbles changed 10 ns after rx_clk\n// rises; everything sent back decoded on tx_clk, its FCS checked) and the PHY's MDIO side (link up), with the stack on\n// a 100 MHz system clock. The design is a UDP echo, wired as in eth_udp's README, plus a datagram of its own on demand.\n// Checks as eth_udp_stack's: MDIO setup and link_up; ARP; ping; a UDP echo; the design's own broadcast datagram while\n// a ping is answered (both intact); ARP and ping back to back; frame_bad for a bad FCS. Prints PASS or every failed\n// check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule stack_mii_phy_mdio (input wire mdc, inout wire mdio);\n    reg [15:0] regs [0:31];\n    integer i, ones = 0, n = -1;\n    reg [1:0] op;\n    reg [4:0] pa, ra;\n    reg [15:0] data;\n    reg drive = 1'b0, value = 1'b1;\n    assign mdio = drive ? value : 1'bz;\n    initial begin\n        for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) regs[i] = 16'h0000;\n        regs[1] = 16'h782D; regs[2] = 16'h001C; regs[3] = 16'hC816;\n    end\n    always @(posedge mdc) begin : slave\n        reg b;\n        b = mdio;\n        if (n < 0) begin\n            if (b === 1'b1) ones = ones + 1;\n            else begin if (ones >= 32) n = 32; ones = 0; end\n        end else begin\n            n = n + 1;\n            if (n == 33 && b !== 1'b1) n = -1;\n            else if (n == 34 || n == 35) op = {op[0], b};\n            else if (n >= 36 && n <= 40) pa = {pa[3:0], b};\n            else if (n >= 41 && n <= 45) begin ra = {ra[3:0], b}; if (n == 45) data = regs[ra]; end\n            else if (op == 2'b10 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n == 46) #50 begin drive = 1'b1; value = 1'b0; end\n                else if (n >= 47 && n <= 62) #50 value = data[62 - n];\n                else if (n == 63) begin #50 drive = 1'b0; n = -1; end\n            end else if (op == 2'b01 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n >= 48) data = {data[14:0], b};\n                if (n == 63) begin regs[ra] = data; n = -1; end\n            end else if (n == 63) n = -1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule eth_udp_stack_mii_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0, rx_clk = 1'b0, tx_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;                  // the system clock, 100 MHz\n    always #20 rx_clk = ~rx_clk;           // 25 MHz\n    initial begin #7; forever #20.016 tx_clk = ~tx_clk; end   // 24.98 MHz, another phase\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}, HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n\n    reg  [3:0]  rxd = 4'd0;\n    reg         rx_dv = 1'b0;\n    wire [3:0]  txd;\n    wire        tx_en, mdc, mdio_out, mdio_oe, mdio, link_up, frame_good, frame_bad, arp_replied, ping_replied;\n    pullup (mdio);\n    assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\n    stack_mii_phy_mdio u_phy_mdio (.mdc(mdc), .mdio(mdio));\n\n    wire [7:0]  udp_data;\n    wire        udp_valid, udp_last, rx_new, payload_ready, send_ready;\n    wire [10:0] udp_length;\n    wire [47:0] peer_mac;\n    wire [31:0] peer_ip;\n    wire [15:0] peer_port;\n    reg         own = 1'b0;          // the design's own datagram instead of the echo\n    reg         own_send = 1'b0;\n    reg  [3:0]  own_index = 4'd0;\n    wire        send = own ? own_send : rx_new;\n    wire [7:0]  payload = own ? 8'hB0 + own_index : udp_data;\n    wire        payload_valid = own ? 1'b1 : udp_valid;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (own && payload_ready) own_index <= own_index + 4'd1;\n\n    eth_udp_stack_mii #(.PORT(16'd1234), .SETTLE_MS(1)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP), .mii_rx_clk(rx_clk), .mii_rxd(rxd), .mii_rx_dv(rx_dv), .mii_rx_er(1'b0),\n        .mii_tx_clk(tx_clk), .mii_txd(txd), .mii_tx_en(tx_en), .mdc(mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(mdio), .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good), .frame_bad(frame_bad), .arp_replied(arp_replied), .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(udp_data), .rx_valid(udp_valid), .rx_last(udp_last), .rx_ready(own ? 1'b0 : payload_ready),\n        .rx_length(udp_length), .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(), .send(send), .send_length(own ? 11'd10 : udp_length),\n        .dst_mac(own ? 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF : peer_mac), .dst_ip(own ? 32'h0A00_FFFF : peer_ip),\n        .dst_port(own ? 16'd4321 : peer_port), .send_ready(send_ready), .payload_data(payload),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready));\n\n    integer goods = 0, bads = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (frame_good) goods = goods + 1;\n        if (frame_bad) bads = bads + 1;\n    end\n\n    // ---- CRC and checksums ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    function [31:0] crc_update(input [31:0] c, input [7:0] d);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] x;\n        begin\n            x = c ^ {24'd0, d};\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) x = {1'b0, x[31:1]} ^ (x[0] ? 32'hEDB8_8320 : 32'd0);\n            crc_update = x;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function [15:0] fold(input [31:0] s);\n        reg [31:0] t;\n        begin\n            t = s;\n            while (t[31:16] != 0) t = t[15:0] + t[31:16];\n            fold = t[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- The host sending -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:1599];\n    integer frame_n;\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n    function [31:0] sum16(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {frame[i], (i + 1 < to) ? frame[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            sum16 = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task ipv4(input [7:0] proto, input integer payload_bytes);\n        begin\n            put48(0, MAC); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0800);\n            frame[14] = 8'h45; frame[15] = 8'h00; put16(16, 20 + payload_bytes); put16(18, 16'h3E01);\n            put16(20, 16'h4000); frame[22] = 8'd64; frame[23] = proto; put16(24, 16'h0000);\n            put32(26, HOST_IP); put32(30, IP);\n            put16(24, ~fold(sum16(14, 34)));\n            frame_n = 34 + payload_bytes;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_arp;\n        begin\n            put48(0, 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0806); put16(14, 16'h0001); put16(16, 16'h0800);\n            frame[18] = 8'd6; frame[19] = 8'd4; put16(20, 16'h0001); put48(22, HOST); put32(28, HOST_IP); put48(32, 48'h0);\n            put32(38, IP);\n            frame_n = 42;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_ping(input integer data_bytes);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd1, 8 + data_bytes);\n            frame[34] = 8'd8; frame[35] = 8'd0; put16(36, 16'h0000); put16(38, 16'h1234); put16(40, 16'h0007);\n            for (i = 0; i < data_bytes; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = i * 3 + 1;\n            put16(36, ~fold(sum16(34, 42 + data_bytes)));\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_udp(input integer n);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd17, 8 + n);\n            put16(34, 16'd50123); put16(36, 16'd1234); put16(38, 8 + n); put16(40, 16'h0000);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = (i * 41) ^ 8'h6C;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task put_nibble(input [3:0] v, input dv);\n        begin @(posedge rx_clk); #10; rxd = v; rx_dv = dv; end\n    endtask\n    task transmit(input bad);\n        integer i, n;\n        reg [31:0] c;\n        reg [7:0] b;\n        begin\n            n = frame_n < 60 ? 60 : frame_n;\n            for (i = frame_n; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'h00;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, frame[i]);\n            c = ~c ^ (bad ? 32'h0001_0000 : 32'd0);\n            for (i = 0; i < 15; i = i + 1) put_nibble(4'h5, 1'b1);\n            put_nibble(4'hD, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < n + 4; i = i + 1) begin\n                b = i < n ? frame[i] : c[8 * (i - n) +: 8];\n                put_nibble(b[3:0], 1'b1);\n                put_nibble(b[7:4], 1'b1);\n            end\n            put_nibble(4'h0, 1'b0);\n            repeat (12) @(posedge rx_clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- What comes back: frames in a list, each with its FCS checked ------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] back [0:7][0:1599];\n    integer back_len [0:7];\n    integer backs = 0, nibbles = 0, raw_n = 0;\n    reg [7:0] shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] raw [0:1607];\n    reg tx_en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge tx_clk) begin\n        if (tx_en) begin\n            shift = {txd, shift[7:4]};\n            nibbles = nibbles + 1;\n            if (nibbles % 2 == 0) begin raw[raw_n] = shift; raw_n = raw_n + 1; end\n        end else if (tx_en_was) begin : done\n            integer i;\n            reg [31:0] c;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 8; i < raw_n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, raw[i]);\n            if (c !== 32'hDEBB_20E3 || raw[7] !== 8'hD5) fail(\"a frame sent with a bad FCS or preamble\");\n            if (backs < 8) begin\n                for (i = 8; i < raw_n - 4; i = i + 1) back[backs][i - 8] = raw[i];\n                back_len[backs] = raw_n - 12;\n            end\n            backs = backs + 1;\n            raw_n = 0;\n            nibbles = 0;\n        end\n        tx_en_was = tx_en;\n    end\n    function [15:0] b16(input integer f, input integer at); b16 = {back[f][at], back[f][at + 1]}; endfunction\n    function [31:0] bsum(input integer f, input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {back[f][i], (i + 1 < to) ? back[f][i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            bsum = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task wait_backs(input integer n);\n        integer t;\n        begin\n            t = 0;\n            while (backs < n && t < 80000) begin @(posedge clk); t = t + 1; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Finds the frame sent back with this EtherType and IP protocol (0 for ARP), or -1.\n    function integer find(input [15:0] ethertype, input [7:0] proto);\n        integer f;\n        begin\n            find = -1;\n            for (f = 0; f < backs && f < 8; f = f + 1)\n                if (b16(f, 12) == ethertype && (ethertype == 16'h0806 || back[f][23] == proto) && find < 0) find = f;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    integer f, i, wrong;\n    initial begin\n        wait (link_up === 1'b1);\n        if (u_phy_mdio.regs[4] !== 16'h0101) fail(\"MDIO didn't advertise 100 full duplex\");\n\n        // ARP.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0806, 8'd0);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no ARP reply\");\n        else if (b16(f, 20) !== 16'h0002 || {back[f][28], back[f][29], back[f][30], back[f][31]} !== IP) fail(\"the ARP reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // Ping.\n        make_ping(56); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no echo reply\");\n        else if (back[f][34] !== 8'd0 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF || fold(bsum(f, 34, 98)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // UDP echo.\n        make_udp(37); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no UDP echo\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 37; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== (((i * 41) ^ 8'h6C) & 8'hFF)) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd50123 || b16(f, 38) !== 16'd45 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF)\n                fail(\"the UDP echo\");\n        end\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // The design's own datagram while a ping is answered: both intact.\n        own = 1'b1;\n        make_ping(200);\n        fork\n            transmit(1'b0);\n            begin\n                // Once the echo reply is going out (its tx_valid drops between bytes), the design sends: the datagram\n                // must wait for the reply's last byte.\n                wait (dut.u_core.sel == 2'd2 && dut.u_core.u_mac.state == 3'd2);\n                repeat (40) @(posedge clk);\n                @(negedge clk); own_send = 1'b1; @(posedge clk); #1; own_send = 1'b0;\n            end\n        join\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (backs != 2) fail(\"the design's datagram and the echo reply: not both sent\");\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"the design's datagram\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== 8'hB0 + i) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 0) !== 16'hFFFF || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd4321) fail(\"the design's datagram's contents\");\n        end\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0 || fold(bsum(f, 34, 242)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply next to the datagram\");\n        own = 1'b0;\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // ARP and ping back to back.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0);\n        make_ping(20); transmit(1'b0);\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (find(16'h0806, 8'd0) < 0 || find(16'h0800, 8'd1) < 0) fail(\"ARP and ping back to back\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // A bad FCS.\n        goods = 0; bads = 0;\n        make_ping(10); transmit(1'b1);\n        repeat (2000) @(posedge clk);\n        if (bads != 1 || goods != 0 || backs != 0) fail(\"a bad FCS\");\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_rgmii/README.md" : "# Ethernet UDP stack (RGMII)\n\n`eth_udp_stack` for a Gigabit PHY on RGMII at 1000 Mbit/s (the Kintex-7 Eco R5's two Realtek RTL8211E): `eth_rgmii_phy`\nand `eth_udp_core` — MAC, MDIO setup and link watch, ARP and ping on their own, and UDP on port `PORT` for the design,\nwith the same design-side ports as `eth_udp_stack` (see `eth_udp`'s README for the handshakes). AMD Xilinx 7-series only.\n\nEvery part runs a byte a clock on `clk`, 125 MHz from an MMCM or PLL. At this speed the MAC asks for a payload byte on\nevery clock, so a design that sends must have the next byte ready when `payload_ready` asks (a block RAM read a clock\nahead, or `eth_udp`'s own received datagram for an echo). MDIO advertises no 10/100 mode: the link comes up at 1000 or\nnot at all. The PHY's reset is the design's to drive (on the Eco R5: `eth_rst_n`, high to run).\n\n```verilog\nwire mdio_out, mdio_oe;\nassign eth_mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\neth_udp_stack_rgmii #(.PORT(16'd1234)) u_net (\n    .clk(clk125), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP),\n    .rgmii_rx_clk(eth_rx_clk), .rgmii_rxd(eth_rxd), .rgmii_rx_ctl(eth_rx_ctl),\n    .rgmii_tx_clk(eth_tx_clk), .rgmii_txd(eth_txd), .rgmii_tx_ctl(eth_tx_ctl),\n    .mdc(eth_mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(eth_mdio),\n    /* status and UDP as eth_udp_stack's */ ...\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_rgmii/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"eth_rgmii_phy\",\n    \"eth_udp_core\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"A small IPv4 stack for a Gigabit Ethernet PHY on RGMII at 1000 Mbit/s (the Kintex-7 Eco R5's RTL8211E), in one core (eth_rgmii_phy and eth_udp_core): MAC, MDIO setup (1000 only) and link watch, ARP and ping answered on their own, and UDP on one port for the design, every part a byte a clock. The byte side on a 125 MHz system clock. AMD Xilinx 7-series only. The same design-side ports as eth_udp_stack.\",\n  \"id\" : \"eth_udp_stack_rgmii\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ethernet\",\n    \"udp\",\n    \"ipv4\",\n    \"arp\",\n    \"ping\",\n    \"icmp\",\n    \"rgmii\",\n    \"gigabit\",\n    \"network\",\n    \"stack\",\n    \"rtl8211e\",\n    \"kintex\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein kleiner IPv4-Stack für einen Gigabit-Ethernet-PHY an RGMII bei 1000 Mbit/s (der RTL8211E des Kintex-7 Eco R5), in einem Kern (eth_rgmii_phy und eth_udp_core): MAC, MDIO-Einrichtung (nur 1000) und Link-Überwachung, ARP und Ping beantwortet er selbst, und UDP auf einem Port für das Design, jeder Teil ein Byte je Takt. Die Byte-Seite an einem 125-MHz-Systemtakt. Nur AMD Xilinx 7-Serie. Dieselben Ports auf der Design-Seite wie eth_udp_stack.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Ethernet-UDP-Stack (RGMII)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Ethernet UDP stack (RGMII)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1234,\n      \"description\" : \"The UDP port received on and sent from\",\n      \"name\" : \"PORT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 125000000,\n      \"description\" : \"The system clock in Hz (125 MHz)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY's MDIO address (from its straps)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PHY_ADDR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 200,\n      \"description\" : \"Milliseconds before the first MDIO transaction\",\n      \"name\" : \"SETTLE_MS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"eth_rgmii_phy's: which clock edge starts a received byte\",\n      \"name\" : \"RX_LATE\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The system clock (125 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our MAC address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Our IPv4 address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII RX_CLK from the PHY (125 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_rx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII RXD (the pins)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_rxd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII RX_CTL\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_rx_ctl\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII TX_CLK\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_tx_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII TXD\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_txd\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"RGMII TX_CTL\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgmii_tx_ctl\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDC (the pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO's value while mdio_oe is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Drive MDIO (assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"MDIO as it is (the pin, with a pull-up)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mdio_in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The PHY reports a link\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"link_up\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a good FCS (to anyone)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_good\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses for each frame with a bad FCS or an error from the PHY\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_bad\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an ARP reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"arp_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when an echo reply is queued\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ping_replied\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A received datagram's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A byte is there\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With the datagram's last byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The design takes the byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Bytes in the datagram handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sender's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"peer_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram is handed over\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_new\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a datagram was dropped (the one before wasn't taken yet)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_dropped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Starts a datagram (with send_ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: payload bytes (0 … 1472)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_length\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's MAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_mac\",\n      \"width\" : 48\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's IP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_ip\",\n      \"width\" : 32\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"With send: the receiver's port\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dst_port\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A datagram can be started\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"send_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is there when payload_ready asks for it (every clock at 1000 Mbit/s)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The payload's byte is taken\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"payload_ready\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"requiresFamilies\" : [\n    \"xilinx7\"\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"eth_udp_stack_rgmii\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_rgmii/rtl/eth_udp_stack_rgmii.v" : "// A small IPv4 stack for a Gigabit PHY on RGMII at 1000 Mbit/s (the Kintex-7 Eco R5's RTL8211E, both RGMII delays in\n// the PHY): eth_rgmii_phy and eth_udp_core (eth_mac and eth_mdio, with eth_arp and eth_icmp_echo answering ARP and ping on\n// their own, and eth_udp on port PORT for the design), every part a byte a clock. AMD Xilinx 7-series only. The byte side\n// runs on `clk`, 125 MHz (an MMCM from the board's clock); eth_rgmii_phy crosses from rgmii_rx_clk. MDIO advertises no\n// 10/100 mode (register 4 = 0001): the link comes up at 1000 with the PHY's own register 9.\n//\n// The design's side is eth_udp's (see there), the same as eth_udp_stack's: received datagrams on rx_*/peer_*, datagrams to\n// send on send_*/payload_*. Pulses for LEDs and counters: `frame_good`, `frame_bad`, `arp_replied`, `ping_replied`.\n\nmodule eth_udp_stack_rgmii #(\n    parameter [15:0]  PORT = 16'd1234,\n    parameter integer CLK_HZ = 125_000_000,\n    parameter [4:0]   PHY_ADDR = 5'd1,\n    parameter integer SETTLE_MS = 200,\n    parameter         RX_LATE = 1          // eth_rgmii_phy's: which edge starts a received byte\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,          // 125 MHz\n    input  wire [47:0] mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] ip,\n    // The PHY's pins\n    input  wire        rgmii_rx_clk,\n    input  wire [3:0]  rgmii_rxd,\n    input  wire        rgmii_rx_ctl,\n    output wire        rgmii_tx_clk,\n    output wire [3:0]  rgmii_txd,\n    output wire        rgmii_tx_ctl,\n    output wire        mdc,\n    output wire        mdio_out,\n    output wire        mdio_oe,\n    input  wire        mdio_in,\n    // Status\n    output wire        link_up,\n    output wire        frame_good,\n    output wire        frame_bad,\n    output wire        arp_replied,\n    output wire        ping_replied,\n    // UDP: received datagrams\n    output wire [7:0]  rx_data,\n    output wire        rx_valid,\n    output wire        rx_last,\n    input  wire        rx_ready,\n    output wire [10:0] rx_length,\n    output wire [47:0] peer_mac,\n    output wire [31:0] peer_ip,\n    output wire [15:0] peer_port,\n    output wire        rx_new,\n    output wire        rx_dropped,\n    // UDP: datagrams to send\n    input  wire        send,\n    input  wire [10:0] send_length,\n    input  wire [47:0] dst_mac,\n    input  wire [31:0] dst_ip,\n    input  wire [15:0] dst_port,\n    output wire        send_ready,\n    input  wire [7:0]  payload_data,\n    input  wire        payload_valid,\n    output wire        payload_ready\n);\n\n    // ---- The PHY ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] phy_rx_data, phy_tx_data;\n    wire       phy_rx_valid, phy_rx_end, phy_rx_error, phy_tx_valid, phy_tx_ready;\n    eth_rgmii_phy #(.RX_LATE(RX_LATE)) u_phy (\n        .clk(clk), .rgmii_rx_clk(rgmii_rx_clk), .rgmii_rxd(rgmii_rxd), .rgmii_rx_ctl(rgmii_rx_ctl),\n        .rgmii_tx_clk(rgmii_tx_clk), .rgmii_txd(rgmii_txd), .rgmii_tx_ctl(rgmii_tx_ctl),\n        .rx_data(phy_rx_data), .rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .rx_end(phy_rx_end), .rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n        .tx_data(phy_tx_data), .tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .tx_ready(phy_tx_ready));\n\n    // ---- MAC, MDIO, ARP, ping, UDP ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    eth_udp_core #(.PORT(PORT), .CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .PHY_ADDR(PHY_ADDR), .SETTLE_MS(SETTLE_MS),\n        .ADVERTISE(16'h0001), .GAP_CLOCKS(12)) u_core (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(mac), .ip(ip),\n        .phy_rx_data(phy_rx_data), .phy_rx_valid(phy_rx_valid), .phy_rx_end(phy_rx_end), .phy_rx_error(phy_rx_error),\n        .phy_tx_data(phy_tx_data), .phy_tx_valid(phy_tx_valid), .phy_tx_ready(phy_tx_ready),\n        .mdc(mdc),\n        .mdio_out(mdio_out),\n        .mdio_oe(mdio_oe),\n        .mdio_in(mdio_in),\n        .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good),\n        .frame_bad(frame_bad),\n        .arp_replied(arp_replied),\n        .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(rx_data),\n        .rx_valid(rx_valid),\n        .rx_last(rx_last),\n        .rx_ready(rx_ready),\n        .rx_length(rx_length),\n        .peer_mac(peer_mac),\n        .peer_ip(peer_ip),\n        .peer_port(peer_port),\n        .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(rx_dropped),\n        .send(send),\n        .send_length(send_length),\n        .dst_mac(dst_mac),\n        .dst_ip(dst_ip),\n        .dst_port(dst_port),\n        .send_ready(send_ready),\n        .payload_data(payload_data),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid),\n        .payload_ready(payload_ready));\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/eth_udp_stack_rgmii/tb/eth_udp_stack_rgmii_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for eth_udp_stack_rgmii end to end over RGMII at 1000 Mbit/s: a host on the wire through a model\n// of the PHY's RGMII side (rx_clk at 125.01 MHz with another phase, a nibble on each edge with eth_rgmii_phy's RX_LATE\n// timing; everything sent back decoded on rgmii_tx_clk's halves, its FCS checked) and the PHY's MDIO side (link up),\n// with the stack on a 125 MHz system clock. The design is a UDP echo, wired as in eth_udp's README, plus a datagram of its own on demand.\n// Checks as eth_udp_stack's: MDIO setup and link_up; ARP; ping; a UDP echo; the design's own broadcast datagram while\n// a ping is answered (both intact); ARP and ping back to back; frame_bad for a bad FCS; and at a byte a clock a\n// 1472-byte UDP echo straight from the receive side and a 1472-byte ping. MDIO advertises no 10/100 mode (0001). Prints PASS or every failed\n// check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule stack_rgmii_phy_mdio (input wire mdc, inout wire mdio);\n    reg [15:0] regs [0:31];\n    integer i, ones = 0, n = -1;\n    reg [1:0] op;\n    reg [4:0] pa, ra;\n    reg [15:0] data;\n    reg drive = 1'b0, value = 1'b1;\n    assign mdio = drive ? value : 1'bz;\n    initial begin\n        for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) regs[i] = 16'h0000;\n        regs[1] = 16'h782D; regs[2] = 16'h001C; regs[3] = 16'hC816;\n    end\n    always @(posedge mdc) begin : slave\n        reg b;\n        b = mdio;\n        if (n < 0) begin\n            if (b === 1'b1) ones = ones + 1;\n            else begin if (ones >= 32) n = 32; ones = 0; end\n        end else begin\n            n = n + 1;\n            if (n == 33 && b !== 1'b1) n = -1;\n            else if (n == 34 || n == 35) op = {op[0], b};\n            else if (n >= 36 && n <= 40) pa = {pa[3:0], b};\n            else if (n >= 41 && n <= 45) begin ra = {ra[3:0], b}; if (n == 45) data = regs[ra]; end\n            else if (op == 2'b10 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n == 46) #50 begin drive = 1'b1; value = 1'b0; end\n                else if (n >= 47 && n <= 62) #50 value = data[62 - n];\n                else if (n == 63) begin #50 drive = 1'b0; n = -1; end\n            end else if (op == 2'b01 && pa == 5'd1) begin\n                if (n >= 48) data = {data[14:0], b};\n                if (n == 63) begin regs[ra] = data; n = -1; end\n            end else if (n == 63) n = -1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule eth_udp_stack_rgmii_tb;\n    parameter RX_LATE = 1;\n    reg clk = 1'b0, rx_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #4 clk = ~clk;                  // the system clock, 125 MHz\n    initial begin #2.7; forever #3.9998 rx_clk = ~rx_clk; end   // 125.01 MHz, another phase\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    localparam [47:0] MAC = 48'h02_47_4C_00_00_01, HOST = 48'h3C_22_FB_A1_B2_C3;\n    localparam [31:0] IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd222}, HOST_IP = {8'd10, 8'd0, 8'd100, 8'd10};\n\n    reg  [3:0]  rxd = 4'd0;\n    reg         rx_ctl = 1'b0;\n    wire [3:0]  txd;\n    wire        tx_clk, tx_ctl, mdc, mdio_out, mdio_oe, mdio, link_up, frame_good, frame_bad, arp_replied, ping_replied;\n    pullup (mdio);\n    assign mdio = mdio_oe ? mdio_out : 1'bz;\n    stack_rgmii_phy_mdio u_phy_mdio (.mdc(mdc), .mdio(mdio));\n\n    wire [7:0]  udp_data;\n    wire        udp_valid, udp_last, rx_new, payload_ready, send_ready;\n    wire [10:0] udp_length;\n    wire [47:0] peer_mac;\n    wire [31:0] peer_ip;\n    wire [15:0] peer_port;\n    reg         own = 1'b0;          // the design's own datagram instead of the echo\n    reg         own_send = 1'b0;\n    reg  [3:0]  own_index = 4'd0;\n    wire        send = own ? own_send : rx_new;\n    wire [7:0]  payload = own ? 8'hB0 + own_index : udp_data;\n    wire        payload_valid = own ? 1'b1 : udp_valid;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (own && payload_ready) own_index <= own_index + 4'd1;\n\n    eth_udp_stack_rgmii #(.PORT(16'd1234), .SETTLE_MS(1), .RX_LATE(RX_LATE)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .mac(MAC), .ip(IP), .rgmii_rx_clk(rx_clk), .rgmii_rxd(rxd), .rgmii_rx_ctl(rx_ctl),\n        .rgmii_tx_clk(tx_clk), .rgmii_txd(txd), .rgmii_tx_ctl(tx_ctl), .mdc(mdc), .mdio_out(mdio_out), .mdio_oe(mdio_oe), .mdio_in(mdio), .link_up(link_up),\n        .frame_good(frame_good), .frame_bad(frame_bad), .arp_replied(arp_replied), .ping_replied(ping_replied),\n        .rx_data(udp_data), .rx_valid(udp_valid), .rx_last(udp_last), .rx_ready(own ? 1'b0 : payload_ready),\n        .rx_length(udp_length), .peer_mac(peer_mac), .peer_ip(peer_ip), .peer_port(peer_port), .rx_new(rx_new),\n        .rx_dropped(), .send(send), .send_length(own ? 11'd10 : udp_length),\n        .dst_mac(own ? 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF : peer_mac), .dst_ip(own ? 32'h0A00_FFFF : peer_ip),\n        .dst_port(own ? 16'd4321 : peer_port), .send_ready(send_ready), .payload_data(payload),\n        .payload_valid(payload_valid), .payload_ready(payload_ready));\n\n    integer goods = 0, bads = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (frame_good) goods = goods + 1;\n        if (frame_bad) bads = bads + 1;\n    end\n\n    // ---- CRC and checksums ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    function [31:0] crc_update(input [31:0] c, input [7:0] d);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] x;\n        begin\n            x = c ^ {24'd0, d};\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) x = {1'b0, x[31:1]} ^ (x[0] ? 32'hEDB8_8320 : 32'd0);\n            crc_update = x;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function [15:0] fold(input [31:0] s);\n        reg [31:0] t;\n        begin\n            t = s;\n            while (t[31:16] != 0) t = t[15:0] + t[31:16];\n            fold = t[15:0];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- The host sending -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] frame [0:1599];\n    integer frame_n;\n    task put16(input integer at, input [15:0] v); begin frame[at] = v[15:8]; frame[at + 1] = v[7:0]; end endtask\n    task put32(input integer at, input [31:0] v); begin put16(at, v[31:16]); put16(at + 2, v[15:0]); end endtask\n    task put48(input integer at, input [47:0] v); begin put16(at, v[47:32]); put32(at + 2, v[31:0]); end endtask\n    function [31:0] sum16(input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {frame[i], (i + 1 < to) ? frame[i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            sum16 = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task ipv4(input [7:0] proto, input integer payload_bytes);\n        begin\n            put48(0, MAC); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0800);\n            frame[14] = 8'h45; frame[15] = 8'h00; put16(16, 20 + payload_bytes); put16(18, 16'h3E01);\n            put16(20, 16'h4000); frame[22] = 8'd64; frame[23] = proto; put16(24, 16'h0000);\n            put32(26, HOST_IP); put32(30, IP);\n            put16(24, ~fold(sum16(14, 34)));\n            frame_n = 34 + payload_bytes;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_arp;\n        begin\n            put48(0, 48'hFFFF_FFFF_FFFF); put48(6, HOST); put16(12, 16'h0806); put16(14, 16'h0001); put16(16, 16'h0800);\n            frame[18] = 8'd6; frame[19] = 8'd4; put16(20, 16'h0001); put48(22, HOST); put32(28, HOST_IP); put48(32, 48'h0);\n            put32(38, IP);\n            frame_n = 42;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_ping(input integer data_bytes);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd1, 8 + data_bytes);\n            frame[34] = 8'd8; frame[35] = 8'd0; put16(36, 16'h0000); put16(38, 16'h1234); put16(40, 16'h0007);\n            for (i = 0; i < data_bytes; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = i * 3 + 1;\n            put16(36, ~fold(sum16(34, 42 + data_bytes)));\n        end\n    endtask\n    task make_udp(input integer n);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            ipv4(8'd17, 8 + n);\n            put16(34, 16'd50123); put16(36, 16'd1234); put16(38, 8 + n); put16(40, 16'h0000);\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[42 + i] = (i * 41) ^ 8'h6C;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // A nibble centred on the edge that takes it (2 ns after the opposite edge): the low one on the rising edge, or with\n    // RX_LATE on the falling edge.\n    task half(input low_half, input [3:0] v, input c);\n        begin\n            if (low_half ^ RX_LATE) @(negedge rx_clk); else @(posedge rx_clk);\n            #2 rxd = v; rx_ctl = c;\n        end\n    endtask\n    task put_byte(input [7:0] b, input dv);\n        begin half(1'b1, b[3:0], dv); half(1'b0, b[7:4], dv); end\n    endtask\n    task transmit(input bad);\n        integer i, n;\n        reg [31:0] c;\n        reg [7:0] b;\n        begin\n            n = frame_n < 60 ? 60 : frame_n;\n            for (i = frame_n; i < n; i = i + 1) frame[i] = 8'h00;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, frame[i]);\n            c = ~c ^ (bad ? 32'h0001_0000 : 32'd0);\n            for (i = 0; i < 7; i = i + 1) put_byte(8'h55, 1'b1);\n            put_byte(8'hD5, 1'b1);\n            for (i = 0; i < n + 4; i = i + 1) begin\n                b = i < n ? frame[i] : c[8 * (i - n) +: 8];\n                put_byte(b, 1'b1);\n            end\n            for (i = 0; i < 12; i = i + 1) put_byte(8'h00, 1'b0);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // ---- What comes back: frames in a list, each with its FCS checked ------------------------------------------\n    reg [7:0] back [0:7][0:1599];\n    integer back_len [0:7];\n    integer backs = 0, raw_n = 0, idle = 1000, min_gap = 1000;\n    reg [7:0] raw [0:1607];\n    reg [3:0] low;\n    reg low_ctl, tx_en_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge tx_clk) begin\n        #1 low = txd; low_ctl = tx_ctl;\n        @(negedge tx_clk); #1;\n        if (low_ctl) begin\n            if (!tx_en_was && idle < min_gap) min_gap = idle;   // the gap before this frame, in bytes\n            raw[raw_n] = {txd, low}; raw_n = raw_n + 1;\n        end else idle = tx_en_was ? 1 : idle + 1;\n        if (!low_ctl && tx_en_was) begin : done\n            integer i;\n            reg [31:0] c;\n            c = 32'hFFFF_FFFF;\n            for (i = 8; i < raw_n; i = i + 1) c = crc_update(c, raw[i]);\n            if (c !== 32'hDEBB_20E3 || raw[7] !== 8'hD5) fail(\"a frame sent with a bad FCS or preamble\");\n            if (backs < 8) begin\n                for (i = 8; i < raw_n - 4; i = i + 1) back[backs][i - 8] = raw[i];\n                back_len[backs] = raw_n - 12;\n            end\n            backs = backs + 1;\n            raw_n = 0;\n        end\n        tx_en_was = low_ctl;\n    end\n    function [15:0] b16(input integer f, input integer at); b16 = {back[f][at], back[f][at + 1]}; endfunction\n    function [31:0] bsum(input integer f, input integer from, input integer to);\n        integer i;\n        reg [31:0] s;\n        begin\n            s = 0;\n            for (i = from; i < to; i = i + 2) s = s + {back[f][i], (i + 1 < to) ? back[f][i + 1] : 8'h00};\n            bsum = s;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task wait_backs(input integer n);\n        integer t;\n        begin\n            t = 0;\n            while (backs < n && t < 80000) begin @(posedge clk); t = t + 1; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Finds the frame sent back with this EtherType and IP protocol (0 for ARP), or -1.\n    function integer find(input [15:0] ethertype, input [7:0] proto);\n        integer f;\n        begin\n            find = -1;\n            for (f = 0; f < backs && f < 8; f = f + 1)\n                if (b16(f, 12) == ethertype && (ethertype == 16'h0806 || back[f][23] == proto) && find < 0) find = f;\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    integer f, i, wrong;\n    initial begin\n        wait (link_up === 1'b1);\n        if (u_phy_mdio.regs[4] !== 16'h0001) fail(\"MDIO didn't advertise 1000 only (register 4 = 0001)\");\n\n        // ARP.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0806, 8'd0);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no ARP reply\");\n        else if (b16(f, 20) !== 16'h0002 || {back[f][28], back[f][29], back[f][30], back[f][31]} !== IP) fail(\"the ARP reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // Ping.\n        make_ping(56); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no echo reply\");\n        else if (back[f][34] !== 8'd0 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF || fold(bsum(f, 34, 98)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // UDP echo.\n        make_udp(37); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no UDP echo\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 37; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== (((i * 41) ^ 8'h6C) & 8'hFF)) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd50123 || b16(f, 38) !== 16'd45 || fold(bsum(f, 14, 34)) !== 16'hFFFF)\n                fail(\"the UDP echo\");\n        end\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // The design's own datagram while a ping is answered: both intact.\n        own = 1'b1;\n        make_ping(200);\n        fork\n            transmit(1'b0);\n            begin\n                // Once the echo reply is going out (its tx_valid drops between bytes), the design sends: the datagram\n                // must wait for the reply's last byte.\n                wait (dut.u_core.sel == 2'd2 && dut.u_core.u_mac.state == 3'd2);\n                repeat (40) @(posedge clk);\n                @(negedge clk); own_send = 1'b1; @(posedge clk); #1; own_send = 1'b0;\n            end\n        join\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (backs != 2) fail(\"the design's datagram and the echo reply: not both sent\");\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"the design's datagram\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== 8'hB0 + i) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || b16(f, 0) !== 16'hFFFF || b16(f, 36) !== 16'd4321) fail(\"the design's datagram's contents\");\n        end\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0 || fold(bsum(f, 34, 242)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the echo reply next to the datagram\");\n        own = 1'b0;\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // ARP and ping back to back.\n        make_arp; transmit(1'b0);\n        make_ping(20); transmit(1'b0);\n        wait_backs(2);\n        if (find(16'h0806, 8'd0) < 0 || find(16'h0800, 8'd1) < 0) fail(\"ARP and ping back to back\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // A bad FCS.\n        goods = 0; bads = 0;\n        make_ping(10); transmit(1'b1);\n        repeat (2000) @(posedge clk);\n        if (bads != 1 || goods != 0 || backs != 0) fail(\"a bad FCS\");\n\n        // At a byte a clock: a full-size UDP echo straight from the receive side, and a full-size ping.\n        make_udp(1472); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd17);\n        if (f < 0) fail(\"no 1472-byte UDP echo\");\n        else begin\n            wrong = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 1472; i = i + 1) if (back[f][42 + i] !== (((i * 41) ^ 8'h6C) & 8'hFF)) wrong = wrong + 1;\n            if (wrong != 0 || back_len[f] != 1514) fail(\"the 1472-byte UDP echo\");\n        end\n        backs = 0;\n        make_ping(1472); transmit(1'b0); wait_backs(1);\n        f = find(16'h0800, 8'd1);\n        if (f < 0 || back_len[f] != 1514 || fold(bsum(f, 34, 1514)) !== 16'hFFFF) fail(\"the 1472-byte ping\");\n        backs = 0;\n\n        // Frames went out back to back above (ARP and ping, the datagram next to a reply): never less than 12 bytes apart.\n        if (min_gap < 12) begin $display(\"FAIL: a gap of %0d bytes between frames\", min_gap); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (min_gap > 40) begin $display(\"FAIL: no frames back to back to measure the gap (%0d)\", min_gap); errors = errors + 1; end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_encoder/README.md" : "# HDMI/DVI encoder\n\nThe words of an HDMI or DVI link, one 10-bit word per lane per pixel clock, for the family cores `hdmi_out` (AMD\nXilinx 7-series), `hdmi_out_gowin`, `hdmi_out_ecp5` and `hdmi_out_gatemate` to serialise. They use it with their\n`HDMI` parameter; a design rarely instantiates it on its own.\n\n- **`HDMI = 0`** — DVI: three TMDS encoders with hsync and vsync on blue's control bits, exactly what the `hdmi_out`\n  cores sent before. `audio_ready` stays low.\n- **`HDMI = 1`** — HDMI 1.4 with sound:\n  - the video preamble and guard band before every active line (the stream runs 12 clocks behind its inputs);\n  - a data island in every line's blanking, also during vertical blanking. It needs 66 characters of blanking for one\n    packet and 98 for two, which every CEA timing has;\n  - Audio Clock Regeneration once a millisecond (N = 6144, CTS = `PIXEL_HZ` / 1000, so give the real pixel clock);\n  - audio samples: 48 kHz, two channels, 16 bits, with IEC 60958 channel status saying so;\n  - the AVI InfoFrame (RGB, full range, `VIC`, `ASPECT`) and the Audio InfoFrame (two channels).\n\nAudio: `audio_ready` pulses at 48 kHz on average once the encoder has measured the line (after three lines), and the\nencoder takes `audio_l`/`audio_r` (signed) in that clock. Hold the next pair on the inputs and step when you see the\npulse; up to eight pairs wait for a packet.\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out #(.HDMI(1), .PIXEL_HZ(74_250_000), .VIC(7'd4), .ASPECT(2'b10)) u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .audio_l(left), .audio_r(right), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n));\n\nalways @(posedge pixel_clk) if (audio_ready) begin\n    left <= next_left;          // whatever makes the sound, one step per request\n    right <= next_right;\nend\n```\n",
    "cores/hdmi_encoder/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"tmds_encoder\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"The three 10-bit TMDS lane words per pixel clock for HDMI or DVI, which the hdmi_out cores serialise. HDMI = 0: DVI (three tmds_encoders). HDMI = 1: HDMI 1.4 with the video preamble and guard band, a data island in each line's blanking (TERC4, BCH error correction) with Audio Clock Regeneration, 48 kHz stereo 16-bit audio samples (audio_ready asks for each pair), and the AVI and Audio InfoFrames.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 115.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 1590,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 872,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 32\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 37.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 23,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 181,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 653,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 1313,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 1158,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 2250,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 499\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 66.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 376,\n        \"DFF\" : 872,\n        \"LUT4\" : 1610,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 155,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 27,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 11,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 4,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 32\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 33,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 2638,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 165.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 98,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 581,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 1691\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"hdmi_encoder\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"tmds\",\n    \"terc4\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"hdmi-audio\",\n    \"infoframe\",\n    \"video\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Die drei 10-Bit-TMDS-Wörter je Pixeltakt für HDMI oder DVI, die die hdmi_out-Kerne serialisieren. HDMI = 0: DVI (drei tmds_encoder). HDMI = 1: HDMI 1.4 mit Video-Präambel und Guard Band, einer Data Island in der Austastlücke jeder Zeile (TERC4, BCH-Fehlerkorrektur) mit Audio Clock Regeneration, 48-kHz-Stereo-Samples zu 16 Bit (audio_ready fordert jedes Paar an) und den AVI- und Audio-InfoFrames.\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI-Encoder\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI encoder\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames; 0: DVI\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 74250000,\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel clock in Hz (HDMI's audio clock: CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PIXEL_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"CEA-861 video code for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60)\",\n      \"name\" : \"VIC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Picture aspect for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 4:3, 2 = 16:9)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ASPECT\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High in the active picture\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"HDMI = 1: the left sample (signed), taken while audio_ready is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"audio_l\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"HDMI = 1: the right sample (signed)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"audio_r\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses at 48 kHz once islands run; the pair on audio_l/audio_r is taken in that clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"audio_ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Lane 0's word (bit 0 first)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"word_blue\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Lane 1's word\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"word_green\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Lane 2's word\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"word_red\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"hdmi_encoder\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/hdmi_encoder/rtl/hdmi_encoder.v" : "// HDMI or DVI encoding for one pixel clock: the three 10-bit lane words (blue, green, red; bit 0 is sent first) that\n// the family cores hdmi_out (7-series), hdmi_out_gowin, hdmi_out_ecp5 and hdmi_out_gatemate serialise.\n//\n// HDMI = 0: DVI, three TMDS encoders (tmds_encoder) with hsync/vsync on blue's control bits: exactly the words the\n//   hdmi_out cores sent before this core existed. audio_ready stays low and the audio inputs are unused.\n// HDMI = 1: HDMI 1.4 with audio:\n//   - the video preamble and video guard band before every active line (the stream runs 12 clocks behind the inputs\n//     so they can come before `de` rises);\n//   - a data island in each line's blanking, also in the vertical blanking: preamble, guard band, one or two\n//     32-character packets in TERC4 with BCH error correction, guard band. It starts 4 characters after the blanking\n//     starts and needs 66 characters of blanking for one packet, 98 for two (every CEA timing has more);\n//   - packets: Audio Clock Regeneration (N = 6144, CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000, once a millisecond), audio samples (two\n//     channels, 16 bits, 48 kHz, IEC 60958 channel status saying 48 kHz and 16 bits), the AVI InfoFrame (RGB, full\n//     range, VIC, picture aspect) and the Audio InfoFrame (two channels), the InfoFrames at every vsync edge.\n// Audio: audio_ready pulses at 48 kHz on average (from PIXEL_HZ); the core takes audio_l/audio_r (signed) in that clock,\n//   so a design holds the next pair on its inputs and steps when it sees the pulse. Up to 8 pairs wait for a packet.\n// Latency: 3 clocks with HDMI = 0 (the encoders), 16 with HDMI = 1 (12 + 3 + one output register).\n\nmodule hdmi_encoder #(\n    parameter HDMI = 1,\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,            // CEA-861 video code: 1 = 640x480p60, 4 = 1280x720p60, 16 = 1920x1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10         // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire [7:0]  red,\n    input  wire [7:0]  green,\n    input  wire [7:0]  blue,\n    input  wire        de,\n    input  wire        hsync,\n    input  wire        vsync,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_l,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_r,\n    output wire        audio_ready,\n    output wire [9:0]  word_blue,\n    output wire [9:0]  word_green,\n    output wire [9:0]  word_red\n);\ngenerate if (HDMI == 0) begin : dvi\n    assign audio_ready = 1'b0;\n    tmds_encoder u_blue  (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(blue),  .c0(hsync), .c1(vsync), .q(word_blue));\n    tmds_encoder u_green (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(green), .c0(1'b0),  .c1(1'b0),  .q(word_green));\n    tmds_encoder u_red   (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(red),   .c0(1'b0),  .c1(1'b0),  .q(word_red));\nend else begin : hdmi\n    localparam integer DELAY = 12;\n    localparam integer CTS_HZ = PIXEL_HZ / 1000;       // N / (128 * 48 kHz) = 1 / 1000 with N = 6144\n    localparam [19:0]  CTS = CTS_HZ;\n    localparam [19:0]  N_VALUE = 20'd6144;\n\n    // ---- the delayed stream and what `de` does next ----------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [27*DELAY-1:0] pipe = 0;                       // the inputs, DELAY clocks deep (27 bits each)\n    reg [DELAY:0] de_seen = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        pipe <= {pipe[27*(DELAY-1)-1:0], red, green, blue, hsync, vsync, de};\n        de_seen <= {de_seen[DELAY-1:0], de};\n    end\n    wire [26:0] s = pipe[27*DELAY-1 -: 27];\n    wire [7:0] s_red = s[26:19], s_green = s[18:11], s_blue = s[10:3];\n    wire s_hsync = s[2], s_vsync = s[1], s_de = s[0];\n    // de_seen[j] is the input delayed j + 1 clocks: de_seen[DELAY-1] is s_de, de_seen[DELAY-1-j] the stream's de j clocks later.\n    wire video_guard    = !s_de && (de_seen[DELAY-2] || de_seen[DELAY-3]);\n    wire video_preamble = !s_de && !de_seen[DELAY-2] && !de_seen[DELAY-3] && (|de_seen[DELAY-4:DELAY-11]);\n\n    // ---- the line: blanking length and line length, measured on the stream ---------------------------------------\n    reg s_de_was = 1'b0;\n    reg [12:0] pos = 0, line_len = 0, blank_len = 0, blank_count = 0;\n    reg [1:0] falls = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        s_de_was <= s_de;\n        if (s_de_was && !s_de) begin                      // blanking starts\n            if (falls != 2'd3) falls <= falls + 2'd1;\n            line_len <= pos + 13'd1;\n            pos <= 0;\n        end else if (falls == 2'd3 && pos == line_len - 13'd1) pos <= 0;   // vertical blanking: the same rhythm\n        else pos <= pos + 13'd1;\n        if (s_de) begin if (blank_count != 0) blank_len <= blank_count; blank_count <= 0; end\n        else blank_count <= blank_count + 13'd1;\n    end\n    // Room for packets: 4 control + 8 preamble + 2 guard + 32 n + 2 guard + 12 control (with the video preamble) + 2.\n    wire [12:0] room = (blank_len > 13'd34) ? blank_len - 13'd34 : 13'd0;\n    wire [1:0]  slots = (falls != 2'd3) ? 2'd0 : (room >= 13'd64) ? 2'd2 : (room >= 13'd32) ? 2'd1 : 2'd0;\n\n    // ---- audio at 48 kHz ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [31:0] sample_acc = 0;\n    reg ready = 1'b0;\n    assign audio_ready = ready;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        ready <= 1'b0;\n        if (slots == 2'd0) sample_acc <= 0;             // no requests before islands can carry them (power-up)\n        else if (sample_acc + 32'd48000 >= PIXEL_HZ) begin sample_acc <= sample_acc + 32'd48000 - PIXEL_HZ; ready <= 1'b1; end\n        else sample_acc <= sample_acc + 32'd48000;\n    end\n    reg [31:0] fifo [0:7];\n    reg [3:0] wp = 0, rp = 0;\n    wire [3:0] fill = wp - rp;\n\n    // ---- what's due ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [19:0] acr_count = 0;\n    reg acr_due = 1'b0, avi_due = 1'b0, aif_due = 1'b0, s_vsync_was = 1'b0;\n\n    // ---- the island --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    localparam [12:0] ISLAND_AT = 13'd4;                 // preamble at 4..11, guard 12..13, packets from 14\n    reg in_island = 1'b0, island_first = 1'b0;\n    reg [1:0] n_packets = 0, slot = 0;\n    reg [4:0] k = 0;                                     // character within the packet\n    reg [2:0] kind0 = 0, kind1 = 0;                      // 1 ACR, 2 audio, 3 AVI, 4 audio InfoFrame\n    reg [23:0] header = 0;\n    reg [55:0] sub0 = 0, sub1 = 0, sub2 = 0, sub3 = 0;\n    reg [7:0] ecc_h = 0, ecc_0 = 0, ecc_1 = 0, ecc_2 = 0, ecc_3 = 0;\n    reg [7:0] frame = 0;                                 // IEC 60958 frame, 0 … 191\n\n    function [7:0] next_ecc(input [7:0] ecc, input b);\n        next_ecc = (ecc >> 1) ^ ((ecc[0] ^ b) ? 8'b10000011 : 8'd0);\n    endfunction\n    function [7:0] sum8(input [23:0] h, input [55:0] a, input [55:0] b);   // checksum over header, PB1…PB13\n        integer j;\n        reg [7:0] t;\n        begin\n            t = h[7:0] + h[15:8] + h[23:16];\n            for (j = 1; j < 7; j = j + 1) t = t + a[8*j +: 8];\n            for (j = 0; j < 7; j = j + 1) t = t + b[8*j +: 8];\n            sum8 = t;\n        end\n    endfunction\n    // Channel status bit `f` of the 192-bit block: 48 kHz (bit 25), 16-bit words (bit 33).\n    function cs_bit(input [7:0] f);\n        cs_bit = (f == 8'd25) || (f == 8'd33);\n    endfunction\n    function [55:0] sample_sub(input [31:0] lr, input [7:0] f);\n        reg [23:0] l, r;\n        reg c, pl, pr;\n        begin\n            l = {lr[31:16], 8'h00}; r = {lr[15:0], 8'h00}; c = cs_bit(f);\n            pl = ^{l, c}; pr = ^{r, c};                  // even parity over the sample and V U C (V = U = 0)\n            sample_sub = {pr, c, 1'b0, 1'b0, pl, c, 1'b0, 1'b0, r, l};\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // The packet for a slot, built when the slot starts.\n    reg [55:0] avi_a, avi_b, aif_a;\n    reg [55:0] p0, p1, p2, p3;\n    reg [23:0] h;\n    reg [2:0] take;\n    reg [3:0] present, bflag;\n    integer m;\n    task load(input [2:0] what);\n        begin\n            p0 = 0; p1 = 0; p2 = 0; p3 = 0;\n            case (what)\n                3'd1: begin\n                    h = 24'h000001;\n                    p0 = {N_VALUE[7:0], N_VALUE[15:8], 4'h0, N_VALUE[19:16], CTS[7:0], CTS[15:8], 4'h0, CTS[19:16], 8'h00};\n                    p1 = p0; p2 = p0; p3 = p0;\n                end\n                3'd2: begin\n                    take = (fill > 4) ? 3'd4 : fill[2:0];\n                    present = 0; bflag = 0;\n                    for (m = 0; m < 4; m = m + 1) if (m < take) begin\n                        present[m] = 1'b1;\n                        if (frame + m == 8'd0 || frame + m == 8'd192) bflag[m] = 1'b1;\n                    end\n                    if (take > 0) p0 = sample_sub(fifo[rp[2:0]], frame);\n                    if (take > 1) p1 = sample_sub(fifo[rp[2:0] + 3'd1], (frame + 8'd1 >= 8'd192) ? frame + 8'd1 - 8'd192 : frame + 8'd1);\n                    if (take > 2) p2 = sample_sub(fifo[rp[2:0] + 3'd2], (frame + 8'd2 >= 8'd192) ? frame + 8'd2 - 8'd192 : frame + 8'd2);\n                    if (take > 3) p3 = sample_sub(fifo[rp[2:0] + 3'd3], (frame + 8'd3 >= 8'd192) ? frame + 8'd3 - 8'd192 : frame + 8'd3);\n                    h = {bflag, 4'h0, 4'h0, present, 8'h02};\n                end\n                3'd3: begin\n                    h = 24'h0D0282;\n                    avi_a = {8'h00, 8'h00, {1'b0, VIC}, 8'h08, {2'b00, ASPECT, 4'b1000}, 8'h00, 8'h00};   // PB6 … PB0\n                    avi_b = 56'd0;\n                    avi_a[7:0] = 8'h00 - sum8(h, avi_a, avi_b);\n                    p0 = avi_a; p1 = avi_b;\n                end\n                default: begin\n                    h = 24'h0A0184;\n                    aif_a = {8'h00, 8'h00, 8'h00, 8'h00, 8'h00, 8'h01, 8'h00};                    // PB1: 2 channels\n                    aif_a[7:0] = 8'h00 - sum8(h, aif_a, 56'd0);\n                    p0 = aif_a;\n                end\n            endcase\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // The island's characters, for the stream's current position.\n    reg [3:0] nib0, nib1, nib2;\n    reg [1:0] mode;                                     // 0 TMDS, 1 video guard, 2 island guard, 3 island data\n    reg ctl0, ctl1, ctl2, ctl3;\n    wire island_start = (pos == ISLAND_AT) && slots != 0 && (acr_due || avi_due || aif_due || fill != 0);\n    reg [12:0] island_end = 0;                          // the first position after the trailing guard band\n    reg in_pre = 1'b0, in_lead = 1'b0, in_trail = 1'b0;\n    reg [2:0] next0, next1;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (ready && fill != 4'd8) begin fifo[wp[2:0]] <= {audio_l, audio_r}; wp <= wp + 4'd1; end\n        if (acr_count + 20'd1 >= CTS_HZ) begin acr_count <= 0; acr_due <= 1'b1; end else acr_count <= acr_count + 20'd1;\n        s_vsync_was <= s_vsync;\n        if (s_vsync != s_vsync_was) begin avi_due <= 1'b1; aif_due <= 1'b1; end\n\n        if (island_start) begin\n            // Up to two packets, most urgent first.\n            next0 = acr_due ? 3'd1 : avi_due ? 3'd3 : aif_due ? 3'd4 : 3'd2;\n            next1 = 3'd0;\n            if (slots == 2'd2) begin\n                if (next0 != 3'd2 && fill != 0) next1 = 3'd2;\n                else if (next0 == 3'd2 && fill > 4'd4) next1 = 3'd2;      // a backlog: two audio packets\n                else if (next0 == 3'd1 && avi_due) next1 = 3'd3;\n                else if (next0 != 3'd4 && aif_due && next0 != 3'd2) next1 = 3'd4;\n            end\n            kind0 <= next0; kind1 <= next1;\n            n_packets <= (next1 != 3'd0) ? 2'd2 : 2'd1;\n            island_end <= ISLAND_AT + 13'd8 + 13'd2 + ((next1 != 3'd0) ? 13'd64 : 13'd32) + 13'd2;\n            in_pre <= 1'b1;\n        end\n        // Preamble 4..11, guard 12..13, packets, guard.\n        if (pos == ISLAND_AT + 13'd8 && in_pre) begin in_pre <= 1'b0; in_lead <= 1'b1; end\n        if (pos == ISLAND_AT + 13'd10 && in_lead) begin\n            in_lead <= 1'b0; in_island <= 1'b1; island_first <= 1'b1; slot <= 0; k <= 0;\n            load(kind0);\n            header <= h; sub0 <= p0; sub1 <= p1; sub2 <= p2; sub3 <= p3;\n            ecc_h <= 0; ecc_0 <= 0; ecc_1 <= 0; ecc_2 <= 0; ecc_3 <= 0;\n            case (kind0) 3'd1: acr_due <= 1'b0; 3'd3: avi_due <= 1'b0; 3'd4: aif_due <= 1'b0;\n                default: begin rp <= rp + {1'b0, take}; frame <= (frame + take >= 8'd192) ? frame + take - 8'd192 : frame + take; end endcase\n        end else if (in_island) begin\n            island_first <= 1'b0;\n            if (k < 5'd24) ecc_h <= next_ecc(ecc_h, header[k]);\n            if (k < 5'd28) begin\n                ecc_0 <= next_ecc(next_ecc(ecc_0, sub0[2*k]), sub0[2*k+1]);\n                ecc_1 <= next_ecc(next_ecc(ecc_1, sub1[2*k]), sub1[2*k+1]);\n                ecc_2 <= next_ecc(next_ecc(ecc_2, sub2[2*k]), sub2[2*k+1]);\n                ecc_3 <= next_ecc(next_ecc(ecc_3, sub3[2*k]), sub3[2*k+1]);\n            end\n            k <= k + 5'd1;\n            if (k == 5'd31) begin\n                if (slot + 2'd1 < n_packets) begin\n                    slot <= slot + 2'd1; k <= 0;\n                    load(kind1);\n                    header <= h; sub0 <= p0; sub1 <= p1; sub2 <= p2; sub3 <= p3;\n                    ecc_h <= 0; ecc_0 <= 0; ecc_1 <= 0; ecc_2 <= 0; ecc_3 <= 0;\n                    case (kind1) 3'd1: acr_due <= 1'b0; 3'd3: avi_due <= 1'b0; 3'd4: aif_due <= 1'b0;\n                        default: begin rp <= rp + {1'b0, take}; frame <= (frame + take >= 8'd192) ? frame + take - 8'd192 : frame + take; end endcase\n                end else begin\n                    in_island <= 1'b0; in_trail <= 1'b1;\n                end\n            end\n        end\n        if (in_trail && pos == island_end) in_trail <= 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    // The bits of the current character (registered: they belong to the stream's position one clock later).\n    function bit_of(input [55:0] p, input [7:0] e, input [5:0] b);    // bit b (0 … 63) of a subpacket with its ECC\n        bit_of = (b < 6'd56) ? p[b] : e[b - 6'd56];\n    endfunction\n    always @(*) begin\n        mode = 2'd0; nib0 = 0; nib1 = 0; nib2 = 0;\n        ctl0 = 1'b0; ctl1 = 1'b0; ctl2 = 1'b0; ctl3 = 1'b0;\n        if (s_de) mode = 2'd0;\n        else if (video_guard) mode = 2'd1;\n        else if (in_lead || in_trail) begin mode = 2'd2; nib0 = {2'b11, s_vsync, s_hsync}; end\n        else if (in_island) begin\n            mode = 2'd3;\n            nib0 = {!island_first, (k < 5'd24) ? header[k] : ecc_h[k - 5'd24], s_vsync, s_hsync};\n            nib1 = {bit_of(sub3, ecc_3, {k, 1'b0}), bit_of(sub2, ecc_2, {k, 1'b0}), bit_of(sub1, ecc_1, {k, 1'b0}), bit_of(sub0, ecc_0, {k, 1'b0})};\n            nib2 = {bit_of(sub3, ecc_3, {k, 1'b1}), bit_of(sub2, ecc_2, {k, 1'b1}), bit_of(sub1, ecc_1, {k, 1'b1}), bit_of(sub0, ecc_0, {k, 1'b1})};\n        end else if (in_pre) begin ctl0 = 1'b1; ctl2 = 1'b1; end\n        else if (video_preamble) ctl0 = 1'b1;\n    end\n\n    // Words: the TMDS encoders take the stream (3 clocks), the other modes wait as long, one register stage at the end.\n    wire [9:0] q_blue, q_green, q_red;\n    tmds_encoder u_blue  (.clk(clk), .de(s_de), .d(s_blue),  .c0(s_hsync), .c1(s_vsync), .q(q_blue));\n    tmds_encoder u_green (.clk(clk), .de(s_de), .d(s_green), .c0(ctl0),    .c1(ctl1),    .q(q_green));\n    tmds_encoder u_red   (.clk(clk), .de(s_de), .d(s_red),   .c0(ctl2),    .c1(ctl3),    .q(q_red));\n    reg [13:0] side0 = 0, side1 = 0, side2 = 0;          // {mode, nib0, nib1, nib2}, three clocks behind\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        side0 <= {mode, nib0, nib1, nib2};\n        side1 <= side0;\n        side2 <= side1;\n    end\n    function [9:0] terc4(input [3:0] d);\n        case (d)\n            4'h0: terc4 = 10'b1010011100; 4'h1: terc4 = 10'b1001100011; 4'h2: terc4 = 10'b1011100100; 4'h3: terc4 = 10'b1011100010;\n            4'h4: terc4 = 10'b0101110001; 4'h5: terc4 = 10'b0100011110; 4'h6: terc4 = 10'b0110001110; 4'h7: terc4 = 10'b0100111100;\n            4'h8: terc4 = 10'b1011001100; 4'h9: terc4 = 10'b0100111001; 4'hA: terc4 = 10'b0110011100; 4'hB: terc4 = 10'b1011000110;\n            4'hC: terc4 = 10'b1010001110; 4'hD: terc4 = 10'b1001110001; 4'hE: terc4 = 10'b0101100011; default: terc4 = 10'b1011000011;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n    wire [1:0] m3 = side2[13:12];\n    reg [9:0] out_blue = 0, out_green = 0, out_red = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        case (m3)\n            2'd1: begin out_blue <= 10'b1011001100; out_green <= 10'b0100110011; out_red <= 10'b1011001100; end\n            2'd2: begin out_blue <= terc4(side2[11:8]); out_green <= 10'b0100110011; out_red <= 10'b0100110011; end\n            2'd3: begin out_blue <= terc4(side2[11:8]); out_green <= terc4(side2[7:4]); out_red <= terc4(side2[3:0]); end\n            default: begin out_blue <= q_blue; out_green <= q_green; out_red <= q_red; end\n        endcase\n    end\n    assign word_blue = out_blue;\n    assign word_green = out_green;\n    assign word_red = out_red;\nend endgenerate\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_encoder/tb/hdmi_encoder_tb.v" : "// hdmi_encoder's testbench: a decoder written like a sink's checks every character of HDMI = 1 and compares HDMI = 0\n// with three plain tmds_encoders. The timing is the testbench's own: 12 MHz pixels, lines of 1000 (640 active, 360\n// blanking), 60 lines (40 active), so every line carries four samples and audio packets are full (all 16 TERC4 values\n// on the data channels). Samples are pseudo-random and recorded as the core takes them; pixels likewise.\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\nmodule hdmi_encoder_tb;\n    localparam integer PIXEL_HZ = 12000000, H_ACTIVE = 640, H_TOTAL = 1000, V_ACTIVE = 40, V_TOTAL = 60;\n    localparam integer H_SYNC_AT = 680, H_SYNC_LEN = 40, V_SYNC_AT = 45, V_SYNC_LEN = 3;\n    localparam integer FRAMES = 4;\n    reg clk = 0;\n    always #41.667 clk = ~clk;\n\n    // Timing and pixels.\n    integer x = 0, y = 0;\n    reg [7:0] red = 0, green = 0, blue = 0;\n    reg de = 0, hsync = 0, vsync = 0;\n    reg [15:0] lfsr = 16'hACE1;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        x <= (x == H_TOTAL - 1) ? 0 : x + 1;\n        if (x == H_TOTAL - 1) y <= (y == V_TOTAL - 1) ? 0 : y + 1;\n        de <= (x < H_ACTIVE) && (y < V_ACTIVE);\n        hsync <= (x >= H_SYNC_AT) && (x < H_SYNC_AT + H_SYNC_LEN);\n        vsync <= (y >= V_SYNC_AT) && (y < V_SYNC_AT + V_SYNC_LEN);\n        lfsr <= {lfsr[14:0], lfsr[15] ^ lfsr[13] ^ lfsr[12] ^ lfsr[10]};\n        red <= lfsr[7:0]; green <= lfsr[15:8] ^ x[7:0]; blue <= x[7:0] + y[7:0];\n    end\n\n    // Audio: a new pseudo-random pair after each request.\n    reg [15:0] audio_l = 16'h1234, audio_r = 16'hFEDC;\n    reg [31:0] alfsr = 32'h1F2E3D4C;\n    wire audio_ready;\n\n    wire [9:0] wb, wg, wr, db, dg, dr, rb, rg, rr;\n    wire audio_ready_dvi;\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(1), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(7'd1), .ASPECT(2'b01)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready), .word_blue(wb), .word_green(wg), .word_red(wr));\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(0)) dvi (\n        .clk(clk), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready_dvi), .word_blue(db), .word_green(dg), .word_red(dr));\n    tmds_encoder r_blue  (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(blue),  .c0(hsync), .c1(vsync), .q(rb));\n    tmds_encoder r_green (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(green), .c0(1'b0),  .c1(1'b0),  .q(rg));\n    tmds_encoder r_red   (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(red),   .c0(1'b0),  .c1(1'b0),  .q(rr));\n\n    // What went in, in order.\n    reg [31:0] sent_audio [0:8191];\n    reg [23:0] sent_pixel [0:65535];\n    integer audio_in = 0, pixel_in = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (audio_ready) begin\n            sent_audio[audio_in] = {audio_l, audio_r}; audio_in = audio_in + 1;\n            alfsr = {alfsr[30:0], alfsr[31] ^ alfsr[21] ^ alfsr[1] ^ alfsr[0]};\n            audio_l <= alfsr[15:0]; audio_r <= alfsr[31:16] ^ 16'h5A5A;\n        end\n        if (de) begin sent_pixel[pixel_in % 65536] = {red, green, blue}; pixel_in = pixel_in + 1; end\n    end\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*72-1:0] what); begin if (errors < 12) $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what); errors = errors + 1; end endtask\n\n    // ---- decoding -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    function [9:0] terc4(input [3:0] d);\n        case (d)\n            4'h0: terc4 = 10'b1010011100; 4'h1: terc4 = 10'b1001100011; 4'h2: terc4 = 10'b1011100100; 4'h3: terc4 = 10'b1011100010;\n            4'h4: terc4 = 10'b0101110001; 4'h5: terc4 = 10'b0100011110; 4'h6: terc4 = 10'b0110001110; 4'h7: terc4 = 10'b0100111100;\n            4'h8: terc4 = 10'b1011001100; 4'h9: terc4 = 10'b0100111001; 4'hA: terc4 = 10'b0110011100; 4'hB: terc4 = 10'b1011000110;\n            4'hC: terc4 = 10'b1010001110; 4'hD: terc4 = 10'b1001110001; 4'hE: terc4 = 10'b0101100011; default: terc4 = 10'b1011000011;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n    function [4:0] terc4_value(input [9:0] w);       // {valid, value}\n        integer v;\n        begin terc4_value = 5'd0; for (v = 0; v < 16; v = v + 1) if (terc4(v) == w) terc4_value = {1'b1, v[3:0]}; end\n    endfunction\n    function [2:0] ctrl_value(input [9:0] w);          // {valid, c1, c0}\n        case (w)\n            10'b1101010100: ctrl_value = 3'b100; 10'b0010101011: ctrl_value = 3'b101;\n            10'b0101010100: ctrl_value = 3'b110; 10'b1010101011: ctrl_value = 3'b111;\n            default: ctrl_value = 3'b000;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n    function [7:0] tmds_value(input [9:0] w);\n        reg [7:0] d;\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            d = w[9] ? ~w[7:0] : w[7:0];\n            tmds_value[0] = d[0];\n            for (i = 1; i < 8; i = i + 1) tmds_value[i] = w[8] ? d[i] ^ d[i-1] : ~(d[i] ^ d[i-1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n    // The ECC computed independently: BCH with G(x) = 1 + x^6 + x^7 + x^8 by long division, the first bit the\n    // highest power of the reflected message (the reflected CRC the core runs bit by bit must equal it).\n    function [7:0] bch(input [63:0] bits, input integer n);\n        reg [71:0] r;\n        integer p, j;\n        begin\n            r = 0;\n            for (j = 0; j < n; j = j + 1) r[n - 1 - j + 8] = bits[j];\n            for (p = n + 7; p >= 8; p = p - 1) if (r[p]) begin r[p] = 0; r[p - 2] = r[p - 2] ^ 1; r[p - 1] = r[p - 1] ^ 1; r[p - 8] = r[p - 8] ^ 1; end\n            for (j = 0; j < 8; j = j + 1) bch[j] = r[7 - j];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // The decoder's state.\n    localparam S_CTRL = 0, S_VPRE = 1, S_VGUARD = 2, S_VIDEO = 3, S_IPRE = 4, S_ILEAD = 5, S_ISLAND = 6, S_ITRAIL = 7;\n    integer state = S_CTRL, run = 0, k = 0, packets = 0, pixel_out = 0, audio_out = 0, lines = 0, frame = 0;\n    integer acr = 0, avi = 0, aif = 0, audio_packets = 0, full_packets = 0, last_acr_at = -1, now = 0, hs_rises = 0;\n    reg [31:0] hdr;\n    reg [63:0] sb0, sb1, sb2, sb3;\n    reg [15:0] seen1 = 0, seen2 = 0;\n    reg hs_was = 0, first = 0;\n    reg [7:0] cs_frame = 0;\n    reg started = 0;\n    reg [9:0] b, g, r;\n    reg [4:0] tb_, tg, tr;\n    reg [2:0] cb, cg, cr;\n\n    task check_packet;\n        reg [7:0] t;\n        integer s, sum, j, present;\n        reg [63:0] sp;\n        reg [15:0] l, rr2;\n        begin\n            packets = packets + 1;\n            if (bch({32'd0, hdr[31:0]}, 24) != hdr[31:24]) fail(\"header ECC\");\n            if (bch(sb0, 56) != sb0[63:56] || bch(sb1, 56) != sb1[63:56] || bch(sb2, 56) != sb2[63:56] || bch(sb3, 56) != sb3[63:56]) fail(\"subpacket ECC\");\n            t = hdr[7:0];\n            case (t)\n                8'h01: begin\n                    acr = acr + 1;\n                    if ({sb0[11:8], sb0[23:16], sb0[31:24]} != PIXEL_HZ / 1000) fail(\"ACR CTS\");\n                    if ({sb0[35:32], sb0[47:40], sb0[55:48]} != 20'd6144) fail(\"ACR N\");\n                    if (sb1[55:0] != sb0[55:0] || sb3[55:0] != sb0[55:0]) fail(\"ACR subpackets differ\");\n                    if (last_acr_at >= 0 && (now - last_acr_at > PIXEL_HZ / 1000 + 2 * H_TOTAL)) fail(\"ACR more than a millisecond apart\");\n                    last_acr_at = now;\n                end\n                8'h02: begin\n                    audio_packets = audio_packets + 1;\n                    present = hdr[11:8];\n                    if (present == 4'hF) full_packets = full_packets + 1;\n                    for (s = 0; s < 4; s = s + 1) if (present[s]) begin\n                        sp = (s == 0) ? sb0 : (s == 1) ? sb1 : (s == 2) ? sb2 : sb3;\n                        l = sp[23:8]; rr2 = sp[47:32];\n                        if (sp[7:0] != 0 || sp[31:24] != 0) fail(\"audio low bytes\");\n                        if ({l, rr2} != sent_audio[audio_out]) begin if (errors < 3) $display(\"  got %h want %h (sample %0d), next %h prev %h\", {l, rr2}, sent_audio[audio_out], audio_out, sent_audio[audio_out+1], (audio_out > 0) ? sent_audio[audio_out-1] : 32'd0); fail(\"audio sample\"); end\n                        audio_out = audio_out + 1;\n                        if (sp[51] != ^{sp[23:0], sp[50]}) fail(\"left parity\");\n                        if (sp[55] != ^{sp[47:24], sp[54]}) fail(\"right parity\");\n                        if (sp[50] != ((cs_frame == 25) || (cs_frame == 33)) || sp[54] != sp[50]) fail(\"channel status\");\n                        if (hdr[20 + s] != (cs_frame == 0)) fail(\"block start flag\");\n                        cs_frame = (cs_frame == 191) ? 0 : cs_frame + 1;\n                    end\n                end\n                8'h82: begin\n                    avi = avi + 1;\n                    sum = hdr[7:0] + hdr[15:8] + hdr[23:16];\n                    for (j = 0; j < 7; j = j + 1) sum = sum + sb0[8*j +: 8] + sb1[8*j +: 8];\n                    if (sum % 256 != 0) fail(\"AVI checksum\");\n                    if (hdr[23:8] != 16'h0D02 || sb0[39:32] != 8'd1 || sb0[23:16] != 8'h18 || sb0[31:24] != 8'h08) fail(\"AVI fields\");\n                end\n                8'h84: begin\n                    aif = aif + 1;\n                    sum = hdr[7:0] + hdr[15:8] + hdr[23:16];\n                    for (j = 0; j < 7; j = j + 1) sum = sum + sb0[8*j +: 8] + sb1[8*j +: 8];\n                    if (sum % 256 != 0) fail(\"audio InfoFrame checksum\");\n                    if (hdr[23:8] != 16'h0A01 || sb0[15:8] != 8'h01) fail(\"audio InfoFrame fields\");\n                end\n                default: fail(\"unknown packet type\");\n            endcase\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        now = now + 1;\n        if (db !== rb || dg !== rg || dr !== rr) begin if (now > 4) fail(\"HDMI = 0 differs from three tmds_encoders\"); end\n        b = wb; g = wg; r = wr;\n        cb = ctrl_value(b); cg = ctrl_value(g); cr = ctrl_value(r);\n        tb_ = terc4_value(b); tg = terc4_value(g); tr = terc4_value(r);\n        if (!started) begin if (cb[2] && cg[2] && cr[2]) started = 1; end\n        else case (state)\n            S_CTRL, S_VPRE, S_IPRE: begin\n                if (cb[2] && cg[2] && cr[2]) begin\n                    if (cg[1:0] == 2'b01 && cr[1:0] == 2'b00) begin run = (state == S_VPRE) ? run + 1 : 1; state = S_VPRE; end\n                    else if (cg[1:0] == 2'b01 && cr[1:0] == 2'b01) begin run = (state == S_IPRE) ? run + 1 : 1; state = S_IPRE; end\n                    else if (cg[1:0] == 2'b00 && cr[1:0] == 2'b00) begin\n                        if (state != S_CTRL) fail(\"preamble cut short\");\n                        state = S_CTRL;\n                    end else fail(\"control bits on green or red outside a preamble\");\n                    if (cb[0] && !hs_was) hs_rises = hs_rises + 1;\n                    hs_was = cb[0];\n                end else if (state == S_VPRE && run == 8 && b == 10'b1011001100 && g == 10'b0100110011 && r == 10'b1011001100) begin\n                    state = S_VGUARD; run = 1;\n                end else if (state == S_IPRE && run == 8 && g == 10'b0100110011 && r == 10'b0100110011 && tb_[4]) begin\n                    if (tb_[3:2] != 2'b11) fail(\"leading guard band bits\");\n                    state = S_ILEAD; run = 1;\n                end else fail(\"a character outside any period\");\n            end\n            S_VGUARD: begin\n                if (b == 10'b1011001100 && g == 10'b0100110011 && r == 10'b1011001100) run = run + 1;\n                else begin\n                    if (run != 2) fail(\"video guard band not two characters\");\n                    state = S_VIDEO; lines = lines + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            S_ILEAD: begin\n                if (g == 10'b0100110011 && r == 10'b0100110011 && tb_[4]) run = run + 1;\n                else begin\n                    if (run != 2) fail(\"leading guard band not two characters\");\n                    state = S_ISLAND; k = 0; first = 1;\n                end\n            end\n            S_ISLAND: ;\n            S_ITRAIL: begin\n                if (g == 10'b0100110011 && r == 10'b0100110011 && tb_[4]) begin run = run + 1; if (tb_[3:2] != 2'b11) fail(\"trailing guard band bits\"); end\n                else begin\n                    if (run != 2) fail(\"trailing guard band not two characters\");\n                    state = S_CTRL;\n                    if (!(cb[2] && cg[2] && cr[2])) fail(\"no control period after an island\");\n                end\n            end\n        endcase\n        // Video and island characters (also the first of each, taken over from the transitions above).\n        if (state == S_VIDEO) begin\n            if (cb[2] && cg[2] && cr[2]) begin\n                state = S_CTRL;\n                if (cb[0] && !hs_was) hs_rises = hs_rises + 1;\n                hs_was = cb[0];\n            end else begin\n                if ({tmds_value(r), tmds_value(g), tmds_value(b)} != sent_pixel[pixel_out % 65536]) fail(\"pixel\");\n                pixel_out = pixel_out + 1;\n            end\n        end\n        if (state == S_ISLAND) begin\n            if (!(tb_[4] && tg[4] && tr[4])) begin\n                if (g == 10'b0100110011 && r == 10'b0100110011 && tb_[4] && k == 0) begin\n                    state = S_ITRAIL; run = 1; if (tb_[3:2] != 2'b11) fail(\"trailing guard band bits\");\n                end else fail(\"not TERC4 inside an island\");\n            end else begin\n                if (tb_[3] != !first) fail(\"channel 0 bit 3\");\n                first = 0;\n                seen1[tg[3:0]] = 1; seen2[tr[3:0]] = 1;\n                if (tb_[0] && !hs_was) hs_rises = hs_rises + 1;\n                hs_was = tb_[0];\n                hdr[k] = tb_[2];\n                sb0[2*k] = tg[0]; sb1[2*k] = tg[1]; sb2[2*k] = tg[2]; sb3[2*k] = tg[3];\n                sb0[2*k+1] = tr[0]; sb1[2*k+1] = tr[1]; sb2[2*k+1] = tr[2]; sb3[2*k+1] = tr[3];\n                k = k + 1;\n                if (k == 32) begin check_packet; k = 0; end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        repeat (FRAMES * H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL) @(posedge clk);\n        $display(\"lines %0d, pixels %0d of %0d, packets %0d (ACR %0d, audio %0d with %0d full, AVI %0d, audio InfoFrame %0d), samples %0d of %0d, hsync %0d\",\n                 lines, pixel_out, pixel_in, packets, acr, audio_packets, full_packets, avi, aif, audio_out, audio_in, hs_rises);\n        if (seen1 != 16'hFFFF || seen2 != 16'hFFFF) begin $display(\"TERC4 values seen: %b %b\", seen1, seen2); fail(\"not every TERC4 value was used\"); end\n        if (lines < (FRAMES - 1) * V_ACTIVE) fail(\"too few video lines\");\n        if (pixel_in - pixel_out > H_ACTIVE) fail(\"pixels missing\");\n        if (audio_in - audio_out > 8) fail(\"samples missing\");\n        if (audio_in < (FRAMES * V_TOTAL - 3) * 4 - 8) fail(\"audio_ready not at 48 kHz\");    // requests start after three lines\n        if (acr < FRAMES * V_TOTAL * H_TOTAL / (PIXEL_HZ / 1000) - 1) fail(\"too few ACR packets\");\n        if (avi < FRAMES - 1 || aif < FRAMES - 1) fail(\"too few InfoFrames\");\n        if (full_packets < audio_packets / 2) fail(\"too few full audio packets\");\n        if (hs_rises < (FRAMES - 1) * V_TOTAL) fail(\"hsync lost in the islands\");\n        if (audio_ready_dvi !== 1'b0) fail(\"audio_ready with HDMI = 0\");\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\"); else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out/README.md" : "# HDMI/DVI output (7-series)\n\nPixels and syncs to an HDMI or DVI monitor from an AMD Xilinx 7-series FPGA: three TMDS\nencoders (`tmds_encoder`), a 10:1 OSERDESE2 serialiser per lane and differential TMDS_33\noutputs, plus the clock lane. DVI signalling by default, which every HDMI\nmonitor shows.\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Sound.** `HDMI = 1` sends HDMI instead (`hdmi_encoder`): the AVI and Audio InfoFrames, and 48 kHz stereo when you\ninstantiate `hdmi_out_audio` (or `hdmi_out_bufr_audio`) — the same ports plus `audio_l`, `audio_r` (signed 16-bit) and `audio_ready`, which\npulses when the core takes the next pair. `PIXEL_HZ` must be the pixel clock the PLL makes (it sets HDMI's audio clock).\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(1), .PIXEL_HZ(74250000), .VIC(7'd4), .ASPECT(2'b10)) u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .audio_l(left), .audio_r(right), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Clocks.** `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (DDR: ten bits per pixel), both from one\nMMCM or PLL so they stay in phase — GateLab's Add Clock keeps outputs that are whole multiples\nat that exact ratio. On the global buffers that covers 720p60 and 1080p30 (74.25 and 371.25\nMHz). For 1080p60 use `hdmi_out_bufr` (below).\n\n**Reset** is synchronous to `pixel_clk`: hold it until the MMCM has locked.\n\n**Pins.** Each lane is a P/N pair of one bank (3.3 V), `IOSTANDARD TMDS_33` on both. Lane 0 is\nblue, 1 green, 2 red. Video timing (`video_timing`) delivers `de` and the syncs; send the colour\nof the same pixel in the same cycle.\n\n## 1080p60: `hdmi_out_bufr`\n\n1080p60 needs 148.5 and 742.5 MHz. The serial clock is over a BUFG's limit, so the serialisers\ntake it from a BUFIO and their word clock from a BUFR dividing the same MMCM output by 5, in the\nclock region of the TMDS pins. A BUFR reaches only that region, and nextpnr-xilinx doesn't know\nit: logic on the BUFR's clock may be placed in the next region and get its clock over ordinary\nwires (GateLab warns after place-and-route). `hdmi_out_bufr` therefore keeps everything but the\nserialisers on a global clock:\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out_bufr u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk),    // BUFG, 148.5 MHz: the MMCM's CLKOUT1 (VCO ÷ 5)\n    .lane_clk(lane_clk),      // BUFR dividing the serial clock by 5: the same frequency\n    .serial_clk(serial_clk),  // BUFIO, 742.5 MHz: CLKOUT0 (VCO ÷ 1)\n    .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\nThe encoded words cross from `pixel_clk` to `lane_clk` through a ring of eight, read half the\nring behind the writer: the clocks have the same frequency and any phase. Keep your own logic\non `pixel_clk`, never on `lane_clk`. The 1080p60 starter template shows the whole clocking.\n\n**The last pixel of a line.** 1080p60 runs about 20 % over the HR banks' specified data rate. It\nworked on GateLab's test board and monitor, with one catch: a line whose last pixel was white came\nout one pixel to the side of the others (lines ending in black were fine; HDMI's video guard band\ndidn't help). `BLACK_LAST_PIXEL` (default 1) therefore sends each line's last pixel black; set it to 0\nfor the picture unchanged. Other boards and monitors may behave differently.\n\n**Simulation** uses a behavioural model of the serialiser (no `SYNTHESIS` define): the testbench\ndecodes the four lanes like a monitor.\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"hdmi_encoder\",\n    \"tmds_encoder\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Pixels and syncs out of an HDMI or DVI connector on AMD Xilinx 7-series: three TMDS encoders, 10:1 OSERDESE2 serialisers and TMDS_33 differential outputs, plus the clock lane. Needs a pixel clock and a five-times serial clock from one MMCM or PLL (720p60: 74.25 and 371.25 MHz). For 1080p60 the module hdmi_out_bufr keeps the design on a global clock and only the serialisers on a BUFR. DVI by default; with HDMI = 1, HDMI with the AVI and Audio InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); for 48 kHz stereo sound instantiate hdmi_out_audio (or hdmi_out_bufr_audio), the same with audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready.\",\n  \"id\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"1080p\",\n    \"1080p60\",\n    \"720p\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"bufr\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"hdmi-audio\",\n    \"oserdes\",\n    \"tmds\",\n    \"video\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel und Synchronsignale an einen HDMI- oder DVI-Anschluss bei AMD Xilinx 7-Serie: drei TMDS-Encoder, 10:1-OSERDESE2-Serialisierer und differentielle TMDS_33-Ausgänge, dazu der Takt-Kanal. Braucht einen Pixeltakt und einen fünffachen seriellen Takt aus einem MMCM oder PLL (720p60: 74,25 und 371,25 MHz). Für 1080p60 hält das Modul hdmi_out_bufr das Design auf einem globalen Takt und nur die Serialisierer auf einem BUFR. Standardmässig DVI; mit HDMI = 1 HDMI mit den AVI- und Audio-InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); für 48-kHz-Stereoton hdmi_out_audio (oder hdmi_out_bufr_audio) instanziieren, dasselbe mit audio_l, audio_r und audio_ready.\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI-Ausgang (7-Serie)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI output (7-series)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI, as before\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 74250000,\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel clock in Hz (HDMI's audio clock: CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PIXEL_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"CEA-861 video code for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60)\",\n      \"name\" : \"VIC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Picture aspect for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 4:3, 2 = 16:9)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ASPECT\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock (74.25 MHz for 720p60 and 1080p30)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"pixel_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Five times the pixel clock, in phase with it\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"serial_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Synchronous to pixel_clk: hold until the clocks are stable\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"reset\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: high in the visible area\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, P (TMDS_33)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_p\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, N\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes 0 (blue), 1 (green), 2 (red), P\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_p\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes, N\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_n\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"requiresFamilies\" : [\n    \"xilinx7\"\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.2.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out/rtl/hdmi_out.v" : "// DVI/HDMI output for AMD Xilinx 7-series (DVI signalling: pixels and syncs, no audio or info\n// frames — every HDMI monitor takes it). Three TMDS encoders (tmds_encoder) and four lanes, each a\n// 10:1 OSERDESE2 master/slave pair in DDR into an OBUFDS: blue (lane 0, with hsync on c0 and vsync\n// on c1), green (1), red (2) and the clock lane (five ones, five zeros per pixel).\n//\n// Clocks: `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (DDR: ten bits per pixel), both from one MMCM or\n// PLL so they're in phase. On the global buffers (BUFG) that's 720p60 and 1080p30 (74.25 / 371.25\n// MHz). 1080p60's 742.5 MHz serial clock is over a BUFG's limit: use `hdmi_out_bufr` below.\n// `reset` is synchronous to `pixel_clk`: hold it until the clocks are stable.\n//\n// The pins take IOSTANDARD TMDS_33 (P and N of each pair, a 3.3 V bank). Bits go out from bit 0.\n// Simulation (no SYNTHESIS) sends the same bits from a behavioural model on both edges of\n// `serial_clk`, so a testbench can decode the lanes like a monitor.\n\n// hdmi_out without sound: hdmi_out_audio with silence, the ports of the cores before HDMI audio (a design that\n// sends sound instantiates hdmi_out_audio, which has audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready). HDMI = 1 still sends the\n// InfoFrames, data islands and all.\nmodule hdmi_out #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    hdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_out (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p),\n        .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n),\n        .audio_l(16'd0), .audio_r(16'd0), .audio_ready());\nendmodule\n\nmodule hdmi_out_audio #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_l,             // HDMI = 1: signed samples, taken while audio_ready is high (48 kHz)\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_r,\n    output wire       audio_ready,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    wire [9:0] word_blue, word_green, word_red;\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_encoder (\n        .clk(pixel_clk), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n        .word_blue(word_blue), .word_green(word_green), .word_red(word_red));\n\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_blue  (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_blue),  .out_p(tmds_d_p[0]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[0]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_green (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_green), .out_p(tmds_d_p[1]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[1]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_red   (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_red),   .out_p(tmds_d_p[2]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[2]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_clock (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(10'b0000011111), .out_p(tmds_clk_p), .out_n(tmds_clk_n));\nendmodule\n\n// hdmi_out for serial clocks over a BUFG's limit (1080p60: 742.5 MHz). The serialisers take\n// `serial_clk` from a BUFIO and `lane_clk` from a BUFR dividing the same MMCM output by 5, in the\n// TMDS pins' clock region. A BUFR reaches that one region only, and nextpnr-xilinx doesn't know:\n// logic it clocks may land in the next region and get its clock over data wires (GateLab's HDMI\n// follow-up, 2026-10-13). So the encoders and everything the design feeds in run on `pixel_clk`, a\n// BUFG at the same frequency from the same MMCM, and only the serialisers, a read pointer and a\n// reset synchroniser are on `lane_clk`. The words cross through a ring of eight, written on\n// `pixel_clk` and read on `lane_clk` about four entries behind: the two clocks have the same\n// frequency, their phase isn't known.\n//\n// BLACK_LAST_PIXEL (default 1) sends each line's last pixel black. At 1080p60 the link runs about\n// 20 % over the HR banks' rating, and on GateLab's test monitor a line whose last pixel was white\n// came out one pixel to the side of the others; a black last pixel cured it. 0 sends the picture\n// unchanged. One register stage in front of the encoders either way, for timing: the pixels come\n// out a clock later than with hdmi_out.\n// hdmi_out_bufr without sound: hdmi_out_bufr_audio with silence, the ports of the cores before HDMI audio (a design that\n// sends sound instantiates hdmi_out_bufr_audio, which has audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready). HDMI = 1 still sends the\n// InfoFrames, data islands and all.\nmodule hdmi_out_bufr #(\n    parameter BLACK_LAST_PIXEL = 1,\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       lane_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    hdmi_out_bufr_audio #(.BLACK_LAST_PIXEL(BLACK_LAST_PIXEL), .HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_out (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .lane_clk(lane_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .red(red), .green(green),\n        .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n),\n        .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p), .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n),\n        .audio_l(16'd0), .audio_r(16'd0), .audio_ready());\nendmodule\n\nmodule hdmi_out_bufr_audio #(\n    parameter BLACK_LAST_PIXEL = 1,\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       lane_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_l,             // HDMI = 1: signed samples, taken while audio_ready is high (48 kHz)\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_r,\n    output wire       audio_ready,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    // One stage: a pixel whose successor has `de` low is the line's last.\n    reg [23:0] colour = 24'd0;\n    reg de_q = 1'b0, hsync_q = 1'b0, vsync_q = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge pixel_clk) begin\n        colour <= {red, green, blue};\n        de_q <= de;\n        hsync_q <= hsync;\n        vsync_q <= vsync;\n    end\n    wire last = BLACK_LAST_PIXEL != 0 && de_q && !de;\n    wire [23:0] shown = last ? 24'd0 : colour;\n\n    wire [9:0] word_blue, word_green, word_red;\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_encoder (\n        .clk(pixel_clk), .red(shown[23:16]), .green(shown[15:8]), .blue(shown[7:0]), .de(de_q), .hsync(hsync_q), .vsync(vsync_q),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n        .word_blue(word_blue), .word_green(word_green), .word_red(word_red));\n\n    // The reset reaches lane_clk through two flip-flops, so the read pointer starts a little after\n    // the write pointer. Starting the write pointer at 3 puts the entry being read half the ring\n    // away from the entry being written (the testbench measures it for every phase), so a lane\n    // clock more or less from the synchroniser can't make them meet.\n    reg [1:0] lane_reset_sync = 2'b11;\n    always @(posedge lane_clk or posedge reset) begin\n        if (reset) lane_reset_sync <= 2'b11;\n        else lane_reset_sync <= {lane_reset_sync[0], 1'b0};\n    end\n    wire lane_reset = lane_reset_sync[1];\n\n    reg [29:0] ring [0:7];\n    reg [2:0] write_at = 3'd3, read_at = 3'd0;\n    integer i;\n    initial for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) ring[i] = 30'd0;   // LUT RAM starts at 0\n    always @(posedge pixel_clk) begin\n        ring[write_at] <= {word_red, word_green, word_blue};\n        write_at <= reset ? 3'd3 : write_at + 3'd1;\n    end\n    always @(posedge lane_clk) read_at <= lane_reset ? 3'd0 : read_at + 3'd1;\n    wire [29:0] words = ring[read_at];\n\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_blue  (.pixel_clk(lane_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(lane_reset), .word(words[9:0]),   .out_p(tmds_d_p[0]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[0]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_green (.pixel_clk(lane_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(lane_reset), .word(words[19:10]), .out_p(tmds_d_p[1]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[1]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_red   (.pixel_clk(lane_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(lane_reset), .word(words[29:20]), .out_p(tmds_d_p[2]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[2]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_clock (.pixel_clk(lane_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(lane_reset), .word(10'b0000011111), .out_p(tmds_clk_p), .out_n(tmds_clk_n));\nendmodule\n\n// One TMDS lane: a 10-bit word per pixel clock, bit 0 first, out of a differential pair.\nmodule hdmi_out_lane (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [9:0] word,\n    output wire       out_p,\n    output wire       out_n\n);\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    wire serial, shift1, shift2;\n    OSERDESE2 #(\n        .DATA_RATE_OQ(\"DDR\"), .DATA_RATE_TQ(\"SDR\"), .DATA_WIDTH(10), .SERDES_MODE(\"MASTER\"),\n        .TRISTATE_WIDTH(1), .TBYTE_CTL(\"FALSE\"), .TBYTE_SRC(\"FALSE\")\n    ) u_master (\n        .CLK(serial_clk), .CLKDIV(pixel_clk), .RST(reset), .OCE(1'b1), .OQ(serial),\n        .D1(word[0]), .D2(word[1]), .D3(word[2]), .D4(word[3]), .D5(word[4]), .D6(word[5]), .D7(word[6]), .D8(word[7]),\n        .SHIFTIN1(shift1), .SHIFTIN2(shift2), .SHIFTOUT1(), .SHIFTOUT2(),\n        .T1(1'b0), .T2(1'b0), .T3(1'b0), .T4(1'b0), .TCE(1'b0), .TBYTEIN(1'b0), .TBYTEOUT(), .TFB(), .TQ(), .OFB()\n    );\n    OSERDESE2 #(\n        .DATA_RATE_OQ(\"DDR\"), .DATA_RATE_TQ(\"SDR\"), .DATA_WIDTH(10), .SERDES_MODE(\"SLAVE\"),\n        .TRISTATE_WIDTH(1), .TBYTE_CTL(\"FALSE\"), .TBYTE_SRC(\"FALSE\")\n    ) u_slave (\n        .CLK(serial_clk), .CLKDIV(pixel_clk), .RST(reset), .OCE(1'b1), .OQ(),\n        .D1(1'b0), .D2(1'b0), .D3(word[8]), .D4(word[9]), .D5(1'b0), .D6(1'b0), .D7(1'b0), .D8(1'b0),\n        .SHIFTIN1(1'b0), .SHIFTIN2(1'b0), .SHIFTOUT1(shift1), .SHIFTOUT2(shift2),\n        .T1(1'b0), .T2(1'b0), .T3(1'b0), .T4(1'b0), .TCE(1'b0), .TBYTEIN(1'b0), .TBYTEOUT(), .TFB(), .TQ(), .OFB()\n    );\n    OBUFDS u_out (.I(serial), .O(out_p), .OB(out_n));\n`else\n    // The word of each pixel clock goes out during the next one, a bit per edge of serial_clk\n    // from bit 0 (a counter of serial edges, back at 0 on every rising pixel_clk edge).\n    reg [9:0] held = 10'd0;\n    reg [3:0] index = 4'd0;\n    always @(posedge pixel_clk) held <= word;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk or negedge serial_clk) index <= (reset || index == 4'd9) ? 4'd0 : index + 4'd1;\n    assign out_p = held[index];\n    assign out_n = ~held[index];\n`endif\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out/tb/hdmi_out_bufr_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for hdmi_out_bufr (its simulation lanes). Four copies run side by side:\n// pixel_clk lags lane_clk by 0, 30 % and 70 % of a period with BLACK_LAST_PIXEL on, and by 50 %\n// with it off — the core must not depend on the phase between its two word clocks. Each copy\n// sends a tiny mode (12 × 4 active, 20 × 6 clocks) with colours that differ per channel, pixel and\n// line; a receiver finds the word boundary from the clock lane like a monitor, decodes each\n// channel and compares every pixel, data enable and sync with what was sent, at the latency the\n// first data word shows. With BLACK_LAST_PIXEL each line's last pixel must arrive black.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule hdmi_out_bufr_check #(\n    parameter real PHASE = 0.0,       // pixel_clk's lag behind lane_clk, in ns (period 50)\n    parameter BLACK_LAST_PIXEL = 1\n) (\n    output reg done = 1'b0,\n    output integer errors\n);\n    // The serial clock (10 ns, DDR: ten bits per word), the BUFR's lane clock counted down from\n    // its edges, and the BUFG's pixel clock: the same frequency, a fixed phase behind.\n    reg serial_clk = 1'b0, lane_clk = 1'b0, pixel_clk = 1'b0;\n    integer serial_edges = 0;\n    always #5 serial_clk = ~serial_clk;\n    always @(serial_clk) begin\n        if (serial_edges % 5 == 0) lane_clk = ~lane_clk;\n        serial_edges = serial_edges + 1;\n    end\n    always @(lane_clk) pixel_clk <= #(PHASE) lane_clk;\n\n    reg reset = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] red = 8'd0, green = 8'd0, blue = 8'd0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    wire tmds_clk_p, tmds_clk_n;\n    wire [2:0] tmds_d_p, tmds_d_n;\n\n    hdmi_out_bufr #(.BLACK_LAST_PIXEL(BLACK_LAST_PIXEL)) dut (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .lane_clk(lane_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n        .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p), .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n)\n    );\n\n    // The crossing: the word the serialisers take may change only when the read pointer moves (a\n    // lane_clk edge). A change at any other time is a write to the entry being read — on hardware\n    // a word caught half-written. (At a phase of 0 the writes fall on lane_clk edges, so the other\n    // phases are the ones that catch it.)\n    realtime lane_edge = -1.0;\n    integer collisions = 0;\n    always @(posedge lane_clk) lane_edge = $realtime;\n    always @(dut.u_out.words) begin\n        if (!reset && !dut.u_out.lane_reset && $realtime != lane_edge) begin\n            if (collisions == 0) $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): the entry being read was written at %0t\", PHASE, $time);\n            collisions = collisions + 1;\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // What should arrive, one entry per pixel clock: {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue}.\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 20, H_ACTIVE = 12, V_TOTAL = 6, V_ACTIVE = 4, FRAMES = 3;\n    reg [26:0] sent [0:H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES + 63];\n    integer pixels = 0, x = 0, y = 0;\n\n    initial begin\n        errors = 0;\n        repeat (3) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        reset <= 1'b0;\n        while (pixels < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n            // New inputs right after an edge, taken at the next one.\n            #1;\n            de = x < H_ACTIVE && y < V_ACTIVE;\n            hsync = x >= 14 && x < 17;\n            vsync = y == 5;\n            red = x * 17 + y * 3 + 7;\n            green = 8'hA5 ^ (x * 5) ^ (y << 4);\n            blue = 8'd200 - x * 9 - y;\n            sent[pixels] = {de, vsync, hsync, (BLACK_LAST_PIXEL != 0 && de && x == H_ACTIVE - 1) ? 24'd0 : {red, green, blue}};\n            pixels = pixels + 1;\n            x = x + 1;\n            if (x == H_TOTAL) begin x = 0; y = (y + 1) % V_TOTAL; end\n            @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        end\n    end\n\n    function [7:0] decode;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        reg [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            v = word[9] ? ~word[7:0] : word[7:0];\n            decode[0] = v[0];\n            for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) decode[k] = word[8] ? (v[k] ^ v[k - 1]) : ~(v[k] ^ v[k - 1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // Control words carry {c1, c0}; anything else is data (3'b1xx).\n    function [2:0] control;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        begin\n            case (word)\n                10'b1101010100: control = 3'b000;\n                10'b0010101011: control = 3'b001;\n                10'b0101010100: control = 3'b010;\n                10'b1010101011: control = 3'b011;\n                default: control = 3'b100;\n            endcase\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    reg [9:0] lane_clock = 10'd0, lane_blue = 10'd0, lane_green = 10'd0, lane_red = 10'd0;\n    integer received = 0, offset = -1, data_words = 0, black_words = 0, syncs = 0, index;\n    reg seen_blanking = 1'b0;   // like a monitor: lock on the blanking first\n    reg [26:0] wanted;\n\n    always @(serial_clk) begin\n        #2.5;\n        lane_clock = {tmds_clk_p, lane_clock[9:1]};\n        lane_blue  = {tmds_d_p[0], lane_blue[9:1]};\n        lane_green = {tmds_d_p[1], lane_green[9:1]};\n        lane_red   = {tmds_d_p[2], lane_red[9:1]};\n        if (tmds_clk_n !== ~tmds_clk_p || tmds_d_n !== ~tmds_d_p) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): a lane's N side isn't the inverse of its P side\", PHASE);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (!reset && !done && lane_clock == 10'b0000011111) word_boundary;\n    end\n\n    task word_boundary;\n        reg [2:0] cb, cg, cr;\n        begin\n            cb = control(lane_blue);\n            cg = control(lane_green);\n            cr = control(lane_red);\n            if (!cb[2] && !cg[2] && !cr[2]) seen_blanking = 1'b1;\n            if (offset < 0 && seen_blanking && cb[2]) begin\n                // The first data word fixes the latency: the first pixel sent with de high.\n                for (index = 0; index < pixels && sent[index][26] == 1'b0; index = index + 1) ;\n                offset = received - index;\n            end\n            if (offset >= 0 && received - offset < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n                wanted = sent[received - offset];\n                if (wanted[26]) begin\n                    data_words = data_words + 1;\n                    if (wanted[23:0] == 24'd0) black_words = black_words + 1;\n                    if (cb[2] !== 1'b1 || cg[2] !== 1'b1 || cr[2] !== 1'b1) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): pixel %0d: a control word during active video\", PHASE, received - offset);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end else if ({decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue)} !== wanted[23:0]) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): pixel %0d: rgb %h %h %h, expected %h %h %h\", PHASE, received - offset,\n                                 decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue), wanted[23:16], wanted[15:8], wanted[7:0]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    if (cb !== {1'b0, wanted[25], wanted[24]} || cg !== 3'b000 || cr !== 3'b000) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): pixel %0d: control words b %b g %b r %b, sent vsync %b hsync %b\", PHASE,\n                                 received - offset, cb, cg, cr, wanted[25], wanted[24]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (wanted[24] || wanted[25]) syncs = syncs + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            received = received + 1;\n            if (errors > 5) done = 1'b1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        wait (pixels == H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES);\n        repeat (12) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        if (offset < 0) begin $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): no data word was received\", PHASE); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (data_words < H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES - H_ACTIVE) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): %0d data words checked, expected about %0d\", PHASE, data_words, H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        // Only the blacked-out last pixels are black: the colours above never are.\n        if (BLACK_LAST_PIXEL != 0 && black_words < V_ACTIVE * FRAMES - 1) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): only %0d black last pixels checked\", PHASE, black_words); errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (syncs < 3 * FRAMES) begin $display(\"FAIL (phase %0.1f ns): only %0d sync words checked\", PHASE, syncs); errors = errors + 1; end\n        done = 1'b1;\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule hdmi_out_bufr_tb;\n    wire [3:0] done;\n    integer errors [0:3];\n    hdmi_out_bufr_check #(.PHASE(0.0),  .BLACK_LAST_PIXEL(1)) check_0  (.done(done[0]), .errors(errors[0]));\n    hdmi_out_bufr_check #(.PHASE(15.0), .BLACK_LAST_PIXEL(1)) check_30 (.done(done[1]), .errors(errors[1]));\n    hdmi_out_bufr_check #(.PHASE(35.0), .BLACK_LAST_PIXEL(1)) check_70 (.done(done[2]), .errors(errors[2]));\n    hdmi_out_bufr_check #(.PHASE(25.0), .BLACK_LAST_PIXEL(0)) check_50 (.done(done[3]), .errors(errors[3]));\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"hdmi_out_bufr_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(1, check_30);\n        $dumpvars(1, check_30.dut);\n        wait (&done);\n        if (errors[0] + errors[1] + errors[2] + errors[3] == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out/tb/hdmi_out_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for hdmi_out (its simulation lanes): a tiny mode (12 × 4 active, 20 × 6\n// clocks, syncs in the blanking) with colours that differ per channel, pixel and line. A receiver\n// samples the four lanes in the middle of every bit, finds the word boundary from the clock lane\n// (0000011111, bit 0 first) like a monitor, decodes each channel and compares every pixel, data\n// enable and sync with what was sent — in order, at the latency the first data word shows.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule hdmi_out_tb;\n    // Pixel clock 50 ns, serial clock 10 ns (five times, DDR: ten bits per pixel), in phase.\n    reg pixel_clk = 1'b0, serial_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #25 pixel_clk = ~pixel_clk;\n    always #5 serial_clk = ~serial_clk;\n\n    reg reset = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] red = 8'd0, green = 8'd0, blue = 8'd0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    wire tmds_clk_p, tmds_clk_n;\n    wire [2:0] tmds_d_p, tmds_d_n;\n\n    hdmi_out dut (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n        .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p), .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n)\n    );\n\n    // What was sent, one entry per pixel clock: {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue}.\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 20, H_ACTIVE = 12, V_TOTAL = 6, V_ACTIVE = 4, FRAMES = 3;\n    reg [26:0] sent [0:H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES + 63];\n    integer pixels = 0, x = 0, y = 0;\n\n    initial begin\n        repeat (3) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        reset <= 1'b0;\n        while (pixels < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n            // New inputs right after an edge, taken by the encoders at the next one.\n            #1;\n            de = x < H_ACTIVE && y < V_ACTIVE;\n            hsync = x >= 14 && x < 17;\n            vsync = y == 5;\n            red = x * 17 + y * 3;\n            green = 8'hA5 ^ (x * 5) ^ (y << 4);\n            blue = 8'd200 - x * 9 - y;\n            sent[pixels] = {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue};\n            pixels = pixels + 1;\n            x = x + 1;\n            if (x == H_TOTAL) begin x = 0; y = (y + 1) % V_TOTAL; end\n            @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        end\n    end\n\n    // The receiver.\n    function [7:0] decode;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        reg [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            v = word[9] ? ~word[7:0] : word[7:0];\n            decode[0] = v[0];\n            for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) decode[k] = word[8] ? (v[k] ^ v[k - 1]) : ~(v[k] ^ v[k - 1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // Control words carry {c1, c0}; anything else is data. Returns 3'b1xx for data.\n    function [2:0] control;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        begin\n            case (word)\n                10'b1101010100: control = 3'b000;\n                10'b0010101011: control = 3'b001;\n                10'b0101010100: control = 3'b010;\n                10'b1010101011: control = 3'b011;\n                default: control = 3'b100;\n            endcase\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    reg [9:0] lane_clock = 10'd0, lane_blue = 10'd0, lane_green = 10'd0, lane_red = 10'd0;\n    integer errors = 0, received = 0, offset = -1, data_words = 0, syncs = 0, index;\n    reg [26:0] wanted;\n\n    always @(serial_clk) begin\n        #2.5;\n        // Bit 0 arrives first: shift in from the top.\n        lane_clock = {tmds_clk_p, lane_clock[9:1]};\n        lane_blue  = {tmds_d_p[0], lane_blue[9:1]};\n        lane_green = {tmds_d_p[1], lane_green[9:1]};\n        lane_red   = {tmds_d_p[2], lane_red[9:1]};\n        if (tmds_clk_n !== ~tmds_clk_p || tmds_d_n !== ~tmds_d_p) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: a lane's N side isn't the inverse of its P side\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (!reset && lane_clock == 10'b0000011111) word_boundary;\n    end\n\n    task word_boundary;\n        reg [2:0] cb, cg, cr;\n        begin\n            cb = control(lane_blue);\n            cg = control(lane_green);\n            cr = control(lane_red);\n            // The first data word fixes the latency: it's the first pixel sent with de high.\n            if (offset < 0 && !cb[2]) begin\n                // still blanking\n            end else if (offset < 0) begin\n                for (index = 0; index < pixels && sent[index][26] == 1'b0; index = index + 1) ;\n                offset = received - index;\n            end\n            if (offset >= 0 && received - offset < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n                wanted = sent[received - offset];\n                if (wanted[26]) begin\n                    data_words = data_words + 1;\n                    if (cb[2] !== 1'b1 || cg[2] !== 1'b1 || cr[2] !== 1'b1) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: a control word during active video\", received - offset);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end else if ({decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue)} !== wanted[23:0]) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: rgb %h %h %h, sent %h %h %h\", received - offset,\n                                 decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue), wanted[23:16], wanted[15:8], wanted[7:0]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    // Blanking: blue carries {vsync, hsync}, the others 00.\n                    if (cb !== {1'b0, wanted[25], wanted[24]} || cg !== 3'b000 || cr !== 3'b000) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: control words b %b g %b r %b, sent vsync %b hsync %b\", received - offset,\n                                 cb, cg, cr, wanted[25], wanted[24]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (wanted[24] || wanted[25]) syncs = syncs + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            received = received + 1;\n            if (errors > 5) begin $display(\"FAIL: too many errors\"); $finish; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"hdmi_out_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, hdmi_out_tb);\n        wait (pixels == H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES);\n        repeat (8) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        if (offset < 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: no data word was received\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (data_words < H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES - H_ACTIVE) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d data words checked, expected about %0d\", data_words, H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (syncs < 3 * FRAMES) begin $display(\"FAIL: only %0d sync words checked\", syncs); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_ecp5/README.md" : "# HDMI/DVI output (ECP5)\n\nPixels and syncs to an HDMI or DVI monitor from a Lattice ECP5: three TMDS encoders\n(`tmds_encoder`) and four lanes, plus the clock lane. DVI signalling by default,\nwhich every HDMI monitor shows. The same module (`hdmi_out`) and ports as the 7-series core, so a\ndesign moves between them.\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Sound.** `HDMI = 1` sends HDMI instead (`hdmi_encoder`): the AVI and Audio InfoFrames, and 48 kHz stereo when you\ninstantiate `hdmi_out_audio` — the same ports plus `audio_l`, `audio_r` (signed 16-bit) and `audio_ready`, which\npulses when the core takes the next pair. `PIXEL_HZ` must be the pixel clock the PLL makes (it sets HDMI's audio clock).\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(1), .PIXEL_HZ(74250000), .VIC(7'd4), .ASPECT(2'b10)) u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .audio_l(left), .audio_r(right), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Lanes.** ECP5 has no 10:1 serialiser. Each lane is a shift register on `serial_clk` that takes\na word every fifth clock and hands two bits per clock to an `ODDRX1F`. The load comes from a\nfree-running one-hot ring of five, straight from a flip-flop, so each bit is one 2:1 choice: on\na -6 part the serial clock closes at about 410 MHz.\n\n**Clocks.** `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (DDR: ten bits per pixel), both from one\nEHXPLLL. From 25 MHz the PLL can't make 74.25 MHz; GateLab's Add Clock asks for the nearest pair\none VCO reaches exactly, 74 and 370 MHz (0.34 % under, within HDMI's 0.5 %).\n\n**Reset** is synchronous to `pixel_clk` (the encoders); the lanes need none.\n\n**Pins.** P and N are two single-ended `LVCMOS33` outputs, N driven with the inverted bits by its\nown `ODDRX1F`, so any two pins of a 3.3 V bank serve, as on the Colorlight i5/i9 extension board.\nLane 0 is blue, 1 green, 2 red.\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_ecp5/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"hdmi_encoder\",\n    \"tmds_encoder\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Pixels and syncs out of an HDMI or DVI connector on Lattice ECP5: three TMDS encoders, per lane a shift register at five times the pixel clock into ODDRX1F outputs, P and N as two LVCMOS33 pins (N inverted), plus the clock lane. Needs a pixel clock and a five-times serial clock from one EHXPLLL (720p60 from 25 MHz: 74 and 370 MHz, 0.34 % under 74.25). DVI by default; with HDMI = 1, HDMI with the AVI and Audio InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); for 48 kHz stereo sound instantiate hdmi_out_audio, the same with audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready. The same module and ports as hdmi_out for 7-series.\",\n  \"id\" : \"hdmi_out_ecp5\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"1080p30\",\n    \"720p\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"ecp5\",\n    \"gpdi\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"hdmi-audio\",\n    \"oddrx1f\",\n    \"tmds\",\n    \"video\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel und Synchronsignale an einen HDMI- oder DVI-Anschluss bei Lattice ECP5: drei TMDS-Encoder, pro Kanal ein Schieberegister mit dem fünffachen Pixeltakt vor ODDRX1F-Ausgängen, P und N als zwei LVCMOS33-Pins (N invertiert), dazu der Takt-Kanal. Braucht einen Pixeltakt und einen fünffachen seriellen Takt aus einem EHXPLLL (720p60 aus 25 MHz: 74 und 370 MHz, 0,34 % unter 74,25). Standardmässig DVI; mit HDMI = 1 HDMI mit den AVI- und Audio-InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); für 48-kHz-Stereoton hdmi_out_audio instanziieren, dasselbe mit audio_l, audio_r und audio_ready. Dasselbe Modul und dieselben Anschlüsse wie hdmi_out für die 7-Serie.\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI-Ausgang (ECP5)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI output (ECP5)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI, as before\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 74250000,\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel clock in Hz (HDMI's audio clock: CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PIXEL_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"CEA-861 video code for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60)\",\n      \"name\" : \"VIC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Picture aspect for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 4:3, 2 = 16:9)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ASPECT\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock (74 MHz for 720p60 from 25 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"pixel_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Five times the pixel clock, in phase with it (the same EHXPLLL)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"serial_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Synchronous to pixel_clk: hold until the clocks are stable\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"reset\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: high in the visible area\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, P (LVCMOS33)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_p\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, N (LVCMOS33, driven inverted)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes 0 (blue), 1 (green), 2 (red), P\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_p\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes, N\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_n\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"requiresFamilies\" : [\n    \"ecp5\"\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"variantOf\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_ecp5/rtl/hdmi_out_ecp5.v" : "// DVI/HDMI output for Lattice ECP5 (Essentials, HDMI on Gowin/ECP5, 2026-10-14): the ports and\n// the encoders of hdmi_out for 7-series, with ECP5's I/O. Three TMDS encoders (tmds_encoder) and\n// four lanes: blue (lane 0, with hsync on c0 and vsync on c1), green (1), red (2) and the clock lane\n// (five ones, five zeros per pixel). ECP5 has no 10:1 serialiser: each lane is a shift register on\n// `serial_clk` that takes a word every fifth clock and hands two bits per clock to an ODDRX1F.\n//\n// Clocks: `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (DDR: ten bits per pixel), both from one EHXPLLL\n// so they stay in phase (GateLab's Add Clock: 25 MHz → 74 and 370 MHz for 720p60, 0.34 % under\n// HDMI's 74.25). The shift register's load comes from a free-running one-hot ring of five, straight\n// from a flip-flop: on a -6 part it closes at about 410 MHz (a counter's compare went into the\n// flip-flops' set/reset logic and reached 280). `reset` is synchronous to `pixel_clk`; the ring\n// needs none.\n//\n// Pins: P and N are two single-ended LVCMOS33 outputs, N driven with the inverted bits by its own\n// ODDRX1F, so any two pins of a bank serve (the Colorlight i5/i9 extension board's HDMI). Bits go\n// out from bit 0. Simulation (no SYNTHESIS) runs the same shift register; only the ODDRX1F is a\n// model (D0 while the clock is high, D1 while low, both taken at the rising edge).\n\n// hdmi_out without sound: hdmi_out_audio with silence, the ports of the cores before HDMI audio (a design that\n// sends sound instantiates hdmi_out_audio, which has audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready). HDMI = 1 still sends the\n// InfoFrames, data islands and all.\nmodule hdmi_out #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    hdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_out (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p),\n        .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n),\n        .audio_l(16'd0), .audio_r(16'd0), .audio_ready());\nendmodule\n\nmodule hdmi_out_audio #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_l,             // HDMI = 1: signed samples, taken while audio_ready is high (48 kHz)\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_r,\n    output wire       audio_ready,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    wire [9:0] word_blue, word_green, word_red;\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_encoder (\n        .clk(pixel_clk), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n        .word_blue(word_blue), .word_green(word_green), .word_red(word_red));\n\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_blue  (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_blue),  .out_p(tmds_d_p[0]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[0]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_green (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_green), .out_p(tmds_d_p[1]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[1]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_red   (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_red),   .out_p(tmds_d_p[2]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[2]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_clock (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(10'b0000011111), .out_p(tmds_clk_p), .out_n(tmds_clk_n));\nendmodule\n\n// One lane: a 10-bit word per pixel clock, bit 0 first, out of two LVCMOS33 pins.\nmodule hdmi_out_lane (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [9:0] word,\n    output wire       out_p,\n    output wire       out_n\n);\n    // Five serial clocks per pixel, two bits per clock: load every fifth clock, shift by two.\n    // N has a shift register of its own with the inverted word, so both ODDRX1Fs take their bits\n    // straight from flip-flops: an inverter between them was the 370 MHz path in a larger design\n    // (354 MHz on the i9 with the image, text and sprite template). Both registers rotate rather\n    // than shift constants in: a constant made Yosys load those bits through the flip-flops'\n    // set/reset (LSR), and the ring's path through a LUT into LSR missed 370 MHz in the test card\n    // (322–357 MHz). The bits rotated round are reloaded before they go out.\n    reg [4:0] ring = 5'b00001;\n    reg [9:0] shift = 10'd0;\n    reg [9:0] shift_n = 10'h3FF;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk) begin\n        ring <= {ring[3:0], ring[4]};\n        shift <= ring[4] ? word : {shift[1:0], shift[9:2]};\n        shift_n <= ring[4] ? ~word : {shift_n[1:0], shift_n[9:2]};\n    end\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    ODDRX1F u_p (.SCLK(serial_clk), .RST(1'b0), .D0(shift[0]), .D1(shift[1]), .Q(out_p));\n    ODDRX1F u_n (.SCLK(serial_clk), .RST(1'b0), .D0(shift_n[0]), .D1(shift_n[1]), .Q(out_n));\n`else\n    reg [1:0] bits = 2'b00, bits_n = 2'b11;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk) begin\n        bits <= shift[1:0];\n        bits_n <= shift_n[1:0];\n    end\n    assign out_p = serial_clk ? bits[0] : bits[1];\n    assign out_n = serial_clk ? bits_n[0] : bits_n[1];\n`endif\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_ecp5/tb/hdmi_out_ecp5_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for hdmi_out on ECP5 (the real shift registers and load ring; only the\n// ODDRX1F is a model): a tiny mode (12 × 4 active, 20 × 6\n// clocks, syncs in the blanking) with colours that differ per channel, pixel and line. A receiver\n// samples the four lanes in the middle of every bit, finds the word boundary from the clock lane\n// (0000011111, bit 0 first) like a monitor, decodes each channel and compares every pixel, data\n// enable and sync with what was sent — in order, at the latency the first data word shows.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule hdmi_out_ecp5_tb;\n    // Pixel clock 50 ns, serial clock 10 ns (five times, DDR: ten bits per pixel), in phase.\n    reg pixel_clk = 1'b0, serial_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #25 pixel_clk = ~pixel_clk;\n    always #5 serial_clk = ~serial_clk;\n\n    reg reset = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] red = 8'd0, green = 8'd0, blue = 8'd0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    wire tmds_clk_p, tmds_clk_n;\n    wire [2:0] tmds_d_p, tmds_d_n;\n\n    hdmi_out dut (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n        .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p), .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n)\n    );\n\n    // What was sent, one entry per pixel clock: {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue}.\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 20, H_ACTIVE = 12, V_TOTAL = 6, V_ACTIVE = 4, FRAMES = 3;\n    reg [26:0] sent [0:H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES + 63];\n    integer pixels = 0, x = 0, y = 0;\n\n    initial begin\n        repeat (3) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        reset <= 1'b0;\n        while (pixels < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n            // New inputs right after an edge, taken by the encoders at the next one.\n            #1;\n            de = x < H_ACTIVE && y < V_ACTIVE;\n            hsync = x >= 14 && x < 17;\n            vsync = y == 5;\n            red = x * 17 + y * 3;\n            green = 8'hA5 ^ (x * 5) ^ (y << 4);\n            blue = 8'd200 - x * 9 - y;\n            sent[pixels] = {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue};\n            pixels = pixels + 1;\n            x = x + 1;\n            if (x == H_TOTAL) begin x = 0; y = (y + 1) % V_TOTAL; end\n            @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        end\n    end\n\n    // The receiver.\n    function [7:0] decode;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        reg [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            v = word[9] ? ~word[7:0] : word[7:0];\n            decode[0] = v[0];\n            for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) decode[k] = word[8] ? (v[k] ^ v[k - 1]) : ~(v[k] ^ v[k - 1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // Control words carry {c1, c0}; anything else is data. Returns 3'b1xx for data.\n    function [2:0] control;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        begin\n            case (word)\n                10'b1101010100: control = 3'b000;\n                10'b0010101011: control = 3'b001;\n                10'b0101010100: control = 3'b010;\n                10'b1010101011: control = 3'b011;\n                default: control = 3'b100;\n            endcase\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    reg [9:0] lane_clock = 10'd0, lane_blue = 10'd0, lane_green = 10'd0, lane_red = 10'd0;\n    integer errors = 0, received = 0, offset = -1, data_words = 0, syncs = 0, index;\n    reg [26:0] wanted;\n\n    always @(serial_clk) begin\n        #2.5;\n        // Bit 0 arrives first: shift in from the top.\n        lane_clock = {tmds_clk_p, lane_clock[9:1]};\n        lane_blue  = {tmds_d_p[0], lane_blue[9:1]};\n        lane_green = {tmds_d_p[1], lane_green[9:1]};\n        lane_red   = {tmds_d_p[2], lane_red[9:1]};\n        if (tmds_clk_n !== ~tmds_clk_p || tmds_d_n !== ~tmds_d_p) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: a lane's N side isn't the inverse of its P side\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (!reset && lane_clock == 10'b0000011111) word_boundary;\n    end\n\n    task word_boundary;\n        reg [2:0] cb, cg, cr;\n        begin\n            cb = control(lane_blue);\n            cg = control(lane_green);\n            cr = control(lane_red);\n            // The first data word fixes the latency: it's the first pixel sent with de high.\n            if (offset < 0 && !cb[2]) begin\n                // still blanking\n            end else if (offset < 0) begin\n                for (index = 0; index < pixels && sent[index][26] == 1'b0; index = index + 1) ;\n                offset = received - index;\n            end\n            if (offset >= 0 && received - offset < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n                wanted = sent[received - offset];\n                if (wanted[26]) begin\n                    data_words = data_words + 1;\n                    if (cb[2] !== 1'b1 || cg[2] !== 1'b1 || cr[2] !== 1'b1) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: a control word during active video\", received - offset);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end else if ({decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue)} !== wanted[23:0]) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: rgb %h %h %h, sent %h %h %h\", received - offset,\n                                 decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue), wanted[23:16], wanted[15:8], wanted[7:0]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    // Blanking: blue carries {vsync, hsync}, the others 00.\n                    if (cb !== {1'b0, wanted[25], wanted[24]} || cg !== 3'b000 || cr !== 3'b000) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: control words b %b g %b r %b, sent vsync %b hsync %b\", received - offset,\n                                 cb, cg, cr, wanted[25], wanted[24]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (wanted[24] || wanted[25]) syncs = syncs + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            received = received + 1;\n            if (errors > 5) begin $display(\"FAIL: too many errors\"); $finish; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"hdmi_out_ecp5_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, hdmi_out_ecp5_tb);\n        wait (pixels == H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES);\n        repeat (8) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        if (offset < 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: no data word was received\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (data_words < H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES - H_ACTIVE) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d data words checked, expected about %0d\", data_words, H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (syncs < 3 * FRAMES) begin $display(\"FAIL: only %0d sync words checked\", syncs); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gatemate/README.md" : "# HDMI/DVI output (GateMate)\n\nPixels and syncs to an HDMI or DVI monitor from a Cologne Chip GateMate: three TMDS encoders\n(`tmds_encoder`) and four lanes, plus the clock lane. DVI signalling by default,\nwhich every HDMI monitor shows. The same module (`hdmi_out`) and ports as the 7-series core, so a\ndesign moves between them.\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Sound.** `HDMI = 1` sends HDMI instead (`hdmi_encoder`): the AVI and Audio InfoFrames, and 48 kHz stereo when you\ninstantiate `hdmi_out_audio` — the same ports plus `audio_l`, `audio_r` (signed 16-bit) and `audio_ready`, which\npulses when the core takes the next pair. `PIXEL_HZ` must be the pixel clock the PLL makes (it sets HDMI's audio clock).\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(1), .PIXEL_HZ(74250000), .VIC(7'd4), .ASPECT(2'b10)) u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .audio_l(left), .audio_r(right), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Lanes.** Each lane is a shift register on `serial_clk` that takes a word every fifth clock and\nhands two bits per clock to a `CC_ODDR`. GateMate's `CC_ODDR` sends D1 before D0 (found on the\nGateMate EVB; Yosys' simulation model of the primitive says the opposite), so bit 0 goes into D1.\n\n**Clocks.** `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (DDR: ten bits per pixel). GateMate's PLL gives\none frequency, so GateLab's Add Clock makes the serial clock with the PLL and the pixel clock with a\ncounter ÷ 5 through a `CC_BUFG`: from the EVB's 10 MHz, 125 and 25 MHz for 640×480 (0.7 % under\n25.175 MHz; monitors take it). The pixel clock therefore arrives a little after its serial edge.\nThe lanes don't count on their own: they watch a bit that toggles with every pixel clock and take\nthe word three serial clocks after the pixel edge, two before the next one, whatever the\ndivider's delay. The load comes from a flip-flop per lane, and each pin's two bits sit in a register\nnext to its `CC_ODDR` (it takes D1 on the falling edge, so that hop has half a clock). In a full\ndesign the serial clock then closes at 150–180 MHz in nextpnr's worst case for speed mode (126\nneeded for 640×480) and about 110 MHz in economy mode, the GateMate EVB's factory setting (JP12) —\n640×480 ran there on the user's monitor anyway, but move JP12 to speed for the guarantee. The\nencoders at 25 MHz have room to spare.\n\n**Reset** is synchronous to `pixel_clk` (the encoders); the lanes need none.\n\n**Pins.** P and N are two single-ended outputs, N driven with the inverted bits by its own\n`CC_ODDR`, so any two pins of a bank serve: on the GateMate EVB a passive HDMI Pmod on PMODB, whose\npairs aren't the chip's (those sit on Pmod pins 1 and 7). Set `DRIVE=12` and `SLEW=fast` on the\npins (the EVB + HDMI Pmod board does). Lane 0 is blue, 1 green, 2 red.\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gatemate/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"hdmi_encoder\",\n    \"tmds_encoder\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Pixels and syncs out of an HDMI or DVI connector on Cologne Chip GateMate: three TMDS encoders, per lane a shift register at five times the pixel clock into CC_ODDR outputs, P and N as two pins (N inverted), plus the clock lane. The lanes take each word a fixed time after the pixel clock's edge, so the pixel clock may come from a divider in the logic: GateLab's Add Clock makes 125 and 25 MHz from the EVB's 10 MHz for 640×480 (0.7 % under 25.175, which monitors take). DVI by default; with HDMI = 1, HDMI with the AVI and Audio InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); for 48 kHz stereo sound instantiate hdmi_out_audio, the same with audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready. The same module and ports as hdmi_out for 7-series.\",\n  \"id\" : \"hdmi_out_gatemate\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"640x480\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"cc_oddr\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"gatemate\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"hdmi-audio\",\n    \"tmds\",\n    \"vga\",\n    \"video\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel und Synchronsignale an einen HDMI- oder DVI-Anschluss bei Cologne Chip GateMate: drei TMDS-Encoder, pro Kanal ein Schieberegister mit dem fünffachen Pixeltakt vor CC_ODDR-Ausgängen, P und N als zwei Pins (N invertiert), dazu der Takt-Kanal. Die Kanäle übernehmen jedes Wort in festem Abstand zur Flanke des Pixeltakts, daher darf der Pixeltakt aus einem Teiler in der Logik kommen: GateLabs Takt hinzufügen macht aus den 10 MHz des EVB 125 und 25 MHz für 640×480 (0,7 % unter 25,175, was Monitore annehmen). Standardmässig DVI; mit HDMI = 1 HDMI mit den AVI- und Audio-InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); für 48-kHz-Stereoton hdmi_out_audio instanziieren, dasselbe mit audio_l, audio_r und audio_ready. Dasselbe Modul und dieselben Anschlüsse wie hdmi_out für die 7-Serie.\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI-Ausgang (GateMate)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI output (GateMate)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI, as before\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 74250000,\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel clock in Hz (HDMI's audio clock: CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PIXEL_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"CEA-861 video code for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60)\",\n      \"name\" : \"VIC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Picture aspect for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 4:3, 2 = 16:9)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ASPECT\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock (25 MHz for 640×480 from the EVB's 10 MHz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"pixel_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Five times the pixel clock (the PLL's output; the pixel clock is it ÷ 5)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"serial_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Synchronous to pixel_clk: hold until the clocks are stable\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"reset\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: high in the visible area\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, P\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_p\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, N (driven inverted)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes 0 (blue), 1 (green), 2 (red), P\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_p\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes, N\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_n\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"requiresFamilies\" : [\n    \"gatemate\"\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"variantOf\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gatemate/rtl/hdmi_out_gatemate.v" : "// DVI/HDMI output for Cologne Chip GateMate (Essentials, GateMate primitives, 2026-10-15): the ports\n// and the encoders of hdmi_out for 7-series, with GateMate's I/O. Three TMDS encoders\n// (tmds_encoder) and four lanes: blue (lane 0, with hsync on c0 and vsync on c1), green (1), red (2)\n// and the clock lane (five ones, five zeros per pixel). Each lane is a shift register on\n// `serial_clk` that takes a word every fifth clock and hands two bits per clock to a CC_ODDR.\n//\n// Clocks: `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (DDR: ten bits per pixel). On GateMate the PLL\n// makes the serial clock and a counter divides it by five for the pixel clock (GateLab's Add Clock:\n// 10 MHz → 125 and 25 MHz for 640×480), so the pixel clock arrives through a CC_BUFG a few\n// nanoseconds after the serial edge it came from. The lanes therefore don't count on their own: a\n// bit that toggles with every pixel clock is watched on `serial_clk`, and the word is taken two\n// serial clocks after its change shows — three after the pixel edge, two before the next word (the\n// spike's timing on the EVB, now independent of the divider's delay and start). `reset` is\n// synchronous to `pixel_clk`.\n//\n// CC_ODDR sends D1 before D0 within a clock (found on the GateMate EVB, 2026-10-15; Yosys' model of\n// the primitive says the opposite): bit 0 goes into D1. Simulation (no SYNTHESIS) models the pin as\n// it is: bit 0 while the clock is high, bit 1 while it's low, both taken at the rising edge.\n//\n// Pins: P and N are two single-ended outputs, N driven with the inverted bits by its own CC_ODDR, so\n// any two pins of a bank serve (the EVB's Pmod pairs sit on pins 1 and 7, not side by side).\n\n// hdmi_out without sound: hdmi_out_audio with silence, the ports of the cores before HDMI audio (a design that\n// sends sound instantiates hdmi_out_audio, which has audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready). HDMI = 1 still sends the\n// InfoFrames, data islands and all.\nmodule hdmi_out #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    hdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_out (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p),\n        .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n),\n        .audio_l(16'd0), .audio_r(16'd0), .audio_ready());\nendmodule\n\nmodule hdmi_out_audio #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_l,             // HDMI = 1: signed samples, taken while audio_ready is high (48 kHz)\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_r,\n    output wire       audio_ready,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    wire [9:0] word_blue, word_green, word_red;\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_encoder (\n        .clk(pixel_clk), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n        .word_blue(word_blue), .word_green(word_green), .word_red(word_red));\n\n    // The lanes, each with its own load timing (below): no signal runs between them, so each sits next to its pins.\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_blue  (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .word(word_blue),  .out_p(tmds_d_p[0]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[0]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_green (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .word(word_green), .out_p(tmds_d_p[1]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[1]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_red   (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .word(word_red),   .out_p(tmds_d_p[2]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[2]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_clock (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .word(10'b0000011111), .out_p(tmds_clk_p), .out_n(tmds_clk_n));\nendmodule\n\n// One lane: a 10-bit word per pixel, bit 0 first, out of two pins.\nmodule hdmi_out_lane (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire [9:0] word,\n    output wire       out_p,\n    output wire       out_n\n);\n    // A pixel-clock toggle, seen on the serial clock: `load` is high for the serial clock before the third serial edge\n    // after the pixel edge, worked out one stage early (seen[1] ^ seen[0] now is seen[2] ^ seen[1] a clock later). Each\n    // lane has its own: the four start together from the configuration and run on the same clocks, so they stay in\n    // step, and the load reaches the lane's ten shift flip-flops without crossing the chip. (One shared load had to\n    // reach lanes on pins far apart: 107–140 MHz depending on placement, 126 needed; GateMate primitives step 7, and\n    // again with HDMI audio's step 4, where a renamed instance moved it to 111.)\n    reg pixel_toggle = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge pixel_clk) pixel_toggle <= ~pixel_toggle;\n    (* keep *) reg [1:0] seen = 2'b00;\n    (* keep *) reg load = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk) begin\n        seen <= {seen[0], pixel_toggle};\n        load <= seen[1] ^ seen[0];\n    end\n\n    reg [9:0] shift = 10'd0;\n    // The bits for P and for N (inverted) in registers of their own next to the pins: CC_ODDR takes\n    // D1 on the falling edge, so only half a serial clock is left for that hop (GateMate primitives\n    // step 7: from the shift register through the inverter it was the slowest path).\n    (* keep *) reg [1:0] bits_p = 2'b00, bits_n = 2'b11;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk) begin\n        shift <= load ? word : {2'b00, shift[9:2]};\n        bits_p <= shift[1:0];\n        bits_n <= ~shift[1:0];\n    end\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    CC_ODDR u_p (.CLK(serial_clk), .DDR(serial_clk), .D0(bits_p[1]), .D1(bits_p[0]), .Q(out_p));\n    CC_ODDR u_n (.CLK(serial_clk), .DDR(serial_clk), .D0(bits_n[1]), .D1(bits_n[0]), .Q(out_n));\n`else\n    reg [1:0] bits = 2'b00;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk) bits <= bits_p;\n    assign out_p = serial_clk ? bits[0] : bits[1];\n    assign out_n = ~out_p;\n`endif\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gatemate/tb/hdmi_out_gatemate_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for hdmi_out on GateMate (the real shift registers and the load from the\n// pixel clock's toggle; only the CC_ODDR is a model). The pixel clock comes from the serial clock\n// divided by five, as GateLab's GateMate clocks make it, and arrives late through the clock buffer:\n// three copies of the core run with it 0, 3.5 and 7 ns behind a 10 ns serial clock. A tiny mode\n// (12 × 4 active, 20 × 6 clocks, syncs in the blanking) with colours that differ per channel, pixel\n// and line. A receiver per copy samples the four lanes in the middle of every bit, finds the word\n// boundary from the clock lane (0000011111, bit 0 first) like a monitor, decodes each channel and\n// compares every pixel, data enable and sync with what was sent — in order, at the latency the\n// first data word shows.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule hdmi_out_gatemate_tb;\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 20, H_ACTIVE = 12, V_TOTAL = 6, V_ACTIVE = 4, FRAMES = 3;\n\n    // Serial clock 10 ns; the pixel clock high for two of five serial clocks, as the divider makes it.\n    reg serial_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 serial_clk = ~serial_clk;\n    reg [2:0] count = 3'd0;\n    reg pixel_divided = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk) begin\n        count <= count == 3'd4 ? 3'd0 : count + 3'd1;\n        pixel_divided <= count < 3'd2;\n    end\n    wire pixel_early = pixel_divided;\n    wire #3.5 pixel_mid = pixel_divided;\n    wire #7 pixel_late = pixel_divided;\n\n    // The inputs follow the undelayed pixel clock; each copy takes them at its own edge.\n    reg reset = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] red = 8'd0, green = 8'd0, blue = 8'd0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n\n    // What was sent, one entry per pixel clock: {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue}.\n    reg [26:0] sent [0:H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES + 63];\n    integer pixels = 0, x = 0, y = 0;\n\n    initial begin\n        repeat (3) @(posedge pixel_early);\n        reset <= 1'b0;\n        while (pixels < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n            #1;\n            de = x < H_ACTIVE && y < V_ACTIVE;\n            hsync = x >= 14 && x < 17;\n            vsync = y == 5;\n            red = x * 17 + y * 3;\n            green = 8'hA5 ^ (x * 5) ^ (y << 4);\n            blue = 8'd200 - x * 9 - y;\n            sent[pixels] = {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue};\n            pixels = pixels + 1;\n            x = x + 1;\n            if (x == H_TOTAL) begin x = 0; y = (y + 1) % V_TOTAL; end\n            @(posedge pixel_early);\n        end\n    end\n\n    wire [2:0] clk_p, clk_n;\n    wire [2:0] d_p [0:2], d_n [0:2];\n    wire [2:0] pixel_clocks = {pixel_late, pixel_mid, pixel_early};\n    genvar i;\n    generate\n        for (i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) begin : copy\n            hdmi_out dut (\n                .pixel_clk(pixel_clocks[i]), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n                .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n                .tmds_clk_p(clk_p[i]), .tmds_clk_n(clk_n[i]), .tmds_d_p(d_p[i]), .tmds_d_n(d_n[i])\n            );\n            hdmi_out_gatemate_tb_rx #(.COPY(i)) rx (\n                .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .clk_p(clk_p[i]), .clk_n(clk_n[i]), .d_p(d_p[i]), .d_n(d_n[i])\n            );\n        end\n    endgenerate\n\n    integer copies_passed = 0;\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"hdmi_out_gatemate_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(1, hdmi_out_gatemate_tb);\n        wait (pixels == H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES);\n        repeat (8) @(posedge pixel_early);\n        copies_passed = copy[0].rx.passed(1'b0) + copy[1].rx.passed(1'b0) + copy[2].rx.passed(1'b0);\n        if (copies_passed == 3) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAIL: %0d of 3 copies passed\", copies_passed);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n\n// The receiver for one copy; reads what was sent from the testbench.\nmodule hdmi_out_gatemate_tb_rx #(parameter COPY = 0) (\n    input wire       serial_clk,\n    input wire       reset,\n    input wire       clk_p,\n    input wire       clk_n,\n    input wire [2:0] d_p,\n    input wire [2:0] d_n\n);\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 20, H_ACTIVE = 12, V_TOTAL = 6, V_ACTIVE = 4, FRAMES = 3;\n\n    function [7:0] decode;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        reg [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            v = word[9] ? ~word[7:0] : word[7:0];\n            decode[0] = v[0];\n            for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) decode[k] = word[8] ? (v[k] ^ v[k - 1]) : ~(v[k] ^ v[k - 1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // Control words carry {c1, c0}; anything else is data. Returns 3'b1xx for data.\n    function [2:0] control;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        begin\n            case (word)\n                10'b1101010100: control = 3'b000;\n                10'b0010101011: control = 3'b001;\n                10'b0101010100: control = 3'b010;\n                10'b1010101011: control = 3'b011;\n                default: control = 3'b100;\n            endcase\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    reg [9:0] lane_clock = 10'd0, lane_blue = 10'd0, lane_green = 10'd0, lane_red = 10'd0;\n    integer errors = 0, received = 0, offset = -1, data_words = 0, syncs = 0, index;\n    reg [26:0] wanted;\n\n    always @(serial_clk) begin\n        #2.5;\n        lane_clock = {clk_p, lane_clock[9:1]};\n        lane_blue  = {d_p[0], lane_blue[9:1]};\n        lane_green = {d_p[1], lane_green[9:1]};\n        lane_red   = {d_p[2], lane_red[9:1]};\n        if (clk_n !== ~clk_p || d_n !== ~d_p) begin\n            if (errors < 5) $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): a lane's N side isn't the inverse of its P side\", COPY);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (!reset && lane_clock == 10'b0000011111) word_boundary;\n    end\n\n    task word_boundary;\n        reg [2:0] cb, cg, cr;\n        begin\n            cb = control(lane_blue);\n            cg = control(lane_green);\n            cr = control(lane_red);\n            if (offset < 0 && cb[2]) begin\n                for (index = 0; index < hdmi_out_gatemate_tb.pixels && hdmi_out_gatemate_tb.sent[index][26] == 1'b0; index = index + 1) ;\n                offset = received - index;\n            end\n            if (offset >= 0 && received - offset < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n                wanted = hdmi_out_gatemate_tb.sent[received - offset];\n                if (wanted[26]) begin\n                    data_words = data_words + 1;\n                    if (cb[2] !== 1'b1 || cg[2] !== 1'b1 || cr[2] !== 1'b1) begin\n                        if (errors < 5) $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): pixel %0d: a control word during active video\", COPY, received - offset);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end else if ({decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue)} !== wanted[23:0]) begin\n                        if (errors < 5) $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): pixel %0d: rgb %h %h %h, sent %h %h %h\", COPY, received - offset,\n                                                 decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue), wanted[23:16], wanted[15:8], wanted[7:0]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    // Blanking: blue carries {vsync, hsync}, the others 00.\n                    if (cb !== {1'b0, wanted[25], wanted[24]} || cg !== 3'b000 || cr !== 3'b000) begin\n                        if (errors < 5) $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): pixel %0d: control words b %b g %b r %b, sent vsync %b hsync %b\", COPY,\n                                                 received - offset, cb, cg, cr, wanted[25], wanted[24]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (wanted[24] || wanted[25]) syncs = syncs + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            received = received + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // 1 when this copy decoded every pixel and sync it should have.\n    function integer passed;\n        input dummy;\n        begin\n            passed = 1;\n            if (offset < 0) begin $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): no data word was received\", COPY); passed = 0; end\n            if (data_words < H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES - H_ACTIVE) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): %0d data words checked, expected about %0d\", COPY, data_words, H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES);\n                passed = 0;\n            end\n            if (syncs < 3 * FRAMES) begin $display(\"FAIL (copy %0d): only %0d sync words checked\", COPY, syncs); passed = 0; end\n            if (errors != 0) passed = 0;\n        end\n    endfunction\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gowin/README.md" : "# HDMI/DVI output (Gowin)\n\nPixels and syncs to an HDMI or DVI monitor from a Gowin GW1N or GW2A: three TMDS encoders\n(`tmds_encoder`), a 10:1 `OSER10` serialiser per lane and true-LVDS outputs (`TLVDS_OBUF`),\nplus the clock lane. DVI signalling by default, which every HDMI monitor shows.\nThe same module (`hdmi_out`) and ports as the 7-series core, so a design moves between them.\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Sound.** `HDMI = 1` sends HDMI instead (`hdmi_encoder`): the AVI and Audio InfoFrames, and 48 kHz stereo when you\ninstantiate `hdmi_out_audio` — the same ports plus `audio_l`, `audio_r` (signed 16-bit) and `audio_ready`, which\npulses when the core takes the next pair. `PIXEL_HZ` must be the pixel clock the PLL makes (it sets HDMI's audio clock).\n\n```verilog\nhdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(1), .PIXEL_HZ(74250000), .VIC(7'd4), .ASPECT(2'b10)) u_hdmi (\n    .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n    .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .audio_l(left), .audio_r(right), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n    .tmds_clk_p(hdmi_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(hdmi_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(hdmi_d_p), .tmds_d_n(hdmi_d_n)\n);\n```\n\n**Clocks.** `serial_clk` (the OSER10's FCLK) is five times `pixel_clk` (its PCLK): an rPLL for\nthe serial clock and a `CLKDIV` with `DIV_MODE \"5\"` on it for the pixel clock. GateLab's Add\nClock makes that pair when the second output is the first ÷ 5: from the Tang Primer 20K's\n27 MHz, 371.25 and 74.25 MHz exactly (720p60, 1080p30).\n\n**Reset** is synchronous to `pixel_clk`: hold it until the PLL has locked.\n\n**Pins.** Each lane is a true-LVDS pair: P on the pair's A site, N on its B site. nextpnr reads\neach pin on its own `IO_LOC` line (the Gowin IDE's `\"x\" P,N` form leaves N unconstrained). Lane 0\nis blue, 1 green, 2 red.\n\nShown on a monitor through the Tang Primer 20K Dock's HDMI connector (2026-10-14).\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gowin/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"hdmi_encoder\",\n    \"tmds_encoder\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Pixels and syncs out of an HDMI or DVI connector on Gowin GW1N/GW2A: three TMDS encoders, 10:1 OSER10 serialisers and true-LVDS outputs, plus the clock lane. Needs a five-times serial clock from an rPLL and the pixel clock from a CLKDIV dividing it by 5 (720p60: 371.25 and 74.25 MHz from 27 MHz). DVI by default; with HDMI = 1, HDMI with the AVI and Audio InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); for 48 kHz stereo sound instantiate hdmi_out_audio, the same with audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready. The same module and ports as hdmi_out for 7-series.\",\n  \"id\" : \"hdmi_out_gowin\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"1080p30\",\n    \"720p\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"gowin\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"hdmi-audio\",\n    \"oser10\",\n    \"tlvds\",\n    \"tmds\",\n    \"video\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel und Synchronsignale an einen HDMI- oder DVI-Anschluss bei Gowin GW1N/GW2A: drei TMDS-Encoder, 10:1-OSER10-Serialisierer und echte LVDS-Ausgänge, dazu der Takt-Kanal. Braucht einen fünffachen seriellen Takt aus einem rPLL und den Pixeltakt aus einem CLKDIV, der ihn durch 5 teilt (720p60: 371,25 und 74,25 MHz aus 27 MHz). Standardmässig DVI; mit HDMI = 1 HDMI mit den AVI- und Audio-InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); für 48-kHz-Stereoton hdmi_out_audio instanziieren, dasselbe mit audio_l, audio_r und audio_ready. Dasselbe Modul und dieselben Anschlüsse wie hdmi_out für die 7-Serie.\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI-Ausgang (Gowin)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"HDMI/DVI output (Gowin)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI, as before\",\n      \"name\" : \"HDMI\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 74250000,\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel clock in Hz (HDMI's audio clock: CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PIXEL_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"CEA-861 video code for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60)\",\n      \"name\" : \"VIC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Picture aspect for the AVI InfoFrame (1 = 4:3, 2 = 16:9)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ASPECT\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock (74.25 MHz for 720p60 and 1080p30): the CLKDIV's output, serial_clk / 5\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"pixel_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Five times the pixel clock (the OSER10's FCLK), from the rPLL\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"serial_clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Synchronous to pixel_clk: hold until the clocks are stable\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"reset\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: high in the visible area\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, P (true LVDS, on the pair's A site)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_p\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS clock lane, N (on the pair's B site)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_clk_n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes 0 (blue), 1 (green), 2 (red), P\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_p\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"TMDS data lanes, N\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tmds_d_n\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"requiresFamilies\" : [\n    \"gowin\"\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"variantOf\" : \"hdmi_out\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gowin/rtl/hdmi_out_gowin.v" : "// DVI/HDMI output for Gowin GW1N/GW2A (Essentials, HDMI on Gowin/ECP5, 2026-10-14): the ports and\n// the encoders of hdmi_out for 7-series, with Gowin's I/O. Three TMDS encoders (tmds_encoder) and\n// four lanes, each a 10:1 OSER10 into a true-LVDS TLVDS_OBUF: blue (lane 0, with hsync on c0 and\n// vsync on c1), green (1), red (2) and the clock lane (five ones, five zeros per pixel).\n//\n// Clocks: `serial_clk` is five times `pixel_clk` (the OSER10's FCLK, DDR: ten bits per pixel) and\n// `pixel_clk` its PCLK, in phase: an rPLL for the serial clock and a CLKDIV dividing it by 5\n// (GateLab's Add Clock makes that pair; 720p60 and 1080p30: 371.25 and 74.25 MHz from 27).\n// `reset` is synchronous to `pixel_clk`: hold it until the PLL has locked.\n//\n// Pins: each lane a true-LVDS pair, P on the pair's A site and N on its B site, each constrained\n// on its own IO_LOC line (nextpnr reads no \"P,N\" form). Bits go out from bit 0 (D0). Simulation\n// (no SYNTHESIS) sends the same bits from a behavioural model on both edges of `serial_clk`.\n\n// hdmi_out without sound: hdmi_out_audio with silence, the ports of the cores before HDMI audio (a design that\n// sends sound instantiates hdmi_out_audio, which has audio_l, audio_r and audio_ready). HDMI = 1 still sends the\n// InfoFrames, data islands and all.\nmodule hdmi_out #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    hdmi_out_audio #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_out (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p),\n        .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n),\n        .audio_l(16'd0), .audio_r(16'd0), .audio_ready());\nendmodule\n\nmodule hdmi_out_audio #(\n    parameter HDMI = 0,                     // 1: HDMI with audio and InfoFrames (hdmi_encoder); 0: DVI as before\n    parameter integer PIXEL_HZ = 74250000,  // the pixel clock, for HDMI's audio clock (CTS = PIXEL_HZ / 1000)\n    parameter [6:0] VIC = 7'd4,             // CEA-861 video code in the AVI InfoFrame: 1 = 640x480, 4 = 720p60, 16 = 1080p60\n    parameter [1:0] ASPECT = 2'b10          // picture aspect: 01 = 4:3, 10 = 16:9\n) (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire       hsync,\n    input  wire       vsync,\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_l,             // HDMI = 1: signed samples, taken while audio_ready is high (48 kHz)\n    input  wire [15:0] audio_r,\n    output wire       audio_ready,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_p,\n    output wire       tmds_clk_n,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_p,\n    output wire [2:0] tmds_d_n\n);\n    wire [9:0] word_blue, word_green, word_red;\n    hdmi_encoder #(.HDMI(HDMI), .PIXEL_HZ(PIXEL_HZ), .VIC(VIC), .ASPECT(ASPECT)) u_encoder (\n        .clk(pixel_clk), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .audio_l(audio_l), .audio_r(audio_r), .audio_ready(audio_ready),\n        .word_blue(word_blue), .word_green(word_green), .word_red(word_red));\n\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_blue  (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_blue),  .out_p(tmds_d_p[0]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[0]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_green (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_green), .out_p(tmds_d_p[1]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[1]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_red   (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(word_red),   .out_p(tmds_d_p[2]), .out_n(tmds_d_n[2]));\n    hdmi_out_lane u_lane_clock (.pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset), .word(10'b0000011111), .out_p(tmds_clk_p), .out_n(tmds_clk_n));\nendmodule\n\n// One lane: a 10-bit word per pixel clock, bit 0 first, out of a true-LVDS pair.\nmodule hdmi_out_lane (\n    input  wire       pixel_clk,\n    input  wire       serial_clk,\n    input  wire       reset,\n    input  wire [9:0] word,\n    output wire       out_p,\n    output wire       out_n\n);\n`ifdef SYNTHESIS\n    wire serial;\n    OSER10 u_oser (\n        .D0(word[0]), .D1(word[1]), .D2(word[2]), .D3(word[3]), .D4(word[4]),\n        .D5(word[5]), .D6(word[6]), .D7(word[7]), .D8(word[8]), .D9(word[9]),\n        .FCLK(serial_clk), .PCLK(pixel_clk), .RESET(reset), .Q(serial)\n    );\n    TLVDS_OBUF u_out (.I(serial), .O(out_p), .OB(out_n));\n`else\n    // The word of each pixel clock goes out during the next one, a bit per edge of serial_clk\n    // from bit 0 (a counter of serial edges, back at 0 on every rising pixel_clk edge).\n    reg [9:0] held = 10'd0;\n    reg [3:0] index = 4'd0;\n    always @(posedge pixel_clk) held <= word;\n    always @(posedge serial_clk or negedge serial_clk) index <= (reset || index == 4'd9) ? 4'd0 : index + 4'd1;\n    assign out_p = held[index];\n    assign out_n = ~held[index];\n`endif\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hdmi_out_gowin/tb/hdmi_out_gowin_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for hdmi_out on Gowin (the simulation lanes: the OSER10's model):\n// a tiny mode (12 × 4 active, 20 × 6\n// clocks, syncs in the blanking) with colours that differ per channel, pixel and line. A receiver\n// samples the four lanes in the middle of every bit, finds the word boundary from the clock lane\n// (0000011111, bit 0 first) like a monitor, decodes each channel and compares every pixel, data\n// enable and sync with what was sent — in order, at the latency the first data word shows.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule hdmi_out_gowin_tb;\n    // Pixel clock 50 ns, serial clock 10 ns (five times, DDR: ten bits per pixel), in phase.\n    reg pixel_clk = 1'b0, serial_clk = 1'b0;\n    always #25 pixel_clk = ~pixel_clk;\n    always #5 serial_clk = ~serial_clk;\n\n    reg reset = 1'b1;\n    reg [7:0] red = 8'd0, green = 8'd0, blue = 8'd0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    wire tmds_clk_p, tmds_clk_n;\n    wire [2:0] tmds_d_p, tmds_d_n;\n\n    hdmi_out dut (\n        .pixel_clk(pixel_clk), .serial_clk(serial_clk), .reset(reset),\n        .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n        .tmds_clk_p(tmds_clk_p), .tmds_clk_n(tmds_clk_n), .tmds_d_p(tmds_d_p), .tmds_d_n(tmds_d_n)\n    );\n\n    // What was sent, one entry per pixel clock: {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue}.\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 20, H_ACTIVE = 12, V_TOTAL = 6, V_ACTIVE = 4, FRAMES = 3;\n    reg [26:0] sent [0:H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES + 63];\n    integer pixels = 0, x = 0, y = 0;\n\n    initial begin\n        repeat (3) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        reset <= 1'b0;\n        while (pixels < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n            // New inputs right after an edge, taken by the encoders at the next one.\n            #1;\n            de = x < H_ACTIVE && y < V_ACTIVE;\n            hsync = x >= 14 && x < 17;\n            vsync = y == 5;\n            red = x * 17 + y * 3;\n            green = 8'hA5 ^ (x * 5) ^ (y << 4);\n            blue = 8'd200 - x * 9 - y;\n            sent[pixels] = {de, vsync, hsync, red, green, blue};\n            pixels = pixels + 1;\n            x = x + 1;\n            if (x == H_TOTAL) begin x = 0; y = (y + 1) % V_TOTAL; end\n            @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        end\n    end\n\n    // The receiver.\n    function [7:0] decode;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        reg [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            v = word[9] ? ~word[7:0] : word[7:0];\n            decode[0] = v[0];\n            for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) decode[k] = word[8] ? (v[k] ^ v[k - 1]) : ~(v[k] ^ v[k - 1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // Control words carry {c1, c0}; anything else is data. Returns 3'b1xx for data.\n    function [2:0] control;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        begin\n            case (word)\n                10'b1101010100: control = 3'b000;\n                10'b0010101011: control = 3'b001;\n                10'b0101010100: control = 3'b010;\n                10'b1010101011: control = 3'b011;\n                default: control = 3'b100;\n            endcase\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    reg [9:0] lane_clock = 10'd0, lane_blue = 10'd0, lane_green = 10'd0, lane_red = 10'd0;\n    integer errors = 0, received = 0, offset = -1, data_words = 0, syncs = 0, index;\n    reg [26:0] wanted;\n\n    always @(serial_clk) begin\n        #2.5;\n        // Bit 0 arrives first: shift in from the top.\n        lane_clock = {tmds_clk_p, lane_clock[9:1]};\n        lane_blue  = {tmds_d_p[0], lane_blue[9:1]};\n        lane_green = {tmds_d_p[1], lane_green[9:1]};\n        lane_red   = {tmds_d_p[2], lane_red[9:1]};\n        if (tmds_clk_n !== ~tmds_clk_p || tmds_d_n !== ~tmds_d_p) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: a lane's N side isn't the inverse of its P side\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (!reset && lane_clock == 10'b0000011111) word_boundary;\n    end\n\n    task word_boundary;\n        reg [2:0] cb, cg, cr;\n        begin\n            cb = control(lane_blue);\n            cg = control(lane_green);\n            cr = control(lane_red);\n            // The first data word fixes the latency: it's the first pixel sent with de high.\n            if (offset < 0 && !cb[2]) begin\n                // still blanking\n            end else if (offset < 0) begin\n                for (index = 0; index < pixels && sent[index][26] == 1'b0; index = index + 1) ;\n                offset = received - index;\n            end\n            if (offset >= 0 && received - offset < H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES) begin\n                wanted = sent[received - offset];\n                if (wanted[26]) begin\n                    data_words = data_words + 1;\n                    if (cb[2] !== 1'b1 || cg[2] !== 1'b1 || cr[2] !== 1'b1) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: a control word during active video\", received - offset);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end else if ({decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue)} !== wanted[23:0]) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: rgb %h %h %h, sent %h %h %h\", received - offset,\n                                 decode(lane_red), decode(lane_green), decode(lane_blue), wanted[23:16], wanted[15:8], wanted[7:0]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    // Blanking: blue carries {vsync, hsync}, the others 00.\n                    if (cb !== {1'b0, wanted[25], wanted[24]} || cg !== 3'b000 || cr !== 3'b000) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: pixel %0d: control words b %b g %b r %b, sent vsync %b hsync %b\", received - offset,\n                                 cb, cg, cr, wanted[25], wanted[24]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (wanted[24] || wanted[25]) syncs = syncs + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            received = received + 1;\n            if (errors > 5) begin $display(\"FAIL: too many errors\"); $finish; end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"hdmi_out_gowin_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, hdmi_out_gowin_tb);\n        wait (pixels == H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES);\n        repeat (8) @(posedge pixel_clk);\n        if (offset < 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: no data word was received\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (data_words < H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES - H_ACTIVE) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d data words checked, expected about %0d\", data_words, H_ACTIVE * V_ACTIVE * FRAMES);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (syncs < 3 * FRAMES) begin $display(\"FAIL: only %0d sync words checked\", syncs); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hid_serial/README.md" : "# Keyboard and mouse over serial\n\nClick into GateLab's HDMI Input panel and your keyboard and mouse go to the board over its USB serial port; Claude's\n`hid_input` tool sends the same. `hid_serial` turns those bytes back into events: USB HID key codes with press and\nrelease, relative mouse moves, absolute pointer positions in the picture's pixels, the wheel, and an \"all up\" when the\ncapture ends (so no key stays down).\n\n```verilog\nwire        key_valid, key_up, move_valid, position_valid, wheel_valid, all_up;\nwire [7:0]  key_usage;\nwire signed [7:0] move_dx, move_dy, wheel_dz;\nwire [13:0] position_x, position_y;\nwire [2:0]  buttons;\nhid_serial #(.CLK_HZ(50_000_000)) u_hid (\n    .clk(clk), .rx(uart_rx),\n    .key_valid(key_valid), .key_usage(key_usage), .key_up(key_up),\n    .move_valid(move_valid), .move_dx(move_dx), .move_dy(move_dy),\n    .position_valid(position_valid), .position_x(position_x), .position_y(position_y),\n    .wheel_valid(wheel_valid), .wheel_dz(wheel_dz), .buttons(buttons), .all_up(all_up));\n```\n\nKey codes are USB HID usages (page 7): `0x04`–`0x1D` a–z, `0x1E`–`0x27` 1–0, `0x28` Return, `0x29` Escape, `0x2A`\nBackspace, `0x2C` Space, `0x4F`–`0x52` the arrows, `0xE0`–`0xE7` Control, Shift, Option and Command (left, then right).\nThey name the key's position, not its character: map them to your system's codes (a Mac Plus, a PS/2 scan code set, a\nterminal's ASCII) in your design.\n\nThe protocol (GateLab HID): a byte with bit 7 set starts a packet, data bytes have it clear.\n\n| Packet | Bytes |\n|---|---|\n| key | `0x80 \\| up << 1 \\| usage[7]`, `usage[6:0]` |\n| mouse move | `0x90 \\| buttons`, `dx`, `dy` (7-bit two's complement, right/down positive) |\n| mouse position | `0xA0 \\| buttons`, `x[13:7]`, `x[6:0]`, `y[13:7]`, `y[6:0]` |\n| wheel | `0xB0`, `dz` |\n| all up | `0xBF` |\n\nA packet cut short by the next command byte is dropped; data bytes outside a packet are ignored.\n",
    "cores/hid_serial/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"uart_rx\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Keyboard and mouse from GateLab's HDMI Input panel (or Claude's hid_input) over the board's UART: USB HID key codes with press and release, relative and absolute mouse, wheel and an all-up event. Resynchronises on the next packet after a lost byte.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 158.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 91,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 122\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 95.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 3,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 7,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 9,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 49,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 36,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 122\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 96.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 22,\n        \"DFF\" : 122,\n        \"LUT4\" : 168,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 4\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 50.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 186\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 98.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 89,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 122\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 265.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 7,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 119,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 105\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"hid_serial\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"keyboard\",\n    \"mouse\",\n    \"hid\",\n    \"serial\",\n    \"uart\",\n    \"input\",\n    \"hdmi\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Tastatur und Maus aus GateLabs HDMI-Eingang (oder Claudes hid_input) über den UART des Boards: USB-HID-Tastencodes mit Drücken und Loslassen, relative und absolute Maus, Mausrad und ein Alles-los-Ereignis. Findet nach einem verlorenen Byte mit dem nächsten Paket wieder Tritt.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Tastatur und Maus über seriell\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Keyboard and mouse over serial\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 115200,\n      \"description\" : \"Bits per second (the Serial Monitor's setting)\",\n      \"name\" : \"BAUD\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Serial input from the board's USB serial port (8N1), idles high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: a key went down or up\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"key_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The key's USB HID usage (page 7; E0–E7 are Control, Shift, Option, Command left and right)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"key_usage\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"1 when the key was released\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"key_up\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: the mouse moved\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"move_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Signed step right (−63…63)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"move_dx\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Signed step down (−63…63)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"move_dy\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: an absolute pointer position arrived\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"position_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pointer column in the picture's pixels\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"position_x\",\n      \"width\" : 14\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pointer row in the picture's pixels\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"position_y\",\n      \"width\" : 14\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: the wheel turned\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"wheel_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Signed wheel step\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"wheel_dz\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Mouse buttons held (bit 0 left, 1 right, 2 middle), from the last move or position\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"buttons\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: every key and button was released (capture ended)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"all_up\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"hid_serial\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/hid_serial/rtl/hid_serial.v" : "// Keyboard and mouse from GateLab over a serial line: the packets GateLab's HDMI Input panel and Claude's hid_input\n// send to a board's UART (8N1), decoded into events. A command byte has bit 7 set, data bytes have it clear:\n//\n//   key             0x80 | up << 1 | usage[7], usage[6:0]       USB HID usage (page 7; E0-E7 are the modifiers)\n//   mouse move      0x90 | buttons, dx, dy                      7-bit two's complement, right/down positive\n//   mouse position  0xA0 | buttons, x[13:7], x[6:0], y[13:7], y[6:0]   absolute, in the picture's pixels\n//   wheel           0xB0, dz                                    7-bit two's complement\n//   all up          0xBF                                        every key and button released\n//\n// buttons: bit 0 left, bit 1 right, bit 2 middle. Each event is a one-clock pulse with its values held until the\n// next one of its kind; `buttons` is a level (the last move or position packet's, cleared by all up). A packet cut\n// short by the next command byte is dropped, so a lost byte costs one packet, never the stream.\n\nmodule hid_serial #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter BAUD   = 115200\n) (\n    input  wire              clk,\n    input  wire              rx,\n\n    output reg               key_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        [7:0]  key_usage = 8'd0,\n    output reg               key_up = 1'b0,\n\n    output reg               move_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg  signed [7:0] move_dx = 8'sd0,\n    output reg  signed [7:0] move_dy = 8'sd0,\n\n    output reg               position_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        [13:0] position_x = 14'd0,\n    output reg        [13:0] position_y = 14'd0,\n\n    output reg               wheel_valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg  signed [7:0] wheel_dz = 8'sd0,\n\n    output reg        [2:0]  buttons = 3'd0,\n    output reg               all_up = 1'b0\n);\n    wire [7:0] data;\n    wire       valid;\n    uart_rx #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .BAUD(BAUD)) u_rx (.clk(clk), .rx(rx), .data(data), .valid(valid), .frame_error());\n\n    reg [7:0] command = 8'd0;       // 0: no packet open\n    reg [2:0] count = 3'd0;         // data bytes taken\n    reg [6:0] b0 = 7'd0, b1 = 7'd0, b2 = 7'd0;\n\n    // A 7-bit two's complement step as 8 bits.\n    function signed [7:0] step(input [6:0] v);\n        step = {v[6], v};\n    endfunction\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        key_valid <= 1'b0;\n        move_valid <= 1'b0;\n        position_valid <= 1'b0;\n        wheel_valid <= 1'b0;\n        all_up <= 1'b0;\n        if (valid) begin\n            if (data[7]) begin\n                count <= 3'd0;\n                if (data == 8'hBF) begin\n                    all_up <= 1'b1;\n                    buttons <= 3'd0;\n                    command <= 8'd0;\n                end else begin\n                    command <= data;\n                end\n            end else if (command != 8'd0) begin\n                count <= count + 3'd1;\n                case (count)\n                    3'd0: b0 <= data[6:0];\n                    3'd1: b1 <= data[6:0];\n                    3'd2: b2 <= data[6:0];\n                    default: ;\n                endcase\n                case (command[7:4])\n                    4'h8: begin                                   // key: one data byte\n                        key_valid <= 1'b1;\n                        key_usage <= {command[0], data[6:0]};\n                        key_up <= command[1];\n                        command <= 8'd0;\n                    end\n                    4'h9: if (count == 3'd1) begin                // move: dx, dy\n                        move_valid <= 1'b1;\n                        move_dx <= step(b0);\n                        move_dy <= step(data[6:0]);\n                        buttons <= command[2:0];\n                        command <= 8'd0;\n                    end\n                    4'hA: if (count == 3'd3) begin                // position: x hi, x lo, y hi, y lo\n                        position_valid <= 1'b1;\n                        position_x <= {b0, b1};\n                        position_y <= {b2, data[6:0]};\n                        buttons <= command[2:0];\n                        command <= 8'd0;\n                    end\n                    4'hB: begin                                   // wheel: dz\n                        wheel_valid <= 1'b1;\n                        wheel_dz <= step(data[6:0]);\n                        command <= 8'd0;\n                    end\n                    default: command <= 8'd0;                     // unknown command: wait for the next one\n                endcase\n            end\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/hid_serial/tb/hid_serial_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for hid_serial: real UART frames (8N1, 10 clocks a bit) of every packet, back to back, and\n// what must not count — data bytes before any command, a packet cut short by the next command, an unknown command.\n// Checks every event's values and order, the buttons level and all_up. Prints PASS or the failed checks.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule hid_serial_tb;\n    localparam CLK_HZ = 1_000_000, BAUD = 100_000;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #500 clk = ~clk;\n\n    reg rx = 1'b1;\n    wire key_valid, key_up, move_valid, position_valid, wheel_valid, all_up;\n    wire [7:0] key_usage;\n    wire signed [7:0] move_dx, move_dy, wheel_dz;\n    wire [13:0] position_x, position_y;\n    wire [2:0] buttons;\n    hid_serial #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .BAUD(BAUD)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .rx(rx), .key_valid(key_valid), .key_usage(key_usage), .key_up(key_up),\n        .move_valid(move_valid), .move_dx(move_dx), .move_dy(move_dy),\n        .position_valid(position_valid), .position_x(position_x), .position_y(position_y),\n        .wheel_valid(wheel_valid), .wheel_dz(wheel_dz), .buttons(buttons), .all_up(all_up));\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    task fail(input [8*64-1:0] what);\n        begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0s\", what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // One 8N1 frame: start bit, eight data bits LSB first, stop bit.\n    task send(input [7:0] b);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            rx = 1'b0; repeat (10) @(posedge clk);\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) begin rx = b[i]; repeat (10) @(posedge clk); end\n            rx = 1'b1; repeat (10) @(posedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Every event as one 48-bit record: kind, then its values.\n    reg [47:0] events [0:63];\n    integer n = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (key_valid)      begin events[n] = {8'h01, 7'd0, key_up, key_usage, 24'd0}; n = n + 1; end\n        if (move_valid)     begin events[n] = {8'h02, 5'd0, buttons, move_dx, move_dy, 16'd0}; n = n + 1; end\n        if (position_valid) begin events[n] = {8'h03, 5'd0, buttons, 2'd0, position_x, 2'd0, position_y}; n = n + 1; end\n        if (wheel_valid)    begin events[n] = {8'h04, wheel_dz, 32'd0}; n = n + 1; end\n        if (all_up)         begin events[n] = {8'h05, 40'd0}; n = n + 1; end\n    end\n\n    integer i;\n    reg [47:0] expected [0:63];\n    integer expected_n = 0;\n    task expect_event(input [47:0] e); begin expected[expected_n] = e; expected_n = expected_n + 1; end endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"hid_serial_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, hid_serial_tb);\n        repeat (20) @(posedge clk);\n\n        send(8'h45);                                   // a data byte before any command: ignored\n        send(8'h80); send(8'h04);                      // key A down\n        expect_event({8'h01, 7'd0, 1'b0, 8'h04, 24'd0});\n        send(8'h83); send(8'h63);                      // key E3 (left Command) up: usage bit 7 in the command byte\n        expect_event({8'h01, 7'd0, 1'b1, 8'hE3, 24'd0});\n        send(8'h91); send(8'h05); send(8'h7F);         // move +5, -1, left\n        expect_event({8'h02, 5'd0, 3'b001, 8'sd5, -8'sd1, 16'd0});\n        if (buttons !== 3'b001) fail(\"buttons after a move with left held\");\n        send(8'h95); send(8'h41); send(8'h3F);         // move -63, +63, left + middle\n        expect_event({8'h02, 5'd0, 3'b101, -8'sd63, 8'sd63, 16'd0});\n        send(8'hA2); send(8'h09); send(8'h7F); send(8'h05); send(8'h4F);   // position 1279, 719, right\n        expect_event({8'h03, 5'd0, 3'b010, 2'd0, 14'd1279, 2'd0, 14'd719});\n        send(8'hB0); send(8'h7D);                      // wheel -3\n        expect_event({8'h04, -8'sd3, 32'd0});\n        send(8'h91); send(8'h05);                      // a move cut short by the next command: dropped\n        send(8'h82); send(8'h28);                      // Return up\n        expect_event({8'h01, 7'd0, 1'b1, 8'h28, 24'd0});\n        send(8'hFE); send(8'h01); send(8'h02);         // an unknown command and its bytes: ignored\n        send(8'hBF);                                   // all up\n        expect_event({8'h05, 40'd0});\n        repeat (5) @(posedge clk);\n        if (buttons !== 3'd0) fail(\"buttons still held after all up\");\n\n        if (n != expected_n) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d events, expected %0d\", n, expected_n);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        for (i = 0; i < expected_n && i < n; i = i + 1)\n            if (events[i] !== expected[i]) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: event %0d is %h, expected %h\", i, events[i], expected[i]);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        else $display(\"FAILED: %0d\", errors);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #20_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: watchdog\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2c_master/README.md" : "# I²C master\n\nRuns one bus command per `go`: `cmd` 0 START (also a repeated start), 1 WRITE `tx_data`\n(`ack` tells whether the device answered), 2 READ into `rx_data` (`ack_read` 1 for every byte\nbut the last), 3 STOP. Wait for `done` (or `ready`) before the next command.\n\nThe bus lines are open drain, so the core never drives them high:\n\n```verilog\ninout wire i2c_scl, i2c_sda;\nassign i2c_scl = scl_oe ? 1'b0 : 1'bz;\nassign i2c_sda = sda_oe ? 1'b0 : 1'bz;\n\ni2c_master #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .I2C_HZ(100000)) u_i2c (\n    .clk(clk), .cmd(cmd), .tx_data(byte), .ack_read(more), .go(go), .ready(i2c_ready),\n    .done(i2c_done), .rx_data(answer), .ack(acked),\n    .scl_oe(scl_oe), .scl_in(i2c_scl), .sda_oe(sda_oe), .sda_in(i2c_sda)\n);\n```\n\nBoth lines need pull-up resistors (2.2–10 kΩ) on the board or enabled in the constraints.\nReading register `r` of a device at address `a`: START, WRITE `a<<1`, WRITE `r`, START,\nWRITE `a<<1 | 1`, READ (ack_read 0), STOP. Devices that stretch the clock are waited for.\n",
    "cores/i2c_master/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"I²C controller for sensors, EEPROMs, RTCs and displays: byte-level START, WRITE, READ and STOP commands, open-drain outputs, clock stretching.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 209.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 69,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 46\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 104.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 4,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 17,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 63,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 37,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 87,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 145.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 12,\n        \"DFF\" : 46,\n        \"LUT4\" : 97,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 13,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 4,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 1,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 61.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 94\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 138.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 68,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 46\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"i2c_master\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"i2c\",\n    \"iic\",\n    \"twi\",\n    \"smbus\",\n    \"sensor\",\n    \"eeprom\",\n    \"rtc\",\n    \"master\",\n    \"controller\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"I²C-Controller für Sensoren, EEPROMs, Echtzeituhren und Displays: Befehle START, WRITE, READ und STOP auf Byte-Ebene, Open-Drain-Ausgänge, Clock-Stretching.\",\n      \"name\" : \"I²C-Master\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"I²C master\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 100000,\n      \"description\" : \"Bus clock: 100000 (standard) or 400000 (fast)\",\n      \"name\" : \"I2C_HZ\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"0 START, 1 WRITE, 2 READ, 3 STOP\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"cmd\",\n      \"width\" : 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte for WRITE\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"READ: 1 = ACK (more bytes follow), 0 = NACK (last byte)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"ack_read\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Run cmd (while ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"go\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"No command running\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse when the command has finished\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte from the last READ\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The device acknowledged the last WRITE\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ack\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"1: pull SCL low (open drain)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"scl_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"SCL as it is on the bus\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"scl_in\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"1: pull SDA low (open drain)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"sda_oe\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"SDA as it is on the bus\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"sda_in\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"i2c_master\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/i2c_master/rtl/i2c_master.v" : "// I2C master, byte level. One command per `go` (while `ready`):\n//   cmd 0 START  — a start, or a repeated start after a byte\n//   cmd 1 WRITE  — send `tx_data`; `ack` says whether the device acknowledged\n//   cmd 2 READ   — receive a byte into `rx_data`; answer ACK if `ack_read` (more bytes to come),\n//                  NACK for the last byte\n//   cmd 3 STOP\n// `done` pulses when the command has finished. A transaction is START, WRITE (address << 1 |\n// read bit), WRITE or READ bytes, STOP.\n//\n// The bus is open drain: `scl_oe` / `sda_oe` high means \"pull the line low\", low means \"let go\"\n// (the pull-up makes it high). `scl_in` / `sda_in` are the lines as they are; they are\n// synchronised here. A device may stretch the clock by holding SCL low: the master waits.\n// At the top: `assign sda = sda_oe ? 1'b0 : 1'bz; assign sda_in = sda;` (the same for SCL).\n\nmodule i2c_master #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter I2C_HZ = 100000\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire [1:0] cmd,\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data,\n    input  wire       ack_read,\n    input  wire       go,\n    output wire       ready,\n    output reg        done = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        ack = 1'b0,\n    output reg        scl_oe = 1'b0,\n    input  wire       scl_in,\n    output reg        sda_oe = 1'b0,\n    input  wire       sda_in\n);\n    localparam START = 2'd0, WRITE = 2'd1, READ = 2'd2, STOP = 2'd3;\n    // Each bit is four quarter periods: SCL low (set SDA), release SCL, SCL high (sample), SCL low.\n    localparam QUARTER = CLK_HZ / (4 * I2C_HZ) > 0 ? CLK_HZ / (4 * I2C_HZ) : 1;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = QUARTER > 1 ? $clog2(QUARTER) : 1;\n\n    reg [1:0] scl_s = 2'b11, sda_s = 2'b11;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        scl_s <= {scl_s[0], scl_in};\n        sda_s <= {sda_s[0], sda_in};\n    end\n    wire scl_high = scl_s[1];\n    wire sda_high = sda_s[1];\n\n    reg       busy = 1'b0;\n    reg [1:0] op = START;\n    reg [1:0] phase = 2'd0;\n    reg [3:0] bit_index = 4'd0;           // 0 … 7 data bits, 8 the acknowledge bit\n    reg [7:0] tx_shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] rx_shift = 8'd0;\n    reg       answer_ack = 1'b0;\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n\n    assign ready = !busy;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        done <= 1'b0;\n        if (!busy) begin\n            if (go) begin\n                busy <= 1'b1;\n                op <= cmd;\n                phase <= 2'd0;\n                bit_index <= 4'd0;\n                clocks <= 0;\n                tx_shift <= tx_data;\n                answer_ack <= ack_read;\n                // Phase 0 of the command; SCL is low here except before a first START.\n                case (cmd)\n                    START: sda_oe <= 1'b0;\n                    WRITE: begin scl_oe <= 1'b1; sda_oe <= !tx_data[7]; end\n                    READ:  begin scl_oe <= 1'b1; sda_oe <= 1'b0; end\n                    STOP:  begin scl_oe <= 1'b1; sda_oe <= 1'b1; end\n                endcase\n            end\n        end else if (phase == 2'd1 && !scl_high) begin\n            // SCL released but still low: a device stretches the clock. Wait.\n            clocks <= 0;\n        end else if (clocks != QUARTER - 1) begin\n            clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            clocks <= 0;\n            case (phase)\n                2'd0: begin\n                    phase <= 2'd1;\n                    scl_oe <= 1'b0;\n                end\n                2'd1: begin\n                    // SCL has been high for a quarter period.\n                    phase <= 2'd2;\n                    case (op)\n                        START: sda_oe <= 1'b1;                   // SDA falls while SCL is high\n                        STOP:  sda_oe <= 1'b0;                   // SDA rises while SCL is high\n                        WRITE: if (bit_index == 4'd8) ack <= !sda_high;\n                        READ:  if (bit_index != 4'd8) rx_shift <= {rx_shift[6:0], sda_high};\n                    endcase\n                end\n                2'd2: begin\n                    if (op == STOP) begin\n                        busy <= 1'b0;\n                        done <= 1'b1;\n                    end else begin\n                        phase <= 2'd3;\n                        scl_oe <= 1'b1;\n                    end\n                end\n                2'd3: begin\n                    if (op == START || bit_index == 4'd8) begin\n                        busy <= 1'b0;\n                        done <= 1'b1;\n                        if (op == READ) rx_data <= rx_shift;\n                    end else begin\n                        bit_index <= bit_index + 1'b1;\n                        phase <= 2'd0;\n                        // Phase 0 of the next bit.\n                        if (op == WRITE) begin\n                            tx_shift <= {tx_shift[6:0], 1'b0};\n                            sda_oe <= (bit_index == 4'd7) ? 1'b0 : !tx_shift[6];\n                        end else begin\n                            sda_oe <= (bit_index == 4'd7) ? answer_ack : 1'b0;\n                        end\n                    end\n                end\n            endcase\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2c_master/tb/i2c_master_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for i2c_master against an I2C target modelled on a small EEPROM at\n// address 0x50 (a register pointer, then data; reads continue from the pointer). The target\n// stretches the clock before it answers each acknowledge, so a master that doesn't wait for SCL\n// reads a wrong ACK. Checked: written bytes land in the target, a repeated start and reads with\n// ACK/NACK return them, an absent address is NACKed, and START/STOP conditions happen exactly\n// where the commands put them (no glitch counts as one).\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule i2c_target_model #(parameter [6:0] ADDRESS = 7'h50, parameter STRETCH_NS = 3000) (\n    input  wire scl,\n    input  wire sda,\n    output reg  scl_low = 1'b0,\n    output reg  sda_low = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam IDLE = 0, RX = 1, RX_ACK = 2, TX = 3, TX_ACK = 4;\n    integer state = IDLE;\n    integer count = 0;\n    reg [7:0] shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] memory [0:255];\n    reg [7:0] pointer = 8'd0;\n    reg addressed = 1'b0, reading = 1'b0, first_data = 1'b0, master_ack = 1'b0;\n    integer starts = 0, stops = 0;\n\n    // START and STOP: SDA changes while SCL is high (not the lines settling at time 0).\n    always @(negedge sda) if (scl === 1'b1 && $time > 0) begin\n        starts = starts + 1;\n        state = RX; count = 0; addressed = 1'b0; sda_low = 1'b0;\n    end\n    always @(posedge sda) if (scl === 1'b1 && $time > 0) begin\n        stops = stops + 1;\n        state = IDLE; sda_low = 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    always @(posedge scl) begin\n        if (state == RX) begin\n            shift = {shift[6:0], sda};\n            count = count + 1;\n        end else if (state == TX_ACK) begin\n            master_ack = !sda;\n        end\n    end\n\n    always @(negedge scl) begin\n        case (state)\n            RX: if (count == 8) begin\n                // Hold SCL low while deciding: the master must wait for it.\n                scl_low = 1'b1;\n                #(STRETCH_NS);\n                if (!addressed) begin\n                    if (shift[7:1] == ADDRESS) begin\n                        addressed = 1'b1; reading = shift[0]; first_data = !shift[0];\n                        sda_low = 1'b1; state = RX_ACK;\n                    end else begin\n                        state = IDLE;            // not us: no ACK\n                    end\n                end else begin\n                    if (first_data) begin pointer = shift; first_data = 1'b0; end\n                    else begin memory[pointer] = shift; pointer = pointer + 1; end\n                    sda_low = 1'b1; state = RX_ACK;\n                end\n                // Data setup time before SCL is let go (releasing both at once would look\n                // like a START).\n                #100;\n                scl_low = 1'b0;\n            end\n            RX_ACK: begin\n                if (reading) begin\n                    shift = memory[pointer]; pointer = pointer + 1;\n                    sda_low = !shift[7]; count = 1; state = TX;\n                end else begin\n                    sda_low = 1'b0; count = 0; state = RX;\n                end\n            end\n            TX: if (count == 8) begin\n                sda_low = 1'b0; state = TX_ACK;\n            end else begin\n                sda_low = !shift[7 - count]; count = count + 1;\n            end\n            TX_ACK: if (master_ack) begin\n                shift = memory[pointer]; pointer = pointer + 1;\n                sda_low = !shift[7]; count = 1; state = TX;\n            end else begin\n                sda_low = 1'b0; state = IDLE;\n            end\n        endcase\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule i2c_master_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #10 clk = ~clk;              // 50 MHz\n\n    reg  [1:0] cmd = 2'd0;\n    reg  [7:0] tx_data = 8'd0;\n    reg        ack_read = 1'b0, go = 1'b0;\n    wire       ready, done, ack, scl_oe, sda_oe;\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire       target_scl_low, target_sda_low;\n\n    // Open-drain bus with pull-ups.\n    wire scl = !(scl_oe || target_scl_low);\n    wire sda = !(sda_oe || target_sda_low);\n\n    i2c_master #(.CLK_HZ(50000000), .I2C_HZ(400000)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .cmd(cmd), .tx_data(tx_data), .ack_read(ack_read), .go(go), .ready(ready),\n        .done(done), .rx_data(rx_data), .ack(ack),\n        .scl_oe(scl_oe), .scl_in(scl), .sda_oe(sda_oe), .sda_in(sda)\n    );\n    i2c_target_model target (.scl(scl), .sda(sda), .scl_low(target_scl_low), .sda_low(target_sda_low));\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    task command(input [1:0] c, input [7:0] d, input a);\n        begin\n            @(negedge clk);\n            while (!ready) @(negedge clk);\n            cmd = c; tx_data = d; ack_read = a; go = 1'b1;\n            @(negedge clk);\n            go = 1'b0;\n            @(posedge done);\n            @(negedge clk);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task write(input [7:0] d, input expect_ack);\n        begin\n            command(2'd1, d, 1'b0);\n            if (ack !== expect_ack) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: write %h: ack %b, expected %b\", d, ack, expect_ack);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task read(input more, input [7:0] expected);\n        begin\n            command(2'd2, 8'h00, more);\n            if (rx_data !== expected) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: read %h, expected %h\", rx_data, expected);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"i2c_master_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, i2c_master_tb);\n        #1000;\n        if (scl !== 1'b1 || sda !== 1'b1) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: the master doesn't release the bus when idle\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        // Write 0xAB, 0xCD to registers 0x10, 0x11.\n        command(2'd0, 0, 0);\n        write(8'hA0, 1'b1);\n        write(8'h10, 1'b1);\n        write(8'hAB, 1'b1);\n        write(8'hCD, 1'b1);\n        command(2'd3, 0, 0);\n        if (target.memory[8'h10] !== 8'hAB || target.memory[8'h11] !== 8'hCD) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: the target holds %h %h, expected ab cd\", target.memory[8'h10], target.memory[8'h11]);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        // Read them back: pointer write, repeated start, two reads (ACK, then NACK).\n        command(2'd0, 0, 0);\n        write(8'hA0, 1'b1);\n        write(8'h10, 1'b1);\n        command(2'd0, 0, 0);\n        write(8'hA1, 1'b1);\n        read(1'b1, 8'hAB);\n        read(1'b0, 8'hCD);\n        command(2'd3, 0, 0);\n\n        // Nobody at 0x51.\n        command(2'd0, 0, 0);\n        write(8'hA2, 1'b0);\n        command(2'd3, 0, 0);\n\n        #2000;\n        if (target.starts != 4 || target.stops != 3) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d starts and %0d stops on the bus, expected 4 and 3\", target.starts, target.stops);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (scl !== 1'b1 || sda !== 1'b1) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: bus not released after STOP\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #5000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2s_clocks/README.md" : "# I2S clocks\n\nMCLK, SCLK and LRCK for an I2S codec that takes its clocks from the FPGA, such as the Cirrus CS4344 (DAC)\nand CS5343 (ADC) on the Digilent Pmod I2S2. Counters only, no PLL: the sample rate is\n`Fs = clock / (MCLK_DIV · RATIO)`.\n\n| Board clock | MCLK_DIV | RATIO | MCLK | Fs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 27 MHz | 2 | 512 | 13.5 MHz | 26.37 kHz |\n| 100 MHz | 8 | 256 | 12.5 MHz | 48.83 kHz |\n| 12 MHz | 2 | 256 | 6 MHz | 23.44 kHz |\n\nSCLK is 64 Fs (32 bit slots a channel, 24 data bits). The two strobes keep transmitters and receivers in\nstep with the pins: `bit_start` (the outputs start slot `slot_next` at the next edge) and `bit_sample` (an\ninput through two flip-flops now shows its bit as it was just before SCLK rose: I2S ADCs change their data\nafter the rising edge).\n\n```verilog\nwire mclk, sclk, lrck, bit_start, bit_sample, frame_start;\nwire [5:0] slot_next, slot;\ni2s_clocks #(.MCLK_DIV(2), .RATIO(512)) u_clocks (\n    .clk(clk), .mclk(mclk), .sclk(sclk), .lrck(lrck),\n    .bit_start(bit_start), .slot_next(slot_next), .bit_sample(bit_sample), .slot(slot),\n    .frame_start(frame_start)\n);\n```\n\nDrive the codec's MCLK, SCLK and LRCK pins from `mclk`, `sclk` and `lrck`; a DAC and an ADC on separate pins\n(as on the Pmod I2S2) can share them. Check the codec's data sheet for the MCLK/LRCK ratios and sample\nrates it accepts.\n",
    "cores/i2s_clocks/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"MCLK, SCLK (64 Fs) and LRCK for I2S codecs that take their clocks from the FPGA (Cirrus CS4344/CS5343, Digilent Pmod I2S2), made from the system clock with counters only: Fs = clock / (MCLK_DIV · RATIO), e.g. 27 MHz / 1024 = 26.37 kHz, 100 MHz / 2048 = 48.83 kHz. Strobes tell i2s_transmitter when to change its bit and i2s_receiver when to take one.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 232.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 22,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 11\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 108.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 5,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 13,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 11,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 26\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 164.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 14,\n        \"DFF\" : 12\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 81.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 27\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 188.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 28,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 11\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 323.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 4,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 12,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 36\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"i2s_clocks\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"i2s\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"codec\",\n    \"dac\",\n    \"adc\",\n    \"mclk\",\n    \"lrck\",\n    \"sclk\",\n    \"pmod\",\n    \"clock\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"MCLK, SCLK (64 Fs) und LRCK für I2S-Codecs, die ihre Takte vom FPGA bekommen (Cirrus CS4344/CS5343, Digilent Pmod I2S2), nur mit Zählern aus dem Systemtakt: Fs = Takt / (MCLK_DIV · RATIO), z. B. 27 MHz / 1024 = 26,37 kHz, 100 MHz / 2048 = 48,83 kHz. Strobes sagen i2s_transmitter, wann es sein Bit wechselt, und i2s_receiver, wann es eines übernimmt.\",\n      \"name\" : \"I2S-Takte\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"I2S clocks\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"System clocks per MCLK (even, at least 2)\",\n      \"name\" : \"MCLK_DIV\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 512,\n      \"description\" : \"MCLK per frame (MCLK/LRCK): 256, 384, 512 or 768; MCLK_DIV · RATIO / 64 at least 8\",\n      \"name\" : \"RATIO\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Master clock to the codec\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mclk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Bit clock, 64 Fs, falls as a bit slot starts\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"sclk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Word clock, Fs: low = left\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"lrck\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The outputs start slot slot_next at the next clock edge\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"bit_start\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The slot (0 … 63) starting at the next edge\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"slot_next\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A two-flip-flop-synchronised input now shows the bit of slot (as it was just before SCLK rose)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"bit_sample\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The current slot\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"slot\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"bit_start for slot 0\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_start\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"i2s_clocks\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.1\"\n}\n",
    "cores/i2s_clocks/rtl/i2s_clocks.v" : "// I2S clocks for a codec that is the clock slave (Cirrus CS4344/CS5343, Digilent Pmod I2S2, most DACs and ADCs):\n// MCLK, SCLK (64 Fs: 32 bits a channel) and LRCK (Fs, low = left) from the system clock, with counters only,\n// all three in step. Fs = clock / (MCLK_DIV · RATIO): 27 MHz / (2 · 512) = 26.37 kHz, 100 MHz / (8 · 256) =\n// 48.83 kHz.\n//\n// The outputs are registered and change together. Two strobes tell transmitters and receivers when:\n//   bit_start   the outputs start bit slot `slot_next` (0 … 63) at the next clock edge (SCLK falls then; LRCK\n//               changes with slot 0 and 32) — a transmitter registers its data bit on it (i2s_transmitter);\n//   bit_sample  an input through a two-flip-flop synchroniser now shows the bit of slot `slot` as it was on\n//               the pin one clock before SCLK rose — a receiver takes it then (i2s_receiver). I2S ADCs change\n//               their data *after* SCLK's rising edge (the CS5343 holds the old bit at least 40 ns; on the\n//               Pmod I2S2 it changed 70–90 ns after), so the bit is taken before that edge, never after it.\n// frame_start is bit_start for slot 0.\n//\n// MCLK_DIV is even and at least 2; RATIO (MCLK per LRCK) a multiple of 64 the codec accepts (256, 384, 512,\n// 768); MCLK_DIV · RATIO / 64 (system clocks per SCLK) at least 8.\n\nmodule i2s_clocks #(\n    parameter MCLK_DIV = 2,\n    parameter RATIO    = 512\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    output reg        mclk = 1'b0,\n    output reg        sclk = 1'b0,\n    output reg        lrck = 1'b0,\n    output wire       bit_start,\n    output wire [5:0] slot_next,\n    output wire       bit_sample,\n    output wire [5:0] slot,\n    output wire       frame_start\n);\n    localparam S = MCLK_DIV * RATIO / 64;               // system clocks per SCLK\n    localparam S_BITS = $clog2(S);\n    localparam M_BITS = MCLK_DIV > 2 ? $clog2(MCLK_DIV) : 1;\n    // The positions as integers first, then their low bits (a typed localparam set from a 32-bit expression is a\n    // truncation warning in Quartus).\n    localparam integer S_LAST_I = S - 1, S_HALF_I = S / 2, S_SAMPLE_I = S / 2;\n    localparam integer M_LAST_I = MCLK_DIV - 1, M_HALF_I = MCLK_DIV / 2;\n    localparam [S_BITS-1:0] S_LAST = S_LAST_I[S_BITS-1:0], S_HALF = S_HALF_I[S_BITS-1:0], S_SAMPLE = S_SAMPLE_I[S_BITS-1:0];\n    localparam [M_BITS-1:0] M_LAST = M_LAST_I[M_BITS-1:0], M_HALF = M_HALF_I[M_BITS-1:0];\n\n    reg [S_BITS-1:0] phase = 0;     // position within the SCLK period\n    reg [5:0]        slot_q = 6'd0;\n    reg [M_BITS-1:0] m = 0;         // position within the MCLK period\n\n    wire             phase_wrap = phase == S_LAST;\n    wire [S_BITS-1:0] phase_n = phase_wrap ? {S_BITS{1'b0}} : phase + 1'b1;\n    wire [M_BITS-1:0] m_n = m == M_LAST ? {M_BITS{1'b0}} : m + 1'b1;\n\n    assign bit_start = phase_wrap;\n    assign slot_next = phase_wrap ? slot_q + 6'd1 : slot_q;\n    assign slot = slot_q;\n    assign bit_sample = phase == S_SAMPLE;\n    assign frame_start = bit_start && slot_next == 6'd0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        phase <= phase_n;\n        slot_q <= slot_next;\n        m <= m_n;\n        // Low for the first half of each period: SCLK falls as a slot starts, MCLK as its count starts.\n        sclk <= phase_n >= S_HALF;\n        mclk <= m_n >= M_HALF;\n        lrck <= slot_next[5];\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2s_clocks/tb/i2s_clocks_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for i2s_clocks at two settings (12 and 16 system clocks per SCLK): the periods and\n// duty of MCLK, SCLK and LRCK, MCLK cycles per frame, LRCK changing with SCLK's falling edge, bit_start and\n// slot_next against the slots counted on the pins, and bit_sample against a data pin that changes to the next\n// slot's bit 5 ns after SCLK rises, as an I2S ADC does (the CS5343 after at least 40 ns): a sample taken after the\n// rising edge gets the next bit. Read through two flip-flops. Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule i2s_clocks_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    task check;\n        input ok;\n        input [8*72-1:0] what;\n        begin\n            if (!ok) begin\n                if (errors < 10) $display(\"FAIL: %0s at %0t\", what, $time);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    wire mclk_a, sclk_a, lrck_a, start_a, sample_a, frame_a;\n    wire [5:0] next_a, slot_a;\n    i2s_clocks #(.MCLK_DIV(2), .RATIO(384)) dut_a (.clk(clk), .mclk(mclk_a), .sclk(sclk_a), .lrck(lrck_a),\n        .bit_start(start_a), .slot_next(next_a), .bit_sample(sample_a), .slot(slot_a), .frame_start(frame_a));\n    wire mclk_b, sclk_b, lrck_b, start_b, sample_b, frame_b;\n    wire [5:0] next_b, slot_b;\n    i2s_clocks #(.MCLK_DIV(4), .RATIO(256)) dut_b (.clk(clk), .mclk(mclk_b), .sclk(sclk_b), .lrck(lrck_b),\n        .bit_start(start_b), .slot_next(next_b), .bit_sample(sample_b), .slot(slot_b), .frame_start(frame_b));\n\n    // One checker per instance, counting in system clocks.\n    `define CHECKER(NAME, MCLK, SCLK, LRCK, START, NEXT, SAMPLE, SLOT, FRAME, MDIV, RAT) \\\n    integer NAME``_t = 0, NAME``_m_rise = -1, NAME``_s_rise = -1, NAME``_s_fall = -1, NAME``_l_fall = -1; \\\n    integer NAME``_m_per_frame = 0, NAME``_pin_slot = -1, NAME``_frames = 0, NAME``_samples = 0; \\\n    reg NAME``_m_was = 0, NAME``_s_was = 0, NAME``_l_was = 0, NAME``_start_was = 0; \\\n    reg [5:0] NAME``_next_was = 0; \\\n    reg [1:0] NAME``_sync = 0; reg NAME``_data = 0; \\\n    always @(posedge clk) begin \\\n        NAME``_t = NAME``_t + 1; \\\n        NAME``_sync <= {NAME``_sync[0], NAME``_data}; \\\n        if (MCLK && !NAME``_m_was) begin \\\n            if (NAME``_m_rise >= 0) check(NAME``_t - NAME``_m_rise == MDIV, `\"NAME MCLK period`\"); \\\n            NAME``_m_rise = NAME``_t; NAME``_m_per_frame = NAME``_m_per_frame + 1; \\\n        end \\\n        if (!MCLK && NAME``_m_was && NAME``_m_rise >= 0) check(NAME``_t - NAME``_m_rise == MDIV / 2, `\"NAME MCLK high half`\"); \\\n        if (SCLK && !NAME``_s_was) begin \\\n            if (NAME``_s_rise >= 0) check(NAME``_t - NAME``_s_rise == MDIV * RAT / 64, `\"NAME SCLK period`\"); \\\n            NAME``_s_rise = NAME``_t; \\\n        end \\\n        if (!SCLK && NAME``_s_was) begin \\\n            if (NAME``_s_rise >= 0) check(NAME``_t - NAME``_s_rise == MDIV * RAT / 128, `\"NAME SCLK high half`\"); \\\n            NAME``_s_fall = NAME``_t; \\\n            NAME``_pin_slot = NAME``_pin_slot >= 0 ? (NAME``_pin_slot + 1) % 64 : -1; \\\n            check(NAME``_start_was, `\"NAME SCLK fell without bit_start the clock before`\"); \\\n            if (NAME``_pin_slot >= 0) check(NAME``_next_was == NAME``_pin_slot, `\"NAME slot_next against the slot on the pins`\"); \\\n        end \\\n        if (SCLK != NAME``_s_was) ; else if (NAME``_start_was && NAME``_s_was) check(0, `\"NAME bit_start without SCLK falling`\"); \\\n        if (LRCK != NAME``_l_was) begin \\\n            check(!SCLK && NAME``_s_was, `\"NAME LRCK changed off SCLK's falling edge`\"); \\\n            if (!LRCK) begin \\\n                if (NAME``_l_fall >= 0) begin \\\n                    check(NAME``_t - NAME``_l_fall == MDIV * RAT, `\"NAME LRCK period`\"); \\\n                    check(NAME``_m_per_frame == RAT, `\"NAME MCLK cycles per frame`\"); \\\n                    NAME``_frames = NAME``_frames + 1; \\\n                end \\\n                NAME``_l_fall = NAME``_t; NAME``_m_per_frame = 0; NAME``_pin_slot = 0; \\\n            end \\\n        end \\\n        if (SAMPLE && NAME``_pin_slot >= 0 && NAME``_t - NAME``_l_fall > 2) begin \\\n            check(SLOT == NAME``_pin_slot, `\"NAME slot at bit_sample`\"); \\\n            check(NAME``_sync[1] == (NAME``_pin_slot[0] ^ NAME``_pin_slot[1]), `\"NAME bit_sample reads the bit of its slot`\"); \\\n            NAME``_samples = NAME``_samples + 1; \\\n        end \\\n        if (FRAME) check(NEXT == 6'd0 && START, `\"NAME frame_start off slot 0`\"); \\\n        NAME``_m_was = MCLK; NAME``_s_was = SCLK; NAME``_l_was = LRCK; NAME``_start_was = START; NAME``_next_was = NEXT; \\\n    end \\\n    /* The ADC: after SCLK rises in slot k, the bit of slot k + 1 (k counted when SCLK fell, a clock before) */ \\\n    always @(posedge SCLK) \\\n        NAME``_data <= #5 (NAME``_pin_slot >= 0 ? ((NAME``_pin_slot + 1) % 64) % 4 == 1 || ((NAME``_pin_slot + 1) % 64) % 4 == 2 : 1'b0);\n\n    `CHECKER(a, mclk_a, sclk_a, lrck_a, start_a, next_a, sample_a, slot_a, frame_a, 2, 384)\n    `CHECKER(b, mclk_b, sclk_b, lrck_b, start_b, next_b, sample_b, slot_b, frame_b, 4, 256)\n\n    initial begin\n        #(10 * 1024 * 6);   // six frames of the longer setting\n        check(a_frames >= 4 && b_frames >= 4, \"too few frames\");\n        check(a_samples >= 4 * 64 && b_samples >= 4 * 64, \"too few samples checked\");\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS (%0d and %0d frames, %0d and %0d bits sampled)\", a_frames, b_frames, a_samples, b_samples);\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2s_receiver/README.md" : "# I2S receiver\n\nReceives 24-bit stereo samples from an I2S ADC (the CS5343 on the Pmod I2S2's line in) with `i2s_clocks`.\nThe data pin goes through two flip-flops; each bit is taken just before SCLK rises (the ADC changes it\nafter the rising edge). `valid` pulses when a frame's left and right samples are complete; both outputs then\nhold that frame.\n\n```verilog\nwire [23:0] in_left, in_right;\nwire in_valid;\ni2s_receiver u_adc (\n    .clk(clk), .bit_sample(bit_sample), .slot(slot), .sdout(adc_sdout),\n    .left(in_left), .right(in_right), .valid(in_valid)\n);\n```\n\nThe ADC must be the clock slave (on the Pmod I2S2: jumper JP1 on SLV), with MCLK, SCLK and LRCK from\n`i2s_clocks`.\n",
    "cores/i2s_receiver/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"i2s_clocks\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Receives 24-bit stereo samples from an I2S ADC (CS5343, Pmod I2S2's line in) with i2s_clocks: the data pin through two flip-flops, each bit taken just before SCLK rises (the ADC changes it after the rising edge). `valid` pulses when a frame's left and right are in.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 188.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 17,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 98\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 120.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 36,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 6,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 20\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 116.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 8,\n        \"DFF\" : 98,\n        \"LUT4\" : 88\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 64.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 111,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 123.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 18,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 98\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 317.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 95,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 7\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"i2s_receiver\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"i2s\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"adc\",\n    \"codec\",\n    \"cs5343\",\n    \"pmod\",\n    \"stereo\",\n    \"sound\",\n    \"microphone\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Empfängt 24-Bit-Stereo-Samples von einem I2S-ADC (CS5343, Line-In des Pmod I2S2) mit i2s_clocks: der Datenpin über zwei Flipflops, jedes Bit kurz vor der steigenden Flanke von SCLK übernommen (der ADC ändert es danach). `valid` pulst, wenn links und rechts eines Rahmens da sind.\",\n      \"name\" : \"I2S-Empfänger\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"I2S receiver\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock (i2s_clocks')\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From i2s_clocks\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"bit_sample\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From i2s_clocks\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"slot\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Serial data from the ADC (the pin)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"sdout\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Left sample of the last frame\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"left\",\n      \"width\" : 24\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Right sample of the last frame\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"right\",\n      \"width\" : 24\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when a frame is complete\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"valid\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"i2s_receiver\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.1\"\n}\n",
    "cores/i2s_receiver/rtl/i2s_receiver.v" : "// I2S receiver for an ADC (24-bit samples, 32 bit slots a channel) with i2s_clocks: `sdout` from the pin\n// through two flip-flops, each bit taken on bit_sample (the value just before SCLK rose: the ADC changes it after), MSB first, the MSB one\n// SCLK after LRCK changes. `left` and `right` hold the last complete frame; `valid` pulses when the right\n// channel's last bit is in (both are then from the same frame).\n\nmodule i2s_receiver (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire        bit_sample,\n    input  wire [5:0]  slot,\n    input  wire        sdout,\n    output reg  [23:0] left = 24'd0,\n    output reg  [23:0] right = 24'd0,\n    output reg         valid = 1'b0\n);\n    reg [1:0]  sync = 2'b00;\n    reg [22:0] shift = 23'd0;\n    reg [23:0] left_frame = 24'd0;\n    wire       data_slot = slot[4:0] >= 5'd1 && slot[4:0] <= 5'd24;\n    wire [23:0] word = {shift, sync[1]};\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        sync <= {sync[0], sdout};\n        valid <= 1'b0;\n        if (bit_sample && data_slot) begin\n            shift <= word[22:0];\n            if (slot == 6'd24) left_frame <= word;\n            if (slot == 6'd56) begin\n                left <= left_frame;\n                right <= word;\n                valid <= 1'b1;\n            end\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2s_receiver/tb/i2s_receiver_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for i2s_receiver with i2s_clocks at 8 system clocks per SCLK: a model of an ADC\n// (CS5343: I2S, 24 bits, each bit put out after SCLK's rising edge — here 5 ns after it, the CS5343 holds the old\n// bit at least 40 ns — so only a sample taken before the rising edge reads the right bit)\n// sends asymmetric samples; each `valid` must bring the frame sent, left and right. Prints PASS or every\n// failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule i2s_receiver_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    wire mclk, sclk, lrck, bit_start, bit_sample, frame_start;\n    wire [5:0] slot_next, slot;\n    i2s_clocks #(.MCLK_DIV(2), .RATIO(256)) u_clocks (.clk(clk), .mclk(mclk), .sclk(sclk), .lrck(lrck),\n        .bit_start(bit_start), .slot_next(slot_next), .bit_sample(bit_sample), .slot(slot), .frame_start(frame_start));\n\n    reg sdout = 1'b0;\n    wire [23:0] left, right;\n    wire valid;\n    i2s_receiver dut (.clk(clk), .bit_sample(bit_sample), .slot(slot), .sdout(sdout), .left(left), .right(right), .valid(valid));\n\n    // The ADC: LRCK's change seen on SCLK's rising edge (LRCK changes on the falling one, so reading it there\n    // would race), a new word for the channel, each bit put out 5 ns after a rising edge for the next slot: the MSB\n    // after the first rising edge, so it's read at the second, one SCLK after LRCK changed (DS687 figure 1).\n    reg [23:0] lfsr = 24'h5F3A91, word = 0;\n    reg [23:0] sent_left [0:63], sent_right [0:63];\n    integer adc_slot = 0, frames_sent = 0;\n    reg lrck_was = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge sclk) begin\n        if (lrck !== lrck_was) begin\n            adc_slot = 0;\n            lrck_was = lrck;\n            lfsr = {lfsr[22:0], lfsr[23] ^ lfsr[22] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[16]};\n            word = lrck ? lfsr ^ 24'h0F1E2D : lfsr;\n            if (!lrck) sent_left[frames_sent % 64] = word;\n            else begin\n                sent_right[frames_sent % 64] = word;\n                frames_sent = frames_sent + 1;\n            end\n        end else begin\n            adc_slot = adc_slot + 1;\n        end\n        sdout <= #5 (adc_slot + 1 >= 1 && adc_slot + 1 <= 24 ? word[24 - (adc_slot + 1)] : 1'b1);   // ones between words\n    end\n\n    integer frames_got = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        if (valid) begin\n            // The first frame may have started before the ADC's first word.\n            if (frames_got > 0 && (left !== sent_left[frames_got % 64] || right !== sent_right[frames_got % 64])) begin\n                if (errors < 8) $display(\"FAIL: frame %0d got %h %h, sent %h %h\", frames_got, left, right,\n                                         sent_left[frames_got % 64], sent_right[frames_got % 64]);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            frames_got = frames_got + 1;\n        end\n\n    initial begin\n        #(10 * 512 * 12);\n        if (frames_got < 10) begin $display(\"FAIL: only %0d frames received\", frames_got); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS (%0d frames)\", frames_got);\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2s_transmitter/README.md" : "# I2S transmitter\n\nSends 24-bit stereo samples to an I2S DAC (the CS4344 on the Pmod I2S2's line out) with `i2s_clocks`. The\nsamples are taken at the start of each frame; `taken` pulses then, so the next samples can be prepared\nduring the frame.\n\n```verilog\ni2s_transmitter u_dac (\n    .clk(clk), .bit_start(bit_start), .slot_next(slot_next),\n    .left({tone, 8'h00}), .right({tone, 8'h00}),\n    .taken(next_sample), .sdin(dac_sdin)\n);\n```\n\nSamples are two's complement; a 16-bit source goes into the top bits. `sdin` changes with SCLK's falling\nedge, the MSB one SCLK after LRCK changes (I2S).\n",
    "cores/i2s_transmitter/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"i2s_clocks\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"Sends 24-bit stereo samples to an I2S DAC (CS4344, Pmod I2S2's line out) with i2s_clocks: MSB first, one SCLK after LRCK changes. Samples are taken at the start of each frame; `taken` pulses then (once per sample period).\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 158.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 58,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 50\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 105.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 4,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 9,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 47,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 103,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 122,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 15\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 96.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 16,\n        \"DFF\" : 50,\n        \"LUT4\" : 89,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 3,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 50.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 130,\n        \"SB_GB\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 105.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 64,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 50\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 276,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 2,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 50,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 38\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"i2s_transmitter\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"i2s\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"dac\",\n    \"codec\",\n    \"cs4344\",\n    \"pmod\",\n    \"stereo\",\n    \"sound\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Sendet 24-Bit-Stereo-Samples an einen I2S-DAC (CS4344, Line-Out des Pmod I2S2) mit i2s_clocks: MSB zuerst, einen SCLK nach dem LRCK-Wechsel. Die Samples werden am Anfang jedes Rahmens übernommen; `taken` pulst dann (einmal pro Abtastperiode).\",\n      \"name\" : \"I2S-Sender\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"I2S transmitter\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock (i2s_clocks')\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From i2s_clocks\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"bit_start\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From i2s_clocks\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"slot_next\",\n      \"width\" : 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Left sample (24-bit, two's complement)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"left\",\n      \"width\" : 24\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Right sample\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"right\",\n      \"width\" : 24\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulses when left and right were taken (frame start)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"taken\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Serial data to the DAC\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"sdin\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"i2s_transmitter\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/i2s_transmitter/rtl/i2s_transmitter.v" : "// I2S transmitter for a DAC (24-bit samples, 32 bit slots a channel): the data bit for i2s_clocks, MSB first,\n// the MSB one SCLK after LRCK changes (slots 1 … 24 left, 33 … 56 right, the rest zero).\n//\n// `left` and `right` are taken at the start of each frame; `taken` pulses then, so new samples can follow\n// within the frame (one sample period). `sdin` is registered and changes with SCLK's falling edge.\n\nmodule i2s_transmitter (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire        bit_start,\n    input  wire [5:0]  slot_next,\n    input  wire [23:0] left,\n    input  wire [23:0] right,\n    output reg         taken = 1'b0,\n    output reg         sdin = 1'b0\n);\n    reg [23:0] word_left = 24'd0, word_right = 24'd0;\n    // Slot 0 starts a frame: it sends a zero, the words are taken with it.\n    wire [23:0] word_now = slot_next[5] ? word_right : word_left;\n    wire [4:0]  in_half = slot_next[4:0];\n    wire        data_slot = in_half >= 5'd1 && in_half <= 5'd24;\n    wire [4:0]  bit_index = 5'd24 - in_half;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        taken <= 1'b0;\n        if (bit_start) begin\n            if (slot_next == 6'd0) begin\n                word_left <= left;\n                word_right <= right;\n                taken <= 1'b1;\n                sdin <= 1'b0;\n            end else begin\n                sdin <= data_slot ? word_now[bit_index] : 1'b0;\n            end\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/i2s_transmitter/tb/i2s_transmitter_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for i2s_transmitter with i2s_clocks at 8 system clocks per SCLK: a model of a DAC\n// (CS4344: I2S, 24 bits, the MSB on the second rising SCLK edge after LRCK changes) decodes the pins, and every\n// frame must bring the samples given when `taken` pulsed. The samples are asymmetric (no bit palindromes).\n// Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule i2s_transmitter_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    wire mclk, sclk, lrck, bit_start, bit_sample, frame_start;\n    wire [5:0] slot_next, slot;\n    i2s_clocks #(.MCLK_DIV(2), .RATIO(256)) u_clocks (.clk(clk), .mclk(mclk), .sclk(sclk), .lrck(lrck),\n        .bit_start(bit_start), .slot_next(slot_next), .bit_sample(bit_sample), .slot(slot), .frame_start(frame_start));\n\n    reg [23:0] left = 24'h000000, right = 24'h000000;\n    wire taken, sdin;\n    i2s_transmitter dut (.clk(clk), .bit_start(bit_start), .slot_next(slot_next), .left(left), .right(right),\n        .taken(taken), .sdin(sdin));\n\n    // New samples after each `taken`; the ones taken are remembered for the DAC's check.\n    reg [23:0] sent_left [0:63], sent_right [0:63];\n    integer frames_taken = 0;\n    reg [23:0] lfsr = 24'h9E3779;\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        if (taken) begin\n            sent_left[frames_taken % 64] = left;\n            sent_right[frames_taken % 64] = right;\n            frames_taken = frames_taken + 1;\n            lfsr = {lfsr[22:0], lfsr[23] ^ lfsr[22] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[16]};\n            left <= lfsr;\n            right <= {lfsr[11:0], lfsr[23:12]} ^ 24'hA5C30F;\n        end\n\n    // The DAC: a slot counter from LRCK's changes, bits taken on SCLK's rising edge.\n    integer dac_slot = 0, frames_heard = 0, taken_per_frame = 0;\n    reg lrck_was = 1'b0;\n    reg [23:0] shift = 0, heard_left = 0;\n    always @(posedge sclk) begin\n        if (lrck !== lrck_was) begin\n            dac_slot = 0;\n            lrck_was = lrck;\n        end else begin\n            dac_slot = dac_slot + 1;\n        end\n        if (dac_slot >= 1 && dac_slot <= 24) shift = {shift[22:0], sdin};\n        if (dac_slot == 24) begin\n            if (!lrck) heard_left = shift;\n            else if (frames_heard < frames_taken) begin\n                if (heard_left !== sent_left[frames_heard % 64] || shift !== sent_right[frames_heard % 64]) begin\n                    if (errors < 8) $display(\"FAIL: frame %0d heard %h %h, sent %h %h\", frames_heard, heard_left, shift,\n                                             sent_left[frames_heard % 64], sent_right[frames_heard % 64]);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n                frames_heard = frames_heard + 1;\n            end\n        end\n        // Bits outside the data slots are zero.\n        if ((dac_slot == 0 || dac_slot > 24) && sdin !== 1'b0) begin\n            if (errors < 8) $display(\"FAIL: a one in slot %0d\", dac_slot + (lrck ? 32 : 0));\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // `taken` once a frame, with slot 0.\n    always @(posedge clk) if (taken && slot != 6'd0) begin\n        $display(\"FAIL: taken off slot 0 (slot %0d)\", slot);\n        errors = errors + 1;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #(10 * 512 * 12);   // twelve frames\n        if (frames_heard < 10) begin $display(\"FAIL: only %0d frames heard\", frames_heard); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS (%0d frames)\", frames_heard);\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/pwm/README.md" : "# PWM generator\n\n`out` is high for `duty` cycles of every `2^WIDTH`. With WIDTH 8 and a 12 MHz clock the period\nis 256 cycles (about 47 kHz), fine for dimming LEDs without flicker. `duty` is read at the start\nof each period, so it may change at any time. For servos (50 Hz, 1–2 ms pulses) use a larger\nWIDTH or a slower clock enable.\n\n```verilog\npwm #(.WIDTH(8)) u_dim (.clk(clk), .duty(brightness), .out(led_pwm));\n```\n",
    "cores/pwm/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Pulse-width modulation with a set resolution: dims LEDs, drives motors and servos (with the right period), makes analog levels with a filter.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 305.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 36,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 17\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 157.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 9,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 9,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 24,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 21\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 233.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 20,\n        \"DFF\" : 17,\n        \"LUT4\" : 13,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 88.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 29\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 180.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 29,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 17\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"pwm\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"pwm\",\n    \"dimming\",\n    \"led\",\n    \"duty\",\n    \"motor\",\n    \"brightness\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Pulsweitenmodulation mit einstellbarer Auflösung: dimmt LEDs, steuert Motoren und Servos (mit passender Periode), erzeugt mit Filter analoge Pegel.\",\n      \"name\" : \"PWM-Generator\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"PWM generator\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"Resolution in bits: a period is 2^WIDTH cycles\",\n      \"name\" : \"WIDTH\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"High cycles per period\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"duty\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"PWM output\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"out\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"pwm\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/pwm/rtl/pwm.v" : "// Pulse-width modulation: `out` is high for `duty` of every 2^WIDTH clock cycles (0 = always\n// low, 2^WIDTH - 1 = all but one cycle). `duty` is taken at the start of each period, so changing\n// it never makes a short glitch.\n\nmodule pwm #(\n    parameter WIDTH = 8\n) (\n    input  wire             clk,\n    input  wire [WIDTH-1:0] duty,\n    output reg              out = 1'b0\n);\n    reg [WIDTH-1:0] counter = 0;\n    reg [WIDTH-1:0] current = 0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        counter <= counter + 1'b1;\n        if (counter == {WIDTH{1'b1}})\n            current <= duty;\n        out <= (counter < current);\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/pwm/tb/pwm_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for pwm (WIDTH 4: 16-cycle periods): the high time of each period for\n// several duty values, the extremes, and a duty change in the middle of a period, which must only\n// take effect with the next one.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule pwm_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    reg [3:0] duty = 4'd0;\n    wire out;\n\n    pwm #(.WIDTH(4)) dut (.clk(clk), .duty(duty), .out(out));\n\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    // High cycles in the next full period (aligned to the counter).\n    task measure(output integer high);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            high = 0;\n            for (i = 0; i < 16; i = i + 1) begin\n                @(posedge clk); #1;\n                if (out) high = high + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task check(input [3:0] value);\n        integer high;\n        begin\n            duty = value;\n            measure(high);      // the period that picks the new value up\n            measure(high);\n            if (high != value) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: duty %0d: high for %0d of 16 cycles\", value, high);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer high;\n    integer i;\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"pwm_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, pwm_tb);\n        // Align to a period boundary (the counter is internal: wait for the wrap).\n        wait (dut.counter == 4'd15);\n        @(posedge clk);\n        #1;\n        for (i = 0; i < 16; i = i + 1) @(posedge clk);\n        check(4'd0); check(4'd1); check(4'd5); check(4'd8); check(4'd15);\n\n        // A change in the middle of a period: that period keeps 15, the next one has 3.\n        duty = 4'd15;\n        measure(high); measure(high);\n        high = 0;\n        for (i = 0; i < 16; i = i + 1) begin\n            if (i == 8) duty = 4'd3;\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            if (out) high = high + 1;\n        end\n        if (high != 15) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: duty changed mid-period: that period high for %0d of 16, expected 15\", high);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        measure(high);\n        if (high != 3) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: the period after a change to 3: high for %0d of 16\", high);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    // A broken design must not hang the simulation.\n    initial begin\n        #100000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/seg7/README.md" : "# Seven-segment display\n\nShows `value` as hexadecimal digits on a multiplexed display (the common kind on boards and\nPMODs: shared segment lines, one enable per digit). Digit 0 shows `value[3:0]`. Each digit is lit\n`REFRESH_HZ` times a second — 250 Hz per digit doesn't flicker.\n\n```verilog\nseg7 #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .DIGITS(4), .SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW(1), .DIGITS_ACTIVE_LOW(1)) u_display (\n    .clk(clk), .value(counter[15:0]), .dots(4'b0000),\n    .segments(seg), .dp(seg_dp), .digit_enable(digit)\n);\n```\n\n`segments` is `{g, f, e, d, c, b, a}`; map each bit to the right pin in the constraints. For\ndecimal numbers, convert to BCD first (one digit per four bits). Check the board's schematic\nfor the polarities: common-anode displays with PNP digit drivers usually need both active low.\n",
    "cores/seg7/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Drives a multiplexed seven-segment display: hexadecimal digits, decimal points, either polarity.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 208,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 51,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 28\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 87.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 7,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 16,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 28\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 180.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 16,\n        \"DFF\" : 28,\n        \"LUT4\" : 22\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 78.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 54\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 139,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 50,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 28\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"seg7\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"seven segment\",\n    \"7-segment\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"hex\",\n    \"digits\",\n    \"led\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Steuert eine gemultiplexte Siebensegmentanzeige: hexadezimale Ziffern, Dezimalpunkte, beide Polaritäten.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Siebensegmentanzeige\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Seven-segment display\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"Number of digits\",\n      \"name\" : \"DIGITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 250,\n      \"description\" : \"How often each digit is lit per second\",\n      \"name\" : \"REFRESH_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: a segment lights when its pin is low\",\n      \"name\" : \"SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: a digit is selected when its pin is low\",\n      \"name\" : \"DIGITS_ACTIVE_LOW\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Four bits per digit, digit 0 lowest\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"value\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Decimal point per digit\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"dots\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Segments {g, f, e, d, c, b, a}\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"segments\",\n      \"width\" : 7\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Decimal point segment\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"dp\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One digit selected at a time\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"digit_enable\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"seg7\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/seg7/rtl/seg7.v" : "// Seven-segment display driver for DIGITS multiplexed digits. Shows `value` in hexadecimal, four\n// bits per digit (digit 0 = the lowest four bits), with a decimal point per digit from `dots`.\n// The digits are lit one after another, each for 1 / (REFRESH_HZ × DIGITS) seconds.\n//\n// `segments` is {g, f, e, d, c, b, a}. Set SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW / DIGITS_ACTIVE_LOW to match the\n// board (common anode displays usually take both low).\n\nmodule seg7 #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ              = 12000000,\n    parameter DIGITS              = 4,\n    parameter REFRESH_HZ          = 250,\n    parameter SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW = 0,\n    parameter DIGITS_ACTIVE_LOW   = 0\n) (\n    input  wire                  clk,\n    input  wire [DIGITS*4-1:0]   value,\n    input  wire [DIGITS-1:0]     dots,\n    output reg  [6:0]            segments = 7'd0,\n    output reg                   dp = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [DIGITS-1:0]     digit_enable = 0\n);\n    localparam SLOT = CLK_HZ / (REFRESH_HZ * DIGITS) > 0 ? CLK_HZ / (REFRESH_HZ * DIGITS) : 1;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = SLOT > 1 ? $clog2(SLOT) : 1;\n    localparam INDEX_BITS = DIGITS > 1 ? $clog2(DIGITS) : 1;\n\n    function [6:0] pattern(input [3:0] digit);\n        case (digit)            // gfedcba\n            4'h0: pattern = 7'b0111111;\n            4'h1: pattern = 7'b0000110;\n            4'h2: pattern = 7'b1011011;\n            4'h3: pattern = 7'b1001111;\n            4'h4: pattern = 7'b1100110;\n            4'h5: pattern = 7'b1101101;\n            4'h6: pattern = 7'b1111101;\n            4'h7: pattern = 7'b0000111;\n            4'h8: pattern = 7'b1111111;\n            4'h9: pattern = 7'b1101111;\n            4'hA: pattern = 7'b1110111;\n            4'hB: pattern = 7'b1111100;\n            4'hC: pattern = 7'b0111001;\n            4'hD: pattern = 7'b1011110;\n            4'hE: pattern = 7'b1111001;\n            default: pattern = 7'b1110001;   // F\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n    reg [INDEX_BITS-1:0] index = 0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (clocks == SLOT - 1) begin\n            clocks <= 0;\n            index <= (index == DIGITS - 1) ? 0 : index + 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n        end\n        segments <= pattern(value[index * 4 +: 4]) ^ {7{SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW != 0}};\n        dp <= dots[index] ^ (SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW != 0);\n        digit_enable <= ({{(DIGITS-1){1'b0}}, 1'b1} << index) ^ {DIGITS{DIGITS_ACTIVE_LOW != 0}};\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/seg7/tb/seg7_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for seg7: four digits showing 0x1A7F with the second dot lit, in both\n// polarities. Over two refresh cycles every digit must be enabled alone, for the same time, with\n// its own pattern and dot.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule seg7_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    wire [6:0] seg_h, seg_l;\n    wire dp_h, dp_l;\n    wire [3:0] en_h, en_l;\n\n    // 4 digits × 100 Hz at 40 kHz \"clock\": 100 clocks per digit.\n    seg7 #(.CLK_HZ(40000), .DIGITS(4), .REFRESH_HZ(100)) high_active (\n        .clk(clk), .value(16'h1A7F), .dots(4'b0010), .segments(seg_h), .dp(dp_h), .digit_enable(en_h));\n    seg7 #(.CLK_HZ(40000), .DIGITS(4), .REFRESH_HZ(100), .SEGMENTS_ACTIVE_LOW(1), .DIGITS_ACTIVE_LOW(1)) low_active (\n        .clk(clk), .value(16'h1A7F), .dots(4'b0010), .segments(seg_l), .dp(dp_l), .digit_enable(en_l));\n\n    // Three digits: the digit counter must wrap by itself, not at a power of two.\n    wire [6:0] seg3;\n    wire dp3;\n    wire [2:0] en3;\n    seg7 #(.CLK_HZ(30000), .DIGITS(3), .REFRESH_HZ(100)) three (\n        .clk(clk), .value(12'h1A7), .dots(3'b000), .segments(seg3), .dp(dp3), .digit_enable(en3));\n    integer three_on [0:2];\n\n    reg [6:0] expected [0:3];\n    integer on_time [0:3];\n    integer d, cycle;\n    initial begin\n        expected[0] = 7'b1110001;   // F\n        expected[1] = 7'b0000111;   // 7\n        expected[2] = 7'b1110111;   // A\n        expected[3] = 7'b0000110;   // 1\n        for (d = 0; d < 4; d = d + 1) on_time[d] = 0;\n        for (d = 0; d < 3; d = d + 1) three_on[d] = 0;\n        $dumpfile(\"seg7_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, seg7_tb);\n        repeat (5) @(posedge clk);\n        for (cycle = 0; cycle < 800; cycle = cycle + 1) begin\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            if (en_l !== ~en_h || seg_l !== ~seg_h || dp_l !== ~dp_h) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: the active-low outputs aren't the inverse of the active-high ones\");\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            case (en_h)\n                4'b0001: d = 0;\n                4'b0010: d = 1;\n                4'b0100: d = 2;\n                4'b1000: d = 3;\n                default: begin d = -1; $display(\"FAIL: digit enables %b: not exactly one digit\", en_h); errors = errors + 1; end\n            endcase\n            if (d >= 0) begin\n                on_time[d] = on_time[d] + 1;\n                if (seg_h !== expected[d]) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: digit %0d shows %b, expected %b\", d, seg_h, expected[d]);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n                if (dp_h !== (d == 1)) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: digit %0d dot %b\", d, dp_h);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n            end\n            case (en3)\n                3'b001: three_on[0] = three_on[0] + 1;\n                3'b010: three_on[1] = three_on[1] + 1;\n                3'b100: three_on[2] = three_on[2] + 1;\n                default: begin $display(\"FAIL: three digits: enables %b\", en3); errors = errors + 1; end\n            endcase\n            if (errors > 5) begin cycle = 800; end\n        end\n        for (d = 0; d < 4; d = d + 1)\n            if (on_time[d] != 200) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: digit %0d lit for %0d of 800 cycles, expected 200\", d, on_time[d]);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        // 800 cycles at 100 cycles per digit: 300, 300, 200 or a rotation of it.\n        if (three_on[0] + three_on[1] + three_on[2] != 800 || three_on[0] > 300 || three_on[1] > 300 || three_on[2] > 300) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: three digits lit %0d, %0d, %0d cycles\", three_on[0], three_on[1], three_on[2]);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #100000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/spi_master/README.md" : "# SPI master\n\nTransfers one byte at a time: raise `start` with `tx_data` while `ready`; `done` pulses with the\nanswer on `rx_data`. Choose the mode with `CPOL` and `CPHA` (mode 0 = both 0, the most common).\n\nChip select is not part of the core, so a transaction can be as many bytes as the device\nneeds: pull your CS pin low, transfer the bytes, release it.\n\n```verilog\nspi_master #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .SPI_HZ(1000000)) u_spi (\n    .clk(clk), .tx_data(cmd), .start(go), .ready(spi_ready), .rx_data(answer), .done(spi_done),\n    .sclk(flash_sck), .mosi(flash_mosi), .miso(flash_miso)\n);\n```\n\n`SPI_HZ` is rounded down so that half a period is a whole number of clock cycles.\n",
    "cores/spi_master/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"SPI controller for flash, sensors, displays and ADCs: one byte per transfer in any of the four SPI modes, chip select under your control.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 228.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 45,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 35\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 112.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 3,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 5,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 21,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 11,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 33\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 207.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 10,\n        \"DFF\" : 35,\n        \"LUT4\" : 60,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 12,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 4,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 77.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 53\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 128.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 39,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 35\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"spi_master\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"spi\",\n    \"master\",\n    \"controller\",\n    \"flash\",\n    \"sensor\",\n    \"adc\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"serial\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"SPI-Controller für Flash, Sensoren, Displays und ADCs: ein Byte pro Übertragung in allen vier SPI-Modi, Chip-Select in deiner Hand.\",\n      \"name\" : \"SPI-Master\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"SPI master\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1000000,\n      \"description\" : \"SPI clock (rounded so a half period is whole clock cycles)\",\n      \"name\" : \"SPI_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock idle level (SPI mode 2 and 3: 1)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CPOL\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"0: sample on the first edge, 1: on the second (modes 1 and 3)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CPHA\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to send\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Start a transfer (while ready)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"start\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"No transfer running\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The byte received in the last transfer\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse at the end of a transfer\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"SPI clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"sclk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data to the device\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"mosi\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data from the device\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"miso\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"spi_master\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/spi_master/rtl/spi_master.v" : "// SPI master: one byte per transfer, most significant bit first, any of the four SPI modes\n// (CPOL = clock idle level, CPHA = 0: sample on the first edge, 1: on the second).\n//\n// Raise `start` with `tx_data` while `ready`; eight bits go out on `mosi` while eight come in on\n// `miso`, and `done` pulses with the received byte on `rx_data`. Chip select is yours: hold your\n// device's CS low around the bytes of one transaction. SCLK = CLK_HZ / (2 × the half period).\n\nmodule spi_master #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter SPI_HZ = 1000000,\n    parameter CPOL   = 0,\n    parameter CPHA   = 0\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data,\n    input  wire       start,\n    output wire       ready,\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        done = 1'b0,\n    output reg        sclk = CPOL,\n    output wire       mosi,\n    input  wire       miso\n);\n    localparam HALF = CLK_HZ / (2 * SPI_HZ) > 0 ? CLK_HZ / (2 * SPI_HZ) : 1;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = HALF > 1 ? $clog2(HALF) : 1;\n\n    reg       busy = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] tx_shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] rx_shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [4:0] edge_index = 5'd0;        // 0 … 15: even = leading edge, odd = trailing edge\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n\n    assign ready = !busy;\n    assign mosi = tx_shift[7];\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        done <= 1'b0;\n        if (!busy) begin\n            sclk <= CPOL;\n            if (start) begin\n                busy <= 1'b1;\n                tx_shift <= tx_data;\n                edge_index <= 5'd0;\n                clocks <= 0;\n            end\n        end else if (clocks == HALF - 1) begin\n            clocks <= 0;\n            if (edge_index == 5'd16) begin\n                // The last half period after the final edge: the clock is idle again.\n                busy <= 1'b0;\n                done <= 1'b1;\n                rx_data <= rx_shift;\n            end else begin\n                sclk <= !sclk;\n                edge_index <= edge_index + 1'b1;\n                if (edge_index[0] == (CPHA != 0)) begin\n                    // Sampling edge.\n                    rx_shift <= {rx_shift[6:0], miso};\n                end else if (CPHA == 0 || edge_index != 5'd0) begin\n                    // Shifting edge (with CPHA = 1 the first bit is already on mosi).\n                    tx_shift <= {tx_shift[6:0], 1'b0};\n                end\n            end\n        end else begin\n            clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/spi_master/tb/spi_master_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for spi_master in all four SPI modes: a device model per mode answers\n// each byte with its own pattern; both directions are checked byte by byte, the clock's idle\n// level, its rate (half period of 4 clocks) and the handshake.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\n// A device in one SPI mode: samples mosi and shifts out miso on the right edges.\nmodule spi_device_model #(parameter CPOL = 0, parameter CPHA = 0) (\n    input  wire sclk,\n    input  wire mosi,\n    output wire miso,\n    input  wire [7:0] next_reply,\n    output reg  [7:0] received = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [3:0] bits = 4'd0\n);\n    reg [7:0] reply = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] shift_in = 8'd0;\n    reg loaded = 1'b0;\n    assign miso = reply[7];\n    // The clock just left its idle level. Computed in the block from sclk itself: a continuous\n    // assignment could still hold the old value when the block runs.\n    reg leading;\n\n    // Load the reply before the first edge of a byte (bits == 0).\n    always @(next_reply) if (bits == 0) reply = next_reply;\n\n    always @(sclk) begin\n        leading = (sclk != CPOL);\n        if ((leading && CPHA == 0) || (!leading && CPHA == 1)) begin\n            shift_in = {shift_in[6:0], mosi};\n            bits = bits + 1;\n            if (bits == 8) begin\n                received = shift_in;\n                bits = 0;\n                reply = next_reply;\n            end\n        end else if ((!leading && CPHA == 0) || (leading && CPHA == 1 && bits != 0)) begin\n            reply = {reply[6:0], 1'b0};\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n\nmodule spi_master_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    reg  [7:0] tx [0:3];\n    reg  [7:0] reply [0:3];\n    initial begin\n        // Not bit palindromes, so a reversed bit order shows.\n        tx[0] = 8'hA6; tx[1] = 8'h31; tx[2] = 8'h0F; tx[3] = 8'hFF;\n        reply[0] = 8'h12; reply[1] = 8'hC4; reply[2] = 8'hF0; reply[3] = 8'h00;\n    end\n\n    genvar mode;\n    generate\n        for (mode = 0; mode < 4; mode = mode + 1) begin : modes\n            reg  [7:0] data = 8'd0;\n            reg        start = 1'b0;\n            wire       ready, done, sclk, mosi, miso;\n            wire [7:0] rx_data, device_received;\n            wire [3:0] device_bits;\n            reg  [7:0] next_reply = 8'd0;\n\n            spi_master #(.CLK_HZ(800), .SPI_HZ(100), .CPOL(mode / 2), .CPHA(mode % 2)) dut (\n                .clk(clk), .tx_data(data), .start(start), .ready(ready), .rx_data(rx_data), .done(done),\n                .sclk(sclk), .mosi(mosi), .miso(miso)\n            );\n            spi_device_model #(.CPOL(mode / 2), .CPHA(mode % 2)) device (\n                .sclk(sclk), .mosi(mosi), .miso(miso), .next_reply(next_reply),\n                .received(device_received), .bits(device_bits)\n            );\n\n            // Clock rate: a half period of 4 system clocks (40 ns).\n            realtime last_edge = 0;\n            always @(sclk) begin\n                if (last_edge != 0 && $realtime - last_edge != 40.0 && $realtime - last_edge < 100.0) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d: SCLK half period %0.0f ns, expected 40\", mode, $realtime - last_edge);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n                last_edge = $realtime;\n            end\n\n            integer n;\n            initial begin\n                repeat (3) @(posedge clk);\n                if (sclk !== mode / 2 || ready !== 1'b1) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d: idle SCLK %b, ready %b\", mode, sclk, ready);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n                for (n = 0; n < 4; n = n + 1) begin\n                    next_reply = reply[n];\n                    @(negedge clk);\n                    data = tx[n];\n                    start = 1'b1;\n                    @(negedge clk);\n                    start = 1'b0;\n                    if (ready) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d: still ready after start\", mode);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    @(posedge done);\n                    #1;\n                    if (rx_data !== reply[n]) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d byte %0d: master received %h, expected %h\", mode, n, rx_data, reply[n]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (device_received !== tx[n] || device_bits !== 0) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d byte %0d: device received %h (%0d bits left), expected %h\", mode, n, device_received, device_bits, tx[n]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    if (sclk !== mode / 2) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d: SCLK not idle after the byte\", mode);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    @(posedge clk);\n                    #1;\n                    if (!ready) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: mode %0d: not ready after done\", mode);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    endgenerate\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"spi_master_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, spi_master_tb);\n        #20000;\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/spi_registers/README.md" : "# SPI registers\n\nLets a microcontroller (an ESP32, an Arduino, a Raspberry Pi) set values anywhere in your design\nover one SPI link: text, numbers, bar heights, colours. Mode 0, most significant bit first. Built\non `spi_slave`, so `clk` must be at least 8 times the SPI clock (148.5 MHz → up to 18 MHz).\n\nOne transaction per chip select:\n\n| Bytes from the controller | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| `01`, address high, address low, data, data, … | each data byte pulses `wr` with `wr_addr`/`wr_data`, then the address counts up |\n| `02`, then any bytes | the replies are `ID`, `VERSION`, `status`, then `00` |\n\nThe reply to the first byte of every transaction is `ID`, so the controller sees the FPGA is\nconfigured. `done` pulses after a write transaction ends — useful for taking a set of values at\nonce. Map the address space to your design's write ports, e.g. text cells below 0x800, bar values\nabove:\n\n```verilog\nspi_registers #(.ADDR_BITS(12)) u_link (\n    .clk(pixel_clk), .sclk(link_sclk), .cs_n(link_cs_n), .mosi(link_mosi), .miso(miso),\n    .wr(wr), .wr_addr(wr_addr), .wr_data(wr_data), .done(), .status(frame_count)\n);\nassign link_miso = link_cs_n ? 1'bz : miso;\n```\n\nFrom an ESP32 (Arduino): `SPI.transfer(0x01); SPI.transfer(addr >> 8); SPI.transfer(addr & 0xFF);`\nthen the data bytes, all with CS low.\n",
    "cores/spi_registers/core.json" : "{\n  \"dependencies\" : [\n    \"spi_slave\"\n  ],\n  \"description\" : \"A microcontroller writes bytes anywhere in your design over SPI (mode 0): a write command with a 16-bit address and auto-increment drives one write port (wr, wr_addr, wr_data) for text cells, bar values or colours; a read command answers ID, VERSION and a status byte; every transaction's first reply is ID. done pulses after a write transaction. Built on spi_slave: SCLK below clk / 8.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 224,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 87,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 76\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 100.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 7,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 22,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 78,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 70,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 130\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 187.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 18,\n        \"DFF\" : 76,\n        \"LUT4\" : 113,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 6\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 71.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 123\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 129.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 86,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 76\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 378.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 4,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 67,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 88\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"spi_registers\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"spi\",\n    \"registers\",\n    \"microcontroller\",\n    \"esp32\",\n    \"arduino\",\n    \"esphome\",\n    \"host\",\n    \"control\",\n    \"display\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein Mikrocontroller schreibt über SPI (Modus 0) Bytes an beliebige Stellen deines Designs: ein Schreibbefehl mit 16-Bit-Adresse und automatischem Weiterzählen bedient einen Schreibport (wr, wr_addr, wr_data) für Textzellen, Balkenwerte oder Farben; ein Lesebefehl antwortet mit ID, VERSION und einem Statusbyte; die erste Antwort jeder Übertragung ist ID. done pulst nach einer Schreibübertragung. Baut auf spi_slave auf: SCLK unter clk / 8.\",\n      \"name\" : \"SPI-Register\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"SPI registers\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of wr_addr (1 … 16)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ADDR_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The first reply of every transaction, and the read's first byte\",\n      \"name\" : \"ID\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The read's second byte\",\n      \"name\" : \"VERSION\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock (on a video design the pixel clock), at least 8 × SCLK\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"SPI clock from the controller\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"sclk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Chip select, low during a transaction\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"cs_n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data from the controller\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mosi\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data to the controller (always driven: drive the pin only while cs_n is low on a shared bus)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"miso\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One clock: write wr_data to wr_addr\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Address of the write\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_addr\",\n      \"width\" : 12\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data of the write\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One clock after CS rises at the end of a write transaction\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The read's third byte (e.g. a frame counter or flags)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"status\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"spi_registers\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/spi_registers/rtl/spi_registers.v" : "// SPI registers: a microcontroller writes bytes anywhere in the design through one SPI link\n// (mode 0, most significant bit first) — text cells, bar values, colours. Built on spi_slave, so\n// SCLK must stay below clk / 8. Everything runs on `clk`; on a video design that's the pixel clock,\n// the same clock text_layer's and bar_graph's write ports use, so no clock crossing is needed.\n//\n// One transaction per chip select (CS low … high):\n//   0x01, address high, address low, data, data, …   writes each data byte to the address, then\n//                                                     the next address (ADDR_BITS wide, wrapping)\n//   0x02, then any bytes                              reads: the replies are ID, VERSION, status,\n//                                                     then 0x00\n// The reply to the first byte of every transaction is ID, so a controller sees the FPGA is there\n// and configured with any command. Other commands are ignored until CS rises. `wr` pulses for one\n// clock with `wr_addr` and `wr_data`; `done` pulses when CS rises after a write transaction, for\n// designs that take a set of values at once.\n\nmodule spi_registers #(\n    parameter ADDR_BITS = 12,   // 1 … 16\n    parameter [7:0] ID = 8'h47,\n    parameter [7:0] VERSION = 8'h01\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire sclk,\n    input  wire cs_n,\n    input  wire mosi,\n    output wire miso,\n    output reg  wr = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [ADDR_BITS-1:0] wr_addr = 0,\n    output reg  [7:0] wr_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg  done = 1'b0,\n    input  wire [7:0] status\n);\n    localparam [7:0] CMD_WRITE = 8'h01, CMD_READ = 8'h02;\n    localparam S_COMMAND = 3'd0, S_ADDR_HI = 3'd1, S_ADDR_LO = 3'd2, S_DATA = 3'd3, S_READ = 3'd4, S_IGNORE = 3'd5;\n\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire       rx_valid;\n    reg  [7:0] tx_data = ID;\n\n    spi_slave link (\n        .clk(clk), .sclk(sclk), .cs_n(cs_n), .mosi(mosi), .miso(miso),\n        .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .tx_data(tx_data)\n    );\n\n    // CS with spi_slave's own two-stage delay, and a third stage to see it rise.\n    reg [2:0] cs_s = 3'b111;\n    always @(posedge clk) cs_s <= {cs_s[1:0], cs_n};\n    wire deselected = cs_s[1];\n    wire cs_rose = cs_s[1] && !cs_s[2];\n\n    reg [2:0] state = S_COMMAND;\n    reg [1:0] reply = 2'd0;\n    reg       wrote = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] address_high = 8'd0;\n    wire [15:0] address = {address_high, rx_data};\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        wr <= 1'b0;\n        done <= 1'b0;\n        if (deselected) begin\n            state <= S_COMMAND;\n            tx_data <= ID;\n            if (cs_rose && wrote) done <= 1'b1;\n            wrote <= 1'b0;\n        end else if (rx_valid) begin\n            tx_data <= 8'd0;\n            case (state)\n                S_COMMAND:\n                    if (rx_data == CMD_WRITE) state <= S_ADDR_HI;\n                    else if (rx_data == CMD_READ) begin\n                        state <= S_READ;\n                        tx_data <= ID;\n                        reply <= 2'd1;\n                    end else state <= S_IGNORE;\n                S_ADDR_HI: begin\n                    address_high <= rx_data;\n                    state <= S_ADDR_LO;\n                end\n                S_ADDR_LO: begin\n                    wr_addr <= address[ADDR_BITS-1:0];\n                    state <= S_DATA;\n                end\n                S_DATA: begin\n                    wr <= 1'b1;\n                    wr_data <= rx_data;\n                    wrote <= 1'b1;\n                end\n                S_READ: begin\n                    case (reply)\n                        2'd1: tx_data <= VERSION;\n                        2'd2: tx_data <= status;\n                        default: tx_data <= 8'd0;\n                    endcase\n                    if (reply != 2'd3) reply <= reply + 2'd1;\n                end\n                default: ;\n            endcase\n        end\n        // The next data byte goes to the next address (after this one was written).\n        if (wr) wr_addr <= wr_addr + 1'b1;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/spi_registers/tb/spi_registers_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for spi_registers: an SPI master (mode 0, MSB first) runs the same set of\n// transactions at three SCLK/clk ratios — 8 (the limit), 11 and 23 clocks per bit — and every write\n// (address, data, order), every reply and every `done` pulse is checked:\n//   1. a write of five bytes at 0x3A5 (auto-increment), first reply ID;\n//   2. a write across the top of the address space (0xFFE, 0xFFF, 0x000);\n//   3. a read: ID, ID, VERSION, status, 0x00;\n//   4. an unknown command: no write, no done;\n//   5. a write with an address but no data: no write, no done;\n//   6. two writes back to back with a short CS high time.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule spi_registers_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;   // 100 MHz\n\n    reg sclk = 1'b0, cs_n = 1'b1, mosi = 1'b0;\n    wire miso;\n    wire wr, done;\n    wire [11:0] wr_addr;\n    wire [7:0] wr_data;\n    reg [7:0] status = 8'hC6;\n\n    spi_registers #(.ADDR_BITS(12), .ID(8'h47), .VERSION(8'h1D)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .sclk(sclk), .cs_n(cs_n), .mosi(mosi), .miso(miso),\n        .wr(wr), .wr_addr(wr_addr), .wr_data(wr_data), .done(done), .status(status)\n    );\n\n    // Every write and done pulse, in order.\n    reg [11:0] log_addr [0:63];\n    reg [7:0]  log_data [0:63];\n    integer writes = 0, dones = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (wr) begin\n            log_addr[writes] <= wr_addr;\n            log_data[writes] <= wr_data;\n            writes <= writes + 1;\n        end\n        if (done) dones <= dones + 1;\n    end\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer half_ns = 40;\n\n    task check(input condition, input [8*48:1] what);\n        if (!condition) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL (%0d ns per bit): %0s\", 2 * half_ns, what);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [7:0] replies [0:15];\n    integer b;\n    task byte_out(input [7:0] out, output [7:0] in);\n        begin\n            for (b = 7; b >= 0; b = b - 1) begin\n                mosi = out[b];\n                #(half_ns) sclk = 1'b1;\n                in[b] = miso;\n                #(half_ns) sclk = 1'b0;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [7:0] tx [0:15];\n    integer count, k;\n    task transaction(input integer n, input integer gap_ns);\n        begin\n            cs_n = 1'b0;\n            #(half_ns);\n            for (k = 0; k < n; k = k + 1) byte_out(tx[k], replies[k]);\n            #(half_ns) cs_n = 1'b1;\n            #(gap_ns);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task wait_idle; #(400); endtask\n\n    integer ratio, w0, d0;\n    initial begin\n        for (ratio = 0; ratio < 3; ratio = ratio + 1) begin\n            half_ns = ratio == 0 ? 40 : ratio == 1 ? 55 : 115;   // 8, 11, 23 clocks per bit\n            #(137);   // a different SCLK phase against clk each round\n\n            // 1. Five bytes at 0x3A5.\n            w0 = writes; d0 = dones;\n            tx[0] = 8'h01; tx[1] = 8'h03; tx[2] = 8'hA5;\n            tx[3] = 8'h13; tx[4] = 8'hC4; tx[5] = 8'h7E; tx[6] = 8'h00; tx[7] = 8'hFF;\n            transaction(8, 200); wait_idle;\n            check(replies[0] == 8'h47, \"1: first reply is not ID\");\n            check(writes - w0 == 5, \"1: not five writes\");\n            for (k = 0; k < 5; k = k + 1) begin\n                check(log_addr[w0 + k] == 12'h3A5 + k, \"1: wrong address\");\n                check(log_data[w0 + k] == tx[3 + k], \"1: wrong data\");\n            end\n            check(dones - d0 == 1, \"1: done not once\");\n\n            // 2. Across the top: 0xFFE, 0xFFF, 0x000.\n            w0 = writes;\n            tx[0] = 8'h01; tx[1] = 8'h0F; tx[2] = 8'hFE; tx[3] = 8'h5A; tx[4] = 8'h96; tx[5] = 8'h3B;\n            transaction(6, 200); wait_idle;\n            check(writes - w0 == 3, \"2: not three writes\");\n            check(log_addr[w0] == 12'hFFE && log_addr[w0 + 1] == 12'hFFF && log_addr[w0 + 2] == 12'h000, \"2: no wrap\");\n            check(log_data[w0 + 2] == 8'h3B, \"2: wrong data after the wrap\");\n\n            // 3. Read: ID, ID, VERSION, status, 0.\n            w0 = writes; d0 = dones;\n            status = 8'hC6 ^ ratio;\n            tx[0] = 8'h02; tx[1] = 8'hAA; tx[2] = 8'h55; tx[3] = 8'h0F; tx[4] = 8'hF0;\n            transaction(5, 200); wait_idle;\n            check(replies[0] == 8'h47 && replies[1] == 8'h47, \"3: no ID\");\n            check(replies[2] == 8'h1D, \"3: no VERSION\");\n            check(replies[3] == (8'hC6 ^ ratio), \"3: no status\");\n            check(replies[4] == 8'h00, \"3: not 0 after status\");\n            check(writes == w0 && dones == d0, \"3: a read wrote\");\n\n            // 4. Unknown command.\n            tx[0] = 8'h55; tx[1] = 8'h01; tx[2] = 8'h00; tx[3] = 8'h10; tx[4] = 8'h77;\n            transaction(5, 200); wait_idle;\n            check(replies[0] == 8'h47 && replies[1] == 8'h00, \"4: wrong replies\");\n            check(writes == w0 && dones == d0, \"4: an unknown command wrote\");\n\n            // 5. An address, no data.\n            tx[0] = 8'h01; tx[1] = 8'h01; tx[2] = 8'h23;\n            transaction(3, 200); wait_idle;\n            check(writes == w0 && dones == d0, \"5: no data but a write or done\");\n\n            // 6. Two writes back to back, CS high for 60 ns (6 clocks).\n            tx[0] = 8'h01; tx[1] = 8'h00; tx[2] = 8'h40; tx[3] = 8'hE1;\n            transaction(4, 60);\n            tx[0] = 8'h01; tx[1] = 8'h00; tx[2] = 8'h80; tx[3] = 8'h2C; tx[4] = 8'hD9;\n            transaction(5, 200); wait_idle;\n            check(writes - w0 == 3, \"6: not three writes\");\n            check(log_addr[w0] == 12'h040 && log_data[w0] == 8'hE1, \"6: first write\");\n            check(log_addr[w0 + 1] == 12'h080 && log_data[w0 + 1] == 8'h2C, \"6: second write\");\n            check(log_addr[w0 + 2] == 12'h081 && log_data[w0 + 2] == 8'hD9, \"6: third write\");\n            check(dones - d0 == 2, \"6: done not twice\");\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #2_000_000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/spi_slave/README.md" : "# SPI slave\n\nLets a microcontroller talk to your design over SPI (mode 0, MSB first). Everything runs in\nyour `clk`; SCLK, CS and MOSI are synchronised, so `clk` must be at least 8 times the SPI clock\n(e.g. 12 MHz handles SPI up to 1.5 MHz).\n\nEvery byte from the master pulses `rx_valid`. The answer for the *next* byte is read from\n`tx_data` when CS falls and right after each `rx_valid` — a register-read protocol therefore\nanswers with one byte of delay, as most SPI devices do.\n\n```verilog\nspi_slave u_spi (\n    .clk(clk), .sclk(spi_sck), .cs_n(spi_cs_n), .mosi(spi_mosi), .miso(spi_miso),\n    .rx_data(command), .rx_valid(command_valid), .tx_data(status)\n);\n```\n",
    "cores/spi_slave/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"SPI device side, mode 0: receives bytes from a microcontroller or other master and answers with your bytes. Oversampled in the system clock.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 295.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 21,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 36\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 117.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 12,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 30\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 293.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 4,\n        \"DFF\" : 36,\n        \"LUT4\" : 42\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 94.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 47\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 185.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 21,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 36\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 324.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 1,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 27,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 27\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"spi_slave\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"spi\",\n    \"slave\",\n    \"peripheral\",\n    \"device\",\n    \"microcontroller\",\n    \"serial\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"SPI-Geräteseite, Modus 0: empfängt Bytes von einem Mikrocontroller oder anderen Master und antwortet mit deinen Bytes. Im Systemtakt abgetastet.\",\n      \"name\" : \"SPI-Slave\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"SPI slave\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"System clock, at least 8 × SCLK\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"SPI clock from the master (asynchronous)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"sclk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Chip select, active low (asynchronous)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"cs_n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data from the master\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"mosi\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data to the master (always driven)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"miso\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The last byte received\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: rx_data holds a new byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx_valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to answer with, taken when CS falls and after every byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"spi_slave\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/spi_slave/rtl/spi_slave.v" : "// SPI slave, mode 0 (SCLK idles low, data sampled on the rising edge), most significant bit\n// first. SCLK, CS and MOSI are synchronised into `clk` and sampled there, so `clk` must be at least\n// 8 times the SPI clock.\n//\n// While CS is low each byte from the master arrives on `rx_data` with a one-cycle `rx_valid`.\n// `tx_data` is taken when CS falls and again after every byte, and goes out on `miso` during\n// the next byte. `miso` is always driven; on a shared bus, drive the pin only while `cs_n` is\n// low: `assign miso_pin = cs_n ? 1'bz : miso;`.\n\nmodule spi_slave (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire       sclk,\n    input  wire       cs_n,\n    input  wire       mosi,\n    output wire       miso,\n    output reg  [7:0] rx_data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        rx_valid = 1'b0,\n    input  wire [7:0] tx_data\n);\n    // Three stages: two to synchronise, one to see edges.\n    reg [2:0] sclk_s = 3'b000;\n    reg [2:0] cs_s = 3'b111;\n    reg [1:0] mosi_s = 2'b00;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        sclk_s <= {sclk_s[1:0], sclk};\n        cs_s <= {cs_s[1:0], cs_n};\n        mosi_s <= {mosi_s[0], mosi};\n    end\n    wire sclk_rise = sclk_s[1] && !sclk_s[2];\n    wire sclk_fall = !sclk_s[1] && sclk_s[2];\n    wire selected = !cs_s[1];\n    wire cs_fell = !cs_s[1] && cs_s[2];\n\n    reg [7:0] rx_shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [7:0] tx_shift = 8'd0;\n    reg [2:0] bit_count = 3'd0;\n    reg       reload = 1'b0;\n\n    assign miso = tx_shift[7];\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rx_valid <= 1'b0;\n        if (cs_fell) begin\n            bit_count <= 3'd0;\n            tx_shift <= tx_data;\n            reload <= 1'b0;\n        end else if (selected) begin\n            if (sclk_rise) begin\n                rx_shift <= {rx_shift[6:0], mosi_s[1]};\n                bit_count <= bit_count + 1'b1;\n                if (bit_count == 3'd7) begin\n                    rx_data <= {rx_shift[6:0], mosi_s[1]};\n                    rx_valid <= 1'b1;\n                    reload <= 1'b1;\n                end\n            end else if (sclk_fall) begin\n                // The next bit: from the shift register, or the next byte after the eighth.\n                if (reload) begin\n                    tx_shift <= tx_data;\n                    reload <= 1'b0;\n                end else begin\n                    tx_shift <= {tx_shift[6:0], 1'b0};\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/spi_slave/tb/spi_slave_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for spi_slave: a mode-0 master sends three bytes in one CS-low\n// transaction (the slave answers with bytes it is handed after each rx_valid), then a second\n// transaction that starts with a fresh reply. SCLK is 10 times slower than clk.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule spi_slave_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;              // 100 MHz\n\n    reg sclk = 1'b0, cs_n = 1'b1, mosi = 1'b0;\n    wire miso;\n    wire [7:0] rx_data;\n    wire rx_valid;\n    reg [7:0] tx_data = 8'h00;\n\n    spi_slave dut (.clk(clk), .sclk(sclk), .cs_n(cs_n), .mosi(mosi), .miso(miso),\n                   .rx_data(rx_data), .rx_valid(rx_valid), .tx_data(tx_data));\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer valids = 0;\n    reg [7:0] replies [0:4];\n    reg [7:0] got [0:7];\n\n    // The design under test hands the next reply over after each byte (like user logic would).\n    always @(posedge clk) if (rx_valid) begin\n        got[valids] = rx_data;\n        valids = valids + 1;\n        tx_data <= replies[valids];\n    end\n\n    localparam HALF = 50;              // SCLK 10 MHz\n\n    task transfer(input [7:0] out_byte, output [7:0] in_byte);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            for (i = 7; i >= 0; i = i - 1) begin\n                mosi = out_byte[i];\n                #(HALF);\n                sclk = 1'b1;\n                in_byte[i] = miso;\n                #(HALF);\n                sclk = 1'b0;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [7:0] in_byte;\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"spi_slave_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, spi_slave_tb);\n        replies[0] = 8'h1E; replies[1] = 8'hC4; replies[2] = 8'h07; replies[3] = 8'h80; replies[4] = 8'h00;\n        tx_data = replies[0];\n        #200;\n\n        // Transaction 1: three bytes.\n        cs_n = 1'b0; #(HALF * 2);\n        transfer(8'hA6, in_byte); if (in_byte !== 8'h1E) begin $display(\"FAIL: reply 1 %h, expected 1e\", in_byte); errors = errors + 1; end\n        transfer(8'h31, in_byte); if (in_byte !== 8'hC4) begin $display(\"FAIL: reply 2 %h, expected c4\", in_byte); errors = errors + 1; end\n        transfer(8'hF0, in_byte); if (in_byte !== 8'h07) begin $display(\"FAIL: reply 3 %h, expected 07\", in_byte); errors = errors + 1; end\n        #(HALF); cs_n = 1'b1; #(HALF * 4);\n\n        // SCLK while not selected is ignored.\n        transfer(8'hFF, in_byte);\n        #(HALF * 4);\n\n        // Transaction 2: the reply loaded when CS falls.\n        cs_n = 1'b0; #(HALF * 2);\n        transfer(8'h0C, in_byte); if (in_byte !== 8'h80) begin $display(\"FAIL: reply 4 %h, expected 80\", in_byte); errors = errors + 1; end\n        #(HALF); cs_n = 1'b1; #(HALF * 4);\n\n        if (valids != 4) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d bytes received, expected 4\", valids);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end else if (got[0] !== 8'hA6 || got[1] !== 8'h31 || got[2] !== 8'hF0 || got[3] !== 8'h0C) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: received %h %h %h %h, expected a6 31 f0 0c\", got[0], got[1], got[2], got[3]);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #100000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/sprite_layer/README.md" : "# Sprite layer\n\nDraws one sprite over a video picture. The picture passes through as a pixel stream — the\nposition from `video_timing`, `de`, both syncs and the colour below — and leaves\n`LATENCY = ROM_LATENCY + 2` clocks later with the sprite on top. Position and timing leave with\nit, so layers chain: feed one layer's `*_out` into the next, and the last one draws on top.\n\nThe sprite's pixels come from an image ROM (Project ▸ Add Image ROM…). The layer drives the\nreader's `x` and `y` through `rom_x`/`rom_y` and takes its colour, `opaque` and `alpha` back:\n\n```verilog\nwire [3:0] gx, gy;\nwire [7:0] gr, gg, gb;\nwire g_opaque;\nghost u_ghost (.clk(pixel_clk), .frame(step), .x(gx), .y(gy),\n               .red(gr), .green(gg), .blue(gb), .opaque(g_opaque));\n\nsprite_layer #(.WIDTH(16), .HEIGHT(16), .SCALE_SHIFT(1), .ROM_LATENCY(2)) u_sprite (\n    .clk(pixel_clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .red(bg_red), .green(bg_green), .blue(bg_blue),\n    .enable(1'b1), .sprite_x(ghost_x), .sprite_y(ghost_y), .flip_x(1'b0), .flip_y(1'b0),\n    .rom_x(gx), .rom_y(gy), .rom_red(gr), .rom_green(gg), .rom_blue(gb),\n    .rom_opaque(g_opaque), .rom_alpha(8'd0),\n    .x_out(x2), .y_out(y2), .de_out(de2), .hsync_out(hsync2), .vsync_out(vsync2),\n    .red_out(red2), .green_out(green2), .blue_out(blue2), .covered(ghost_here));\n```\n\n- **Place** the sprite with `sprite_x`/`sprite_y` (its top left corner in screen pixels); change\n  them at `frame_start` so it never tears. `SCALE_SHIFT` draws each image pixel 2, 4 or 8 times\n  as wide and high. `flip_x`/`flip_y` mirror it (a character walking the other way).\n- **Animate** with the reader's `frame` input (a sprite sheet: Add Image ROM ▸ Frames); the\n  layer doesn't need to know.\n- **Transparency:** pixels the reader calls transparent (palette index 0, the mask bit) show the\n  picture below. With an alpha format set `ALPHA = 1` and connect `rom_alpha`: the reader's\n  premultiplied colour is blended, `out = colour + (255 − alpha) × below / 255`, rounded, which\n  takes three 8 × 8 multipliers (DSP blocks where the family has them).\n- **Collisions:** `covered` is high on the output pixels the sprite covers; AND two layers'\n  `covered` in the same frame to see whether two sprites touch.\n- `ROM_LATENCY` must be the reader's latency: 2 for a whole image or a sprite sheet, 3 for tiles\n  (the generated module's header says it).\n\nSeveral sprites are several layers, each with its own reader (a ROM can be added twice, or as a\ncopy with another name). The order of the chain is the drawing order.\n",
    "cores/sprite_layer/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Draws a sprite from an image ROM (Add Image ROM) over a video picture: position, ×1–×8 scaling, mirroring, transparency by palette index 0 or mask, or alpha blending. Layers chain; LATENCY = ROM_LATENCY + 2 clocks.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 155.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 89,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 204\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 95.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 18,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 44,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 53\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 128.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 46,\n        \"DFF\" : 204,\n        \"LUT4\" : 217,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 8,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 4,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 2,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 46.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 257\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 96.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 90,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 204\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 312.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 6,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 126,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 107\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"sprite_layer\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"sprite\",\n    \"video\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"vga\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"image\",\n    \"rom\",\n    \"alpha\",\n    \"overlay\",\n    \"game\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Zeichnet ein Sprite aus einem Bild-ROM (Add Image ROM) über ein Videobild: Position, Skalierung ×1–×8, Spiegelung, Transparenz über Palettenindex 0 oder Maske oder Alpha-Überblendung. Ebenen lassen sich verketten; LATENCY = ROM_LATENCY + 2 Takte.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Sprite-Ebene\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Sprite layer\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 11,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of x (video_timing's x)\",\n      \"name\" : \"X_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of y\",\n      \"name\" : \"Y_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 16,\n      \"description\" : \"Sprite width in image pixels (a frame's width for a sprite sheet)\",\n      \"name\" : \"WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 16,\n      \"description\" : \"Sprite height in image pixels\",\n      \"name\" : \"HEIGHT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Each image pixel is 2^SCALE_SHIFT screen pixels wide and high\",\n      \"name\" : \"SCALE_SHIFT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"The image ROM reader's read latency (2, or 3 for tiles)\",\n      \"name\" : \"ROM_LATENCY\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: blend the reader's premultiplied colour by its alpha output\",\n      \"name\" : \"ALPHA\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel column from video_timing (or the previous layer's x_out)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"x\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel row\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"y\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: the pixel is visible\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Draw the sprite\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"enable\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Screen column of the sprite's left edge\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"sprite_x\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Screen row of the sprite's top edge\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"sprite_y\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Mirror left to right\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"flip_x\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Mirror top to bottom\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"flip_y\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the image ROM reader's x\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_x\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the image ROM reader's y\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_y\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the reader: red\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the reader: green\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the reader: blue\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the reader: the pixel is not transparent\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_opaque\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the reader: alpha (ALPHA = 1; tie to 0 otherwise)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rom_alpha\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"x, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"x_out\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"y, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"y_out\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"de, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"de_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"hsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"vsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red with the layer drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"red_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green with the layer drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"green_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue with the layer drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The sprite covers this output pixel (collisions)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"covered\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"sprite_layer\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/sprite_layer/rtl/sprite_layer.v" : "// Sprite layer: draws one sprite from an image ROM over the picture passing through. The picture\n// arrives as a pixel stream (position, timing and the colour below the sprite) and leaves\n// LATENCY = ROM_LATENCY + 2 clocks later with the sprite on top; position and timing leave with it,\n// so layers chain: the first layer's outputs feed the second, and the last layer draws on top.\n//\n// The sprite's pixels come from an image ROM reader (Project ▸ Add Image ROM…): the layer drives\n// its x and y (`rom_x`, `rom_y`) and reads its colour, `opaque` and (with ALPHA = 1) `alpha` back\n// ROM_LATENCY clocks later. A sprite sheet's frame goes straight to the reader. Transparent\n// pixels (palette index 0, the mask, or alpha 0) show the picture below. With ALPHA = 1 the\n// reader's premultiplied colour is blended: out = colour + (255 - alpha) × below / 255.\n//\n// `sprite_x`/`sprite_y` place the sprite's top left corner on the screen; SCALE_SHIFT draws each\n// image pixel as 2^SCALE_SHIFT × 2^SCALE_SHIFT screen pixels. Change them at the frame start to\n// move the sprite without tearing. `covered` marks output pixels the sprite covers (collisions).\n\nmodule sprite_layer #(\n    parameter X_BITS = 11,\n    parameter Y_BITS = 10,\n    parameter WIDTH = 16,\n    parameter HEIGHT = 16,\n    parameter SCALE_SHIFT = 0,\n    parameter ROM_LATENCY = 2,\n    parameter ALPHA = 0\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire [X_BITS-1:0] x,\n    input  wire [Y_BITS-1:0] y,\n    input  wire de,\n    input  wire hsync,\n    input  wire vsync,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire enable,\n    input  wire [X_BITS-1:0] sprite_x,\n    input  wire [Y_BITS-1:0] sprite_y,\n    input  wire flip_x,\n    input  wire flip_y,\n    output reg  [$clog2(WIDTH)-1:0] rom_x = 0,\n    output reg  [$clog2(HEIGHT)-1:0] rom_y = 0,\n    input  wire [7:0] rom_red,\n    input  wire [7:0] rom_green,\n    input  wire [7:0] rom_blue,\n    input  wire rom_opaque,\n    input  wire [7:0] rom_alpha,\n    output reg  [X_BITS-1:0] x_out = 0,\n    output reg  [Y_BITS-1:0] y_out = 0,\n    output reg  de_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  hsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  vsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [7:0] red_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] green_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] blue_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  covered = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam XB = $clog2(WIDTH);\n    localparam YB = $clog2(HEIGHT);\n\n    // ---- Cycle 0: is the pixel inside the sprite, and which image pixel is it? -------------\n    wire [X_BITS:0] dx = {1'b0, x} - {1'b0, sprite_x};\n    wire [Y_BITS:0] dy = {1'b0, y} - {1'b0, sprite_y};\n    wire inside_x = !dx[X_BITS] && (dx >> SCALE_SHIFT) < WIDTH;\n    wire inside_y = !dy[Y_BITS] && (dy >> SCALE_SHIFT) < HEIGHT;\n    wire [X_BITS:0] u = dx >> SCALE_SHIFT;\n    wire [Y_BITS:0] v = dy >> SCALE_SHIFT;\n    wire in_sprite = de && enable && inside_x && inside_y;\n\n    reg inside_1 = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        rom_x <= flip_x ? WIDTH - 1 - u[XB-1:0] : u[XB-1:0];\n        rom_y <= flip_y ? HEIGHT - 1 - v[YB-1:0] : v[YB-1:0];\n        inside_1 <= in_sprite;\n    end\n\n    // ---- The ROM's clocks: the picture and `in_sprite` wait for its colour --------------------\n    localparam PW = X_BITS + Y_BITS + 3 + 24;\n    localparam STAGES = ROM_LATENCY + 1;\n    reg [PW*STAGES-1:0] pass = 0;\n    wire [PW*(STAGES+1)-1:0] pass_next = {pass, x, y, de, hsync, vsync, red, green, blue};\n    reg [ROM_LATENCY-1:0] inside_pipe = 0;\n    wire [ROM_LATENCY:0] inside_next = {inside_pipe, inside_1};\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        pass <= pass_next[PW*STAGES-1:0];\n        inside_pipe <= inside_next[ROM_LATENCY-1:0];\n    end\n    wire [PW-1:0] below = pass[PW*STAGES-1 -: PW];\n    wire [7:0] below_red = below[23:16];\n    wire [7:0] below_green = below[15:8];\n    wire [7:0] below_blue = below[7:0];\n    wire hit = inside_pipe[ROM_LATENCY-1];\n\n    // ---- Cycle ROM_LATENCY + 1: the sprite over the picture --------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] mixed_red, mixed_green, mixed_blue;\n    generate\n        if (ALPHA != 0) begin : blend\n            // (255 - alpha) × below / 255, rounded: t = product + 128, (t + t / 256) / 256.\n            wire [7:0] rest = 8'd255 - rom_alpha;\n            wire [16:0] t_red = rest * below_red + 17'd128;\n            wire [16:0] t_green = rest * below_green + 17'd128;\n            wire [16:0] t_blue = rest * below_blue + 17'd128;\n            wire [8:0] s_red = rom_red + ((t_red + (t_red >> 8)) >> 8);\n            wire [8:0] s_green = rom_green + ((t_green + (t_green >> 8)) >> 8);\n            wire [8:0] s_blue = rom_blue + ((t_blue + (t_blue >> 8)) >> 8);\n            assign mixed_red = s_red[8] ? 8'd255 : s_red[7:0];\n            assign mixed_green = s_green[8] ? 8'd255 : s_green[7:0];\n            assign mixed_blue = s_blue[8] ? 8'd255 : s_blue[7:0];\n        end else begin : replace\n            assign mixed_red = rom_opaque ? rom_red : below_red;\n            assign mixed_green = rom_opaque ? rom_green : below_green;\n            assign mixed_blue = rom_opaque ? rom_blue : below_blue;\n        end\n    endgenerate\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        {x_out, y_out, de_out, hsync_out, vsync_out} <= below[PW-1:24];\n        red_out <= hit ? mixed_red : below_red;\n        green_out <= hit ? mixed_green : below_green;\n        blue_out <= hit ? mixed_blue : below_blue;\n        covered <= hit && rom_opaque;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/sprite_layer/tb/sprite_layer_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for sprite_layer. A small screen (24 × 12 visible, 28 × 14 in all) is\n// scanned for eight frames while the sprites move: at the top left corner, inside, half off the\n// right and bottom edges, in the blanking, switched off. Three layers, each with its own ROM\n// model and latency, are checked pixel by pixel against a reference written apart from the RTL:\n//   a: 5 × 3 sprite (not a power of two), ×1, ROM latency 2, palette-style opaque/transparent;\n//   b: 4 × 4 sprite, ×2, ROM latency 3, flipped in x on even frames and in y on odd ones;\n//   c: 4 × 2 sprite, ×1, ROM latency 2, ALPHA = 1 with a premultiplied colour and alpha ramp.\n// Every output (colour, covered, and the position and timing passed through) is compared.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule sprite_layer_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 28, H_ACTIVE = 24, V_TOTAL = 14, V_ACTIVE = 12, FRAMES = 8;\n\n    reg [4:0] x = 0;\n    reg [3:0] y = 0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] red = 0, green = 0, blue = 0;\n    reg enable = 1'b1;\n    reg [4:0] sx = 0;\n    reg [3:0] sy = 0;\n    reg flip_x = 1'b0, flip_y = 1'b0;\n\n    // The picture below: a colour from the position, so every pixel differs.\n    function [23:0] background(input integer px, input integer py);\n        reg [7:0] r, g, b;\n        begin\n            r = {px[4:0], 3'b101}; g = {py[3:0], 4'b0110}; b = 8'h3C ^ (px + py);\n            background = {r, g, b};\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- ROM models: pixel (u, v) → colour, opaque, alpha ------------------------------------\n    function [23:0] colour_a(input integer u, input integer v);\n        reg [7:0] r, g, b;\n        begin\n            r = 8'h10 * u + 8'h01; g = 8'h20 * v + 8'h02; b = 8'h80 + u * 3 + v;\n            colour_a = {r, g, b};\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function opaque_a(input integer u, input integer v);\n        opaque_a = ((u * 2 + v) % 3) != 0;\n    endfunction\n    function [23:0] colour_b(input integer u, input integer v);\n        reg [7:0] r, g, b;\n        begin\n            r = 8'hF0 - u * 16; g = 8'h11 * v; b = 8'h40 + u * 4 + v;\n            colour_b = {r, g, b};\n        end\n    endfunction\n    function opaque_b(input integer u, input integer v);\n        opaque_b = !(u == 0 && v == 3) && !(u == 2 && v == 1);\n    endfunction\n    function [7:0] alpha_c(input integer u, input integer v);\n        alpha_c = (u * 2 + v) * 36;    // 0, 36, … 252: transparent, ramp, almost opaque\n    endfunction\n    function [23:0] colour_c(input integer u, input integer v);\n        // Premultiplied: each channel at most alpha — except red at the two most opaque steps,\n        // which is too bright on purpose, so the sum must be clipped at 255.\n        reg [7:0] a, r, g, b;\n        begin\n            a = alpha_c(u, v); r = a >= 216 ? 8'd255 : a; g = a / 2; b = a / 3;\n            colour_c = {r, g, b};\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- The three layers in a chain: a, then b on top, then c on top -----------------------\n    wire [2:0] rax;\n    wire [1:0] rbx, ray, rby;\n    wire [1:0] rcx;\n    wire rcy;\n    reg [7:0] a_r = 0, a_g = 0, a_b = 0, b_r = 0, b_g = 0, b_b = 0, c_r = 0, c_g = 0, c_b = 0, c_a = 0;\n    reg a_o = 0, b_o = 0, c_o = 0;\n    reg [26:0] a_rom_1 = 0, b_rom_1 = 0, b_rom_2 = 0, c_rom_1 = 0;\n    reg [7:0] c_alpha_1 = 0;\n    // Latency 2: registered address, then registered data.\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        a_rom_1 <= {opaque_a(rax, ray), colour_a(rax, ray), 2'b00};\n        {a_o, a_r, a_g, a_b} <= a_rom_1[26:2];\n        b_rom_1 <= {opaque_b(rbx, rby), colour_b(rbx, rby), 2'b00};\n        b_rom_2 <= b_rom_1;\n        {b_o, b_r, b_g, b_b} <= b_rom_2[26:2];\n        c_rom_1 <= {alpha_c(rcx, rcy) != 0, colour_c(rcx, rcy), 2'b00};\n        c_alpha_1 <= alpha_c(rcx, rcy);\n        {c_o, c_r, c_g, c_b} <= c_rom_1[26:2];\n        c_a <= c_alpha_1;\n    end\n\n    wire [4:0] ax, bx, cx;\n    wire [3:0] ay, by, cy;\n    wire ade, ahs, avs, bde, bhs, bvs, cde, chs, cvs, a_cov, b_cov, c_cov;\n    wire [7:0] ar, ag, ab, br, bg, bb, cr, cg, cb;\n    // b and c sit a fixed distance from a's position.\n    wire [4:0] bsx = sx + 5'd6;\n    wire [3:0] bsy = sy + 4'd1;\n    wire [4:0] csx = sx + 5'd1;\n    wire [3:0] csy = sy + 4'd4;\n\n    sprite_layer #(.X_BITS(5), .Y_BITS(4), .WIDTH(5), .HEIGHT(3), .SCALE_SHIFT(0), .ROM_LATENCY(2)) dut_a (\n        .clk(clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .enable(enable), .sprite_x(sx), .sprite_y(sy), .flip_x(1'b0), .flip_y(1'b0),\n        .rom_x(rax), .rom_y(ray), .rom_red(a_r), .rom_green(a_g), .rom_blue(a_b), .rom_opaque(a_o), .rom_alpha(8'd0),\n        .x_out(ax), .y_out(ay), .de_out(ade), .hsync_out(ahs), .vsync_out(avs), .red_out(ar), .green_out(ag), .blue_out(ab), .covered(a_cov));\n    sprite_layer #(.X_BITS(5), .Y_BITS(4), .WIDTH(4), .HEIGHT(4), .SCALE_SHIFT(1), .ROM_LATENCY(3)) dut_b (\n        .clk(clk), .x(ax), .y(ay), .de(ade), .hsync(ahs), .vsync(avs), .red(ar), .green(ag), .blue(ab),\n        .enable(1'b1), .sprite_x(bsx), .sprite_y(bsy), .flip_x(flip_x), .flip_y(flip_y),\n        .rom_x(rbx), .rom_y(rby), .rom_red(b_r), .rom_green(b_g), .rom_blue(b_b), .rom_opaque(b_o), .rom_alpha(8'd0),\n        .x_out(bx), .y_out(by), .de_out(bde), .hsync_out(bhs), .vsync_out(bvs), .red_out(br), .green_out(bg), .blue_out(bb), .covered(b_cov));\n    sprite_layer #(.X_BITS(5), .Y_BITS(4), .WIDTH(4), .HEIGHT(2), .SCALE_SHIFT(0), .ROM_LATENCY(2), .ALPHA(1)) dut_c (\n        .clk(clk), .x(bx), .y(by), .de(bde), .hsync(bhs), .vsync(bvs), .red(br), .green(bg), .blue(bb),\n        .enable(1'b1), .sprite_x(csx), .sprite_y(csy), .flip_x(1'b0), .flip_y(1'b0),\n        .rom_x(rcx), .rom_y(rcy), .rom_red(c_r), .rom_green(c_g), .rom_blue(c_b), .rom_opaque(c_o), .rom_alpha(c_a),\n        .x_out(cx), .y_out(cy), .de_out(cde), .hsync_out(chs), .vsync_out(cvs), .red_out(cr), .green_out(cg), .blue_out(cb), .covered(c_cov));\n\n    // ---- Reference: what each layer makes of the pixel that went in LATENCY clocks earlier ---\n    localparam LAT_A = 4, LAT_B = 5, LAT_C = 4;\n    localparam CYCLES = H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES;\n    reg [4:0] in_x [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg [3:0] in_y [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_de [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_hs [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_vs [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_en [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg [4:0] in_sx [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg [3:0] in_sy [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_fx [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_fy [0:CYCLES-1];\n\n    function [23:0] mix(input [7:0] alpha, input [23:0] colour, input [23:0] below);\n        integer channel, value;\n        begin\n            for (channel = 0; channel < 3; channel = channel + 1) begin\n                // colour + round((255 - alpha) × below / 255), at most 255.\n                value = colour[8 * channel +: 8] + ((255 - alpha) * below[8 * channel +: 8] * 2 + 255) / 510;\n                mix[8 * channel +: 8] = value > 255 ? 255 : value;\n            end\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    integer errors = 0, checks = 0, covered_a = 0, covered_b = 0, blended = 0, t, t_a, t_b, t_c;\n    integer px, py, u, v;\n    reg [23:0] want_a, want_b, want_c, below;\n    reg cov_a, cov_b, cov_c, in_sprite;\n\n    task check_layer(input [8*1-1:0] name, input [23:0] got, input got_cov, input [23:0] want, input want_cov,\n                     input [4:0] got_x, input [3:0] got_y, input got_de, input got_hs, input got_vs, input integer t0);\n        begin\n            checks = checks + 1;\n            if (got !== want || got_cov !== want_cov) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: layer %s at (%0d, %0d): colour %h covered %b, expected %h %b\", name, in_x[t0], in_y[t0], got, got_cov, want, want_cov);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            if (got_x !== in_x[t0] || got_y !== in_y[t0] || got_de !== in_de[t0] || got_hs !== in_hs[t0] || got_vs !== in_vs[t0]) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: layer %s passes (%0d, %0d) de %b hs %b vs %b, expected (%0d, %0d) %b %b %b\", name, got_x, got_y, got_de, got_hs, got_vs,\n                         in_x[t0], in_y[t0], in_de[t0], in_hs[t0], in_vs[t0]);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Sprite a's top left per frame: corner, inside, half off right and bottom, in the blanking, off.\n    function [8:0] position(input integer frame);\n        case (frame)\n            0: position = {5'd0, 4'd0};\n            1: position = {5'd3, 4'd2};\n            2: position = {5'd9, 4'd5};\n            3: position = {5'd21, 4'd10};\n            4: position = {5'd14, 4'd7};\n            5: position = {5'd25, 4'd3};\n            6: position = {5'd2, 4'd8};\n            default: position = {5'd7, 4'd1};\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"sprite_layer_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, sprite_layer_tb);\n        for (t = 0; t < CYCLES + LAT_A + LAT_B + LAT_C; t = t + 1) begin\n            // Drive the pixel of cycle t.\n            if (t < CYCLES) begin\n                px = t % H_TOTAL;\n                py = (t / H_TOTAL) % V_TOTAL;\n                if (px == 0 && py == 0) begin\n                    {sx, sy} = position(t / (H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL));\n                    enable = (t / (H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL)) != 6;\n                    flip_x = (t / (H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL)) % 2 == 0;\n                    flip_y = !flip_x;\n                end\n                x = px; y = py;\n                de = px < H_ACTIVE && py < V_ACTIVE;\n                hsync = px >= 25;\n                vsync = py == 13;\n                {red, green, blue} = background(px, py);\n                in_x[t] = x; in_y[t] = y; in_de[t] = de; in_hs[t] = hsync; in_vs[t] = vsync;\n                in_en[t] = enable; in_sx[t] = sx; in_sy[t] = sy; in_fx[t] = flip_x; in_fy[t] = flip_y;\n            end\n            @(posedge clk);\n            #1;\n            // Check each layer's output against the pixel that entered it LATENCY clocks before.\n            t_a = t - LAT_A + 1;\n            t_b = t_a - LAT_B;\n            t_c = t_b - LAT_C;\n            if (t_c >= 0 && t_c < CYCLES) begin\n                px = in_x[t_c]; py = in_y[t_c];\n                below = background(px, py);\n                // a\n                in_sprite = in_de[t_c] && in_en[t_c] && px >= in_sx[t_c] && px < in_sx[t_c] + 5 && py >= in_sy[t_c] && py < in_sy[t_c] + 3;\n                u = px - in_sx[t_c]; v = py - in_sy[t_c];\n                cov_a = in_sprite && opaque_a(u, v);\n                want_a = cov_a ? colour_a(u, v) : below;\n                // b, over a\n                in_sprite = in_de[t_c] && px >= in_sx[t_c] + 6 && px < in_sx[t_c] + 6 + 8 && py >= in_sy[t_c] + 1 && py < in_sy[t_c] + 1 + 8;\n                u = (px - in_sx[t_c] - 6) / 2; v = (py - in_sy[t_c] - 1) / 2;\n                if (in_fx[t_c]) u = 3 - u;\n                if (in_fy[t_c]) v = 3 - v;\n                cov_b = in_sprite && opaque_b(u, v);\n                want_b = cov_b ? colour_b(u, v) : want_a;\n                // c, blended over b\n                in_sprite = in_de[t_c] && px >= in_sx[t_c] + 1 && px < in_sx[t_c] + 1 + 4 && py >= in_sy[t_c] + 4 && py < in_sy[t_c] + 4 + 2;\n                u = px - in_sx[t_c] - 1; v = py - in_sy[t_c] - 4;\n                cov_c = in_sprite && alpha_c(u, v) != 0;\n                want_c = in_sprite ? mix(alpha_c(u, v), colour_c(u, v), want_b) : want_b;\n                if (in_sprite && alpha_c(u, v) != 0 && alpha_c(u, v) != 255) blended = blended + 1;\n                if (cov_a) covered_a = covered_a + 1;\n                if (cov_b) covered_b = covered_b + 1;\n                check_layer(\"c\", {cr, cg, cb}, c_cov, want_c, cov_c, cx, cy, cde, chs, cvs, t_c);\n            end\n            // Layers a and b on their own (their outputs feed the next layer).\n            if (t_a >= 0 && t_a < CYCLES) begin\n                px = in_x[t_a]; py = in_y[t_a];\n                in_sprite = in_de[t_a] && in_en[t_a] && px >= in_sx[t_a] && px < in_sx[t_a] + 5 && py >= in_sy[t_a] && py < in_sy[t_a] + 3;\n                u = px - in_sx[t_a]; v = py - in_sy[t_a];\n                cov_a = in_sprite && opaque_a(u, v);\n                check_layer(\"a\", {ar, ag, ab}, a_cov, cov_a ? colour_a(u, v) : background(px, py), cov_a, ax, ay, ade, ahs, avs, t_a);\n            end\n            if (errors > 10) t = CYCLES + 100;\n        end\n        // The sprites must really have been drawn, in every way.\n        if (covered_a < 40 || covered_b < 60 || blended < 20) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: too little drawn (a %0d, b %0d, blended %0d)\", covered_a, covered_b, blended);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS: %0d pixels checked, %0d + %0d covered, %0d blended\", checks, covered_a, covered_b, blended);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/sync_fifo/README.md" : "# FIFO (one clock)\n\nA queue of `2^DEPTH_LOG2` words. Write with `wr_en` while not `full`; read with `rd_en` while not\n`empty` — the word appears on `rd_data` **one cycle after** `rd_en`, because the read is\nregistered (that's what lets the tools use block RAM). Writes when full and reads when empty are\nignored, so a simple producer and consumer can't corrupt it.\n\n```verilog\nsync_fifo #(.WIDTH(8), .DEPTH_LOG2(4)) u_fifo (\n    .clk(clk), .rst(1'b0),\n    .wr_data(rx_byte), .wr_en(rx_valid), .full(),\n    .rd_en(tx_take), .rd_data(tx_byte), .empty(fifo_empty), .count()\n);\n```\n\nFor two clocks use `async_fifo`.\n",
    "cores/sync_fifo/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"First-in first-out buffer in one clock domain, with full, empty and fill count. Registered read for block RAM.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 219.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 54,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 18,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 84.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 3,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 6,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 18,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 13,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 70\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 150.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 18,\n        \"DFF\" : 18,\n        \"LUT4\" : 31,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 6,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 2,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 67.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 43,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 103,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 52,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 18,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 2\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"sync_fifo\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"fifo\",\n    \"buffer\",\n    \"queue\",\n    \"memory\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"First-in-first-out-Puffer in einer Taktdomäne, mit Voll, Leer und Füllstand. Registriertes Lesen für Block-RAM.\",\n      \"name\" : \"FIFO (ein Takt)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"FIFO (one clock)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"Bits per word\",\n      \"name\" : \"WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 4,\n      \"description\" : \"2^DEPTH_LOG2 words\",\n      \"name\" : \"DEPTH_LOG2\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Synchronous reset, active high: empties the FIFO\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rst\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Word to write\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Write wr_data (ignored when full)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"wr_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"No room for another word\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"full\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Read a word (ignored when empty)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rd_en\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The word read, one cycle after rd_en\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"rd_data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"No word to read\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"empty\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Words stored\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"count\",\n      \"width\" : 5\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"sync_fifo\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/sync_fifo/rtl/sync_fifo.v" : "// Synchronous FIFO: first in, first out, one clock. 2^DEPTH_LOG2 entries of WIDTH bits.\n//\n// Write: `wr_en` with `wr_data` stores a word unless `full`. Read: `rd_en` takes a word unless\n// `empty`; it appears on `rd_data` one cycle later and stays until the next read (a registered\n// read, so the memory can be block RAM). `count` is the number of stored words. `rst` (synchronous, active high) empties it.\n\nmodule sync_fifo #(\n    parameter WIDTH      = 8,\n    parameter DEPTH_LOG2 = 4\n) (\n    input  wire                  clk,\n    input  wire                  rst,\n    input  wire [WIDTH-1:0]      wr_data,\n    input  wire                  wr_en,\n    output wire                  full,\n    input  wire                  rd_en,\n    output reg  [WIDTH-1:0]      rd_data = 0,\n    output wire                  empty,\n    output wire [DEPTH_LOG2:0]   count\n);\n    localparam DEPTH = 1 << DEPTH_LOG2;\n\n    reg [WIDTH-1:0] memory [0:DEPTH-1];\n    // One bit more than the address: equal pointers are empty, pointers a full turn apart full.\n    reg [DEPTH_LOG2:0] wr_ptr = 0;\n    reg [DEPTH_LOG2:0] rd_ptr = 0;\n\n    assign count = wr_ptr - rd_ptr;\n    assign empty = (wr_ptr == rd_ptr);\n    assign full  = (count == DEPTH);\n\n    wire write = wr_en && !full;\n    wire read  = rd_en && !empty;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (write) memory[wr_ptr[DEPTH_LOG2-1:0]] <= wr_data;\n        if (read)  rd_data <= memory[rd_ptr[DEPTH_LOG2-1:0]];\n    end\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (rst) begin\n            wr_ptr <= 0;\n            rd_ptr <= 0;\n        end else begin\n            if (write) wr_ptr <= wr_ptr + 1'b1;\n            if (read)  rd_ptr <= rd_ptr + 1'b1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/sync_fifo/tb/sync_fifo_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for sync_fifo (8 bits × 4 entries): fill to full, writes while full\n// are dropped, drain to empty, reads while empty change nothing, then random traffic checked\n// against a reference queue, and a reset.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule sync_fifo_tb;\n    localparam DEPTH = 4;\n\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    reg rst = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] wr_data = 8'd0;\n    reg wr_en = 1'b0, rd_en = 1'b0;\n    wire full, empty;\n    wire [7:0] rd_data;\n    wire [2:0] count;\n\n    sync_fifo #(.WIDTH(8), .DEPTH_LOG2(2)) dut (\n        .clk(clk), .rst(rst), .wr_data(wr_data), .wr_en(wr_en), .full(full),\n        .rd_en(rd_en), .rd_data(rd_data), .empty(empty), .count(count)\n    );\n\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    // Reference queue.\n    reg [7:0] queue [0:255];\n    integer head = 0, tail = 0;\n    reg expect_data = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] expected = 8'd0;\n    reg can_read, can_write;\n    reg [7:0] held = 8'd0;\n\n    // Drive on the falling edge; check on the rising edge like the design sees it.\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (expect_data && rd_data !== expected) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: read %h, expected %h\", rd_data, expected);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        else if (!expect_data && !rst && rd_data !== held) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: rd_data changed to %h without a read (was %h)\", rd_data, held);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        held = rd_data;\n        expect_data = 1'b0;\n        if (rst) begin\n            head = 0; tail = 0;\n        end else begin\n            if (count !== tail - head) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: count %0d, expected %0d\", count, tail - head);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            if (full !== (tail - head == DEPTH) || empty !== (tail == head)) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: full=%b empty=%b with %0d entries\", full, empty, tail - head);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            // Both decided on the state before this edge, as `full` and `empty` are.\n            can_read = (tail != head);\n            can_write = (tail - head < DEPTH);\n            if (rd_en && can_read) begin\n                expected = queue[head % 256];\n                expect_data = 1'b1;\n                head = head + 1;\n            end\n            if (wr_en && can_write) begin\n                queue[tail % 256] = wr_data;\n                tail = tail + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    task step(input w, input [7:0] d, input r);\n        begin\n            @(negedge clk);\n            wr_en = w; wr_data = d; rd_en = r;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    integer i;\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"sync_fifo_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, sync_fifo_tb);\n        step(0, 0, 0);\n        for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) step(1, 8'h10 + i, 0);   // two too many\n        step(0, 0, 0);\n        if (!full) begin $display(\"FAIL: not full after 6 writes\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n        for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) step(0, 0, 1);           // two too many\n        step(0, 0, 0);\n        if (!empty) begin $display(\"FAIL: not empty after draining\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n\n        for (i = 0; i < 400; i = i + 1) step($random % 2, $random, $random % 2);\n        step(0, 0, 0);\n\n        // Reset empties it.\n        step(1, 8'hAA, 0); step(1, 8'hBB, 0);\n        @(negedge clk) begin wr_en = 0; rst = 1; end\n        @(negedge clk) rst = 0;\n        step(0, 0, 0);\n        if (!empty || count != 0) begin $display(\"FAIL: not empty after reset\"); errors = errors + 1; end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #100000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/text_layer/README.md" : "# Text layer\n\nDraws a grid of characters over a video picture: a score, a title, a console. Like\n`sprite_layer` it passes the pixel stream through — position, `de`, syncs and the colour below —\nand leaves `LATENCY = FONT_LATENCY + 2` clocks later with the text on top, so layers chain.\n\nThe grid is `COLUMNS × ROWS` cells at (`TEXT_X`, `TEXT_Y`), each one of the font's cells\n(`CELL_WIDTH × CELL_HEIGHT`, powers of two) times `2^SCALE_SHIFT`. The glyphs come from a font\nROM (Project ▸ Add Font ROM…): the layer drives its `code`, `row` and `column` and reads `pixel`.\n\n```verilog\nwire [7:0] code;\nwire [2:0] row, column;\nwire pixel;\nfont u_font (.clk(pixel_clk), .code(code), .row(row), .column(column), .row_bits(), .pixel(pixel));\n\ntext_layer #(.COLUMNS(32), .ROWS(1), .SCALE_SHIFT(1), .TEXT_X(64), .TEXT_Y(440),\n             .INIT_FILE(\"src/title.hex\")) u_text (\n    .clk(pixel_clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync),\n    .red(bg_red), .green(bg_green), .blue(bg_blue),\n    .write(1'b0), .write_address(5'd0), .write_code(8'd0), .write_colour(4'd0),\n    .font_code(code), .font_row(row), .font_column(column), .font_pixel(pixel),\n    .x_out(x2), .y_out(y2), .de_out(de2), .hsync_out(hsync2), .vsync_out(vsync2),\n    .red_out(red2), .green_out(green2), .blue_out(blue2), .ink());\n```\n\n- **The text** lives in the layer's own RAM, one 12-bit word per cell: a colour (an index into\n  `PALETTE`, 16 colours, the CGA palette by default with 15 = white) and the character code.\n  `INIT_FILE` fills it at start: a hex file with one word per cell, row by row (`F41` is a white\n  \"A\", `E20` a yellow space); its path is relative to the project folder. Without one the cells\n  are `BLANK_CODE` (a space) in white.\n- **Change it** at any time with `write`: `write_address` = row × `COLUMNS` + column, the code\n  and the colour (a score that counts up, a message that types itself).\n- **Codes** are the font's: with ASCII or Latin-1 the character's code, with a font of its own\n  characters (Add Font ROM ▸ Characters ▸ Custom) its place in the list. `CODE_BITS` is the\n  reader's code width.\n- **Background:** pixels without ink show the picture below, or `BACKGROUND_COLOUR` inside the\n  grid with `BACKGROUND = 1` (a box behind the text). `ink` marks the pixels a character draws.\n- `FONT_LATENCY` must be the reader's latency: 1 for GateLab's font ROMs.\n",
    "cores/text_layer/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Draws a grid of characters from a font ROM (Add Font ROM) over a video picture: its own text RAM with a colour per cell (16-colour palette), written at any time or loaded from a hex file, scaling, an optional background. Layers chain; LATENCY = FONT_LATENCY + 2 clocks.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 148.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 199,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 163,\n        \"TRELLIS_RAMW\" : 15\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 95.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 8,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 17,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 60,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 93,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 191,\n        \"CPE_RAMIO\" : 61,\n        \"RAM_HALF\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 229.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 24,\n        \"DFF\" : 163,\n        \"LUT4\" : 263,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 11,\n        \"RAM16SDP4\" : 15\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 68.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 220,\n        \"ICESTORM_RAM\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 117.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"DP8KC\" : 1,\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 81,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 184\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 280.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 2,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 156,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 110\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"text_layer\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"text\",\n    \"font\",\n    \"characters\",\n    \"video\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"vga\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"console\",\n    \"overlay\",\n    \"rom\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Zeichnet ein Zeichenraster aus einem Font-ROM (Add Font ROM) über ein Videobild: eigener Text-RAM mit einer Farbe pro Zelle (Palette mit 16 Farben), jederzeit beschreibbar oder aus einer Hex-Datei geladen, Skalierung, optionaler Hintergrund. Ebenen lassen sich verketten; LATENCY = FONT_LATENCY + 2 Takte.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Text-Ebene\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Text layer\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 11,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of x (video_timing's x)\",\n      \"name\" : \"X_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of y\",\n      \"name\" : \"Y_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 40,\n      \"description\" : \"Characters per row\",\n      \"name\" : \"COLUMNS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Rows of characters\",\n      \"name\" : \"ROWS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"The font's cell width in pixels (a power of two)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CELL_WIDTH\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"The font's cell height in pixels (a power of two)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CELL_HEIGHT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Each font pixel is 2^SCALE_SHIFT screen pixels wide and high\",\n      \"name\" : \"SCALE_SHIFT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Screen column of the grid's left edge\",\n      \"name\" : \"TEXT_X\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"Screen row of the grid's top edge\",\n      \"name\" : \"TEXT_Y\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 8,\n      \"description\" : \"Width of the font ROM reader's code input\",\n      \"name\" : \"CODE_BITS\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 1,\n      \"description\" : \"The font ROM reader's read latency\",\n      \"name\" : \"FONT_LATENCY\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Hex file with the starting text, one word per cell (colour, code: F41 is a white A); empty: blank\",\n      \"name\" : \"INIT_FILE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 32,\n      \"description\" : \"The code of an empty cell when there is no INIT_FILE\",\n      \"name\" : \"BLANK_CODE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: the grid's cells show BACKGROUND_COLOUR where there is no ink\",\n      \"name\" : \"BACKGROUND\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"The grid's background as 24'hRRGGBB\",\n      \"name\" : \"BACKGROUND_COLOUR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"16 colours as 24'hRRGGBB each, index 0 in the lowest bits; the CGA palette by default (15 is white)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PALETTE\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel column from video_timing (or the previous layer's x_out)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"x\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel row\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"y\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: the pixel is visible\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"red\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"green\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue of the picture below\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Write write_code and write_colour into the cell at write_address\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Cell to write: row × COLUMNS + column\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write_address\",\n      \"width\" : 7\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Character code\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write_code\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Palette index of the character\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"write_colour\",\n      \"width\" : 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the font ROM reader's code\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"font_code\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the font ROM reader's row\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"font_row\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"To the font ROM reader's column\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"font_column\",\n      \"width\" : 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"From the font ROM reader: pixel\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"font_pixel\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"x, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"x_out\",\n      \"width\" : 11\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"y, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"y_out\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"de, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"de_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"hsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"vsync, LATENCY clocks later\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync_out\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Red with the layer drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"red_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Green with the layer drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"green_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Blue with the layer drawn\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"blue_out\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"A character draws this output pixel\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ink\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"text_layer\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.1\"\n}\n",
    "cores/text_layer/rtl/text_layer.v" : "// Text layer: draws a grid of characters from a font ROM over the picture passing through. Like\n// sprite_layer it takes a pixel stream (position, timing and the colour below) and passes it on\n// LATENCY = FONT_LATENCY + 2 clocks later with the text on top, so layers chain.\n//\n// The grid is COLUMNS × ROWS cells at (TEXT_X, TEXT_Y) on the screen, each CELL_WIDTH ×\n// CELL_HEIGHT pixels (the font's cells, powers of two) times 2^SCALE_SHIFT. The text lives in a\n// small RAM of its own, one word per cell: a 4-bit colour (an index into PALETTE) and an 8-bit\n// character code. It starts from INIT_FILE (hex, one word per cell row by row, e.g. F41 = white\n// \"A\"), or blank (BLANK_CODE in colour 15) without one; `write` changes a cell at any time\n// (address = row × COLUMNS + column).\n//\n// The glyphs come from a font ROM reader (Project ▸ Add Font ROM…): the layer drives its code,\n// row and column and reads `pixel` back FONT_LATENCY clocks later. Pixels without ink show the\n// picture below, or BACKGROUND_COLOUR inside the grid with BACKGROUND = 1. `ink` marks output\n// pixels drawn by a character.\n\nmodule text_layer #(\n    parameter X_BITS = 11,\n    parameter Y_BITS = 10,\n    parameter COLUMNS = 40,\n    parameter ROWS = 2,\n    parameter CELL_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter CELL_HEIGHT = 8,\n    parameter SCALE_SHIFT = 0,\n    parameter TEXT_X = 0,\n    parameter TEXT_Y = 0,\n    parameter CODE_BITS = 8,\n    parameter FONT_LATENCY = 1,\n    parameter INIT_FILE = \"\",\n    parameter [7:0] BLANK_CODE = 8'h20,\n    parameter BACKGROUND = 0,\n    parameter [23:0] BACKGROUND_COLOUR = 24'h000000,\n    // 16 colours, index 0 in the lowest 24 bits: the CGA palette (15 = white).\n    parameter [383:0] PALETTE = {\n        24'hFFFFFF, 24'hFFFF55, 24'hFF55FF, 24'hFF5555, 24'h55FFFF, 24'h55FF55, 24'h5555FF, 24'h555555,\n        24'hAAAAAA, 24'hAA5500, 24'hAA00AA, 24'hAA0000, 24'h00AAAA, 24'h00AA00, 24'h0000AA, 24'h000000\n    }\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    input  wire [X_BITS-1:0] x,\n    input  wire [Y_BITS-1:0] y,\n    input  wire de,\n    input  wire hsync,\n    input  wire vsync,\n    input  wire [7:0] red,\n    input  wire [7:0] green,\n    input  wire [7:0] blue,\n    input  wire write,\n    input  wire [$clog2(COLUMNS * ROWS)-1:0] write_address,\n    input  wire [7:0] write_code,\n    input  wire [3:0] write_colour,\n    output wire [CODE_BITS-1:0] font_code,\n    output reg  [$clog2(CELL_HEIGHT)-1:0] font_row = 0,\n    output reg  [$clog2(CELL_WIDTH)-1:0] font_column = 0,\n    input  wire font_pixel,\n    output reg  [X_BITS-1:0] x_out = 0,\n    output reg  [Y_BITS-1:0] y_out = 0,\n    output reg  de_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  hsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  vsync_out = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [7:0] red_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] green_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  [7:0] blue_out = 8'd0,\n    output reg  ink = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam CELLS = COLUMNS * ROWS;\n    localparam AB = $clog2(CELLS);\n    localparam CB = COLUMNS > 1 ? $clog2(COLUMNS) : 1;\n    localparam RB = ROWS > 1 ? $clog2(ROWS) : 1;\n    localparam CW = $clog2(CELL_WIDTH);\n    localparam CH = $clog2(CELL_HEIGHT);\n    localparam GRID_WIDTH = COLUMNS * CELL_WIDTH;\n    localparam GRID_HEIGHT = ROWS * CELL_HEIGHT;\n\n    // ---- The text ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg [11:0] cells [0:CELLS-1];\n    integer i;\n    initial begin\n        if (INIT_FILE != \"\") $readmemh(INIT_FILE, cells);\n        else for (i = 0; i < CELLS; i = i + 1) cells[i] = {4'hF, BLANK_CODE};\n    end\n\n    // ---- Cycle 0: which cell, and which pixel of its glyph? ---------------------------------\n    wire [X_BITS:0] dx = {1'b0, x} - TEXT_X;\n    wire [Y_BITS:0] dy = {1'b0, y} - TEXT_Y;\n    wire [X_BITS:0] u = dx >> SCALE_SHIFT;\n    wire [Y_BITS:0] v = dy >> SCALE_SHIFT;\n    wire in_grid = de && !dx[X_BITS] && !dy[Y_BITS] && u < GRID_WIDTH && v < GRID_HEIGHT;\n    wire [X_BITS:0] column = u >> CW;\n    wire [Y_BITS:0] row = v >> CH;\n    // row × COLUMNS as a sum of shifted rows, one per set bit of COLUMNS (120 = four), with the row\n    // as narrow as ROWS allows. Written as `*`, Yosys maps it to a DSP48 on 7-series once the grid\n    // is large (33 × 120 cells), and nextpnr-xilinx fed that DSP's clock over ordinary wires.\n    function [AB-1:0] times_columns(input [RB-1:0] r);\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            times_columns = {AB{1'b0}};\n            for (k = 0; k < 31; k = k + 1)\n                if (((COLUMNS >> k) & 1) != 0) times_columns = times_columns + ({{AB{1'b0}}, r} << k);\n        end\n    endfunction\n    wire [AB-1:0] address = times_columns(row[RB-1:0]) + column[CB-1:0];\n\n    reg [11:0] cell_word = 12'd0;\n    reg inside_1 = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (write) cells[write_address] <= {write_colour, write_code};\n        cell_word <= cells[address];\n        font_row <= v[CH-1:0];\n        font_column <= u[CW-1:0];\n        inside_1 <= in_grid;\n    end\n    assign font_code = cell_word[CODE_BITS-1:0];\n\n    // ---- The font's clocks: the picture, the colour and `in_grid` wait for the glyph ---------\n    localparam PW = X_BITS + Y_BITS + 3 + 24;\n    localparam STAGES = FONT_LATENCY + 1;\n    reg [PW*STAGES-1:0] pass = 0;\n    wire [PW*(STAGES+1)-1:0] pass_next = {pass, x, y, de, hsync, vsync, red, green, blue};\n    reg [5*FONT_LATENCY-1:0] cell_pipe = 0;\n    wire [5*(FONT_LATENCY+1)-1:0] cell_next = {cell_pipe, inside_1, cell_word[11:8]};\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        pass <= pass_next[PW*STAGES-1:0];\n        cell_pipe <= cell_next[5*FONT_LATENCY-1:0];\n    end\n    wire [PW-1:0] below = pass[PW*STAGES-1 -: PW];\n    wire hit = cell_pipe[5*FONT_LATENCY-1];\n    wire [3:0] colour = cell_pipe[5*FONT_LATENCY-2 -: 4];\n    wire [23:0] ink_colour = PALETTE[24*colour +: 24];\n\n    // ---- Cycle FONT_LATENCY + 1: the text over the picture ----------------------------------\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        {x_out, y_out, de_out, hsync_out, vsync_out} <= below[PW-1:24];\n        if (hit && font_pixel) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= ink_colour;\n        else if (hit && BACKGROUND != 0) {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= BACKGROUND_COLOUR;\n        else {red_out, green_out, blue_out} <= below[23:0];\n        ink <= hit && font_pixel;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/text_layer/tb/text_layer_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for text_layer. A small screen (32 × 16 visible, 36 × 18 in all) is\n// scanned for four frames through two layers in a chain, each with its own font model:\n//   t1: 5 × 2 cells of 4 × 4 at (3, 1), ×1, font latency 1, 8-bit codes, the picture shows\n//       through where there is no ink;\n//   t2: 2 × 3 cells of 8 × 4 at (13, 3), ×2 — half off the right and bottom edges —, font latency\n//       2, 4-bit codes (a font of its own characters), BACKGROUND = 1.\n// Cells start blank, some are written before the first frame, others in the vertical blanking of\n// later frames. Every output pixel (colour, ink, and the position and timing passed through) is\n// compared with a reference written apart from the RTL.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule text_layer_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    localparam H_TOTAL = 36, H_ACTIVE = 32, V_TOTAL = 18, V_ACTIVE = 16, FRAMES = 4;\n    localparam [23:0] BOX = 24'h123456;\n\n    reg [5:0] x = 0;\n    reg [4:0] y = 0;\n    reg de = 1'b0, hsync = 1'b0, vsync = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] red = 0, green = 0, blue = 0;\n\n    function [23:0] background(input integer px, input integer py);\n        reg [7:0] r, g, b;\n        begin\n            r = {px[5:0], 2'b01}; g = {py[4:0], 3'b110}; b = 8'h5A ^ (px * 3 + py);\n            background = {r, g, b};\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // The CGA palette, as the core's default.\n    function [23:0] palette(input [3:0] index);\n        case (index)\n            0: palette = 24'h000000; 1: palette = 24'h0000AA; 2: palette = 24'h00AA00; 3: palette = 24'h00AAAA;\n            4: palette = 24'hAA0000; 5: palette = 24'hAA00AA; 6: palette = 24'hAA5500; 7: palette = 24'hAAAAAA;\n            8: palette = 24'h555555; 9: palette = 24'h5555FF; 10: palette = 24'h55FF55; 11: palette = 24'h55FFFF;\n            12: palette = 24'hFF5555; 13: palette = 24'hFF55FF; 14: palette = 24'hFFFF55; default: palette = 24'hFFFFFF;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    // A font model: one pixel of a glyph, different for every code, row and column.\n    function glyph(input integer code, input integer row, input integer column);\n        glyph = ((code * 5 + row * 3 + column * 7 + code / 4) % 3) == 0;\n    endfunction\n\n    // ---- Writing cells ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n    reg w1 = 1'b0, w2 = 1'b0;\n    reg [3:0] a1 = 0;\n    reg [2:0] a2 = 0;\n    reg [7:0] code_1 = 0, code_2 = 0;\n    reg [3:0] colour_1 = 0, colour_2 = 0;\n    reg [11:0] shadow1 [0:9];\n    reg [11:0] shadow2 [0:5];\n\n    // ---- The two layers in a chain, each with its font model --------------------------------\n    wire [7:0] code1;\n    wire [3:0] code2;\n    wire [1:0] row1, column1, row2;\n    wire [2:0] column2;\n    reg pixel1 = 1'b0, pixel2_1 = 1'b0, pixel2 = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        pixel1 <= glyph(code1, row1, column1);\n        pixel2_1 <= glyph(code2, row2, column2);\n        pixel2 <= pixel2_1;\n    end\n\n    wire [5:0] x1, x2;\n    wire [4:0] y1, y2;\n    wire de1, hs1, vs1, de2, hs2, vs2, ink1, ink2;\n    wire [7:0] r1, g1, b1, r2, g2, b2;\n\n    text_layer #(.X_BITS(6), .Y_BITS(5), .COLUMNS(5), .ROWS(2), .CELL_WIDTH(4), .CELL_HEIGHT(4), .SCALE_SHIFT(0),\n                 .TEXT_X(3), .TEXT_Y(1), .CODE_BITS(8), .FONT_LATENCY(1)) dut1 (\n        .clk(clk), .x(x), .y(y), .de(de), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .red(red), .green(green), .blue(blue),\n        .write(w1), .write_address(a1), .write_code(code_1), .write_colour(colour_1),\n        .font_code(code1), .font_row(row1), .font_column(column1), .font_pixel(pixel1),\n        .x_out(x1), .y_out(y1), .de_out(de1), .hsync_out(hs1), .vsync_out(vs1), .red_out(r1), .green_out(g1), .blue_out(b1), .ink(ink1));\n    text_layer #(.X_BITS(6), .Y_BITS(5), .COLUMNS(2), .ROWS(3), .CELL_WIDTH(8), .CELL_HEIGHT(4), .SCALE_SHIFT(1),\n                 .TEXT_X(13), .TEXT_Y(3), .CODE_BITS(4), .FONT_LATENCY(2), .BACKGROUND(1), .BACKGROUND_COLOUR(BOX)) dut2 (\n        .clk(clk), .x(x1), .y(y1), .de(de1), .hsync(hs1), .vsync(vs1), .red(r1), .green(g1), .blue(b1),\n        .write(w2), .write_address(a2), .write_code(code_2), .write_colour(colour_2),\n        .font_code(code2), .font_row(row2), .font_column(column2), .font_pixel(pixel2),\n        .x_out(x2), .y_out(y2), .de_out(de2), .hsync_out(hs2), .vsync_out(vs2), .red_out(r2), .green_out(g2), .blue_out(b2), .ink(ink2));\n\n    // ---- Reference --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    localparam LAT1 = 3, LAT2 = 4;\n    localparam CYCLES = H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL * FRAMES;\n    reg [5:0] in_x [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg [4:0] in_y [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_de [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_hs [0:CYCLES-1];\n    reg in_vs [0:CYCLES-1];\n\n    integer errors = 0, checks = 0, inked1 = 0, inked2 = 0, boxed = 0, t, t1, t2, i;\n    integer px, py, u, v, cell_index;\n    reg [23:0] want1, want2;\n    reg ink_1, ink_2, in1, in2;\n\n    task check(input [8*2-1:0] name, input [23:0] got, input got_ink, input [23:0] want, input want_ink,\n               input [5:0] got_x, input [4:0] got_y, input got_de, input got_hs, input got_vs, input integer t0);\n        begin\n            checks = checks + 1;\n            if (got !== want || got_ink !== want_ink) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %s at (%0d, %0d): colour %h ink %b, expected %h %b\", name, in_x[t0], in_y[t0], got, got_ink, want, want_ink);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            if (got_x !== in_x[t0] || got_y !== in_y[t0] || got_de !== in_de[t0] || got_hs !== in_hs[t0] || got_vs !== in_vs[t0]) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %s passes (%0d, %0d) de %b hs %b vs %b, expected (%0d, %0d) %b %b %b\", name, got_x, got_y, got_de, got_hs, got_vs,\n                         in_x[t0], in_y[t0], in_de[t0], in_hs[t0], in_vs[t0]);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // What layer 1 draws at (px, py) over `below`.\n    task layer1(input integer px, input integer py, input [23:0] below, output [23:0] out, output inked);\n        begin\n            u = px - 3; v = py - 1;\n            in1 = u >= 0 && v >= 0 && u < 20 && v < 8;\n            cell_index = (v / 4) * 5 + u / 4;\n            inked = in1 && glyph(shadow1[cell_index][7:0], v % 4, u % 4);\n            out = inked ? palette(shadow1[cell_index][11:8]) : below;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // What layer 2 draws at (px, py) over `below`.\n    task layer2(input integer px, input integer py, input [23:0] below, output [23:0] out, output inked);\n        begin\n            u = (px - 13) / 2; v = (py - 3) / 2;\n            in2 = px >= 13 && py >= 3 && u < 16 && v < 12;\n            cell_index = (v / 4) * 2 + u / 8;\n            inked = in2 && glyph(shadow2[cell_index][3:0], v % 4, u % 8);\n            out = inked ? palette(shadow2[cell_index][11:8]) : in2 ? BOX : below;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // Before the scan a write takes a clock of its own; during it, writes go along with the pixels.\n    task write1(input [3:0] address, input [7:0] c, input [3:0] k);\n        begin\n            w1 = 1'b1; a1 = address; code_1 = c; colour_1 = k;\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            w1 = 1'b0;\n            shadow1[address] = {k, c};\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task write2(input [2:0] address, input [7:0] c, input [3:0] k);\n        begin\n            w2 = 1'b1; a2 = address; code_2 = c; colour_2 = k;\n            @(posedge clk); #1;\n            w2 = 1'b0;\n            shadow2[address] = {k, c};\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // The writes of frames 1 and 2, in their first blanking line: step k of 0 … 2.\n    task schedule(input integer frame, input integer k);\n        begin\n            case (frame * 4 + k)\n                4: begin w1 = 1'b1; a1 = 4; code_1 = \"L\"; colour_1 = 4'd1; w2 = 1'b1; a2 = 5; code_2 = 8'd14; colour_2 = 4'd2; end\n                5: begin w1 = 1'b1; a1 = 9; code_1 = \"B\"; colour_1 = 4'd15; end\n                8: begin w1 = 1'b1; a1 = 0; code_1 = 8'h7E; colour_1 = 4'd6; w2 = 1'b1; a2 = 0; code_2 = 8'd1; colour_2 = 4'd4; end\n                9: begin w2 = 1'b1; a2 = 2; code_2 = 8'd7; colour_2 = 4'd8; end\n                default: ;\n            endcase\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"text_layer_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, text_layer_tb);\n        for (i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) shadow1[i] = {4'hF, 8'h20};\n        for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) shadow2[i] = {4'hF, 8'h20};\n        // Before the first frame: \"GATE\" in four colours, and two cells of layer 2.\n        write1(0, \"G\", 4'd14); write1(1, \"A\", 4'd12); write1(2, \"T\", 4'd10); write1(3, \"E\", 4'd9);\n        write1(7, \"!\", 4'd3);\n        write2(0, 8'd5, 4'd11); write2(3, 8'd9, 4'd13);\n        for (t = 0; t < CYCLES + LAT1 + LAT2; t = t + 1) begin\n            px = t % H_TOTAL;\n            py = (t / H_TOTAL) % V_TOTAL;\n            // In the vertical blanking of frames 1 and 2 (no pixel of the grid is on its way), change cells.\n            if (t < CYCLES && py == V_ACTIVE && px < 3) schedule(t / (H_TOTAL * V_TOTAL), px);\n            if (t < CYCLES) begin\n                x = px; y = py;\n                de = px < H_ACTIVE && py < V_ACTIVE;\n                hsync = px >= 33;\n                vsync = py == 17;\n                {red, green, blue} = background(px, py);\n                in_x[t] = x; in_y[t] = y; in_de[t] = de; in_hs[t] = hsync; in_vs[t] = vsync;\n            end\n            @(posedge clk);\n            #1;\n            if (w1) shadow1[a1] = {colour_1, code_1};\n            if (w2) shadow2[a2] = {colour_2, code_2};\n            w1 = 1'b0; w2 = 1'b0;\n            t1 = t - LAT1 + 1;\n            t2 = t1 - LAT2;\n            if (t1 >= 0 && t1 < CYCLES) begin\n                if (in_de[t1]) layer1(in_x[t1], in_y[t1], background(in_x[t1], in_y[t1]), want1, ink_1);\n                else begin want1 = background(in_x[t1], in_y[t1]); ink_1 = 1'b0; end\n                if (ink_1) inked1 = inked1 + 1;\n                check(\"t1\", {r1, g1, b1}, ink1, want1, ink_1, x1, y1, de1, hs1, vs1, t1);\n            end\n            if (t2 >= 0 && t2 < CYCLES) begin\n                if (in_de[t2]) begin\n                    layer1(in_x[t2], in_y[t2], background(in_x[t2], in_y[t2]), want1, ink_1);\n                    layer2(in_x[t2], in_y[t2], want1, want2, ink_2);\n                    if (in2 && !ink_2) boxed = boxed + 1;\n                end else begin want2 = background(in_x[t2], in_y[t2]); ink_2 = 1'b0; end\n                if (ink_2) inked2 = inked2 + 1;\n                check(\"t2\", {r2, g2, b2}, ink2, want2, ink_2, x2, y2, de2, hs2, vs2, t2);\n            end\n            if (errors > 10) t = CYCLES + 100;\n        end\n        if (inked1 < 100 || inked2 < 100 || boxed < 100) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: too little drawn (%0d + %0d inked, %0d background)\", inked1, inked2, boxed);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS: %0d pixels checked, %0d + %0d inked, %0d background\", checks, inked1, inked2, boxed);\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/tmds_encoder/README.md" : "# TMDS encoder (DVI/HDMI)\n\nOne channel of the TMDS code DVI and HDMI send: while `de` is high, a colour byte becomes a\n10-bit word with few transitions and a running DC balance; while it is low, the control word for\n`{c1, c0}`. A DVI/HDMI link uses three: blue (with `hsync` on `c0` and `vsync` on `c1`), green\nand red (control bits 0).\n\n```verilog\ntmds_encoder u_blue (.clk(pixel_clk), .de(de), .d(blue), .c0(hsync), .c1(vsync), .q(tmds_blue));\n```\n\n`q` is registered (one clock of latency) and goes out bit 0 first, ten bits per pixel. The\nserialiser that sends it is family-specific: on AMD Xilinx 7-series use `hdmi_out`, which has\nthe encoders, serialisers and differential outputs in one core.\n\nHDMI's smallest modes (720p60, 1080p30) need a 74.25 MHz pixel clock: the measured speeds (Add Core ▸ Measured)\nshow which families reach it (AMD Xilinx 7-series, ECP5 and Gowin do; on iCE40 and GateMate it\nsuits 640×480 at 25 MHz).\n",
    "cores/tmds_encoder/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"One channel of DVI/HDMI's TMDS code: a byte becomes a DC-balanced 10-bit word while data is enabled, the control word for c1/c0 in blanking. Three clocks of latency (pipelined for 1080p60 on 7-series); the serialiser is family-specific.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 188,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 139,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 48\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 63.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 3,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 15,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 25,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 15,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 41,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 103,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 13\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 180.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 32,\n        \"DFF\" : 48,\n        \"LUT4\" : 127,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 28,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 10,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 5,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 48.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 116\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 95.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 127,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 48\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 198.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 9,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 39,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 116\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"tmds_encoder\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"tmds\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"video\",\n    \"8b10b\",\n    \"encoder\",\n    \"display\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein Kanal des TMDS-Codes von DVI/HDMI: Ein Byte wird bei aktivem Datenfreigabesignal zu einem gleichspannungsfreien 10-Bit-Wort, in der Austastlücke zum Steuerwort für c1/c0. Drei Takte Latenz (für 1080p60 auf der 7-Serie in Stufen); der Serialisierer ist familienspezifisch.\",\n      \"name\" : \"TMDS-Encoder (DVI/HDMI)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"TMDS encoder (DVI/HDMI)\",\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data enable: high in the visible area\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The channel's colour byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"d\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Control bit 0 in blanking (blue: hsync)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"c0\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Control bit 1 in blanking (blue: vsync)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"c1\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The TMDS word, bit 0 goes out first\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"q\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"tmds_encoder\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.1.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/tmds_encoder/rtl/tmds_encoder.v" : "// TMDS encoder for DVI and HDMI (DVI 1.0, 3.3): one channel. While `de` is high it turns a byte\n// into a 10-bit word with few transitions and a running DC balance; while `de` is low it sends\n// one of the four control words for {c1, c0} (blue carries hsync on c0 and vsync on c1). Three\n// clocks of latency: `q` belongs to the inputs of three cycles before. Bit 0 of `q` goes out first.\n//\n// Three stages, so a full-colour 1080p60 picture meets its 148.5 MHz on a -1 7-series part (one\n// stage reached 105 MHz on an XC7S15 with real colours; colour bars of only 00 and FF hid it, the\n// encoder collapsing to two words): (1) count the byte's ones, (2) the transition-minimized word\n// and its ones minus zeros, (3) the DC balance against the running disparity — the only loop.\n//\n// The ones in a byte are counted with a table per nibble and one add: a sum of eight bits goes\n// through Yosys' maccmap, whose split carry cells nextpnr-xilinx couldn't place (GateLab's HDMI\n// spike, 2026-10-13).\n\nmodule tmds_encoder (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire       de,\n    input  wire [7:0] d,\n    input  wire       c0,\n    input  wire       c1,\n    output reg  [9:0] q = 10'd0\n);\n    function [2:0] nibble_ones;\n        input [3:0] v;\n        case (v)\n            4'h0: nibble_ones = 3'd0;\n            4'h1, 4'h2, 4'h4, 4'h8: nibble_ones = 3'd1;\n            4'h3, 4'h5, 4'h6, 4'h9, 4'hA, 4'hC: nibble_ones = 3'd2;\n            4'h7, 4'hB, 4'hD, 4'hE: nibble_ones = 3'd3;\n            default: nibble_ones = 3'd4;\n        endcase\n    endfunction\n\n    function [3:0] byte_ones;\n        input [7:0] v;\n        byte_ones = {1'b0, nibble_ones(v[3:0])} + {1'b0, nibble_ones(v[7:4])};\n    endfunction\n\n    // Stage 1: the byte's ones.\n    reg [7:0] d1 = 8'd0;\n    reg [3:0] ones1 = 4'd0;\n    reg de1 = 1'b0, c01 = 1'b0, c11 = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        d1 <= d;\n        ones1 <= byte_ones(d);\n        de1 <= de; c01 <= c0; c11 <= c1;\n    end\n\n    // Stage 2: transition-minimized code, XOR or XNOR chain (qm[8] says which: 1 = XOR), and\n    // `diff`, the number of ones minus the number of zeros in qm[7:0], in whole bits.\n    wire use_xnor = ones1 > 4 || (ones1 == 4 && d1[0] == 1'b0);\n    wire [8:0] qm;\n    assign qm[0] = d1[0];\n    genvar i;\n    generate\n        for (i = 1; i < 8; i = i + 1) begin : stage\n            assign qm[i] = use_xnor ? ~(qm[i - 1] ^ d1[i]) : (qm[i - 1] ^ d1[i]);\n        end\n    endgenerate\n    assign qm[8] = ~use_xnor;\n    wire [3:0] qm_ones = byte_ones(qm[7:0]);\n    reg [8:0] qm2 = 9'd0;\n    reg signed [5:0] diff = 6'sd0;\n    reg de2 = 1'b0, c02 = 1'b0, c12 = 1'b0;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        qm2 <= qm;\n        diff <= $signed({2'b0, qm_ones, 1'b0}) - 6'sd8;\n        de2 <= de1; c02 <= c01; c12 <= c11;\n    end\n\n    // Stage 3: DC balance against the running disparity.\n    wire signed [5:0] qm8_twice = qm2[8] ? 6'sd2 : 6'sd0;\n    wire signed [5:0] not_qm8_twice = qm2[8] ? 6'sd0 : 6'sd2;\n    reg signed [5:0] disparity = 6'sd0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (!de2) begin\n            disparity <= 6'sd0;\n            case ({c12, c02})\n                2'b00: q <= 10'b1101010100;\n                2'b01: q <= 10'b0010101011;\n                2'b10: q <= 10'b0101010100;\n                default: q <= 10'b1010101011;\n            endcase\n        end else if (disparity == 0 || diff == 0) begin\n            q <= {~qm2[8], qm2[8], qm2[8] ? qm2[7:0] : ~qm2[7:0]};\n            disparity <= qm2[8] ? disparity + diff : disparity - diff;\n        end else if ((disparity > 0 && diff > 0) || (disparity < 0 && diff < 0)) begin\n            q <= {1'b1, qm2[8], ~qm2[7:0]};\n            disparity <= disparity + qm8_twice - diff;\n        end else begin\n            q <= {1'b0, qm2[8], qm2[7:0]};\n            disparity <= disparity - not_qm8_twice + diff;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/tmds_encoder/tb/tmds_encoder_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for tmds_encoder: 4000 cycles of pseudo-random bytes in bursts of\n// active video (with 0x00, 0xFF and 0x10 runs, which lean hardest on the DC balance) and blanking\n// with every control word. Each output word is compared with a reference written from the DVI 1.0\n// algorithm (integer counts, 3.3.1), decoded back to the byte, and the running disparity of\n// the sent words is checked to stay small.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule tmds_encoder_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    reg de = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] d = 8'd0;\n    reg c0 = 1'b0, c1 = 1'b0;\n    wire [9:0] q;\n\n    tmds_encoder dut (.clk(clk), .de(de), .d(d), .c0(c0), .c1(c1), .q(q));\n\n    // The reference: the specification's encoder.\n    integer cnt = 0;\n\n    function integer ones8;\n        input [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            ones8 = 0;\n            for (k = 0; k < 8; k = k + 1) ones8 = ones8 + v[k];\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    task reference;\n        input in_de;\n        input [7:0] in_d;\n        input in_c0, in_c1;\n        output [9:0] word;\n        reg [8:0] qm;\n        integer k, n1, n0;\n        begin\n            if (!in_de) begin\n                cnt = 0;\n                case ({in_c1, in_c0})\n                    2'b00: word = 10'b1101010100;\n                    2'b01: word = 10'b0010101011;\n                    2'b10: word = 10'b0101010100;\n                    default: word = 10'b1010101011;\n                endcase\n            end else begin\n                qm[0] = in_d[0];\n                if (ones8(in_d) > 4 || (ones8(in_d) == 4 && in_d[0] == 1'b0)) begin\n                    for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) qm[k] = ~(qm[k - 1] ^ in_d[k]);\n                    qm[8] = 1'b0;\n                end else begin\n                    for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) qm[k] = qm[k - 1] ^ in_d[k];\n                    qm[8] = 1'b1;\n                end\n                n1 = ones8(qm[7:0]);\n                n0 = 8 - n1;\n                if (cnt == 0 || n1 == n0) begin\n                    word = {~qm[8], qm[8], qm[8] ? qm[7:0] : ~qm[7:0]};\n                    cnt = qm[8] ? cnt + (n1 - n0) : cnt + (n0 - n1);\n                end else if ((cnt > 0 && n1 > n0) || (cnt < 0 && n0 > n1)) begin\n                    word = {1'b1, qm[8], ~qm[7:0]};\n                    cnt = cnt + 2 * qm[8] + (n0 - n1);\n                end else begin\n                    word = {1'b0, qm[8], qm[7:0]};\n                    cnt = cnt - 2 * (1 - qm[8]) + (n1 - n0);\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    // A receiver's decoder for a data word.\n    function [7:0] decode;\n        input [9:0] word;\n        reg [7:0] v;\n        integer k;\n        begin\n            v = word[9] ? ~word[7:0] : word[7:0];\n            decode[0] = v[0];\n            for (k = 1; k < 8; k = k + 1) decode[k] = word[8] ? (v[k] ^ v[k - 1]) : ~(v[k] ^ v[k - 1]);\n        end\n    endfunction\n\n    // The encoder's latency; the expected words wait in a short history.\n    localparam LATENCY = 3;\n    reg [9:0] expected_q [0:LATENCY-1];\n    reg [7:0] sent_d [0:LATENCY-1];\n    reg sent_de [0:LATENCY-1];\n    integer errors = 0, cycle, balance = 0, worst = 0, words = 0, k;\n    reg [9:0] expected;\n    reg [15:0] lfsr = 16'hACE1;\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"tmds_encoder_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, tmds_encoder_tb);\n        for (cycle = 0; cycle < 4000; cycle = cycle + 1) begin\n            // Inputs for this clock: bursts of active video, blanking with each control word.\n            lfsr = {lfsr[14:0], lfsr[15] ^ lfsr[13] ^ lfsr[12] ^ lfsr[10]};\n            de = (cycle % 97) < 71;\n            {c1, c0} = cycle[5:4];\n            if (cycle >= 300 && cycle < 360) d = 8'h00;\n            else if (cycle >= 400 && cycle < 460) d = 8'hFF;\n            else if (cycle >= 500 && cycle < 560) d = 8'h10;\n            else d = lfsr[7:0];\n            reference(de, d, c0, c1, expected);\n            for (k = LATENCY - 1; k > 0; k = k - 1) begin\n                expected_q[k] = expected_q[k - 1]; sent_d[k] = sent_d[k - 1]; sent_de[k] = sent_de[k - 1];\n            end\n            expected_q[0] = expected; sent_d[0] = d; sent_de[0] = de;\n            @(posedge clk);\n            #1;\n            // q now holds the word for the inputs LATENCY - 1 edges before this one.\n            if (cycle >= LATENCY - 1) begin\n                if (q !== expected_q[LATENCY - 1]) begin\n                    $display(\"FAIL: cycle %0d: word %b, expected %b (de %b, d %h)\", cycle, q, expected_q[LATENCY - 1], sent_de[LATENCY - 1], sent_d[LATENCY - 1]);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n                if (sent_de[LATENCY - 1]) begin\n                    if (decode(q) !== sent_d[LATENCY - 1]) begin\n                        $display(\"FAIL: cycle %0d: %b decodes to %h, sent %h\", cycle, q, decode(q), sent_d[LATENCY - 1]);\n                        errors = errors + 1;\n                    end\n                    for (k = 0; k < 10; k = k + 1) balance = balance + (q[k] ? 1 : -1);\n                    if (balance > worst) worst = balance;\n                    if (-balance > worst) worst = -balance;\n                    words = words + 1;\n                end else begin\n                    balance = 0;\n                end\n            end\n            if (errors > 5) cycle = 4000;\n        end\n        // DVI keeps the running disparity bounded; without DC balance a run of 0x10 drifts far.\n        if (worst > 24) begin $display(\"FAIL: running disparity reached %0d bits\", worst); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (words < 2500) begin $display(\"FAIL: only %0d data words\", words); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #200000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/tone_generator/README.md" : "# Sine tone generator\n\nA sine tone at any frequency for audio tests and beeps: a phase accumulator and a 256-point sine table\n(16-bit signed, ±32767). Each `advance` adds `step` to the phase; `sample` follows one clock later.\n\n```\nfrequency = step · sample_rate / 2^PHASE_BITS\nstep      = frequency · 2^24 / sample_rate     (1 kHz at 48 kHz: 349525)\n```\n\n```verilog\nwire signed [15:0] tone;\ntone_generator u_tone (.clk(clk), .advance(next_sample), .step(24'd349525), .sample(tone));\n```\n\nGive it `i2s_transmitter`'s `taken` as `advance`, and shift the sample down for a quieter tone\n(`tone >>> 2` is −12 dBFS).\n",
    "cores/tone_generator/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"A sine tone at any frequency: a phase accumulator and a 256-point sine (16-bit signed, ±32767). Each `advance` (a sample period: i2s_transmitter's `taken`) adds `step`; frequency = step · rate / 2^PHASE_BITS (1 kHz at 48 kHz: step 349525 with 24 bits).\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 120.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 149,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 40\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 115.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 23,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 68,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 102,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 49,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 169,\n        \"CPE_MX4\" : 31\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 74.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 52,\n        \"DFF\" : 40,\n        \"LUT4\" : 74,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 17,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 3\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 32.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 184\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 51.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 215,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 40\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"xilinx7\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"xc7a35t\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 131.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CARRY4\" : 13,\n        \"SLICE_FFX\" : 40,\n        \"SLICE_LUTX\" : 145\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"tone_generator\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"tone\",\n    \"sine\",\n    \"audio\",\n    \"oscillator\",\n    \"dds\",\n    \"nco\",\n    \"beep\",\n    \"sound\",\n    \"test tone\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Ein Sinuston beliebiger Frequenz: ein Phasenakkumulator und ein 256-Punkte-Sinus (16 Bit mit Vorzeichen, ±32767). Jedes `advance` (eine Abtastperiode: `taken` von i2s_transmitter) addiert `step`; Frequenz = step · Rate / 2^PHASE_BITS (1 kHz bei 48 kHz: step 349525 mit 24 Bit).\",\n      \"name\" : \"Sinuston-Generator\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Sine tone generator\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 24,\n      \"description\" : \"Phase accumulator bits (step resolution)\",\n      \"name\" : \"PHASE_BITS\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Step to the next sample (one clock pulse)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"advance\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Phase step per sample\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"step\",\n      \"width\" : 24\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Sine sample of the phase before the step, one clock after advance (signed)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"sample\",\n      \"width\" : 16\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"tone_generator\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/tone_generator/rtl/tone_generator.v" : "// Sine tone generator: a phase accumulator and a 256-point sine (from a 65-entry quarter wave), 16-bit signed\n// full scale (±32767). Each `advance` adds `step` to the phase; `sample` is the sine of the phase before it,\n// one clock later. Frequency = step · rate / 2^PHASE_BITS, with `rate` the advances a second (the sample rate:\n// i2s_transmitter's `taken`). Scale the sample down (>>>) for a quieter tone.\n\nmodule tone_generator #(\n    parameter PHASE_BITS = 24\n) (\n    input  wire                  clk,\n    input  wire                  advance,\n    input  wire [PHASE_BITS-1:0] step,\n    output reg  signed [15:0]    sample = 16'sd0\n);\n    reg [PHASE_BITS-1:0] phase = {PHASE_BITS{1'b0}};\n    wire [7:0] index = phase[PHASE_BITS-1 -: 8];\n    // Quadrant by the top two bits; the table runs 0 … 64 (sin 0 … sin pi/2).\n    wire [6:0] at = index[6] ? 7'd64 - {1'b0, index[5:0]} : {1'b0, index[5:0]};\n    reg [14:0] quarter;\n    always @(*) begin\n        case (at)\n            7'd0: quarter = 15'd0;\n            7'd1: quarter = 15'd804;\n            7'd2: quarter = 15'd1608;\n            7'd3: quarter = 15'd2410;\n            7'd4: quarter = 15'd3212;\n            7'd5: quarter = 15'd4011;\n            7'd6: quarter = 15'd4808;\n            7'd7: quarter = 15'd5602;\n            7'd8: quarter = 15'd6393;\n            7'd9: quarter = 15'd7179;\n            7'd10: quarter = 15'd7962;\n            7'd11: quarter = 15'd8739;\n            7'd12: quarter = 15'd9512;\n            7'd13: quarter = 15'd10278;\n            7'd14: quarter = 15'd11039;\n            7'd15: quarter = 15'd11793;\n            7'd16: quarter = 15'd12539;\n            7'd17: quarter = 15'd13279;\n            7'd18: quarter = 15'd14010;\n            7'd19: quarter = 15'd14732;\n            7'd20: quarter = 15'd15446;\n            7'd21: quarter = 15'd16151;\n            7'd22: quarter = 15'd16846;\n            7'd23: quarter = 15'd17530;\n            7'd24: quarter = 15'd18204;\n            7'd25: quarter = 15'd18868;\n            7'd26: quarter = 15'd19519;\n            7'd27: quarter = 15'd20159;\n            7'd28: quarter = 15'd20787;\n            7'd29: quarter = 15'd21403;\n            7'd30: quarter = 15'd22005;\n            7'd31: quarter = 15'd22594;\n            7'd32: quarter = 15'd23170;\n            7'd33: quarter = 15'd23731;\n            7'd34: quarter = 15'd24279;\n            7'd35: quarter = 15'd24811;\n            7'd36: quarter = 15'd25329;\n            7'd37: quarter = 15'd25832;\n            7'd38: quarter = 15'd26319;\n            7'd39: quarter = 15'd26790;\n            7'd40: quarter = 15'd27245;\n            7'd41: quarter = 15'd27683;\n            7'd42: quarter = 15'd28105;\n            7'd43: quarter = 15'd28510;\n            7'd44: quarter = 15'd28898;\n            7'd45: quarter = 15'd29268;\n            7'd46: quarter = 15'd29621;\n            7'd47: quarter = 15'd29956;\n            7'd48: quarter = 15'd30273;\n            7'd49: quarter = 15'd30571;\n            7'd50: quarter = 15'd30852;\n            7'd51: quarter = 15'd31113;\n            7'd52: quarter = 15'd31356;\n            7'd53: quarter = 15'd31580;\n            7'd54: quarter = 15'd31785;\n            7'd55: quarter = 15'd31971;\n            7'd56: quarter = 15'd32137;\n            7'd57: quarter = 15'd32285;\n            7'd58: quarter = 15'd32412;\n            7'd59: quarter = 15'd32521;\n            7'd60: quarter = 15'd32609;\n            7'd61: quarter = 15'd32678;\n            7'd62: quarter = 15'd32728;\n            7'd63: quarter = 15'd32757;\n            7'd64: quarter = 15'd32767;\n            default: quarter = 15'd32767;\n        endcase\n    end\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        if (advance) begin\n            phase <= phase + step;\n            sample <= index[7] ? -$signed({1'b0, quarter}) : $signed({1'b0, quarter});\n        end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/tone_generator/tb/tone_generator_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for tone_generator: every sample of three tones (1/32 of the rate, an odd step, and\n// a step that wraps the phase fast) against round(32767 · sin(2π · phase / 2^24)) of the phase before the step,\n// within one count (the table's own rounding), and the frequency from the sign changes. Prints PASS or every\n// failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule tone_generator_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    reg advance = 1'b0;\n    reg [23:0] step = 24'd0;\n    wire signed [15:0] sample;\n    tone_generator dut (.clk(clk), .advance(advance), .step(step), .sample(sample));\n\n    real pi = 3.14159265358979;\n    reg [23:0] phase = 24'd0;\n    integer n, k, want, crossings;\n    reg signed [15:0] last;\n    reg [23:0] steps [0:2];\n\n    initial begin\n        steps[0] = 24'h080000;   // 1/32 of the rate\n        steps[1] = 24'h01A7C3;   // an odd step\n        steps[2] = 24'h7F1235;   // just under half the rate\n        for (k = 0; k < 3; k = k + 1) begin\n            step = steps[k];\n            crossings = 0;\n            last = 0;\n            for (n = 0; n < 4096; n = n + 1) begin\n                @(negedge clk) advance = 1'b1;\n                @(negedge clk) advance = 1'b0;\n                // The sample of the phase before this step: the table has 256 points.\n                want = $rtoi($floor(32767.0 * $sin(2.0 * pi * phase[23:16] / 256.0) + 0.5));\n                if (sample - want > 1 || want - sample > 1) begin\n                    if (errors < 8) $display(\"FAIL: step %h, sample %0d is %0d, expected %0d\", step, n, sample, want);\n                    errors = errors + 1;\n                end\n                if (n > 0 && ((last < 0) != (sample < 0))) crossings = crossings + 1;\n                last = sample;\n                phase = phase + step;\n            end\n            // Two sign changes a period: 4096 samples · step / 2^24 periods.\n            if (k == 0 && (crossings < 254 || crossings > 258)) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0d sign changes for 1/32 of the rate (256 expected)\", crossings);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #10000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/uart_rx/README.md" : "# UART receiver\n\nReceives 8N1 bytes on `rx`. The input is synchronised to `clk`, so it can come straight from a\npin. Each byte arrives with a one-cycle `valid` pulse; `data` keeps the byte until the next one.\n\n```verilog\nuart_rx #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .BAUD(115200)) u_rx (\n    .clk(clk), .rx(uart_rx), .data(rx_byte), .valid(rx_valid), .frame_error()\n);\n```\n\nA byte whose stop bit is low is dropped and reported on `frame_error`. After a break (the line\nheld low) the receiver waits for the line to go high before it accepts the next start bit. The\ntestbench shows it works with a sender 2 % off the nominal baud rate.\n",
    "cores/uart_rx/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Receives bytes from a serial line, 8N1: input synchroniser, glitch filter on the start bit, framing errors reported.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 223.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 53,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 25\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 95.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 2,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 6,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 14,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 23,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 24,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 64\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 150.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 18,\n        \"DFF\" : 25,\n        \"LUT4\" : 72,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 17,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 5,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 59.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 64\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 149.7,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 56,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 25\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"uart_rx\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"serial\",\n    \"rs232\",\n    \"uart\",\n    \"receive\",\n    \"8n1\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Empfängt Bytes von einer seriellen Leitung, 8N1: Eingangssynchronisierung, Glitch-Filter für das Startbit, meldet Rahmenfehler.\",\n      \"name\" : \"UART-Empfänger\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"UART receiver\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 115200,\n      \"description\" : \"Bits per second\",\n      \"name\" : \"BAUD\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Serial input (asynchronous), idles high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rx\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The last byte received\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: data holds a new byte\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse: a byte with a low stop bit was dropped\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_error\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"uart_rx\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/uart_rx/rtl/uart_rx.v" : "// UART receiver, 8N1. Synchronises `rx` with two flip-flops, starts on a falling edge, checks the\n// start bit in its middle (a short glitch is ignored) and samples every bit in its middle.\n//\n// `valid` is high for one cycle when a byte with a high stop bit has arrived; a low stop bit\n// drops the byte and pulses `frame_error` instead. A line held low (a break) is not a new byte:\n// the next start bit needs a falling edge.\n\nmodule uart_rx #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter BAUD   = 115200\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire       rx,\n    output reg  [7:0] data = 8'd0,\n    output reg        valid = 1'b0,\n    output reg        frame_error = 1'b0\n);\n    localparam CLKS_PER_BIT = (CLK_HZ + BAUD / 2) / BAUD;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = CLKS_PER_BIT > 1 ? $clog2(CLKS_PER_BIT) : 1;\n\n    localparam IDLE  = 2'd0;\n    localparam START = 2'd1;\n    localparam DATA  = 2'd2;\n    localparam STOP  = 2'd3;\n\n    // The idle line is high.\n    reg [1:0] sync = 2'b11;\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        sync <= {sync[0], rx};\n    wire rx_sync = sync[1];\n\n    reg rx_last = 1'b1;\n    always @(posedge clk)\n        rx_last <= rx_sync;\n    wire rx_fell = rx_last && !rx_sync;\n\n    reg [1:0] state = IDLE;\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n    reg [2:0] bit_index = 3'd0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        valid <= 1'b0;\n        frame_error <= 1'b0;\n        case (state)\n            IDLE:\n                if (rx_fell) begin\n                    clocks <= 0;\n                    state <= START;\n                end\n\n            START:\n                if (clocks == CLKS_PER_BIT / 2 - 1) begin\n                    clocks <= 0;\n                    bit_index <= 3'd0;\n                    state <= rx_sync ? IDLE : DATA;\n                end else begin\n                    clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n                end\n\n            DATA:\n                if (clocks == CLKS_PER_BIT - 1) begin\n                    clocks <= 0;\n                    data[bit_index] <= rx_sync;\n                    bit_index <= bit_index + 1'b1;\n                    if (bit_index == 3'd7)\n                        state <= STOP;\n                end else begin\n                    clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n                end\n\n            STOP:\n                if (clocks == CLKS_PER_BIT - 1) begin\n                    clocks <= 0;\n                    valid <= rx_sync;\n                    frame_error <= !rx_sync;\n                    state <= IDLE;\n                end else begin\n                    clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n                end\n        endcase\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/uart_rx/tb/uart_rx_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for uart_rx: bytes back to back, a glitch that isn't a start bit, a\n// frame with a low stop bit followed by a break, then a byte that must still arrive. The sender\n// runs 2 % slow to show the receiver tolerates clock mismatch. Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule uart_rx_tb;\n    localparam CLK_HZ = 1600000;\n    localparam BAUD = 100000;          // 16 clocks per bit\n    localparam BIT_NS = 10200;         // the sender's bit: 2 % longer than 10 us\n\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    reg rx = 1'b1;\n    wire [7:0] data;\n    wire valid;\n    wire frame_error;\n\n    uart_rx #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .BAUD(BAUD)) dut (.clk(clk), .rx(rx), .data(data), .valid(valid), .frame_error(frame_error));\n\n    always #312.5 clk = ~clk;          // 1.6 MHz\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer bytes = 0;\n    integer frame_errors = 0;\n    reg [7:0] last = 8'h00;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (valid) begin\n            bytes = bytes + 1;\n            last = data;\n        end\n        if (frame_error) frame_errors = frame_errors + 1;\n        if (valid && frame_error) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: valid and frame_error together\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n    end\n\n    task send(input [7:0] value, input stop);\n        integer i;\n        begin\n            rx = 1'b0;\n            #(BIT_NS);\n            for (i = 0; i < 8; i = i + 1) begin\n                rx = value[i];\n                #(BIT_NS);\n            end\n            rx = stop;\n            #(BIT_NS);\n            rx = 1'b1;\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    task expect_byte(input [7:0] value, input integer count);\n        begin\n            #(BIT_NS);\n            if (bytes != count) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: %0d bytes received, expected %0d\", bytes, count);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end else if (last !== value) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: received %h, expected %h\", last, value);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"uart_rx_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, uart_rx_tb);\n        #(BIT_NS * 2);\n\n        send(8'h55, 1'b1); expect_byte(8'h55, 1);\n        send(8'hA3, 1'b1); expect_byte(8'hA3, 2);\n        send(8'h00, 1'b1); expect_byte(8'h00, 3);\n        send(8'hFF, 1'b1); expect_byte(8'hFF, 4);\n\n        // A glitch of a fifth of a bit is not a start bit.\n        rx = 1'b0; #(BIT_NS / 5); rx = 1'b1;\n        #(BIT_NS * 12);\n        if (bytes != 4 || frame_errors != 0) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: a glitch produced a byte or an error (%0d bytes, %0d frame errors)\", bytes, frame_errors);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        // Low stop bit, then the line stays low (a break): one frame error, no byte.\n        send(8'h3C, 1'b0);\n        rx = 1'b0; #(BIT_NS * 20); rx = 1'b1;\n        #(BIT_NS * 2);\n        if (bytes != 4) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: a frame with a low stop bit or a break produced a byte\");\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (frame_errors != 1) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d frame errors, expected 1\", frame_errors);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        // Still receiving afterwards.\n        send(8'h96, 1'b1); expect_byte(8'h96, 5);\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    // A broken design must not hang the simulation.\n    initial begin\n        #5000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/uart_tx/README.md" : "# UART transmitter\n\nSends one byte per handshake over `tx`: start bit, eight data bits (least significant first),\nstop bit. Set `CLK_HZ` to your clock and `BAUD` to the rate; the bit time is rounded to whole\nclock cycles, so keep `CLK_HZ / BAUD` large (12 MHz / 115200 ≈ 104, an error of 0.2 %).\n\n```verilog\nuart_tx #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .BAUD(115200)) u_tx (\n    .clk(clk), .data(byte), .valid(send), .ready(tx_ready), .tx(uart_tx)\n);\n```\n\nA byte is taken in the cycle where `valid` and `ready` are both high. To send a string, step to\nthe next character on that cycle.\n",
    "cores/uart_tx/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Sends bytes over a serial line, 8N1, at any baud rate the clock allows. Valid/ready handshake.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 264.6,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 36,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 20\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 121.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 5,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 6,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 7,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 12,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 22\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 184.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 14,\n        \"DFF\" : 20,\n        \"LUT4\" : 77,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 30,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 13,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 3,\n        \"MUX2_LUT8\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 88.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 30\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 154.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 34,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 20\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"uart_tx\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"serial\",\n    \"rs232\",\n    \"uart\",\n    \"transmit\",\n    \"8n1\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Sendet Bytes über eine serielle Leitung, 8N1, mit jeder Baudrate, die der Takt erlaubt. Valid/Ready-Handshake.\",\n      \"name\" : \"UART-Sender\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"UART transmitter\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 115200,\n      \"description\" : \"Bits per second\",\n      \"name\" : \"BAUD\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Byte to send\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"data\",\n      \"width\" : 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"data holds a byte to send\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Takes a byte this cycle if valid is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Serial output, idles high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"tx\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"uart_tx\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/uart_tx/rtl/uart_tx.v" : "// UART transmitter, 8N1 (one start bit, eight data bits LSB first, one stop bit).\n//\n// Handshake: present a byte on `data` and raise `valid`; the byte is taken in the cycle where\n// `valid` and `ready` are both high. `ready` is low while a byte is on the line. `tx` idles high.\n// CLK_HZ / BAUD should be at least 2; the bit time is rounded to whole clock cycles.\n\nmodule uart_tx #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ = 12000000,\n    parameter BAUD   = 115200\n) (\n    input  wire       clk,\n    input  wire [7:0] data,\n    input  wire       valid,\n    output wire       ready,\n    output wire       tx\n);\n    localparam CLKS_PER_BIT = (CLK_HZ + BAUD / 2) / BAUD;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = CLKS_PER_BIT > 1 ? $clog2(CLKS_PER_BIT) : 1;\n\n    // Stop bit, data, start bit; shifted out from bit 0.\n    reg [9:0] shift = 10'h3FF;\n    reg [3:0] bits_left = 4'd0;\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n\n    assign ready = (bits_left == 4'd0);\n    assign tx = ready ? 1'b1 : shift[0];\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (ready) begin\n            if (valid) begin\n                shift <= {1'b1, data, 1'b0};\n                bits_left <= 4'd10;\n                clocks <= 0;\n            end\n        end else if (clocks == CLKS_PER_BIT - 1) begin\n            clocks <= 0;\n            shift <= {1'b1, shift[9:1]};\n            bits_left <= bits_left - 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/uart_tx/tb/uart_tx_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for uart_tx: sends bytes back to back and decodes the line bit by bit\n// in the middle of each bit time. Prints PASS or every failed check.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule uart_tx_tb;\n    localparam CLK_HZ = 1000000;\n    localparam BAUD = 100000;          // 10 clocks per bit\n    localparam CLKS_PER_BIT = 10;\n    localparam BYTES = 4;\n\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    reg [7:0] data = 8'h00;\n    reg valid = 1'b0;\n    wire ready;\n    wire tx;\n\n    uart_tx #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ), .BAUD(BAUD)) dut (.clk(clk), .data(data), .valid(valid), .ready(ready), .tx(tx));\n\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    reg [7:0] message [0:BYTES-1];\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer sent = 0;\n\n    // Feed the bytes through the handshake as fast as `ready` allows.\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (valid && ready) begin\n            sent <= sent + 1;\n            if (sent + 1 < BYTES) data <= message[sent + 1];\n            else valid <= 1'b0;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // The receiving side: wait for a start bit, then sample each bit in its middle.\n    task receive(output [7:0] byte_out, output framing_ok);\n        integer bit_index;\n        begin\n            @(negedge tx);\n            repeat (CLKS_PER_BIT / 2) @(posedge clk);\n            if (tx !== 1'b0) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: start bit not low in its middle\");\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            for (bit_index = 0; bit_index < 8; bit_index = bit_index + 1) begin\n                repeat (CLKS_PER_BIT) @(posedge clk);\n                byte_out[bit_index] = tx;\n            end\n            repeat (CLKS_PER_BIT) @(posedge clk);\n            framing_ok = (tx === 1'b1);\n        end\n    endtask\n\n    reg [7:0] received;\n    reg framing_ok;\n    integer n;\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"uart_tx_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, uart_tx_tb);\n        message[0] = 8'h55; message[1] = 8'hA3; message[2] = 8'h00; message[3] = 8'hFF;\n\n        repeat (3) @(posedge clk);\n        if (tx !== 1'b1 || ready !== 1'b1) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: idle line should be high and ready (tx=%b ready=%b)\", tx, ready);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        data <= message[0];\n        valid <= 1'b1;\n\n        for (n = 0; n < BYTES; n = n + 1) begin\n            receive(received, framing_ok);\n            if (received !== message[n]) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: byte %0d: received %h, expected %h\", n, received, message[n]);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n            if (!framing_ok) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: byte %0d: stop bit not high\", n);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end\n\n        // After the last byte the line goes idle and stays there.\n        repeat (CLKS_PER_BIT * 3) @(posedge clk);\n        if (tx !== 1'b1 || ready !== 1'b1) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: line not idle after the last byte (tx=%b ready=%b)\", tx, ready);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (sent != BYTES) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: %0d bytes taken by the handshake, expected %0d\", sent, BYTES);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #2000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/video_timing/README.md" : "# Video timing (VGA/DVI)\n\nGenerates the timing of a video mode from the pixel clock: `hsync`, `vsync`, `active` (the\nvisible area, \"data enable\" for DVI) and the position `x`, `y` of the pixel being sent. Compute\nthe colour from `x`/`y` and output it while `active`, black otherwise:\n\n```verilog\nvideo_timing u_timing (.clk(pixel_clk), .hsync(vga_hs), .vsync(vga_vs), .active(de),\n                       .frame_start(), .x(x), .y(y));\nassign {vga_r, vga_g, vga_b} = de ? {x[7:4], y[7:4], 4'h8} : 12'h000;   // a colour gradient\n```\n\nThe default is 640×480 at 60 Hz: pixel clock 25.175 MHz (a PLL making 25 MHz is fine for\nmost monitors). Other modes come from the parameters (e.g. 800×600 at 60 Hz: 800/40/128/88,\n600/1/4/23, positive syncs, 40 MHz). For DVI or HDMI the outputs still need TMDS encoding and\nserialisers, which are family-specific.\n\nHDMI modes (CEA-861, both syncs positive; set `H_SYNC_POSITIVE` and `V_SYNC_POSITIVE` to 1):\n\n| Mode | Pixel clock | H active/front/sync/back | V active/front/sync/back |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 720p60 | 74.25 MHz | 1280/110/40/220 | 720/5/5/20 |\n| 1080p30 | 74.25 MHz | 1920/88/44/148 | 1080/4/5/36 |\n| 1080p60 | 148.5 MHz | 1920/88/44/148 | 1080/4/5/36 |\n\nOn AMD Xilinx 7-series, `hdmi_out` encodes and serialises them.\n",
    "cores/video_timing/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Sync, active-area and pixel-position signals for a VGA or DVI/HDMI display; 640×480 at 60 Hz by default, any mode by parameters.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 231.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 100,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 44\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 102.4,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 3,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 27,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 13,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 34,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 20,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 62\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 226.3,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 76,\n        \"DFF\" : 44,\n        \"LUT4\" : 12\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 91.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 97\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 112.8,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 104,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 44\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"video_timing\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"vga\",\n    \"dvi\",\n    \"hdmi\",\n    \"video\",\n    \"display\",\n    \"sync\",\n    \"timing\",\n    \"640x480\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Sync-, Aktivbereich- und Pixelpositionssignale für ein VGA- oder DVI/HDMI-Display; standardmäßig 640×480 bei 60 Hz, jeder Modus über Parameter.\",\n      \"name\" : \"Video-Timing (VGA/DVI)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"Video timing (VGA/DVI)\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 640,\n      \"description\" : \"Visible pixels per line\",\n      \"name\" : \"H_ACTIVE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 16,\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal front porch\",\n      \"name\" : \"H_FRONT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 96,\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync width\",\n      \"name\" : \"H_SYNC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 48,\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal back porch\",\n      \"name\" : \"H_BACK\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 480,\n      \"description\" : \"Visible lines\",\n      \"name\" : \"V_ACTIVE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 10,\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical front porch\",\n      \"name\" : \"V_FRONT\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 2,\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync width\",\n      \"name\" : \"V_SYNC\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 33,\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical back porch\",\n      \"name\" : \"V_BACK\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: hsync is high during sync\",\n      \"name\" : \"H_SYNC_POSITIVE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 0,\n      \"description\" : \"1: vsync is high during sync\",\n      \"name\" : \"V_SYNC_POSITIVE\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel clock (25.175 MHz for 640×480 at 60 Hz)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Horizontal sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"hsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Vertical sync\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"vsync\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The pixel is in the visible area (data enable)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"active\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"One-cycle pulse at the first pixel of a frame\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"frame_start\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel column (0 … H_ACTIVE - 1 when active)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"x\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel row (0 … V_ACTIVE - 1 when active)\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"y\",\n      \"width\" : 10\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"video_timing\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.1\"\n}\n",
    "cores/video_timing/rtl/video_timing.v" : "// Video timing generator for VGA and DVI/HDMI: horizontal and vertical sync, the active area\n// (`active`, also called data enable) and the position of the pixel being drawn. Defaults:\n// 640×480 at 60 Hz, which needs a 25.175 MHz pixel clock (25 MHz works on almost every monitor).\n//\n// `x` and `y` count from 0 at the top left of the active area; outside it they keep counting\n// through the blanking (x up to H_TOTAL - 1). `frame_start` pulses at x = 0, y = 0. Syncs follow\n// the SYNC_POSITIVE parameters (640×480 uses negative syncs for both).\n\nmodule video_timing #(\n    parameter H_ACTIVE = 640,\n    parameter H_FRONT  = 16,\n    parameter H_SYNC   = 96,\n    parameter H_BACK   = 48,\n    parameter V_ACTIVE = 480,\n    parameter V_FRONT  = 10,\n    parameter V_SYNC   = 2,\n    parameter V_BACK   = 33,\n    parameter H_SYNC_POSITIVE = 0,\n    parameter V_SYNC_POSITIVE = 0\n) (\n    input  wire clk,\n    output reg  hsync = 1'b0,\n    output reg  vsync = 1'b0,\n    output reg  active = 1'b0,\n    output reg  frame_start = 1'b0,\n    output reg  [$clog2(H_ACTIVE + H_FRONT + H_SYNC + H_BACK)-1:0] x = 0,\n    output reg  [$clog2(V_ACTIVE + V_FRONT + V_SYNC + V_BACK)-1:0] y = 0\n);\n    localparam H_TOTAL = H_ACTIVE + H_FRONT + H_SYNC + H_BACK;\n    localparam V_TOTAL = V_ACTIVE + V_FRONT + V_SYNC + V_BACK;\n\n    // The position of the next pixel; the outputs describe the current one.\n    reg [$clog2(H_TOTAL)-1:0] h = 0;\n    reg [$clog2(V_TOTAL)-1:0] v = 0;\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (h == H_TOTAL - 1) begin\n            h <= 0;\n            v <= (v == V_TOTAL - 1) ? 0 : v + 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            h <= h + 1'b1;\n        end\n        x <= h;\n        y <= v;\n        active <= (h < H_ACTIVE) && (v < V_ACTIVE);\n        frame_start <= (h == 0) && (v == 0);\n        hsync <= ((h >= H_ACTIVE + H_FRONT) && (h < H_ACTIVE + H_FRONT + H_SYNC)) == (H_SYNC_POSITIVE != 0);\n        vsync <= ((v >= V_ACTIVE + V_FRONT) && (v < V_ACTIVE + V_FRONT + V_SYNC)) == (V_SYNC_POSITIVE != 0);\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/video_timing/tb/video_timing_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for video_timing in a tiny mode (8×4 active, 15×9 total — neither a power of two, so the counters must wrap by themselves, positive\n// horizontal and negative vertical sync): over three frames, every cycle's x, y, active area,\n// both syncs and the frame start are compared with a reference count.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule video_timing_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #5 clk = ~clk;\n\n    wire hsync, vsync, active, frame_start;\n    wire [3:0] x;\n    wire [3:0] y;\n\n    video_timing #(\n        .H_ACTIVE(8), .H_FRONT(2), .H_SYNC(3), .H_BACK(2),\n        .V_ACTIVE(4), .V_FRONT(1), .V_SYNC(2), .V_BACK(2),\n        .H_SYNC_POSITIVE(1), .V_SYNC_POSITIVE(0)\n    ) dut (.clk(clk), .hsync(hsync), .vsync(vsync), .active(active), .frame_start(frame_start), .x(x), .y(y));\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n    integer hx = 0, vy = 0, frames = 0, cycle;\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"video_timing_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, video_timing_tb);\n        @(posedge frame_start);\n        for (cycle = 0; cycle < 15 * 9 * 3; cycle = cycle + 1) begin\n            #1;\n            if (frame_start) frames = frames + 1;\n            if (x !== hx || y !== vy) begin $display(\"FAIL: position (%0d, %0d), expected (%0d, %0d)\", x, y, hx, vy); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (active !== (hx < 8 && vy < 4)) begin $display(\"FAIL: active %b at (%0d, %0d)\", active, hx, vy); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (hsync !== (hx >= 10 && hx < 13)) begin $display(\"FAIL: hsync %b at x %0d\", hsync, hx); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (vsync !== !(vy >= 5 && vy < 7)) begin $display(\"FAIL: vsync %b at y %0d\", vsync, vy); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (frame_start !== (hx == 0 && vy == 0)) begin $display(\"FAIL: frame_start %b at (%0d, %0d)\", frame_start, hx, vy); errors = errors + 1; end\n            if (errors > 5) cycle = 1000;\n            @(posedge clk);\n            hx = hx + 1;\n            if (hx == 15) begin hx = 0; vy = (vy + 1) % 9; end\n        end\n        if (frames != 3) begin $display(\"FAIL: %0d frame starts in three frames\", frames); errors = errors + 1; end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #100000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/ws2812/README.md" : "# WS2812 LED driver\n\nSends colours to a chain of WS2812 or WS2812B LEDs (\"NeoPixels\"). Give it one pixel per\nhandshake — `rgb` is `{red, green, blue}`, the core sends it in the green-red-blue order the\nLEDs expect — the first pixel goes to the LED nearest to the FPGA. Stop handing pixels over and,\n`RESET_US` later, `idle` rises: the LEDs show the new frame, and the next pixel starts at the\nfirst LED again.\n\n```verilog\nws2812 #(.CLK_HZ(CLK_HZ)) u_leds (\n    .clk(clk), .rgb(pixel), .valid(pixel_valid), .ready(pixel_ready), .dout(led_data), .idle(frame_shown)\n);\n```\n\nTiming: 0.4 / 0.8 µs high for 0 / 1, 1.25 µs per bit, rounded to the clock — use 8 MHz or\nmore. The LEDs run at 5 V; most accept the FPGA's 3.3 V data line when the first LED is close,\notherwise add a level shifter.\n",
    "cores/ws2812/core.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"Drives chains of WS2812/WS2812B (NeoPixel) RGB LEDs over one wire: pixel-by-pixel handshake, automatic latch after the last pixel.\",\n  \"families\" : {\n    \"ecp5\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"45k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 157.2,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 102,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 55\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gatemate\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"CCGM1A1\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 57.5,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"CPE_ADDF\" : 1,\n        \"CPE_ADDF2\" : 30,\n        \"CPE_BRIDGE\" : 21,\n        \"CPE_CPLINES\" : 82,\n        \"CPE_FF\" : 80,\n        \"CPE_LT\" : 135\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"gowin\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"GW1NR-LV9QN88PC6/I5\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 168.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ALU\" : 34,\n        \"DFF\" : 55,\n        \"LUT4\" : 61,\n        \"MUX2_LUT5\" : 8,\n        \"MUX2_LUT6\" : 3,\n        \"MUX2_LUT7\" : 1\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"ice40\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"up5k\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 62.1,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"ICESTORM_LC\" : 83\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    },\n    \"machxo2\" : {\n      \"device\" : \"4000HC\",\n      \"fmaxMHz\" : 84.9,\n      \"resources\" : {\n        \"TRELLIS_COMB\" : 144,\n        \"TRELLIS_FF\" : 55\n      },\n      \"tested\" : true\n    }\n  },\n  \"id\" : \"ws2812\",\n  \"keywords\" : [\n    \"ws2812\",\n    \"ws2812b\",\n    \"neopixel\",\n    \"rgb\",\n    \"led\",\n    \"strip\",\n    \"addressable\"\n  ],\n  \"licence\" : \"CC0-1.0\",\n  \"localized\" : {\n    \"de\" : {\n      \"description\" : \"Steuert Ketten von WS2812/WS2812B-RGB-LEDs (NeoPixel) über eine Leitung: Handshake pro Pixel, automatisches Übernehmen nach dem letzten Pixel.\",\n      \"name\" : \"WS2812-LED-Treiber\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"name\" : \"WS2812 LED driver\",\n  \"parameters\" : [\n    {\n      \"default\" : 12000000,\n      \"description\" : \"Clock frequency in Hz (8 MHz or more)\",\n      \"name\" : \"CLK_HZ\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"default\" : 300,\n      \"description\" : \"Low time that ends a frame, in µs (WS2812B: 280 or more)\",\n      \"name\" : \"RESET_US\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"ports\" : [\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Clock\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"clk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Pixel colour {red, green, blue}\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"rgb\",\n      \"width\" : 24\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"rgb holds the next pixel\",\n      \"dir\" : \"in\",\n      \"name\" : \"valid\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Takes a pixel this cycle if valid is high\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"ready\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"Data line to the first LED\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"dout\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\" : \"The line has been low for RESET_US: the LEDs show the frame\",\n      \"dir\" : \"out\",\n      \"name\" : \"idle\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"top\" : \"ws2812\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.0.0\"\n}\n",
    "cores/ws2812/rtl/ws2812.v" : "// WS2812 / WS2812B (\"NeoPixel\") LED chain driver. Each pixel is 24 bits sent as green, red, blue,\n// most significant bit first, on one wire: a 1 is 0.8 µs high + 0.45 µs low, a 0 is 0.4 µs high +\n// 0.85 µs low (1.25 µs per bit, within the ±150 ns the LEDs accept).\n//\n// Hand the pixels over one by one with `rgb` = {red, green, blue} and `valid`; a pixel is taken\n// when `valid` and `ready` are both high. When no pixel follows, the line stays low and after\n// RESET_US the LEDs show what they received: `idle` goes high, and the next pixel starts again at\n// the first LED.\n\nmodule ws2812 #(\n    parameter CLK_HZ   = 12000000,\n    parameter RESET_US = 300\n) (\n    input  wire        clk,\n    input  wire [23:0] rgb,\n    input  wire        valid,\n    output wire        ready,\n    output reg         dout = 1'b0,\n    output wire        idle\n);\n    // Clock cycles, rounded to the nearest cycle.\n    localparam T0H   = (CLK_HZ * 2 + 2500000) / 5000000;     // 0.4 µs\n    localparam T1H   = (CLK_HZ * 4 + 2500000) / 5000000;     // 0.8 µs\n    localparam TBIT  = (CLK_HZ * 5 + 2000000) / 4000000;     // 1.25 µs\n    localparam TRESET = (CLK_HZ / 1000000) * RESET_US;\n    localparam COUNTER_BITS = $clog2(TRESET > TBIT ? TRESET + 1 : TBIT + 1);\n\n    reg        sending = 1'b0;\n    reg [23:0] shift = 24'd0;               // green, red, blue\n    reg [4:0]  bit_index = 5'd0;\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] clocks = 0;\n    reg [COUNTER_BITS-1:0] low_clocks = 0;\n\n    assign ready = !sending;\n    assign idle = !sending && (low_clocks == TRESET);\n\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        if (!sending) begin\n            dout <= 1'b0;\n            if (low_clocks != TRESET) low_clocks <= low_clocks + 1'b1;\n            if (valid) begin\n                sending <= 1'b1;\n                shift <= {rgb[15:8], rgb[23:16], rgb[7:0]};\n                bit_index <= 5'd0;\n                clocks <= 0;\n                dout <= 1'b1;\n                low_clocks <= 0;\n            end\n        end else begin\n            clocks <= clocks + 1'b1;\n            if (clocks == (shift[23] ? T1H : T0H) - 1) dout <= 1'b0;\n            if (clocks == TBIT - 1) begin\n                clocks <= 0;\n                if (bit_index == 5'd23) begin\n                    sending <= 1'b0;\n                end else begin\n                    bit_index <= bit_index + 1'b1;\n                    shift <= {shift[22:0], 1'b0};\n                    dout <= 1'b1;\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "cores/ws2812/tb/ws2812_tb.v" : "// Self-checking testbench for ws2812 at 20 MHz: three pixels back to back, decoded from the\n// line by pulse width the way an LED does; high times and bit periods must be within the\n// datasheet tolerances, the colour order on the wire must be green, red, blue, and `idle` must\n// rise RESET_US after the last bit — then a second frame starts again at the first LED.\n\n`timescale 1ns / 1ps\n\nmodule ws2812_tb;\n    reg clk = 1'b0;\n    always #25 clk = ~clk;             // 20 MHz\n\n    reg  [23:0] rgb = 24'd0;\n    reg         valid = 1'b0;\n    wire        ready, dout, idle;\n\n    ws2812 #(.CLK_HZ(20000000), .RESET_US(50)) dut (.clk(clk), .rgb(rgb), .valid(valid), .ready(ready), .dout(dout), .idle(idle));\n\n    integer errors = 0;\n\n    // The receiving LED chain: bits by pulse width; a low time of over 50 us ends the frame.\n    reg [23:0] received [0:7];\n    integer bits = 0, pixels = 0, frames = 0;\n    realtime rise = 0, last_rise = 0;\n    always @(posedge dout) begin\n        if (last_rise != 0 && $realtime - last_rise < 20000) begin\n            if ($realtime - last_rise < 1100 || $realtime - last_rise > 1400) begin\n                $display(\"FAIL: bit period %0.0f ns\", $realtime - last_rise);\n                errors = errors + 1;\n            end\n        end else if (last_rise != 0) begin\n            frames = frames + 1;           // a reset gap: the next bit is LED 0 again\n        end\n        rise = $realtime;\n        last_rise = $realtime;\n    end\n    realtime last_fall = 0;\n    always @(negedge dout) begin\n        last_fall = $realtime;\n        if (rise != 0) begin\n            if ($realtime - rise >= 600) begin\n                if ($realtime - rise > 950) begin $display(\"FAIL: a 1 high for %0.0f ns\", $realtime - rise); errors = errors + 1; end\n                received[pixels] = {received[pixels][22:0], 1'b1};\n            end else begin\n                if ($realtime - rise < 250) begin $display(\"FAIL: a 0 high for %0.0f ns\", $realtime - rise); errors = errors + 1; end\n                received[pixels] = {received[pixels][22:0], 1'b0};\n            end\n            bits = bits + 1;\n            if (bits == 24) begin bits = 0; pixels = pixels + 1; end\n        end\n    end\n\n    reg [23:0] colours [0:2];     // {r, g, b}\n    integer sent = 0;\n    always @(posedge clk) if (valid && ready) begin\n        sent <= sent + 1;\n        if (sent + 1 < 3) rgb <= colours[sent + 1];\n        else valid <= 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        $dumpfile(\"ws2812_tb.vcd\");\n        $dumpvars(0, ws2812_tb);\n        colours[0] = 24'hFF0000;   // red\n        colours[1] = 24'h00A501;   // green-ish with a blue LSB\n        colours[2] = 24'h12345E;\n        #100000;                   // longer than a reset: starts idle\n        if (!idle || !ready || dout) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: not idle at the start (idle %b, ready %b, dout %b)\", idle, ready, dout);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        @(negedge clk);\n        rgb = colours[0];\n        valid = 1'b1;\n        wait (pixels == 3);\n        @(posedge idle);\n        // idle after the last bit's 1.25 us slot plus RESET_US.\n        if ($realtime - last_fall < 50000 || $realtime - last_fall > 52000) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: idle %0.0f ns after the last pulse, expected about 50.5 us\", $realtime - last_fall);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (received[0] !== 24'h00FF00 || received[1] !== 24'hA50001 || received[2] !== 24'h34125E) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: LEDs received %h %h %h (GRB), expected 00ff00 a50001 34125e\", received[0], received[1], received[2]);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n\n        // A second frame: one pixel.\n        @(negedge clk);\n        rgb = 24'h0000FF;\n        sent = 2;\n        valid = 1'b1;\n        wait (pixels == 4);\n        if (frames != 1 || received[3] !== 24'h0000FF) begin\n            $display(\"FAIL: second frame: %0d reset gaps, pixel %h\", frames, received[3]);\n            errors = errors + 1;\n        end\n        if (errors == 0) $display(\"PASS\");\n        $finish;\n    end\n\n    initial begin\n        #1000000;\n        $display(\"FAIL: timeout\");\n        $finish;\n    end\nendmodule\n",
    "pack.json" : "{\n  \"description\" : \"GateLab's own basic cores, each with a self-checking testbench: UART, SPI, I²C, debouncer, edge detector, FIFOs, clock divider, PWM, seven-segment display, WS2812 LEDs, VGA/DVI timing, sprite, text, bar graph and analogue clock layers, I2S audio (clocks, transmitter, receiver) and a sine tone generator, SPI registers for a microcontroller, TMDS encoding, an HDMI/DVI encoder with audio and HDMI/DVI outputs for AMD Xilinx 7-series, Gowin, ECP5 and GateMate, Ethernet over an RMII, MII or RGMII PHY (MAC, MDIO, ARP, ping, UDP and stacks of them all), and keyboard and mouse from GateLab over the board's UART. CC0 1.0: no conditions, no attribution needed.\",\n  \"documentation\" : \"https://github.com/richardklingler/fpgacores\",\n  \"id\" : \"gatelab.essentials\",\n  \"maintainer\" : \"GateLab\",\n  \"name\" : \"GateLab Essentials\",\n  \"schema\" : 1,\n  \"type\" : \"library\",\n  \"version\" : \"1.13.0\"\n}\n"
  },
  "schema" : 1
}
